  {
    "id": "T1Q1",
    "type": "multi",
    "stem": "Contoso needs workspace-level version control with branching for its Research division. Assume both Git providers are permitted by tenant governance and all connection prerequisites are met. Which TWO offered configurations use a supported Fabric Git provider?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Store at the semantic models and reports in Data Lake Gen2 storage."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Modify the settings of the Research workspaces to use a GitHub repository."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Modify the settings of the Research division workspaces to use an Azure Repos repository."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Store all the semantic models and reports in Microsoft OneDrive."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": "cs_contoso",
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q1",
    "sourceId": "T1Q1",
    "gradingEnabled": true,
    "scientificStatus": "APPROVED_CORRECTED",
    "r10Status": "AMBIGUOUS",
    "reviewNote": "Current provider list supports both GitHub and Azure DevOps.",
    "remaining": "",
    "explanation": "B and C use supported Git providers, GitHub and Azure DevOps/Azure Repos respectively. A and D are file storage choices and are not the offered workspace Git integration configurations. The shared original case and source single-choice key are preserved separately.",
    "answer": "BC",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "You need to ensure that Contoso can use version control to meet the data analytics requirements and the general requirements.\nWhat should you do?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Store at the semantic models and reports in Data Lake Gen2 storage.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Modify the settings of the Research workspaces to use a GitHub repository.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Modify the settings of the Research division workspaces to use an Azure Repos repository.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Store all the semantic models and reports in Microsoft OneDrive.",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The case study asks for *\"version control that supports branching\"* for the Research division semantic models and reports. Fabric **Git integration** is configured per workspace, under **Workspace settings → Git integration**, so the action is to point the **Research division workspaces** at a Git repository.\n\n- **A / D** – Data Lake Gen2 and OneDrive are file stores. Neither gives you branches, merges or commit history, and neither is wired into Fabric's source-control experience.\n- **B vs C** – when this question was written the doc said outright: *\"Currently, only Git in Azure Repos with the same tenant as the Fabric tenant is supported.\"* That made **C** the only workable answer, and the community is 97% on C.\n\n**Doc drift (worth knowing).** Microsoft added **GitHub** support in September 2024, so the current doc lists *both* Azure Repos and GitHub as supported providers. On today's docs B is no longer wrong on the technology — the remaining discriminator is wording: the requirement is scoped to the *Research division* workspaces (C), while B says \"the Research workspaces\". Pick **C**; it is right under both the old and the current documentation."
    },
