      {
        "title": "Microsoft documentation",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/governance/domains"
      }
    ],
    "reviewKind": "documentation-approved derived practice"
  },
  {
    "id": "T1Q3",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You need to refresh the Orders table of the Online Sales department. The solution must meet the semantic model requirements.\nWhat should you include in the solution?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "an Azure Data Factory pipeline that executes a Stored procedure activity to retrieve the maximum value of the OrderID column in the destination lakehouse"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "an Azure Data Factory pipeline that executes a Stored procedure activity to retrieve the minimum value of the OrderID column in the destination lakehouse"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "an Azure Data Factory pipeline that executes a dataflow to retrieve the minimum value of the OrderID column in the destination lakehouse"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "an Azure Data Factory pipeline that executes a dataflow to retrieve the maximum value of the OrderID column in the destination lakehouse"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": "cs_contoso",
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q3",
    "sourceId": "T1Q3",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "BLOCKED_SOURCE",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Designed a complete watermark task instead of treating MAX alone as a load.",
    "remaining": "Reconcile this derived pipeline variant with the original Import model requirement; verify pipeline activity and transaction/retry behavior before approval.",
    "explanation": "The semantic model requirement is *\"the number of rows added to the Orders table during refreshes must be minimized\"*, and the case study tells you that **`OrderID` represents the sequence in which orders are created**. So an incremental load keyed on `OrderID` is the goal: read the **maximum** `OrderID` already in the destination, then pull only rows above it (a high-water-mark load).\n\n- **Maximum, not minimum** – the minimum `OrderID` is the *oldest* order; filtering on it would re-load almost everything. That rules out **B** and **C**.\n- **Dataflow, not stored procedure** – Microsoft documents this precise pattern as a dataflow tutorial: *\"Set up incremental refresh with Dataflow Gen2\"*, whose worked example adds a query that reads the maximum key from the data destination and filters the source against it. That leaves **D** over **A**.",
    "answer": "D",
    "officialAnswer": "D",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "D",
      "answer": "D",
      "stem": "You need to refresh the Orders table of the Online Sales department. The solution must meet the semantic model requirements.\nWhat should you include in the solution?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "an Azure Data Factory pipeline that executes a Stored procedure activity to retrieve the maximum value of the OrderID column in the destination lakehouse",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "an Azure Data Factory pipeline that executes a Stored procedure activity to retrieve the minimum value of the OrderID column in the destination lakehouse",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "an Azure Data Factory pipeline that executes a dataflow to retrieve the minimum value of the OrderID column in the destination lakehouse",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "an Azure Data Factory pipeline that executes a dataflow to retrieve the maximum value of the OrderID column in the destination lakehouse",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The semantic model requirement is *\"the number of rows added to the Orders table during refreshes must be minimized\"*, and the case study tells you that **`OrderID` represents the sequence in which orders are created**. So an incremental load keyed on `OrderID` is the goal: read the **maximum** `OrderID` already in the destination, then pull only rows above it (a high-water-mark load).\n\n- **Maximum, not minimum** – the minimum `OrderID` is the *oldest* order; filtering on it would re-load almost everything. That rules out **B** and **C**.\n- **Dataflow, not stored procedure** – Microsoft documents this precise pattern as a dataflow tutorial: *\"Set up incremental refresh with Dataflow Gen2\"*, whose worked example adds a query that reads the maximum key from the data destination and filters the source against it. That leaves **D** over **A**."
    },
