          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "external_table(ResearchProduct)",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The case study puts the shortcut **`ResearchProduct` inside `Lakehouse1`**, and requires that all Research division data *\"be presented as managed tables in Lakehouse explorer\"*. A shortcut created under the lakehouse's **Tables** area is surfaced as a table, so it can be queried by name with Spark SQL:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM Lakehouse1.ResearchProduct\")\n```\n\nThe OneLake shortcuts doc shows exactly these two forms side by side — `spark.read.format(\"delta\").load(\"Tables/MyShortcut\")` and `spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM MyLakehouse.MyShortcut\")`.\n\n- **A** is the right *API* but the wrong *path*: the shortcut lives at `Tables/ResearchProduct`, not `Tables/productline1/ResearchProduct`. Productline1 is the workspace name, which is not part of the lakehouse's relative table path.\n- **C / D** – `external_table(...)` is not a Fabric notebook construct at all (and C is missing a closing quote)."
    },
