          "options": [
            "Build Reports on the default dataset",
            "Read All Apache Spark",
            "Read All SQL analytics endpoint data"
          ],
          "answer": "Read All Apache Spark"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You need to assign permissions for the data store in the AnalyticsPOC workspace. The solution must meet the security requirements.\nWhich additional permissions should you assign when you share the data store? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**Read All Apache Spark — Build Reports on the default dataset — Read All Apache Spark.**\n\nThese three checkboxes are the *additional* permissions offered when you share a lakehouse or warehouse. Sharing by itself already grants plain **Read** on the item; each extra box widens that. Match each one to the security requirement:\n\n| Group | Requirement in the case study | Grant |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **DataEngineers** | *\"read from and write to the data store. **No access must be granted to datasets or reports**.\"* | **Read All Apache Spark** |\n| **DataAnalysts** | *\"create Power BI reports by using the semantic models created by the analytics engineers.\"* | **Build Reports on the default dataset** |\n| **DataScientists** | *\"read from the data store, but not write to it. **They will access the data by using a Spark notebook**.\"* | **Read All Apache Spark** |\n\n- **DataEngineers** – the *\"no access to datasets or reports\"* clause eliminates *Build Reports on the default dataset* outright. Of the two remaining, Spark is the one that matches an engineer's read/write workload; the SQL analytics endpoint over a lakehouse is **read-only**, so it cannot satisfy \"write to\".\n- **DataScientists** – the requirement names the access path explicitly (\"by using a Spark notebook\"), so it is *Read All Apache Spark*. This grant is read-only on its own, which also satisfies \"but not write to it\".\n\n*Note:* the community splits on DataEngineers vs DataScientists (some swap Spark and SQL endpoint), but the two highest-voted comments (+91, +40) both land on Spark for the engineers, and the \"Spark notebook\" wording for the scientists is decisive."
    },
