          "answer": "WHEN ListPrice BETWEEN 50 AND 1000) THEN 'medium'"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You need to resolve the issue with the pricing group classification.\nHow should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**`VIEW` — `CASE` — `WHEN ListPrice BETWEEN 50 AND 1000) THEN 'medium'`.**\n\n**The PDF's answer-key image is wrong on two of the three blanks** (it marks `TABLE` and `> 50 AND < 1000`). Here is the reasoning.\n\n**Blank 1 — `VIEW`.** The requirement is *\"The product pricing group logic must be maintained by the analytics engineers in a **single location**. The pricing group data must be made available in the data store for T-SQL queries **and in the default semantic model**.\"*\n- A **view** stores the logic once and re-evaluates it on every read, so it can never drift from `dbo.Products` — that is what \"single location\" means. Views in a Fabric warehouse are queryable with T-SQL and are picked up by the default semantic model, which satisfies both halves of the requirement (the case study separately states that the semantic models *\"must include only tables **or views** from the dimensional model\"*).\n- A **TABLE** (via CTAS) materialises a **copy**. The moment a list price changes, the copy is stale, and you now maintain the logic *and* a refresh job — the opposite of a single location.\n- **FUNCTION** / **PROCEDURE** do not fit the `CREATE x [dbo].[ProductsWithPricingGroup] AS SELECT ...` shape shown, and a stored procedure is not exposed to the default semantic model.\n\n**Blank 2 — `CASE`.** The block is `WHEN ... THEN ... END AS PricingGroup`, which is a searched `CASE` expression. `COALESCE` returns the first non-null argument, `IIF` takes exactly one condition, and `SET` is not an expression.\n\n**Blank 3 — `BETWEEN`.** The spec is: low `<= 50`, medium `> 50 and <= 1000`, high `> 1000`. Check each option against **1,000**, the boundary that breaks two of them:\n\n| Option | ListPrice = 1000 | Verdict |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `> 50 AND < 1000` | no branch matches → **NULL** | wrong |\n| `>= 50 AND < 1000` | no branch matches → **NULL** | wrong |\n| `BETWEEN 50 AND 1000` | `BETWEEN` is inclusive → **'medium'** | correct |\n\n`BETWEEN` also includes 50, which looks like an overlap with the low group — but it is not one, because **`CASE` evaluates its `WHEN` clauses in order and stops at the first match**. `ListPrice = 50` is caught by the preceding `WHEN ListPrice <= 50 THEN 'low'`, so it never reaches the `BETWEEN`. The documented behaviour: *\"Evaluates, in the order specified, `Boolean_expression` for each `WHEN` clause … Returns `result_expression` of the first `Boolean_expression` that evaluates to TRUE.\"*\n\n*Note:* the top comment (+77) makes exactly this argument. A second comment (+34) complains that all three options are wrong because none literally reads `> 50 AND <= 1000` — true, but it overlooks the short-circuit, which makes `BETWEEN` behave correctly. A third commenter who sat the exam reports Microsoft has since changed the live item to `WHEN(ListPrice > 50 AND ListPrice <= 1000)`; if you see that variant, pick it."
