  {
    "id": "T1Q9",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "Which type of data store should you recommend in the AnalyticsPOC workspace?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "a data lake"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "a warehouse"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "a lakehouse"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "an external Hive metastore"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": "cs_litware",
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q9",
    "sourceId": "T1Q9",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "The technical requirements for the AnalyticsPOC data store call for **\"semi-structured and unstructured data\"**, and state that files loaded to OneLake *\"will be stored in the Parquet format and will meet Delta Lake specifications\"*. A **lakehouse** is the Fabric item built for exactly that: Delta/Parquet files in OneLake, with both a Files area (any format) and a Tables area, readable by Spark and by T-SQL through its SQL analytics endpoint.\n\n- **A** – \"a data lake\" is not a Fabric item you create; OneLake is the lake, and the lakehouse is how you use it.\n- **B** – a **warehouse** is relational and fully structured. It has no place to put unstructured files.\n- **D** – an external Hive metastore is a metadata catalog, not a data store, and Fabric does not use one.\n\nThe community is 100% on C.",
    "answer": "C",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "Which type of data store should you recommend in the AnalyticsPOC workspace?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "a data lake",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "a warehouse",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "a lakehouse",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "an external Hive metastore",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The technical requirements for the AnalyticsPOC data store call for **\"semi-structured and unstructured data\"**, and state that files loaded to OneLake *\"will be stored in the Parquet format and will meet Delta Lake specifications\"*. A **lakehouse** is the Fabric item built for exactly that: Delta/Parquet files in OneLake, with both a Files area (any format) and a Tables area, readable by Spark and by T-SQL through its SQL analytics endpoint.\n\n- **A** – \"a data lake\" is not a Fabric item you create; OneLake is the lake, and the lakehouse is how you use it.\n- **B** – a **warehouse** is relational and fully structured. It has no place to put unstructured files.\n- **D** – an external Hive metastore is a metadata catalog, not a data store, and Fabric does not use one.\n\nThe community is 100% on C."
    },
