        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Only **A** is valid T-SQL. The rule to apply is the logical order of evaluation: `FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY`. `WHERE` filters **rows** before grouping and cannot see aggregates; `HAVING` filters **groups** after aggregation and cannot see `SELECT` aliases.\n\n```sql\nSELECT ProductID, ProductName, SUM(Amount) AS TotalAmount\nFROM Staging.Sales\nWHERE DATEPART(YEAR, SaleDate) = '2023'   -- row filter, pre-aggregation\nGROUP BY ProductID, ProductName\nHAVING SUM(Amount) > 10000              -- group filter, repeats the aggregate\n```\n\n- **B** – puts the year test in `HAVING`, but `SaleDate` is neither in the `GROUP BY` list nor inside an aggregate, so it is not valid in a `HAVING` clause.\n- **C** – uses `SUM(Amount)` inside `WHERE`. Aggregates are not allowed there (`WHERE` runs before grouping), and there is no `GROUP BY` to support the aggregate in the `SELECT` list either.\n- **D** – references the alias `TotalAmount` in `HAVING`. `SELECT` aliases are only visible to `ORDER BY`; in `HAVING` you must repeat `SUM(Amount)`."
    },
