            "WHERE X = 1"
          ],
          "answer": "WHERE X = 1"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a data warehouse that contains a table named Stage.Customers. Stage.Customers contains all the customer record updates from a\ncustomer relationship management (CRM) system. There can be multiple updates per customer.\nYou need to write a T-SQL query that will return the customer ID, name. postal code, and the last updated time of the most recent row for each\ncustomer ID.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**`ROW_NUMBER()` — `WHERE X = 1`.** This is the canonical \"latest row per key\" pattern: number the rows inside each customer, newest first, then keep number 1.\n\n```sql\nWITH CUSTOMERBASE AS (\n  SELECT [CustomerID], [CustomerName], [PostalCode], [LastUpdated]\n    , x = ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY CustomerID ORDER BY LastUpdated DESC)\n  FROM [LakehousePOC].[dbo].[CustomerChanges]\n)\nSELECT CustomerID, CustomerName, PostalCode, LastUpdated\nFROM CUSTOMERBASE\nWHERE X = 1;\n```\n\n**Blank 1.** `ROW_NUMBER()` *\"numbers the output of a result set … starting at 1 for the first row in each partition.\"* With `ORDER BY LastUpdated DESC`, row 1 is the most recent update for that `CustomerID`.\n- `LAST_VALUE()` returns a *value* from the window frame, not a rank, so there would be nothing to filter on — and by default its frame ends at the current row, a classic trap.\n- `NTILE()` splits a partition into N buckets; it requires an argument and does not identify a single newest row.\n\n**Blank 2.** Window functions cannot be referenced in `WHERE` in the same query level — that is precisely why the `ROW_NUMBER()` is computed in the CTE and filtered in the outer query. `WHERE X = 1` keeps the newest row per customer.\n- `Having Max(LastUpdated) = 1` is nonsense: there is no `GROUP BY`, and comparing a datetime to 1 is a type error.\n- `WHERE LastUpdated = Max(LastUpdated)` puts an aggregate in `WHERE`, which T-SQL rejects.\n\nThe PDF supplies only \"Box 1: ROW_NUMBER()\" with no key for box 2; the top comments (+39, +17, +5) all give the full query above."
    },
