    "r10Status": "AMBIGUOUS",
    "reviewNote": "Removed vague summary claim with an explicit non-guarantee statement.",
    "remaining": "Read current Microsoft powerbiclient QuickVisualize docs and execute notebook widget; check temporary/saved report lifecycle.",
    "explanation": "**No — Yes — Yes.** The code is the `powerbiclient` **quick visualize** path:\n\n```python\nfrom powerbiclient import QuickVisualize, get_dataset_config, Report\n\nPBI_visualize = QuickVisualize(get_dataset_config(df))\nPBI_visualize\n```\n\n1. **Embeds an existing report → No.** Embedding an *existing* report is the `Report` class (`Report(group_id=..., report_id=...)`). It is imported on line 1 but never used. `QuickVisualize` starts from a DataFrame, and there is no report id anywhere in the code.\n2. **Creates a Power BI report → Yes.** `QuickVisualize` *\"create[s] a Power BI report from your data\"* — Fabric autogenerates a new report over the DataFrame passed through `get_dataset_config(df)`.\n3. **Displays a summary of the DataFrame → Yes.** Evaluating `PBI_visualize` on the last line renders the widget inline in the notebook, and the autogenerated report is a set of summary visuals over the DataFrame's columns.\n\n*Note:* statement 3 is contested. The single highest-voted comment (+49) answers **No**, reading \"summary\" as `df.describe()`-style summary *statistics*. Two other comments (+22, +12) answer **Yes**, and the PDF's answer-key image marks **Yes**. The wording is \"a summary of the DataFrame\", not \"summary statistics\", and the autogenerated report is exactly that — so Yes is the better reading.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-yesno",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "statements": [
        {
          "text": "The code embeds an existing Power BI report.",
