    "id": "T1Q14",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse.\nYou use a dataflow to load a new dataset from OneLake to the warehouse.\nYou need to add a PowerQuery step to identify the maximum values for the numeric columns.\nWhich function should you include in the step?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Table.MaxN"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Table.Max"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Table.Range"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Table.Profile"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q14",
    "sourceId": "T1Q14",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "Read the ask precisely: the maximum values for **the numeric columns** — plural, across columns, in **one** Power Query step.\n\n**`Table.Profile`** does exactly that. It returns one row *per column* with the columns `Min`, `Max`, `Average`, `StandardDeviation`, `Count`, `NullCount` and `DistinctCount` — so a single step yields the maximum of every numeric column at once.\n\n- **B `Table.Max`** returns *one row* — the largest row of the table according to a comparison criterion you must supply. It gives you a record, not a per-column maximum, and you would need a separate step per column.\n- **A `Table.MaxN`** is the same idea for the *N* largest rows.\n- **C `Table.Range`** returns a slice of rows by position. Nothing to do with maxima.\n\n*Note:* about a third of the community votes B, arguing that \"profiling\" is a pane in the UI rather than a step. But `Table.Profile` is a genuine M function that you can add as a step, and it is the only option that answers the question for all numeric columns at once.",
    "answer": "D",
    "officialAnswer": "D",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "D",
      "answer": "D",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse.\nYou use a dataflow to load a new dataset from OneLake to the warehouse.\nYou need to add a PowerQuery step to identify the maximum values for the numeric columns.\nWhich function should you include in the step?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Table.MaxN",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Table.Max",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Table.Range",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Table.Profile",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Read the ask precisely: the maximum values for **the numeric columns** — plural, across columns, in **one** Power Query step.\n\n**`Table.Profile`** does exactly that. It returns one row *per column* with the columns `Min`, `Max`, `Average`, `StandardDeviation`, `Count`, `NullCount` and `DistinctCount` — so a single step yields the maximum of every numeric column at once.\n\n- **B `Table.Max`** returns *one row* — the largest row of the table according to a comparison criterion you must supply. It gives you a record, not a per-column maximum, and you would need a separate step per column.\n- **A `Table.MaxN`** is the same idea for the *N* largest rows.\n- **C `Table.Range`** returns a slice of rows by position. Nothing to do with maxima.\n\n*Note:* about a third of the community votes B, arguing that \"profiling\" is a pane in the UI rather than a step. But `Table.Profile` is a genuine M function that you can add as a step, and it is the only option that answers the question for all numeric columns at once."
    },
