    "id": "T1Q17",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a Microsoft Power BI report named Report1. Report1 includes a Python visual.\nData displayed by the visual is grouped automatically and duplicate rows are NOT displayed.\nYou need all rows to appear in the visual.\nWhat should you do?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Reference the columns in the Python code by index."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Modify the Sort Column By property for all columns."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Add a unique field to each row."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Modify the Summarize By property for all columns."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q17",
    "sourceId": "T1Q17",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "Power BI aggregates the data it hands to a Python (or R) visual: identical rows are collapsed before the script ever runs. The documented workaround is to make every row distinguishable. From the *Tips* section of the Python visuals doc: *\"In some cases, you might not want automatic grouping to occur, or you might want all rows to appear, including duplicates. In those cases, you can **add an index field to your dataset that causes all rows to be considered unique** and prevents grouping.\"*\n\nAdding a field whose value is different on every row is exactly **C — add a unique field to each row**.\n\n- **A** – *\"Reference the columns in the Python code by index\"* is about how your script addresses `dataset` columns. It changes nothing about the grouping Power BI has already applied before the script runs.\n- **B** – *Sort Column By* controls display order only.\n- **D** – *Summarize By* governs how a numeric column is aggregated in a visual (Sum, Average, …). Setting it to *Don't summarize* stops measures being summed, but the row-level de-duplication that feeds the Python visual still happens.\n\n*Note:* the highest-voted comment (+73) answers **A** — but it quotes the doc sentence above, which describes adding an **index field** (a new column of unique values), not \"referencing columns by index\". A later comment (+13) catches the mix-up. The PDF key and 53% of the community are on **C**.",
    "answer": "C",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a Microsoft Power BI report named Report1. Report1 includes a Python visual.\nData displayed by the visual is grouped automatically and duplicate rows are NOT displayed.\nYou need all rows to appear in the visual.\nWhat should you do?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Reference the columns in the Python code by index.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Modify the Sort Column By property for all columns.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Add a unique field to each row.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Modify the Summarize By property for all columns.",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Power BI aggregates the data it hands to a Python (or R) visual: identical rows are collapsed before the script ever runs. The documented workaround is to make every row distinguishable. From the *Tips* section of the Python visuals doc: *\"In some cases, you might not want automatic grouping to occur, or you might want all rows to appear, including duplicates. In those cases, you can **add an index field to your dataset that causes all rows to be considered unique** and prevents grouping.\"*\n\nAdding a field whose value is different on every row is exactly **C — add a unique field to each row**.\n\n- **A** – *\"Reference the columns in the Python code by index\"* is about how your script addresses `dataset` columns. It changes nothing about the grouping Power BI has already applied before the script runs.\n- **B** – *Sort Column By* controls display order only.\n- **D** – *Summarize By* governs how a numeric column is aggregated in a visual (Sum, Average, …). Setting it to *Don't summarize* stops measures being summed, but the row-level de-duplication that feeds the Python visual still happens.\n\n*Note:* the highest-voted comment (+73) answers **A** — but it quotes the doc sentence above, which describes adding an **index field** (a new column of unique values), not \"referencing columns by index\". A later comment (+13) catches the mix-up. The PDF key and 53% of the community are on **C**."
    },
