    "id": "T1Q23",
    "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1. Warehouse1 contains three schemas named schemaA, schemaB, and\nschemaC.\nYou need to ensure that a user named User1 can truncate tables in schemaA only.\nHow should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "blanks": [
      {
        "label": "GRANT  ______",
        "options": [
          "ALTER",
          "CONNECT",
          "EXECUTE"
        ],
        "answer": "ALTER"
      },
      {
        "label": "ON  ______  TO User1;",
        "options": [
          "DATABASE::schemaA",
          "OBJECT::schemaA",
          "SCHEMA::schemaA"
        ],
        "answer": "SCHEMA::schemaA"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q23",
    "sourceId": "T1Q23",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**`GRANT ALTER ON SCHEMA::schemaA TO User1;`**\n\n**The permission — `ALTER`.** There is no `TRUNCATE` permission in T-SQL. `TRUNCATE TABLE` requires, at minimum, **`ALTER` permission on the table**: *\"The minimum permission required is ALTER on table_name.\"* Granting `ALTER` at the schema level covers every table in that schema, present and future.\n- `CONNECT` only lets a principal open a connection to the database.\n- `EXECUTE` applies to programmable objects (procedures, functions), not to truncating tables.\n\n**The securable — `SCHEMA::schemaA`.** The requirement is *schemaA only*. Schema-scoped grants use the `SCHEMA::` class, and this scopes the permission to exactly one schema — schemaB and schemaC are untouched.\n- `DATABASE::schemaA` is the wrong class *and* the wrong scope: it would name the whole database (and `schemaA` is not a database).\n- `OBJECT::schemaA` scopes to a single object such as one table; `schemaA` is a schema, not an object.\n\n*Note:* one commenter points out that Fabric Warehouse did not originally support `TRUNCATE TABLE`. That does not change the answer — the question is testing the GRANT syntax, and `ALTER ON SCHEMA::` is the least-privilege grant for it.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "GRANT  ______",
          "options": [
            "ALTER",
            "CONNECT",
            "EXECUTE"
          ],
          "answer": "ALTER"
        },
        {
          "label": "ON  ______  TO User1;",
          "options": [
            "DATABASE::schemaA",
            "OBJECT::schemaA",
            "SCHEMA::schemaA"
          ],
          "answer": "SCHEMA::schemaA"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1. Warehouse1 contains three schemas named schemaA, schemaB, and\nschemaC.\nYou need to ensure that a user named User1 can truncate tables in schemaA only.\nHow should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**`GRANT ALTER ON SCHEMA::schemaA TO User1;`**\n\n**The permission — `ALTER`.** There is no `TRUNCATE` permission in T-SQL. `TRUNCATE TABLE` requires, at minimum, **`ALTER` permission on the table**: *\"The minimum permission required is ALTER on table_name.\"* Granting `ALTER` at the schema level covers every table in that schema, present and future.\n- `CONNECT` only lets a principal open a connection to the database.\n- `EXECUTE` applies to programmable objects (procedures, functions), not to truncating tables.\n\n**The securable — `SCHEMA::schemaA`.** The requirement is *schemaA only*. Schema-scoped grants use the `SCHEMA::` class, and this scopes the permission to exactly one schema — schemaB and schemaC are untouched.\n- `DATABASE::schemaA` is the wrong class *and* the wrong scope: it would name the whole database (and `schemaA` is not a database).\n- `OBJECT::schemaA` scopes to a single object such as one table; `schemaA` is a schema, not an object.\n\n*Note:* one commenter points out that Fabric Warehouse did not originally support `TRUNCATE TABLE`. That does not change the answer — the question is testing the GRANT syntax, and `ALTER ON SCHEMA::` is the least-privilege grant for it."
    },
    "sourceVisualReferences": {
      "exhibits": [
        "p081_i1.png"
      ],
      "answerImages": [
        "p081_i2.png"
      ],
      "optionImages": []
    },
    "visualResolution": "structured_answer_area_verified",
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "TRUNCATE TABLE (Transact-SQL) — Permissions",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/truncate-table-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
