  {
    "id": "T1Q24",
    "type": "multi",
    "stem": "You plan to deploy Microsoft Power BI items by using Fabric deployment pipelines. You have a deployment pipeline that contains three stages\nnamed Development, Test, and Production. A workspace is assigned to each stage.\nYou need to provide Power BI developers with access to the pipeline. The solution must meet the following requirements:\nEnsure that the developers can deploy items to the workspaces for Development and Test.\nPrevent the developers from deploying items to the workspace for Production.\nFollow the principle of least privilege.\nWhich three levels of access should you assign to the developers? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Build permission to the production semantic models"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Admin access to the deployment pipeline"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Viewer access to the Development and Test workspaces"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Viewer access to the Production workspace"
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "Contributor access to the Development and Test workspaces"
      },
      {
        "letter": "F",
        "text": "Contributor access to the Production workspace"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q24",
    "sourceId": "T1Q24",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "Deployment pipelines need permission in **two** places: on the **pipeline** (to see and operate it) and on each **workspace** (to read from the source and write to the target). Least privilege then decides the level in each workspace.\n\n- **B — Admin access to the deployment pipeline.** Pipeline access is a single role: users granted access to a pipeline get pipeline admin rights. Without it the developers cannot open or run the pipeline at all.\n- **E — Contributor access to the Development and Test workspaces.** Deploying *into* a stage requires a workspace role that can write items — **Contributor** is the lowest such role, so it satisfies least privilege while still allowing the required deployments.\n- **D — Viewer access to the Production workspace.** The developers must be *prevented* from deploying to Production but still need the pipeline to function. **Viewer** is read-only: they can see the Production stage and compare it, but any deployment into it is blocked. Giving them nothing at all would be an option in principle, but Viewer is the level offered and it is read-only.\n\nRejected:\n- **A** *Build permission on the production semantic models* – lets someone build new content on a model; it has nothing to do with deploying and adds privilege without need.\n- **C** *Viewer on Development and Test* – read-only, so they could not deploy to the two stages they are supposed to deploy to.\n- **F** *Contributor on Production* – would let them deploy to Production, which is the one thing the requirements forbid.",
    "answer": "BDE",
    "officialAnswer": "BDE",
    "original": {
      "type": "multi",
      "officialAnswer": "BDE",
      "answer": "BDE",
      "stem": "You plan to deploy Microsoft Power BI items by using Fabric deployment pipelines. You have a deployment pipeline that contains three stages\nnamed Development, Test, and Production. A workspace is assigned to each stage.\nYou need to provide Power BI developers with access to the pipeline. The solution must meet the following requirements:\nEnsure that the developers can deploy items to the workspaces for Development and Test.\nPrevent the developers from deploying items to the workspace for Production.\nFollow the principle of least privilege.\nWhich three levels of access should you assign to the developers? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Build permission to the production semantic models",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Admin access to the deployment pipeline",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Viewer access to the Development and Test workspaces",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Viewer access to the Production workspace",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "E",
          "text": "Contributor access to the Development and Test workspaces",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "F",
          "text": "Contributor access to the Production workspace",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Deployment pipelines need permission in **two** places: on the **pipeline** (to see and operate it) and on each **workspace** (to read from the source and write to the target). Least privilege then decides the level in each workspace.\n\n- **B — Admin access to the deployment pipeline.** Pipeline access is a single role: users granted access to a pipeline get pipeline admin rights. Without it the developers cannot open or run the pipeline at all.\n- **E — Contributor access to the Development and Test workspaces.** Deploying *into* a stage requires a workspace role that can write items — **Contributor** is the lowest such role, so it satisfies least privilege while still allowing the required deployments.\n- **D — Viewer access to the Production workspace.** The developers must be *prevented* from deploying to Production but still need the pipeline to function. **Viewer** is read-only: they can see the Production stage and compare it, but any deployment into it is blocked. Giving them nothing at all would be an option in principle, but Viewer is the level offered and it is read-only.\n\nRejected:\n- **A** *Build permission on the production semantic models* – lets someone build new content on a model; it has nothing to do with deploying and adds privilege without need.\n- **C** *Viewer on Development and Test* – read-only, so they could not deploy to the two stages they are supposed to deploy to.\n- **F** *Contributor on Production* – would let them deploy to Production, which is the one thing the requirements forbid."
    },
