    "id": "T1Q25",
    "type": "multi",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace that contains a DirectQuery semantic model. The model queries a data source that has 500 million rows.\nYou have a Microsoft Power Bi report named Report1 that uses the model. Report1 contains visuals on multiple pages.\nYou need to reduce the query execution time for the visuals on all the pages.\nWhat are two features that you can use? Each correct answer presents a complete solution,\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "user-defined aggregations"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "automatic aggregation"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "query caching"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "OneLake integration"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q25",
    "sourceId": "T1Q25",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "r10Status": "AMBIGUOUS",
    "reviewNote": "Scoped licensed/source-supported aggregation behavior and removed universal speed guarantee.",
    "remaining": "Complete current primary-doc section review, source screenshot/case checks and native fixture where material; then independently challenge every final option.",
    "explanation": "Both aggregation features answer a DAX query from a small pre-summarised cache instead of sending it to the 500-million-row source, and both work for **every** visual on **every** page — which is what \"all the pages\" demands.\n\n- **A — user-defined aggregations.** You build an aggregation table yourself and map its columns to the detail table. The doc: *\"Aggregations in Power BI can improve query performance over very large DirectQuery semantic models.\"* Queries that can be answered from the aggregation hit the cache; only the rest fall through to DirectQuery.\n- **B — automatic aggregation.** The same idea, managed for you: Power BI trains on the query log and maintains the aggregation table itself. It is designed specifically for DirectQuery models.\n\nRejected:\n- **C — query caching.** Not available for DirectQuery. The doc's limitation is explicit: query caching applies to models in **Import** mode; it does not apply to DirectQuery or live connections. It also only helps the *initial* page load for returning users, not \"all the pages\".\n- **D — OneLake integration.** A semantic-model setting that writes **Import**-mode table data into OneLake as Delta. It does nothing for a DirectQuery model and is not a query-performance feature.",
    "answer": "AB",
    "officialAnswer": "AB",
    "original": {
      "type": "multi",
      "officialAnswer": "AB",
      "answer": "AB",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace that contains a DirectQuery semantic model. The model queries a data source that has 500 million rows.\nYou have a Microsoft Power Bi report named Report1 that uses the model. Report1 contains visuals on multiple pages.\nYou need to reduce the query execution time for the visuals on all the pages.\nWhat are two features that you can use? Each correct answer presents a complete solution,\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "user-defined aggregations",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "automatic aggregation",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "query caching",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "OneLake integration",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Both aggregation features answer a DAX query from a small pre-summarised cache instead of sending it to the 500-million-row source, and both work for **every** visual on **every** page — which is what \"all the pages\" demands.\n\n- **A — user-defined aggregations.** You build an aggregation table yourself and map its columns to the detail table. The doc: *\"Aggregations in Power BI can improve query performance over very large DirectQuery semantic models.\"* Queries that can be answered from the aggregation hit the cache; only the rest fall through to DirectQuery.\n- **B — automatic aggregation.** The same idea, managed for you: Power BI trains on the query log and maintains the aggregation table itself. It is designed specifically for DirectQuery models.\n\nRejected:\n- **C — query caching.** Not available for DirectQuery. The doc's limitation is explicit: query caching applies to models in **Import** mode; it does not apply to DirectQuery or live connections. It also only helps the *initial* page load for returning users, not \"all the pages\".\n- **D — OneLake integration.** A semantic-model setting that writes **Import**-mode table data into OneLake as Delta. It does nothing for a DirectQuery model and is not a query-performance feature."
    },
