    "id": "T1Q26",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains 30 CSV files in OneLake. The files are updated daily.\nYou create a Microsoft Power BI semantic model named Model1 that uses the CSV files as a data source. You configure incremental refresh for\nModel1 and publish the model to a Premium capacity in the Fabric tenant.\nWhen you initiate a refresh of Model1, the refresh fails after running out of resources.\nWhat is a possible cause of the failure?\nThe partitioning column type is unchanged and RangeStart/RangeEnd filters are valid. Focus on repeated local partition filtering of the CSV input.",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Query folding is occurring."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Only refresh complete days is selected."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "XMLA Endpoint is set to Read Only."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Query folding is NOT occurring."
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "The delta type of the column used to partition the data has changed."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q26",
    "sourceId": "T1Q26",
    "gradingEnabled": true,
    "scientificStatus": "APPROVED_CORRECTED",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Replaced mandatory-folding misconception with a documented possible resource cause.",
    "remaining": "",
    "explanation": "D is a possible resource-exhaustion cause: nonfolded partition filters can repeatedly read input and use the mashup engine to filter each partition. Import incremental refresh is not universally impossible without folding; it may be inefficient. Folding generally pushes filtering to the source. Complete-day selection and XMLA read-only do not explain the specified repeated local filtering; the candidate rules out a type change.",
    "answer": "D",
    "officialAnswer": "D",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "D",
      "answer": "D",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains 30 CSV files in OneLake. The files are updated daily.\nYou create a Microsoft Power BI semantic model named Model1 that uses the CSV files as a data source. You configure incremental refresh for\nModel1 and publish the model to a Premium capacity in the Fabric tenant.\nWhen you initiate a refresh of Model1, the refresh fails after running out of resources.\nWhat is a possible cause of the failure?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Query folding is occurring.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Only refresh complete days is selected.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "XMLA Endpoint is set to Read Only.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Query folding is NOT occurring.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "E",
          "text": "The delta type of the column used to partition the data has changed.",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**Incremental refresh depends on query folding.** The `RangeStart` / `RangeEnd` parameters are supposed to be translated into a filter that the *source* applies, so each partition reads only its own slice. Microsoft states the requirement directly: *\"Incremental refresh is designed for data sources that support query folding.\"* And the failure mode is documented too — when folding does not happen, *\"the Power Query engine … must retrieve all rows and then apply the filter locally\"*, once **per partition**.\n\nCSV files are flat files: there is no query engine behind them to push a filter into, so nothing folds. Every partition drags in all 30 files in full and filters them in memory, and the refresh runs out of resources. That is exactly **D**.\n\n- **A** – folding *occurring* is the healthy case; it would make the refresh cheap, not fail.\n- **B** – *Only refresh complete days* trims the newest partial day. It reduces work slightly; it cannot cause resource exhaustion.\n- **C** – the XMLA endpoint governs external-tool connectivity. It has no bearing on a refresh initiated in the service.\n- **E** – changing the data type of the partition column breaks incremental refresh with a *schema/type* error, not an out-of-resources failure.\n\n*Note:* 37% of the community picks E, and one highly-voted comment objects that folding \"does not make sense on a CSV source\". That objection is the answer restated: because a CSV cannot fold, the filters land in memory — which is precisely why the refresh dies."
    },
    "sourceVisualReferences": {
      "exhibits": [],
      "answerImages": [],
      "optionImages": []
    },
    "visualResolution": "no_exhibit_needed",
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Microsoft documentation",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-troubleshoot"
      }
    ],
    "reviewKind": "documentation-approved derived practice"
  },
  {
    "id": "T1Q27",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that uses a Microsoft Power BI Premium capacity.\nYou need to enable scale-out for a semantic model.\nWhat should you do first?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "At the semantic model level, set Large dataset storage format to Off."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "At the tenant level, set Create and use Metrics to Enabled."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "At the semantic model level, set Large dataset storage format to On."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "At the tenant level, set Data Activator to Enabled."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q27",
    "sourceId": "T1Q27",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "Semantic model **scale-out** creates read-only replicas of a model so that read queries and refreshes do not contend. Its documented prerequisite is the large-model storage format: *\"Before you can enable scale-out for a semantic model, the model must be in large semantic model storage format.\"* So the **first** step is to set **Large dataset storage format = On** at the semantic model level.\n\n- **A** – setting it *Off* is the opposite of what is required.\n- **B** *Create and use Metrics* – enables Power BI goals/scorecards. Unrelated.\n- **D** *Data Activator* – the real-time alerting item (now Activator). Unrelated.",
    "answer": "C",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that uses a Microsoft Power BI Premium capacity.\nYou need to enable scale-out for a semantic model.\nWhat should you do first?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "At the semantic model level, set Large dataset storage format to Off.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "At the tenant level, set Create and use Metrics to Enabled.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "At the semantic model level, set Large dataset storage format to On.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "At the tenant level, set Data Activator to Enabled.",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Semantic model **scale-out** creates read-only replicas of a model so that read queries and refreshes do not contend. Its documented prerequisite is the large-model storage format: *\"Before you can enable scale-out for a semantic model, the model must be in large semantic model storage format.\"* So the **first** step is to set **Large dataset storage format = On** at the semantic model level.\n\n- **A** – setting it *Off* is the opposite of what is required.\n- **B** *Create and use Metrics* – enables Power BI goals/scorecards. Unrelated.\n- **D** *Data Activator* – the real-time alerting item (now Activator). Unrelated."
    },
