  {
    "id": "T1Q30",
    "type": "multi",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model. The model uses Direct Lake mode.\nYou suspect that some DAX queries load unnecessary columns into memory.\nYou need to identify the frequently used columns that are loaded into memory.\nWhat are two ways to achieve the goal? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Use the Analyze in Excel feature."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Use the Vertipaq Analyzer tool."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Query the $System.DISCOVER_STORAGE_TABLE_COLUMN_SEGMENTS dynamic management view (DMV)."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Query the DISCOVER_MEMORYGRANT dynamic management view (DMV)."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q30",
    "sourceId": "T1Q30",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "BLOCKED_SOURCE",
    "r10Status": "AMBIGUOUS",
    "reviewNote": "Removed unsupported equivalence between in-memory state and access frequency.",
    "remaining": "Complete current primary-doc section review, source screenshot/case checks and native fixture where material; then independently challenge every final option.",
    "explanation": "In Direct Lake, columns are **paged into memory on demand** (transcoded from Parquet the first time a query touches them) and stay resident until evicted. To see which columns are actually resident, you need column-segment-level telemetry.\n\n- **C — `$System.DISCOVER_STORAGE_TABLE_COLUMN_SEGMENTS`.** This DMV reports the in-memory segments per column, including the Direct-Lake-specific `TEMPERATURE` and `LAST_ACCESSED` columns. Temperature is literally a frequency-of-use score, so this is the direct answer to \"which frequently used columns are loaded into memory\".\n- **B — VertiPaq Analyzer.** The standard tool (built into DAX Studio and Tabular Editor) that reads those same DMVs and presents per-column size and cardinality. Microsoft's own Direct Lake analysis guidance points at it.\n\nRejected:\n- **A Analyze in Excel** – connects a PivotTable to the model. It tells you nothing about memory residency.\n- **D `DISCOVER_MEMORYGRANT`** – reports memory *grants for running queries*, not which columns are paged in.",
    "answer": "BC",
    "officialAnswer": "BC",
    "original": {
      "type": "multi",
      "officialAnswer": "BC",
      "answer": "BC",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model. The model uses Direct Lake mode.\nYou suspect that some DAX queries load unnecessary columns into memory.\nYou need to identify the frequently used columns that are loaded into memory.\nWhat are two ways to achieve the goal? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Use the Analyze in Excel feature.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Use the Vertipaq Analyzer tool.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Query the $System.DISCOVER_STORAGE_TABLE_COLUMN_SEGMENTS dynamic management view (DMV).",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Query the DISCOVER_MEMORYGRANT dynamic management view (DMV).",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "In Direct Lake, columns are **paged into memory on demand** (transcoded from Parquet the first time a query touches them) and stay resident until evicted. To see which columns are actually resident, you need column-segment-level telemetry.\n\n- **C — `$System.DISCOVER_STORAGE_TABLE_COLUMN_SEGMENTS`.** This DMV reports the in-memory segments per column, including the Direct-Lake-specific `TEMPERATURE` and `LAST_ACCESSED` columns. Temperature is literally a frequency-of-use score, so this is the direct answer to \"which frequently used columns are loaded into memory\".\n- **B — VertiPaq Analyzer.** The standard tool (built into DAX Studio and Tabular Editor) that reads those same DMVs and presents per-column size and cardinality. Microsoft's own Direct Lake analysis guidance points at it.\n\nRejected:\n- **A Analyze in Excel** – connects a PivotTable to the model. It tells you nothing about memory residency.\n- **D `DISCOVER_MEMORYGRANT`** – reports memory *grants for running queries*, not which columns are paged in."
    },
    "sourceVisualReferences": {
      "exhibits": [],
      "answerImages": [],
      "optionImages": []
    },
    "visualResolution": "no_exhibit_needed",
