    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q31",
    "sourceId": "T1Q31",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "BLOCKED_SOURCE",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Source pixels confirm CompanyID + ProductID form the Product primary key.",
    "remaining": "Specify Company business attributes and target dimensional requirements before choosing slot 2.",
    "explanation": "**The ProductID column — Omitted.**\n\nThe source is a normalised OLTP model in which `Company` sits beside `Product`, `Customer` and `OrderItem`, and both `Product` and `Customer` carry a **composite primary key** that includes `CompanyID`. The target is a star schema: fact `OrderItem`, dimensions `Date`, `Product`, `Customer`.\n\n**Blank 2 — Omitted.** The stated goal is *\"analysis of order items by date, product, and customer\"*. `Company` is not one of those dimensions, and in the exhibit the `Company` table has **exactly one column — `CompanyID`**, its own key. A table with no descriptive attributes carries nothing to analyse by and nothing to denormalise; keeping it would only add a join. Dropping it is the correct dimensional-modelling call.\n\n**Blank 1 — The ProductID column.** This follows from blank 2: once `Company` is out of the model, `CompanyID` is no longer part of the key structure, so `OrderItem` relates to `Product` on `ProductID` alone. That is also the only shape a Power BI relationship can take — **model relationships are single-column**; the doc states you *\"can't create relationships based on multiple columns\"*. That rules out *Both the CompanyID and the ProductID columns* outright, whatever you do with `Company`.\n\n**⚠ This question is genuinely contested — read this before trusting the grade.** Weighted by upvotes the community splits:\n\n| Blank | Reading | Weight |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | The ProductID column | **64** |\n| 1 | Both CompanyID and ProductID | 32 |\n| 1 | A new combined key | 13 |\n| 2 | Denormalized into Customer and Product | **84** |\n| 2 | Omitted | 25 |\n\nSo the community prefers *ProductID* for blank 1 (agreeing with the key) but *Denormalized* for blank 2 (disagreeing with it). Note that the most popular combination, *ProductID + Denormalized*, is **internally inconsistent**: if you denormalise `Company` into `Product` and `Customer`, `CompanyID` survives as an attribute and the product key stays composite — so the relationship could not be on `ProductID` alone. The two self-consistent pairs are **ProductID + Omitted** (the answer key) and **a new combined key + Denormalized**. The key's pair is the one the exam rewards, and the attribute-free `Company` table supports it.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "The relationship between OrderItem and Product must be based on:",
          "options": [
            "The ProductID column",
            "Both the CompanyID and the ProductID columns",
            "A new key that combines the CompanyID and ProductID columns"
          ],
          "answer": "The ProductID column"
        },
        {
          "label": "The Company entity must be:",
          "options": [
            "Omitted",
            "Denormalized into the Product entity only",
            "Denormalized into the Customer and Product entities"
          ],
          "answer": "Omitted"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have the source data model shown in the following exhibit.\nThe primary keys of the tables are indicated by a key symbol beside the columns involved in each key.\nYou need to create a dimensional data model that will enable the analysis of order items by date, product, and customer.\nWhat should you include in the solution? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**The ProductID column — Omitted.**\n\nThe source is a normalised OLTP model in which `Company` sits beside `Product`, `Customer` and `OrderItem`, and both `Product` and `Customer` carry a **composite primary key** that includes `CompanyID`. The target is a star schema: fact `OrderItem`, dimensions `Date`, `Product`, `Customer`.\n\n**Blank 2 — Omitted.** The stated goal is *\"analysis of order items by date, product, and customer\"*. `Company` is not one of those dimensions, and in the exhibit the `Company` table has **exactly one column — `CompanyID`**, its own key. A table with no descriptive attributes carries nothing to analyse by and nothing to denormalise; keeping it would only add a join. Dropping it is the correct dimensional-modelling call.\n\n**Blank 1 — The ProductID column.** This follows from blank 2: once `Company` is out of the model, `CompanyID` is no longer part of the key structure, so `OrderItem` relates to `Product` on `ProductID` alone. That is also the only shape a Power BI relationship can take — **model relationships are single-column**; the doc states you *\"can't create relationships based on multiple columns\"*. That rules out *Both the CompanyID and the ProductID columns* outright, whatever you do with `Company`.\n\n**⚠ This question is genuinely contested — read this before trusting the grade.** Weighted by upvotes the community splits:\n\n| Blank | Reading | Weight |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | The ProductID column | **64** |\n| 1 | Both CompanyID and ProductID | 32 |\n| 1 | A new combined key | 13 |\n| 2 | Denormalized into Customer and Product | **84** |\n| 2 | Omitted | 25 |\n\nSo the community prefers *ProductID* for blank 1 (agreeing with the key) but *Denormalized* for blank 2 (disagreeing with it). Note that the most popular combination, *ProductID + Denormalized*, is **internally inconsistent**: if you denormalise `Company` into `Product` and `Customer`, `CompanyID` survives as an attribute and the product key stays composite — so the relationship could not be on `ProductID` alone. The two self-consistent pairs are **ProductID + Omitted** (the answer key) and **a new combined key + Denormalized**. The key's pair is the one the exam rewards, and the attribute-free `Company` table supports it."
    },
