    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q32",
    "sourceId": "T1Q32",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "Both goals — less memory **and** faster refresh — point at the same two culprits in the table: a very high-cardinality column and a calculated column.\n\n- **A — split `OrderDateTime` into separate date and time columns.** VertiPaq compresses column by column, and compression collapses as cardinality rises. A `Date/Time` down to the second over 100 million rows is close to unique per row — the worst case for the dictionary. Split it and you get at most a few thousand distinct dates and 86,400 distinct times, both of which compress hard. This is Microsoft's own guidance: *\"split the column into separate date and time columns\"* to reduce model size.\n- **D — replace `TotalSalesAmount` with a measure.** It is currently a **calculated column** (`Quantity * Price`). Calculated columns are **materialised at refresh and stored in the model**, so they cost both refresh time and memory — 100 million decimal values of it. A measure is evaluated at query time and stores nothing. The guidance: *\"calculated columns … increase the model size and can prolong data refresh time … consider a measure instead.\"*\n\nRejected:\n- **B — replace `TotalQuantity` with a calculated column.** `TotalQuantity` is already a **measure** (see the Description column). This swaps the cheap thing for the expensive thing — backwards on both goals.\n- **C — convert `Quantity` to Text.** Text columns are larger and slower than integers and cannot be aggregated. Strictly worse.",
    "answer": "AD",
    "officialAnswer": "AD",
    "original": {
      "type": "multi",
      "officialAnswer": "AD",
      "answer": "AD",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 uses Import mode. Model1 contains a table named Orders.\nOrders has 100 million rows and the following fields.\nYou need to reduce the memory used by Model1 and the time it takes to refresh the model.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Split OrderDateTime into separate date and time columns.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Replace TotalQuantity with a calculated column.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Convert Quantity into the Text data type.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Replace TotalSalesAmount with a measure.",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Both goals — less memory **and** faster refresh — point at the same two culprits in the table: a very high-cardinality column and a calculated column.\n\n- **A — split `OrderDateTime` into separate date and time columns.** VertiPaq compresses column by column, and compression collapses as cardinality rises. A `Date/Time` down to the second over 100 million rows is close to unique per row — the worst case for the dictionary. Split it and you get at most a few thousand distinct dates and 86,400 distinct times, both of which compress hard. This is Microsoft's own guidance: *\"split the column into separate date and time columns\"* to reduce model size.\n- **D — replace `TotalSalesAmount` with a measure.** It is currently a **calculated column** (`Quantity * Price`). Calculated columns are **materialised at refresh and stored in the model**, so they cost both refresh time and memory — 100 million decimal values of it. A measure is evaluated at query time and stores nothing. The guidance: *\"calculated columns … increase the model size and can prolong data refresh time … consider a measure instead.\"*\n\nRejected:\n- **B — replace `TotalQuantity` with a calculated column.** `TotalQuantity` is already a **measure** (see the Description column). This swaps the cheap thing for the expensive thing — backwards on both goals.\n- **C — convert `Quantity` to Text.** Text columns are larger and slower than integers and cannot be aggregated. Strictly worse."
    },
