    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q34",
    "sourceId": "T1Q34",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "BLOCKED_SOURCE",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Inspected screenshot shows material diagnostic icons absent from semantic exhibit.",
    "remaining": "Resolve screenshot diagnostic symbols and any occluded state against current UI; add accessible state column and retest.",
    "explanation": "**Some — the Microsoft Power Query engine.**\n\nRead the **step folding indicators** down the Applied steps pane. Power Query marks each step with one of these:\n\n| Indicator | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| *(no icon)* / plain | folds — will be evaluated at the source |\n| **orange/red warning icon** | **not folded** — this step breaks folding |\n| grey | folding could not be determined |\n\n**Blank 1 — Some.** The early steps (`Source`, `Navigation 1`) fold, but **`Capitalized each word`** carries the not-folded marker. Folding stops at the first step that cannot be translated into the source query, so everything before it folds and everything from it onward does not. That is *some*, not *All* and not *None*.\n\n**Blank 2 — the Microsoft Power Query engine.** `Added custom` sits **after** the break, so it can never be pushed down. The doc: once folding is broken, the remaining transformations *\"are processed by the Power Query engine\"* — data is pulled out of the source and transformed locally. It is also worth noting this query does an **Appended query** across two different lakehouses; a query that spans two sources has no single engine to fold into, which is why the options naming a lakehouse engine are wrong.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
