    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "The Copy data activity's **Destination** tab has a *Table action* setting with two choices, and their documented meanings decide this:\n\n| Table action | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Append** | adds rows to the existing table, keeping its schema |\n| **Overwrite** | **drops and recreates the table**, so both the schema and all the data are replaced |\n\nThe two requirements — *replace Table1 with the schema of the external data* and *replace all the data* — are exactly what **Overwrite** does, which matters here because *\"the schema of the external data changes regularly\"*.\n\n- **A** *add additional columns* – appends constant/metadata columns to the source; it does not replace anything.\n- **C** *Enable staging* – routes the copy through interim storage for throughput. No effect on schema or on replacing rows.\n- **D** *Enable partition discovery* – interprets a folder hierarchy as partition columns when reading.\n- **E** *Recursively* – reads sub-folders of the source path.\n\nNote that *truncate*-style behaviour would not be enough even if it were offered: truncating removes rows but leaves the old schema in place.",
    "answer": "B",
    "officialAnswer": "B",
