    "explanation": "Two separate constraints, both driven by \"query … by using the SQL endpoint\".\n\n- **D — create the shortcut in the Tables section.** The SQL analytics endpoint only exposes what the lakehouse recognises as a **table**. A shortcut in the **Files** section is unmanaged storage: it is visible to Spark and to `OPENROWSET`-style file access, but it is not surfaced as a table, so the SQL endpoint cannot query it. Table-section shortcuts are auto-discovered and appear in the endpoint.\n- **E — use the delta format.** The Tables area of a lakehouse is Delta Lake territory: *\"The Tables section … only supports Delta Lake tables.\"* A folder is promoted to a table when it carries a `_delta_log`; a bare Parquet or CSV folder dropped into Tables is flagged as unidentified and never reaches the SQL endpoint.\n\nRejected:\n- **A** – Files section, as above: invisible to the SQL endpoint.\n- **B Parquet** / **C CSV** – neither is a table format on its own. Delta *is* Parquet plus a transaction log, so choosing delta gives you the Parquet storage anyway, plus the log that makes it a table.\n\n*Note:* the vote is close (DE 50% / BD 44%), and the main dissenting comment claims *\"Delta format not supported by S3\"*. That is incorrect — Delta Lake is an open format that stores `_delta_log` alongside the Parquet files, and it works on S3 exactly as it does on ADLS. Amazon S3 is a supported shortcut target, so nothing blocks delta there.",
    "answer": "DE",
    "officialAnswer": "DE",
    "original": {
      "type": "multi",
      "officialAnswer": "DE",
      "answer": "DE",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse.\nYou plan to query sales data files by using the SQL endpoint. The files will be in an Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) storage bucket.\nYou need to recommend which file format to use and where to create a shortcut.\nWhich two actions should you include in the recommendation? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Create a shortcut in the Files section.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Use the Parquet format",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Use the CSV format.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Create a shortcut in the Tables section.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "E",
          "text": "Use the delta format.",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Two separate constraints, both driven by \"query … by using the SQL endpoint\".\n\n- **D — create the shortcut in the Tables section.** The SQL analytics endpoint only exposes what the lakehouse recognises as a **table**. A shortcut in the **Files** section is unmanaged storage: it is visible to Spark and to `OPENROWSET`-style file access, but it is not surfaced as a table, so the SQL endpoint cannot query it. Table-section shortcuts are auto-discovered and appear in the endpoint.\n- **E — use the delta format.** The Tables area of a lakehouse is Delta Lake territory: *\"The Tables section … only supports Delta Lake tables.\"* A folder is promoted to a table when it carries a `_delta_log`; a bare Parquet or CSV folder dropped into Tables is flagged as unidentified and never reaches the SQL endpoint.\n\nRejected:\n- **A** – Files section, as above: invisible to the SQL endpoint.\n- **B Parquet** / **C CSV** – neither is a table format on its own. Delta *is* Parquet plus a transaction log, so choosing delta gives you the Parquet storage anyway, plus the log that makes it a table.\n\n*Note:* the vote is close (DE 50% / BD 44%), and the main dissenting comment claims *\"Delta format not supported by S3\"*. That is incorrect — Delta Lake is an open format that stores `_delta_log` alongside the Parquet files, and it works on S3 exactly as it does on ADLS. Amazon S3 is a supported shortcut target, so nothing blocks delta there."
    },
