      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "From the Source tab, select Enable partition discovery."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "From the Destination tabs, set Mode to Overwrite."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q38",
    "sourceId": "T1Q38",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "This is a UI-sequencing question: the partition column picker **does not exist** until the table action allows the table to be rebuilt. Microsoft's partitioning tutorial gives the order of operations verbatim: *\"Expand **Advanced**, in **Table action**, select **Overwrite**, and then select **Enable partition**. Under **Partition columns**, select **Add column**, and choose the column you want to use as the partition column.\"*\n\nThe reason is structural: **partitioning is part of a table's physical layout**, fixed when the table is written. `Table1` already exists and is **unpartitioned**, so you cannot bolt a partition scheme onto it by appending — the table has to be dropped and recreated, which is what **Overwrite** does. Hence **D**, *first*.\n\n- **B — select the partition column** is the *goal*, not the first step; the option is greyed out/absent until Overwrite is set.\n- **A — Append** keeps the existing unpartitioned layout, so the partition option never appears.\n- **C — Enable partition discovery** is a **Source** setting. It reads partitions *out of* a source folder hierarchy; it has nothing to do with how the destination is written.",
    "answer": "D",
    "officialAnswer": "D",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "D",
      "answer": "D",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains an unpartitioned table named Table1.\nYou plan to copy data to Table1 and partition the table based on a date column in the source data.\nYou create a Copy activity to copy the data to Table1.\nYou need to specify the partition column in the Destination settings of the Copy activity.\nWhat should you do first?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "From the Destination tab, set Mode to Append.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "From the Destination tab, select the partition column.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "From the Source tab, select Enable partition discovery.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "From the Destination tabs, set Mode to Overwrite.",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "This is a UI-sequencing question: the partition column picker **does not exist** until the table action allows the table to be rebuilt. Microsoft's partitioning tutorial gives the order of operations verbatim: *\"Expand **Advanced**, in **Table action**, select **Overwrite**, and then select **Enable partition**. Under **Partition columns**, select **Add column**, and choose the column you want to use as the partition column.\"*\n\nThe reason is structural: **partitioning is part of a table's physical layout**, fixed when the table is written. `Table1` already exists and is **unpartitioned**, so you cannot bolt a partition scheme onto it by appending — the table has to be dropped and recreated, which is what **Overwrite** does. Hence **D**, *first*.\n\n- **B — select the partition column** is the *goal*, not the first step; the option is greyed out/absent until Overwrite is set.\n- **A — Append** keeps the existing unpartitioned layout, so the partition option never appears.\n- **C — Enable partition discovery** is a **Source** setting. It reads partitions *out of* a source folder hierarchy; it has nothing to do with how the destination is written."
    },
