    "id": "T1Q39",
    "type": "yesno",
    "stem": "Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the\nproblem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also\npossible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen.\nYour network contains an on-premises Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) domain named contoso.com that syncs with a Microsoft Entra\ntenant by using Microsoft Entra Connect.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model.\nYou enable dynamic row-level security (RLS) for the model and deploy the model to the Fabric service.\nYou query a measure that includes the USERNAME() function, and the query returns a blank result.\nYou need to ensure that the measure returns the user principal name (UPN) of a user.\nSolution: You add user objects to the list of synced objects in Microsoft Entra Connect.\nDoes this meet the goal?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q39",
    "sourceId": "T1Q39",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Removed false universal USERNAME return-format claim.",
    "remaining": "Read exact identity section, obtain measure and tested identity/RLS context.",
    "explanation": "**No.** This is one of four \"same scenario, different solution\" items in the set. The problem is *which DAX function the measure calls*, not how identities reach Microsoft Entra ID.\n\nSyncing more user objects through Entra Connect changes nothing about `USERNAME()`. If the user can already sign in to Fabric and run the report, their identity is by definition already present in Entra ID — so the sync is not the missing piece. The documented fix is to call **`USERPRINCIPALNAME()`**, which *\"returns the user principal name\"* — reliably, in Desktop and in the service alike. `USERNAME()` returns `DOMAIN\\user` in Desktop, and is the wrong tool when you specifically need a UPN.\n\nAcross the set:\n\n| Question | Proposed solution | Meets the goal? |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **T1Q39** | add user objects to Entra Connect sync | **No** |\n| T1Q129 | use `USEROBJECTID()` | No — returns the object GUID, not a UPN |\n| **T1Q130** | use `USERPRINCIPALNAME()` | **Yes** |\n| T1Q134 | create a role in the model | No — roles scope RLS; they do not change what the function returns |\n\n*Note:* the vote is a close 58/42. The \"Yes\" camp argues an unsynced user would produce a blank — but such a user could not have run the report at all, and only one solution in the set can be the intended fix.",
    "answer": "B",
    "officialAnswer": "B",
    "original": {
      "type": "yesno",
      "officialAnswer": "B",
      "answer": "B",
      "stem": "Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the\nproblem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also\npossible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen.\nYour network contains an on-premises Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) domain named contoso.com that syncs with a Microsoft Entra\ntenant by using Microsoft Entra Connect.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model.\nYou enable dynamic row-level security (RLS) for the model and deploy the model to the Fabric service.\nYou query a measure that includes the USERNAME() function, and the query returns a blank result.\nYou need to ensure that the measure returns the user principal name (UPN) of a user.\nSolution: You add user objects to the list of synced objects in Microsoft Entra Connect.\nDoes this meet the goal?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Yes",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "No",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**No.** This is one of four \"same scenario, different solution\" items in the set. The problem is *which DAX function the measure calls*, not how identities reach Microsoft Entra ID.\n\nSyncing more user objects through Entra Connect changes nothing about `USERNAME()`. If the user can already sign in to Fabric and run the report, their identity is by definition already present in Entra ID — so the sync is not the missing piece. The documented fix is to call **`USERPRINCIPALNAME()`**, which *\"returns the user principal name\"* — reliably, in Desktop and in the service alike. `USERNAME()` returns `DOMAIN\\user` in Desktop, and is the wrong tool when you specifically need a UPN.\n\nAcross the set:\n\n| Question | Proposed solution | Meets the goal? |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **T1Q39** | add user objects to Entra Connect sync | **No** |\n| T1Q129 | use `USEROBJECTID()` | No — returns the object GUID, not a UPN |\n| **T1Q130** | use `USERPRINCIPALNAME()` | **Yes** |\n| T1Q134 | create a role in the model | No — roles scope RLS; they do not change what the function returns |\n\n*Note:* the vote is a close 58/42. The \"Yes\" camp argues an unsynced user would produce a blank — but such a user could not have run the report at all, and only one solution in the set can be the intended fix."
    },
