    "id": "T1Q40",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have source data in a folder on a local computer.\nYou need to create a solution that will use Fabric to populate a data store. The solution must meet the following requirements:\nSupport the use of dataflows to load and append data to the data store.\nEnsure that Delta tables are V-Order optimized and compacted automatically.\nWhich type of data store should you use?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "a lakehouse"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "an Azure SQL database"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "a warehouse"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "a KQL database"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q40",
    "sourceId": "T1Q40",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "r10Status": "AMBIGUOUS",
    "reviewNote": "Removed universal default-optimization claim.",
    "remaining": "Verify V-Order profile/version settings and dataflow local-gateway requirements; run actual ingest/maintenance fixture.",
    "explanation": "Both requirements point at the lakehouse:\n\n- **Dataflows can load and *append* to it.** Dataflow Gen2 lists Lakehouse as a data destination with Append and Replace update methods.\n- **Its tables *are* Delta tables.** The requirement is phrased as *\"ensure that **Delta tables** are V-Order optimized and compacted automatically\"* — Delta tables are the thing you manage in a lakehouse. Fabric's Spark runtime applies V-Order on write and uses **Optimize Write** to bin-pack small files as they are produced, so the maintenance happens without you scheduling it.\n\nRejected:\n- **B — Azure SQL database** is not a Fabric data store and has no Delta tables or V-Order at all.\n- **D — KQL database** is for real-time/telemetry ingestion. Dataflows do not target it, and V-Order is not a KQL concept.\n- **C — warehouse** is the genuine contender (see the note below), but its storage is managed for you as SQL tables; you do not work with \"Delta tables\" there the way the requirement describes.\n\n**⚠ Doc drift — read this.** When this question was written, V-Order was **on by default** for Fabric Spark, which made the lakehouse an easy fit. The current doc reverses that default: *\"In Fabric, **V-order is disabled by default for all newly created workspaces** to optimize performance for write-heavy data engineering workloads\"* (`spark.sql.parquet.vorder.default = false`). Meanwhile the warehouse doc still guarantees it unconditionally: *\"Regardless of how you ingest data into warehouses, the data ingestion task optimizes the parquet files it produces by using V-Order write optimization.\"*\n\nSo on today's documentation a warehouse is arguably the safer read of \"automatically\", which is why 33% of the community votes **C**. The exam key and 67% of the community are on **A**, and \"Delta tables\" is the wording that gets you there — but be aware the ground has shifted under this question.",
    "answer": "A",
    "officialAnswer": "A",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "A",
      "answer": "A",
      "stem": "You have source data in a folder on a local computer.\nYou need to create a solution that will use Fabric to populate a data store. The solution must meet the following requirements:\nSupport the use of dataflows to load and append data to the data store.\nEnsure that Delta tables are V-Order optimized and compacted automatically.\nWhich type of data store should you use?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "a lakehouse",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "an Azure SQL database",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "a warehouse",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "a KQL database",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Both requirements point at the lakehouse:\n\n- **Dataflows can load and *append* to it.** Dataflow Gen2 lists Lakehouse as a data destination with Append and Replace update methods.\n- **Its tables *are* Delta tables.** The requirement is phrased as *\"ensure that **Delta tables** are V-Order optimized and compacted automatically\"* — Delta tables are the thing you manage in a lakehouse. Fabric's Spark runtime applies V-Order on write and uses **Optimize Write** to bin-pack small files as they are produced, so the maintenance happens without you scheduling it.\n\nRejected:\n- **B — Azure SQL database** is not a Fabric data store and has no Delta tables or V-Order at all.\n- **D — KQL database** is for real-time/telemetry ingestion. Dataflows do not target it, and V-Order is not a KQL concept.\n- **C — warehouse** is the genuine contender (see the note below), but its storage is managed for you as SQL tables; you do not work with \"Delta tables\" there the way the requirement describes.\n\n**⚠ Doc drift — read this.** When this question was written, V-Order was **on by default** for Fabric Spark, which made the lakehouse an easy fit. The current doc reverses that default: *\"In Fabric, **V-order is disabled by default for all newly created workspaces** to optimize performance for write-heavy data engineering workloads\"* (`spark.sql.parquet.vorder.default = false`). Meanwhile the warehouse doc still guarantees it unconditionally: *\"Regardless of how you ingest data into warehouses, the data ingestion task optimizes the parquet files it produces by using V-Order write optimization.\"*\n\nSo on today's documentation a warehouse is arguably the safer read of \"automatically\", which is why 33% of the community votes **C**. The exam key and 67% of the community are on **A**, and \"Delta tables\" is the wording that gets you there — but be aware the ground has shifted under this question."
    },
