    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**Yes — Yes — Yes.** The code is\n\n```python\ndf.write.partitionBy(\"year\", \"month\", \"day\").mode(\"overwrite\").parquet(\"Files/SalesOrder\")\n```\n\n1. **Hierarchy of folders → Yes.** `partitionBy` writes **Hive-style partition directories**, one nested level per key in the order given:\n```\nFiles/SalesOrder/year=2024/month=03/day=17/part-0000….snappy.parquet\n```\nThree keys, three nested levels.\n2. **Read in parallel across multiple nodes → Yes.** Each partition directory holds one or more independent Parquet files, and Spark assigns files to tasks that run across the executors. Partitioned Parquet is precisely the layout that enables parallel reads (and partition pruning, when a query filters on year/month/day).\n3. **Use file compression → Yes.** Parquet is compressed by default — Spark's `spark.sql.parquet.compression.codec` defaults to **snappy**, which is why the files are written as `*.snappy.parquet`. You would have to set the codec to `none` to get uncompressed output.\n\nThe community agrees (+39 on \"yes yes yes\").",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-yesno",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "statements": [
        {
          "text": "The results will form a hierarchy of folders for each partition key.",
          "answer": "Yes"
        },
        {
          "text": "The resulting file partitions can be read in parallel across multiple nodes.",
          "answer": "Yes"
        },
        {
          "text": "The resulting file partitions will use file compression.",
          "answer": "Yes"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse.\nYou are using a Fabric notebook to save a large DataFrame by using the following code. df.write.partitionBy(“year”, “month”,\n“day”).mode(“overwrite”).parquet(“Files/SalesOrder”)\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**Yes — Yes — Yes.** The code is\n\n```python\ndf.write.partitionBy(\"year\", \"month\", \"day\").mode(\"overwrite\").parquet(\"Files/SalesOrder\")\n```\n\n1. **Hierarchy of folders → Yes.** `partitionBy` writes **Hive-style partition directories**, one nested level per key in the order given:\n```\nFiles/SalesOrder/year=2024/month=03/day=17/part-0000….snappy.parquet\n```\nThree keys, three nested levels.\n2. **Read in parallel across multiple nodes → Yes.** Each partition directory holds one or more independent Parquet files, and Spark assigns files to tasks that run across the executors. Partitioned Parquet is precisely the layout that enables parallel reads (and partition pruning, when a query filters on year/month/day).\n3. **Use file compression → Yes.** Parquet is compressed by default — Spark's `spark.sql.parquet.compression.codec` defaults to **snappy**, which is why the files are written as `*.snappy.parquet`. You would have to set the codec to `none` to get uncompressed output.\n\nThe community agrees (+39 on \"yes yes yes\")."
    },
