    "explanation": "You want every column *except* `VendorID` turned into attribute/value rows, keeping `VendorID` as the identifier. You have selected `VendorID`, so the command that acts on **everything else** is **Unpivot other columns**.\n\nThe doc distinguishes the three unpivot commands:\n\n| Command | Acts on |\n| --- | --- |\n| Unpivot columns | the columns you selected |\n| **Unpivot other columns** | **every column you did *not* select** |\n| Unpivot only selected columns | the selected columns, and only those |\n\n*Unpivot other columns* has a second advantage the doc calls out: *\"Use this command in a query when not all columns are known. New columns added during a refresh operation are also unpivoted.\"*\n\n- **B Unpivot columns** would unpivot `VendorID` itself — the opposite of what is wanted.\n- **A Group by** aggregates rows; it does not reshape columns into rows.\n- **D Split column** divides one column into several by delimiter or position.\n- **E Remove other columns** deletes the other columns instead of unpivoting them.",
