    "id": "T1Q44",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse.\nSeveral times a day, the performance of all warehouse queries degrades. You suspect that Fabric is throttling the compute used by the warehouse.\nWhat should you use to identify whether throttling is occurring?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "the Capacity settings"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "the Monitoring hub"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "dynamic management views (DMVs)"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "the Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q44",
    "sourceId": "T1Q44",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "The **Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app** is the tool Microsoft points you at for throttling. It reports capacity units consumed, overage, carry-forward and the throttling state over time, so it shows both *whether* throttling is happening and *what* caused it.\n\nThere is also a decisive practical reason the alternatives fail. From the throttling doc: *\"Just like most Warehouse operations, dynamic management views (DMVs) are also classified as background and covered by the Background Rejection policy. As a result, **DMVs cannot be queried when capacity is throttled**. Even though DMVs aren't available, capacity admins can go to **Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app** to understand the root cause.\"*\n\n- **C DMVs** – exactly the thing that stops responding during throttling.\n- **A Capacity settings** – shows the SKU and its configuration, not live consumption.\n- **B Monitoring hub** – lists item runs and their status. It can show queries are slow; it cannot tell you the capacity is throttled.",
    "answer": "D",
    "officialAnswer": "D",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "D",
      "answer": "D",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse.\nSeveral times a day, the performance of all warehouse queries degrades. You suspect that Fabric is throttling the compute used by the warehouse.\nWhat should you use to identify whether throttling is occurring?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "the Capacity settings",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "the Monitoring hub",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "dynamic management views (DMVs)",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "the Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The **Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app** is the tool Microsoft points you at for throttling. It reports capacity units consumed, overage, carry-forward and the throttling state over time, so it shows both *whether* throttling is happening and *what* caused it.\n\nThere is also a decisive practical reason the alternatives fail. From the throttling doc: *\"Just like most Warehouse operations, dynamic management views (DMVs) are also classified as background and covered by the Background Rejection policy. As a result, **DMVs cannot be queried when capacity is throttled**. Even though DMVs aren't available, capacity admins can go to **Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app** to understand the root cause.\"*\n\n- **C DMVs** – exactly the thing that stops responding during throttling.\n- **A Capacity settings** – shows the SKU and its configuration, not live consumption.\n- **B Monitoring hub** – lists item runs and their status. It can show queries are slow; it cannot tell you the capacity is throttled."
    },
