  {
    "id": "T1Q45",
    "type": "hotspot-yesno",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace that uses the default Spark starter pool and runtime version 1.2.\nYou plan to read a CSV file named Sales_raw.csv in a lakehouse, select columns, and save the data as a Delta table to the managed area of the\nlakehouse. Sales_raw.csv contains 12 columns.\nYou have the following code.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "statements": [
      {
        "text": "The Spark engine will read only the 'SalesOrderNumber', 'OrderDate', 'CustomerName', 'UnitPrice' columns from Sales_raw.csv.",
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "text": "Removing the partition will reduce the execution time of the query.",
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "text": "Adding inferSchema='true' to the options will increase the execution time of the query.",
        "answer": "Yes"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "Spark DataFrame read, select, partition, save",
        "language": "python",
        "content": "from pyspark.sql.functions import year\n\n(spark\n    .read\n    .format(\"csv\")\n    .option(\"header\", \"true\")\n    .load(\"Files/sales_raw.csv\")\n    .select('SalesOrderNumber', 'OrderDate', 'CustomerName', 'UnitPrice')\n    .withColumn(\"Year\", year(\"OrderDate\"))\n    .write\n    .partitionBy('Year')\n    .saveAsTable(\"sales\")\n)"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q45",
    "sourceId": "T1Q45",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Separated write cost from downstream read performance and removed deprecated runtime premise.",
    "remaining": "Inspect original screenshot typos and run supported Spark read/write/explain and downstream Year-filter comparisons.",
    "explanation": "**No — No — Yes.** The code reads a 12-column CSV, keeps four columns, derives `Year` and writes a Delta table partitioned by `Year`.\n\n**1. Reads only the four selected columns → No.** Column pruning is a *columnar-format* optimisation: Parquet and Delta store each column separately, so Spark can skip the rest on disk. **CSV is row-based** — every byte of every line has to be read and parsed to find the field delimiters. The `.select(...)` prunes columns *after* the rows are read, so the whole file is still read.\n\n**2. Removing the partition will reduce the execution time of the query → No.** Partitioning by `Year` is what lets a later query skip irrelevant folders entirely (**partition pruning**). Remove it and every query scans the whole table, so query time goes **up**, not down. On the write side the cost is small here — a handful of distinct years means a handful of output folders.\n\n**3. `inferSchema=\"true\"` increases execution time → Yes.** Schema inference is an **extra pass over the data**: Spark reads the file once to work out each column's type, then reads it again to load it. Supplying an explicit schema avoids that second pass, which is why Microsoft recommends it for large files.\n\n*Note:* statement 2 is contested. The highest-voted comment (+44) answers **Yes**, reading \"the query\" as this write job and arguing that `partitionBy` adds file-creation overhead. The next comment (+23) and the PDF answer-key image both answer **No**. The wording says *\"the query\"*, and partitioning is a read-side optimisation, so **No** is the better reading — but if you interpret it as the write job, the other side has a case.\n\n*(Two OCR slips in the exhibit: `.red` is `.read`, and `\"heade\"` is `\"header\"`.)*",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-yesno",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "statements": [
        {
          "text": "The Spark engine will read only the 'SalesOrderNumber', 'OrderDate', 'CustomerName', 'UnitPrice' columns from Sales_raw.csv.",
          "answer": "No"
        },
        {
          "text": "Removing the partition will reduce the execution time of the query.",
          "answer": "No"
        },
        {
          "text": "Adding inferSchema='true' to the options will increase the execution time of the query.",
          "answer": "Yes"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace that uses the default Spark starter pool and runtime version 1.2.\nYou plan to read a CSV file named Sales_raw.csv in a lakehouse, select columns, and save the data as a Delta table to the managed area of the\nlakehouse. Sales_raw.csv contains 12 columns.\nYou have the following code.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**No — No — Yes.** The code reads a 12-column CSV, keeps four columns, derives `Year` and writes a Delta table partitioned by `Year`.\n\n**1. Reads only the four selected columns → No.** Column pruning is a *columnar-format* optimisation: Parquet and Delta store each column separately, so Spark can skip the rest on disk. **CSV is row-based** — every byte of every line has to be read and parsed to find the field delimiters. The `.select(...)` prunes columns *after* the rows are read, so the whole file is still read.\n\n**2. Removing the partition will reduce the execution time of the query → No.** Partitioning by `Year` is what lets a later query skip irrelevant folders entirely (**partition pruning**). Remove it and every query scans the whole table, so query time goes **up**, not down. On the write side the cost is small here — a handful of distinct years means a handful of output folders.\n\n**3. `inferSchema=\"true\"` increases execution time → Yes.** Schema inference is an **extra pass over the data**: Spark reads the file once to work out each column's type, then reads it again to load it. Supplying an explicit schema avoids that second pass, which is why Microsoft recommends it for large files.\n\n*Note:* statement 2 is contested. The highest-voted comment (+44) answers **Yes**, reading \"the query\" as this write job and arguing that `partitionBy` adds file-creation overhead. The next comment (+23) and the PDF answer-key image both answer **No**. The wording says *\"the query\"*, and partitioning is a read-side optimisation, so **No** is the better reading — but if you interpret it as the write job, the other side has a case.\n\n*(Two OCR slips in the exhibit: `.red` is `.read`, and `\"heade\"` is `\"header\"`.)*"
    },
