      },
      "stem": "You are creating a data flow in Fabric to ingest data from an Azure SQL database by using a T-SQL statement.\nYou need to ensure that any foldable Power Query transformation steps are processed by the Microsoft SQL Server engine.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once,\nor not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**Value — NativeQuery — EnableFolding.** The completed step is:\n\n```m\nQuery = Value.NativeQuery(\n    Database,\n    \"SELECT * FROM customer WHERE country IN ('USA', 'UK')\",\n    null,\n    [EnableFolding = true]\n)\n```\n\n- **`Value.NativeQuery`** is the M function that runs a native (T-SQL) statement against a source. Its signature is `Value.NativeQuery(target as any, query as text, optional parameters as any, optional options as nullable record)`.\n- **`[EnableFolding = true]`** is the fourth argument, the options **record**. By default a native query is a folding *dead end* — Power Query cannot see inside your SQL, so every later step is evaluated locally. Setting this flag tells the mashup engine it may continue folding on top of the native query: *\"you can indicate that you want to fold the transformations … by setting `EnableFolding` to true.\"* That is precisely the requirement, \"any foldable Power Query transformation steps are processed by the Microsoft SQL Server engine\".\n\nDistractors: **`Table`** and **`Record`** are the wrong M namespaces (`Table.NativeQuery` does not exist); **`Optimize`** is not an M function; **`StopFolding`** is not a real option (and would do the opposite of what is asked)."
    },
