  {
    "id": "T1Q49",
    "type": "multi",
    "stem": "You need to create a data loading pattern for a Type 1 slowly changing dimension (SCD).\nWhich two actions should you include in the process? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Update rows when the non-key attributes have changed."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Insert new rows when the natural key exists in the dimension table, and the non-key attribute values have changed."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Update the effective end date of rows when the non-key attribute values have changed."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Insert new records when the natural key is a new value in the table."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q49",
    "sourceId": "T1Q49",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "A **Type 1** slowly changing dimension **overwrites** — it keeps no history. That single fact settles all four options.\n\n- **A — update rows when the non-key attributes have changed.** This *is* Type 1: *\"A Type 1 SCD always reflects the latest values, and when changes in source data are detected, the dimension table data is overwritten.\"*\n- **D — insert new records when the natural key is a new value.** A key you have never seen is a genuinely new member, so it is inserted. Every SCD type does this; together with A it forms the complete upsert (MERGE) pattern.\n\nRejected:\n- **B — insert a new row when the key exists and attributes changed.** That is **Type 2**: a second row per member, versioned. Type 1 updates in place instead.\n- **C — update the effective end date.** Effective start/end dates are the **Type 2** validity window. A Type 1 dimension has no such columns, because there is no history to bound.",
    "answer": "AD",
    "officialAnswer": "AD",
    "original": {
      "type": "multi",
      "officialAnswer": "AD",
      "answer": "AD",
      "stem": "You need to create a data loading pattern for a Type 1 slowly changing dimension (SCD).\nWhich two actions should you include in the process? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Update rows when the non-key attributes have changed.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Insert new rows when the natural key exists in the dimension table, and the non-key attribute values have changed.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Update the effective end date of rows when the non-key attribute values have changed.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Insert new records when the natural key is a new value in the table.",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "A **Type 1** slowly changing dimension **overwrites** — it keeps no history. That single fact settles all four options.\n\n- **A — update rows when the non-key attributes have changed.** This *is* Type 1: *\"A Type 1 SCD always reflects the latest values, and when changes in source data are detected, the dimension table data is overwritten.\"*\n- **D — insert new records when the natural key is a new value.** A key you have never seen is a genuinely new member, so it is inserted. Every SCD type does this; together with A it forms the complete upsert (MERGE) pattern.\n\nRejected:\n- **B — insert a new row when the key exists and attributes changed.** That is **Type 2**: a second row per member, versioned. Type 1 updates in place instead.\n- **C — update the effective end date.** Effective start/end dates are the **Type 2** validity window. A Type 1 dimension has no such columns, because there is no history to bound."
    },
