  {
    "id": "T1Q51",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You are analyzing customer purchases in a Fabric notebook by using PySpark.\nYou have the following DataFrames:\ntransactions: Contains five columns named transaction_id, customer_id, product_id, amount, and date and has 10 million rows, with each row\nrepresenting a transaction. customers: Contains customer details in 1,000 rows and three columns named customer_id, name, and country.\nYou need to join the DataFrames on the customer_id column. The solution must minimize data shuffling.\nYou write the following code.\nfrom pyspark.sql import functions as F\nresults =\nWhich code should you run to populate the results DataFrame?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "transactions.join(F.broadcast(customers), transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id)"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "transactions.join(customers, transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id).distinct()"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "transactions.join(customers, transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id)"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "transactions.crossJoin(customers).where(transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id)"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q51",
    "sourceId": "T1Q51",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "A plain join of a **10,000,000-row** DataFrame to a **1,000-row** one triggers a *shuffle hash / sort-merge* join: Spark hash-partitions **both** sides on `customer_id` and sends every row across the network. That is the shuffle the question wants eliminated.\n\n`F.broadcast(customers)` forces a **broadcast hash join**: the tiny DataFrame is copied once to every executor, and each executor then joins its local slice of `transactions` against the in-memory copy. The large side never moves. Microsoft's guidance: *\"Broadcast hash join … avoids shuffling the large table, which significantly improves performance\"*, and it is the recommended pattern when one side is small.\n\n- **C** – the same join without the hint. Spark *may* broadcast it automatically if the table is under `spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold` (10 MB by default) and statistics are available, but it is not guaranteed — so it does not *minimize* shuffling by construction.\n- **B** – C plus a `.distinct()`, which adds **another** full shuffle. Strictly worse.\n- **D** – a `crossJoin` materialises 10 billion row pairs before filtering. Catastrophic.",
    "answer": "A",
    "officialAnswer": "A",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "A",
      "answer": "A",
      "stem": "You are analyzing customer purchases in a Fabric notebook by using PySpark.\nYou have the following DataFrames:\ntransactions: Contains five columns named transaction_id, customer_id, product_id, amount, and date and has 10 million rows, with each row\nrepresenting a transaction. customers: Contains customer details in 1,000 rows and three columns named customer_id, name, and country.\nYou need to join the DataFrames on the customer_id column. The solution must minimize data shuffling.\nYou write the following code.\nfrom pyspark.sql import functions as F\nresults =\nWhich code should you run to populate the results DataFrame?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "transactions.join(F.broadcast(customers), transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id)",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "transactions.join(customers, transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id).distinct()",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "transactions.join(customers, transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id)",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "transactions.crossJoin(customers).where(transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id)",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "A plain join of a **10,000,000-row** DataFrame to a **1,000-row** one triggers a *shuffle hash / sort-merge* join: Spark hash-partitions **both** sides on `customer_id` and sends every row across the network. That is the shuffle the question wants eliminated.\n\n`F.broadcast(customers)` forces a **broadcast hash join**: the tiny DataFrame is copied once to every executor, and each executor then joins its local slice of `transactions` against the in-memory copy. The large side never moves. Microsoft's guidance: *\"Broadcast hash join … avoids shuffling the large table, which significantly improves performance\"*, and it is the recommended pattern when one side is small.\n\n- **C** – the same join without the hint. Spark *may* broadcast it automatically if the table is under `spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold` (10 MB by default) and statistics are available, but it is not guaranteed — so it does not *minimize* shuffling by construction.\n- **B** – C plus a `.distinct()`, which adds **another** full shuffle. Strictly worse.\n- **D** – a `crossJoin` materialises 10 billion row pairs before filtering. Catastrophic."
    },
