        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**Yes.** `df.summary()` *\"computes specified statistics for numeric and string columns\"*, and with no arguments its default set is exactly:\n\n**count, mean, stddev, min, 25%, 50%, 75%, max**\n\nThat contains all four requested values — min, max, mean and standard deviation — and the doc's own wording covers the *\"all the string and numeric columns\"* part of the requirement.\n\n```\n+-------+------------------+------------------+\n|summary|          fruit_id|            amount|\n+-------+------------------+------------------+\n|  count|                 3|                 3|\n|   mean|1.6666666666666667|11.666666666666666|\n| stddev|0.5773502691896257| 2.886751345948129|\n|    min|                 1|                10|\n|    max|                 2|                15|\n+-------+------------------+------------------+\n```\n\n*Note:* the vote is a narrow 54/46. The \"No\" camp argues that mean and stddev are meaningless for string columns — true, but the function still *runs over* string columns (returning count/min/max for them), which is what the documented description claims and what the question asks. `summary()` is the broader of the two options here; `describe()` returns only count/mean/stddev/min/max."
    },
