      ],
      "explanation": "Two tenant settings have to change, and the **principle of least privilege** (a stated Litware requirement) decides the *scope* of each: security groups, never the whole organization.\n\n- **B — *Users can try Microsoft Fabric paid features* for specific security groups.** This is what lets the PoC team turn on Fabric capabilities. Litware already has the groups defined (`FabricAdmins`, `AnalyticsTeam`, …), and the PoC is explicitly limited to the analytics team.\n- **E — *Users can create Fabric items* for specific security groups.** Without it the team can see Fabric but create nothing. Again scoped to the groups.\n\nRejected:\n- **A** – the same setting as B but **for the entire organization**. It works, but it hands paid features to everyone — a direct least-privilege violation, and the security requirement says *\"Only Fabric administrators and the analytics team must be able to see the Fabric items created as part of the PoC.\"*\n- **C** – *Allow Azure AD guest users…* concerns external identities. No guests are mentioned anywhere in the scenario.\n- **D** – the same setting as E but enabled broadly with **exclusions**. Deny-listing is the inverse of least privilege: anyone added to the tenant later is allowed by default. Allow-listing specific groups (E) is correct."
