  {
    "id": "T1Q61",
    "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
    "stem": "You need to design a semantic model for the customer satisfaction report.\nWhich data source authentication method and mode should you use? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "blanks": [
      {
        "label": "Authentication method:",
        "options": [
          "Basic authentication",
          "Service principal authentication",
          "Single sign-on (SSO) authentication"
        ],
        "answer": "Single sign-on (SSO) authentication"
      },
      {
        "label": "Mode:",
        "options": [
          "Direct Lake",
          "DirectQuery",
          "Import"
        ],
        "answer": "Direct Lake"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": "cs_litware",
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q61",
    "sourceId": "T1Q61",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "BLOCKED_SOURCE",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "No uniquely justified storage mode across unspecified Direct Lake variant/security assumptions.",
    "remaining": "Resolve exact SQL/OneLake security topology, model support and case-wide retired default-model references.",
    "explanation": "**Single sign-on (SSO) authentication — Direct Lake.**\n\n**Mode — Direct Lake.** Two report requirements pin it down:\n- *\"Shows data as soon as the data is updated in the data store.\"* Import caches data and only refreshes on a schedule, so Import is out. Direct Lake reads the Delta tables in OneLake, so new data is visible immediately.\n- *\"Minimizes the execution time of report queries.\"* DirectQuery sends a SQL query per visual and is the slowest of the three. Direct Lake gives Import-like in-memory speed without the copy — which is its whole reason to exist.\n\n**Authentication — SSO.** The requirement is *\"Ensures that the report respects any table-level security specified in the source data store.\"* Source-side security can only be applied if the query carries the **end user's** identity. The doc: *\"By default, Direct Lake models use single sign-on (SSO), so the effective permissions of the interactive user determine if the user is allowed or denied access to the data. If the Direct Lake model is configured to use a **fixed identity**, the effective permission of the **fixed identity** determines if users … can access the data.\"*\n\n- **Service principal authentication** is a fixed identity: every user would query as the service principal, and the source's table-level security would be evaluated once, for that one account — defeating the requirement.\n- **Basic authentication** (username/password) is likewise a single fixed credential, and is not offered for OneLake sources.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "Authentication method:",
          "options": [
            "Basic authentication",
            "Service principal authentication",
            "Single sign-on (SSO) authentication"
          ],
          "answer": "Single sign-on (SSO) authentication"
        },
        {
          "label": "Mode:",
          "options": [
            "Direct Lake",
            "DirectQuery",
            "Import"
          ],
          "answer": "Direct Lake"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You need to design a semantic model for the customer satisfaction report.\nWhich data source authentication method and mode should you use? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**Single sign-on (SSO) authentication — Direct Lake.**\n\n**Mode — Direct Lake.** Two report requirements pin it down:\n- *\"Shows data as soon as the data is updated in the data store.\"* Import caches data and only refreshes on a schedule, so Import is out. Direct Lake reads the Delta tables in OneLake, so new data is visible immediately.\n- *\"Minimizes the execution time of report queries.\"* DirectQuery sends a SQL query per visual and is the slowest of the three. Direct Lake gives Import-like in-memory speed without the copy — which is its whole reason to exist.\n\n**Authentication — SSO.** The requirement is *\"Ensures that the report respects any table-level security specified in the source data store.\"* Source-side security can only be applied if the query carries the **end user's** identity. The doc: *\"By default, Direct Lake models use single sign-on (SSO), so the effective permissions of the interactive user determine if the user is allowed or denied access to the data. If the Direct Lake model is configured to use a **fixed identity**, the effective permission of the **fixed identity** determines if users … can access the data.\"*\n\n- **Service principal authentication** is a fixed identity: every user would query as the service principal, and the source's table-level security would be evaluated once, for that one account — defeating the requirement.\n- **Basic authentication** (username/password) is likewise a single fixed credential, and is not offered for OneLake sources."
    },
