    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "The technical requirement is specific: *\"The dimensional model must contain a date dimension. **There is no existing data source for the date dimension.** The Litware fiscal year matches the calendar year. The date dimension must always contain dates from 2010 through the end of the current year.\"*\n\nSo the dates must be **generated**, not copied, and the table must be **extended each year**. Two tools can generate and load rows on a schedule:\n\n- **A — a dataflow.** Power Query can build a date table from scratch (`List.Dates`, or a generated column set) and write it to the data store as its destination. Low-code, which matches Litware's stated preference.\n- **D — a Stored procedure activity in a pipeline.** T-SQL generates the calendar (a recursive CTE or a tally table), the procedure writes the rows, and the pipeline runs it on a schedule so the current year is always covered.\n\nRejected:\n- **B — a Copy activity.** Copy moves data from a source to a destination. There **is no source** — that is stated outright — so there is nothing for it to copy.\n- **C — populate the date dimension *view* by using T-SQL.** You do not *populate* a view; a view is a stored query over existing data, and there is no underlying date data for it to select from. The wording alone disqualifies it.\n\n*Note:* a few commenters object that the AnalyticsPOC data store is a **lakehouse** (see T1Q9), and a lakehouse's SQL analytics endpoint is read-only — no stored procedures. That is a genuine inconsistency in the question set; the exam still expects **AD**, and 89% of the community votes that way.",
    "answer": "AD",
    "officialAnswer": "AD",
    "original": {
      "type": "multi",
      "officialAnswer": "AD",
      "answer": "AD",
      "stem": "You need to implement the date dimension in the data store. The solution must meet the technical requirements.\nWhat are two ways to achieve the goal? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Populate the date dimension table by using a dataflow.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Populate the date dimension table by using a Copy activity in a pipeline.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Populate the date dimension view by using T-SQL.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Populate the date dimension table by using a Stored procedure activity in a pipeline.",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The technical requirement is specific: *\"The dimensional model must contain a date dimension. **There is no existing data source for the date dimension.** The Litware fiscal year matches the calendar year. The date dimension must always contain dates from 2010 through the end of the current year.\"*\n\nSo the dates must be **generated**, not copied, and the table must be **extended each year**. Two tools can generate and load rows on a schedule:\n\n- **A — a dataflow.** Power Query can build a date table from scratch (`List.Dates`, or a generated column set) and write it to the data store as its destination. Low-code, which matches Litware's stated preference.\n- **D — a Stored procedure activity in a pipeline.** T-SQL generates the calendar (a recursive CTE or a tally table), the procedure writes the rows, and the pipeline runs it on a schedule so the current year is always covered.\n\nRejected:\n- **B — a Copy activity.** Copy moves data from a source to a destination. There **is no source** — that is stated outright — so there is nothing for it to copy.\n- **C — populate the date dimension *view* by using T-SQL.** You do not *populate* a view; a view is a stored query over existing data, and there is no underlying date data for it to select from. The wording alone disqualifies it.\n\n*Note:* a few commenters object that the AnalyticsPOC data store is a **lakehouse** (see T1Q9), and a lakehouse's SQL analytics endpoint is read-only — no stored procedures. That is a genuine inconsistency in the question set; the exam still expects **AD**, and 89% of the community votes that way."
    },
