    "id": "T1Q63",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You need to ensure the data loading activities in the AnalyticsPOC workspace are executed in the appropriate sequence. The solution must meet\nthe technical requirements.\nWhat should you do?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Create a dataflow that has multiple steps and schedule the dataflow."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Create and schedule a Spark notebook."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Create and schedule a Spark job definition."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Create a pipeline that has dependencies between activities and schedule the pipeline."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": "cs_litware",
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q63",
    "sourceId": "T1Q63",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "The binding requirement is *\"The data load process must ensure that the raw and cleansed data is updated **completely before** populating the dimensional model\"* — i.e. ordered execution with dependencies between steps.\n\nA **data pipeline** is Fabric's orchestrator. You lay out the activities on a canvas and connect them with **`On success`** dependency arrows, so the next activity does not start until the previous one has finished successfully. Schedule the pipeline and the whole sequence runs in order, every time.\n\n- **A — a dataflow with multiple steps.** Steps inside a dataflow are transformations within *one* query, not orchestration across the separate load activities. A dataflow cannot sequence other items.\n- **B / C — a Spark notebook or Spark job definition.** Both run code; neither orchestrates other Fabric items, and hand-rolling dependency logic in Spark would maximise, not minimise, effort.",
    "answer": "D",
    "officialAnswer": "D",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "D",
      "answer": "D",
      "stem": "You need to ensure the data loading activities in the AnalyticsPOC workspace are executed in the appropriate sequence. The solution must meet\nthe technical requirements.\nWhat should you do?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Create a dataflow that has multiple steps and schedule the dataflow.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Create and schedule a Spark notebook.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Create and schedule a Spark job definition.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Create a pipeline that has dependencies between activities and schedule the pipeline.",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The binding requirement is *\"The data load process must ensure that the raw and cleansed data is updated **completely before** populating the dimensional model\"* — i.e. ordered execution with dependencies between steps.\n\nA **data pipeline** is Fabric's orchestrator. You lay out the activities on a canvas and connect them with **`On success`** dependency arrows, so the next activity does not start until the previous one has finished successfully. Schedule the pipeline and the whole sequence runs in order, every time.\n\n- **A — a dataflow with multiple steps.** Steps inside a dataflow are transformations within *one* query, not orchestration across the separate load activities. A dataflow cannot sequence other items.\n- **B / C — a Spark notebook or Spark job definition.** Both run code; neither orchestrates other Fabric items, and hand-rolling dependency logic in Spark would maximise, not minimise, effort."
    },
