    "id": "T1Q69",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have an Azure Repos repository named Repo1 and a Fabric-enabled Microsoft Power BI Premium capacity. The capacity contains two\nworkspaces named Workspace1 and Workspace2. Git integration is enabled at the workspace level.\nYou plan to use Microsoft Power BI Desktop and Workspace1 to make version-controlled changes to a semantic model stored in Repo1. The\nchanges will be built and deployed to Workspace2 by using Azure Pipelines.\nYou need to ensure that report and semantic model definitions are saved as individual text files in a folder hierarchy. The solution must minimize\ndevelopment and maintenance effort.\nIn which file format should you save the changes?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "PBIP"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "PBIDS"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "PBIT"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "PBIX"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q69",
    "sourceId": "T1Q69",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**PBIP.** The requirement — *\"report and semantic model definitions … saved as individual text files in a folder hierarchy\"* — is a verbatim description of the **Power BI Project** format. Saving as `.pbip` produces:\n\n```\nMyProject.pbip\nMyProject.Report/        definition/ …            (report pages, visuals)\nMyProject.SemanticModel/ definition/tables/*.tmdl (model metadata as TMDL text)\n```\n\nEvery artefact is a separate text file, which is what makes meaningful diffs, merges and Azure Pipelines builds possible — and it keeps development effort low because Desktop writes the structure for you.\n\n- **D PBIX** – one binary archive. Git can store it, but every change is an opaque whole-file blob: no diffs, no merges.\n- **C PBIT** – a binary template (PBIX minus data). Same problem.\n- **B PBIDS** – a small connection-definition file that opens a new report against a data source. It contains no report or model at all.\n\n*(Same fact tested from a different angle in T1Q22.)*",
    "answer": "A",
    "officialAnswer": "A",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "A",
      "answer": "A",
      "stem": "You have an Azure Repos repository named Repo1 and a Fabric-enabled Microsoft Power BI Premium capacity. The capacity contains two\nworkspaces named Workspace1 and Workspace2. Git integration is enabled at the workspace level.\nYou plan to use Microsoft Power BI Desktop and Workspace1 to make version-controlled changes to a semantic model stored in Repo1. The\nchanges will be built and deployed to Workspace2 by using Azure Pipelines.\nYou need to ensure that report and semantic model definitions are saved as individual text files in a folder hierarchy. The solution must minimize\ndevelopment and maintenance effort.\nIn which file format should you save the changes?",
      "options": [
