            "label": "Bronze layer:  (items are \"Pipelines with Copy activities\")",
            "answer": "An Invoke pipeline activity"
          },
          {
            "label": "Silver layer:  (items are \"Dataflows\")",
            "answer": "A pipeline Dataflow activity"
          },
          {
            "label": "Gold layer:  (items are \"Stored procedures\")",
            "answer": "A pipeline Stored procedure activity"
          }
        ]
      },
      "stem": "You are implementing a medallion architecture in a single Fabric workspace.\nYou have a lakehouse that contains the Bronze and Silver layers and a warehouse that contains the Gold layer.\nYou create the items required to populate the layers as shown in the following table.\nYou need to ensure that the layers are populated daily in sequential order such that Silver is populated only after Bronze is complete, and Gold is\npopulated only after Silver is complete. The solution must minimize development effort and complexity.\nWhat should you use to execute each set of items? To answer, drag the appropriate options to the correct items. Each option may be used once,\nmore than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**A schedule — An Invoke pipeline activity — A pipeline Dataflow activity — A pipeline Stored procedure activity.**\n\nThe question asks what should **execute each set of items that already exists**, so match each layer to the activity that runs *that kind of item* from the orchestration pipeline:\n\n| Layer | Items you already built | Activity that executes them |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Bronze | **Pipelines** with Copy activities | **Invoke pipeline activity** |\n| Silver | Dataflows | Dataflow activity |\n| Gold | Stored procedures | Stored procedure activity |\n\nChaining the three with `On success` arrows gives the required Bronze → Silver → Gold ordering, and a **schedule** on the orchestration pipeline makes it run daily. (A schedule is the *trigger* for the outer pipeline; it is not an activity you place inside one.)\n\n**The PDF answer-key image is wrong on the Bronze layer** — it marks *A pipeline Copy activity*. The table states that the Bronze items **are already \"Pipelines with Copy activities\"**. Putting a bare Copy activity in the orchestration pipeline would mean *re-implementing* those copies by hand, leaving the pipelines you built unused — which directly contradicts *\"minimize development effort and complexity\"*. The **Invoke pipeline activity** exists precisely to call an existing pipeline from another one.\n\nThe community is emphatic: the three top comments (+30, +14, +8) all correct the key the same way.\n\n- **A Spark job definition** is a distractor — no layer here uses Spark."
