    "id": "T1Q71",
    "type": "dragdrop",
    "stem": "You are building a solution by using a Fabric notebook.\nYou have a Spark DataFrame assigned to a variable named df. The DataFrame returns four columns.\nYou need to change the data type of a string column named Age to integer. The solution must return a DataFrame that includes all the columns.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once,\nor not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "drag": {
      "items": [
        "cast",
        "col",
        "get",
        "select",
        "selectExpr",
        "transform",
        "withColumn"
      ],
      "reuse": true,
      "targets": [
        {
          "label": "df.  ______  (\"age\", …",
          "answer": "withColumn"
        },
        {
          "label": "…,  ______  (\"age\").",
          "answer": "col"
        },
        {
          "label": "….  ______  (\"int\")).show()",
          "answer": "cast"
        }
      ]
    },
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "Original Spark DataFrame transformation template",
        "language": "python",
        "content": "df.[Target 1](\"age\", [Target 2](\"age\").[Target 3](\"int\")).show()"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q71",
    "sourceId": "T1Q71",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "BLOCKED_RUNTIME",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Repaired return type and identifier casing; no PySpark engine run.",
    "remaining": "Run valid/invalid/NULL values in actual target Fabric Spark runtime; documentation review still pending.",
    "explanation": "**withColumn — col — cast.**\n\n```python\ndf.withColumn(\"age\", col(\"age\").cast(\"int\")).show()\n```\n\n- **`withColumn`** is the method that satisfies *\"return a DataFrame that includes **all** the columns\"*. It *\"returns a new DataFrame by adding a column or **replacing the existing column that has the same name**\"* — so passing the existing name `\"age\"` swaps that one column and leaves the other three untouched.\n- **`col(\"age\")`** builds the `Column` reference that the new value is computed from.\n- **`.cast(\"int\")`** converts it from string to integer.\n\nDistractors:\n- **`select`** / **`selectExpr`** would return **only** the columns you list, dropping the other three — this is the trap the \"all the columns\" requirement is guarding.\n- **`transform`** applies a function to the whole DataFrame; wrong shape for this call.\n- **`get`** is not a DataFrame or Column method for this purpose.\n\n*(`df.age.cast(\"int\")` and `col(\"age\").cast(IntegerType())` are equivalent alternatives, but only `col` is on offer here.)*",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
