    "id": "T1Q72",
    "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
    "stem": "You have an Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 account named storage1 that contains a Parquet file named sales.parquet.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1.\nUsing a notebook in Workspace1, you need to load the content of the file to the default lakehouse. The solution must ensure that the content will\ndisplay automatically as a table named Sales in Lakehouse explorer.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "blanks": [
      {
        "label": "df.write.mode(\"overwrite\").format(\"  ______  \")",
        "options": [
          "csv",
          "delta",
          "parquet"
        ],
        "answer": "delta"
      },
      {
        "label": ".saveAsTable(\"  ______  \")",
        "options": [
          "sales",
          "files/sales",
          "tables/sales"
        ],
        "answer": "sales"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "Original lakehouse table creation template",
        "language": "python",
        "content": "df = spark.read.parquet(\n    \"abfss://fs1@storage1.dfs.core.windows.net/files/sales.parquet\")\ndf.write.mode(\"overwrite\").format(\"[Dropdown 1]\") \\\n    .saveAsTable(\"[Dropdown 2]\")"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q72",
    "sourceId": "T1Q72",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**delta — sales.**\n\n```python\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\"abfss://fs1@storage1.dfs.core.windows.net/files/sales.parquet\")\ndf.write.mode(\"overwrite\").format(\"delta\").saveAsTable(\"sales\")\n```\n\n**Format — `delta`.** The requirement is that the content *\"display automatically as a **table** named Sales in Lakehouse explorer\"*. Lakehouse managed tables are Delta tables; writing the source `parquet` through unchanged (or `csv`) would leave files, not a table.\n\n**Name — `sales`.** This is the key distinction from T1Q65: **`saveAsTable()` takes a table *name*, not a path.** The lakehouse resolves it to the `Tables/` area for you. Passing `tables/sales` or `files/sales` would be interpreted as part of the identifier and is wrong — path-style arguments belong to `.save()`.\n\n| Method | Argument |\n| --- | --- |\n| `.saveAsTable(name)` | `\"sales\"` |\n| `.save(path)` | `\"Tables/sales\"` |\n\n*Note:* a handful of comments answer *tables/sales*, conflating the two APIs; the highest-voted (+46) and the answer key both give the bare name.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "df.write.mode(\"overwrite\").format(\"  ______  \")",
          "options": [
            "csv",
            "delta",
            "parquet"
          ],
          "answer": "delta"
        },
        {
          "label": ".saveAsTable(\"  ______  \")",
          "options": [
            "sales",
            "files/sales",
            "tables/sales"
          ],
          "answer": "sales"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have an Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 account named storage1 that contains a Parquet file named sales.parquet.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1.\nUsing a notebook in Workspace1, you need to load the content of the file to the default lakehouse. The solution must ensure that the content will\ndisplay automatically as a table named Sales in Lakehouse explorer.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**delta — sales.**\n\n```python\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\"abfss://fs1@storage1.dfs.core.windows.net/files/sales.parquet\")\ndf.write.mode(\"overwrite\").format(\"delta\").saveAsTable(\"sales\")\n```\n\n**Format — `delta`.** The requirement is that the content *\"display automatically as a **table** named Sales in Lakehouse explorer\"*. Lakehouse managed tables are Delta tables; writing the source `parquet` through unchanged (or `csv`) would leave files, not a table.\n\n**Name — `sales`.** This is the key distinction from T1Q65: **`saveAsTable()` takes a table *name*, not a path.** The lakehouse resolves it to the `Tables/` area for you. Passing `tables/sales` or `files/sales` would be interpreted as part of the identifier and is wrong — path-style arguments belong to `.save()`.\n\n| Method | Argument |\n| --- | --- |\n| `.saveAsTable(name)` | `\"sales\"` |\n| `.save(path)` | `\"Tables/sales\"` |\n\n*Note:* a handful of comments answer *tables/sales*, conflating the two APIs; the highest-voted (+46) and the answer key both give the bare name."
    },
