            "COALESCE",
            "IIF"
          ],
          "answer": "COALESCE"
        },
        {
          "label": "______  (ListPrice, SalePrice) AS OrderPrice",
          "options": [
            "COALESCE",
            "LEAST",
            "MIN"
          ],
          "answer": "LEAST"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named Sales.Orders. Sales.Orders contains the following columns.\nYou need to write a T-SQL query that will return the following columns.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\n\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**COALESCE — LEAST.**\n\n```sql\nSELECT OrderID, CustomerID, ProductID,\n  COALESCE(Weight, Quantity, 1) AS ChargedQuantity,\n  LEAST(ListPrice, SalePrice)   AS OrderPrice\nFROM Sales.Orders;\n```\n\n**Blank 1 — `COALESCE`.** `Weight` and `Quantity` are both nullable (see the schema), and the requirement is a fallback chain: use the weight, else the quantity, else 1. `COALESCE` *\"evaluates the arguments in order and returns the current value of the first expression that initially doesn't evaluate to NULL\"* — and it takes any number of arguments, which is what makes the three-item list work.\n- `IIF` takes a boolean and exactly two results; it cannot chain three fallbacks.\n- `CHOOSE(index, …)` picks by ordinal position, not by nullability.\n\n**Blank 2 — `LEAST`.** You want the smaller of two values **on the same row**. `LEAST` *\"returns the minimum value from a list of one or more expressions\"*, row by row.\n- `MIN` is an **aggregate**: it collapses rows into groups and would require a `GROUP BY`. This is the trap.\n- `COALESCE` returns the first non-null, which is not the same as the smaller one (`ListPrice` is `NOT NULL`, so it would always win)."
    },
