  {
    "id": "T1Q77",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains a Delta table that has one million Parquet files.\nYou need to remove files that were NOT referenced by the table during the past 30 days. The solution must ensure that the transaction log remains\nconsistent, and the ACID properties of the table are maintained.\nWhat should you do?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "From OneLake file explorer, delete the files."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Run the OPTIMIZE command and specify the Z-order parameter."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Run the OPTIMIZE command and specify the V-order parameter."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Run the VACUUM command."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q77",
    "sourceId": "T1Q77",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "r10Status": "AMBIGUOUS",
    "reviewNote": "Added explicit thirty-day retention instead of an unparameterized command.",
    "remaining": "Read exact current VACUUM retention docs; use only a disposable fixture/dry-run, then validate file age/streaming/time-travel impact.",
    "explanation": "**`VACUUM`** is the Delta command for removing data files that are no longer referenced by the table and are older than the retention threshold. It is transaction-log aware: it reads the log to determine exactly which files are still reachable (including by time travel), so the log stays consistent and ACID guarantees hold.\n\n```sql\nVACUUM customer RETAIN 720 HOURS   -- 30 days\n```\n\n- **A — delete the files from OneLake file explorer.** This is the trap, and it is destructive. Deleting Parquet files behind Delta's back leaves the transaction log referencing files that no longer exist, which corrupts the table and breaks ACID — precisely what the question forbids.\n- **B — `OPTIMIZE … ZORDER`.** Compacts small files and co-locates data by column value to improve data skipping. It *creates* new files and leaves the old ones behind; it deletes nothing.\n- **C — `OPTIMIZE … V-ORDER`.** Same story with a different write-time layout optimisation.\n\n`OPTIMIZE` and `VACUUM` are the standard pairing: optimize to compact, then vacuum to clean up what compaction orphaned.",
    "answer": "D",
    "officialAnswer": "D",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "D",
      "answer": "D",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains a Delta table that has one million Parquet files.\nYou need to remove files that were NOT referenced by the table during the past 30 days. The solution must ensure that the transaction log remains\nconsistent, and the ACID properties of the table are maintained.\nWhat should you do?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "From OneLake file explorer, delete the files.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Run the OPTIMIZE command and specify the Z-order parameter.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Run the OPTIMIZE command and specify the V-order parameter.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Run the VACUUM command.",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**`VACUUM`** is the Delta command for removing data files that are no longer referenced by the table and are older than the retention threshold. It is transaction-log aware: it reads the log to determine exactly which files are still reachable (including by time travel), so the log stays consistent and ACID guarantees hold.\n\n```sql\nVACUUM customer RETAIN 720 HOURS   -- 30 days\n```\n\n- **A — delete the files from OneLake file explorer.** This is the trap, and it is destructive. Deleting Parquet files behind Delta's back leaves the transaction log referencing files that no longer exist, which corrupts the table and breaks ACID — precisely what the question forbids.\n- **B — `OPTIMIZE … ZORDER`.** Compacts small files and co-locates data by column value to improve data skipping. It *creates* new files and leaves the old ones behind; it deletes nothing.\n- **C — `OPTIMIZE … V-ORDER`.** Same story with a different write-time layout optimisation.\n\n`OPTIMIZE` and `VACUUM` are the standard pairing: optimize to compact, then vacuum to clean up what compaction orphaned."
    },
