  {
    "id": "T1Q80",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1. Warehouse1 contains two schemas name schema1 and schema2 and a\ntable named schema1.city.\nYou need to make a copy of schema1.city in schema2. The solution must minimize the copying of data.\nWhich T-SQL statement should you run?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "INSERT INTO schema2.city SELECT * FROM schema1.city;"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "SELECT * INTO schema2.city FROM schema1.city;"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "CREATE TABLE schema2.city AS CLONE OF schema1.city;"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "CREATE TABLE schema2.city AS SELECT * FROM schema1.city;"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q80",
    "sourceId": "T1Q80",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**Zero-copy clone.** `CREATE TABLE … AS CLONE OF` creates a new table that **points at the existing Parquet files** rather than duplicating them. The doc: *\"Microsoft Fabric offers the capability to create near-instantaneous **zero-copy clones** with minimal storage costs.\"* Only the metadata is written, so \"minimize the copying of data\" is satisfied absolutely — no data is copied at all.\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE schema2.city AS CLONE OF schema1.city;\n```\n\nCloning across **schemas within the same warehouse** is supported, which is exactly this scenario.\n\n- **D `CREATE TABLE … AS SELECT` (CTAS)** – the runner-up at 33% of the vote. It produces an independent physical copy: every row is read and rewritten. Correct result, maximum data movement.\n- **A `INSERT INTO … SELECT`** – also a full copy, and it requires `schema2.city` to exist first.\n- **B `SELECT * INTO`** – full copy again, and `SELECT … INTO` is not supported in Fabric Warehouse.\n\nThe clone is independent after creation: changes to either table do not affect the other, because Delta files are immutable and each table gets its own log going forward.",
    "answer": "C",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1. Warehouse1 contains two schemas name schema1 and schema2 and a\ntable named schema1.city.\nYou need to make a copy of schema1.city in schema2. The solution must minimize the copying of data.\nWhich T-SQL statement should you run?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "INSERT INTO schema2.city SELECT * FROM schema1.city;",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "SELECT * INTO schema2.city FROM schema1.city;",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "CREATE TABLE schema2.city AS CLONE OF schema1.city;",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "CREATE TABLE schema2.city AS SELECT * FROM schema1.city;",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**Zero-copy clone.** `CREATE TABLE … AS CLONE OF` creates a new table that **points at the existing Parquet files** rather than duplicating them. The doc: *\"Microsoft Fabric offers the capability to create near-instantaneous **zero-copy clones** with minimal storage costs.\"* Only the metadata is written, so \"minimize the copying of data\" is satisfied absolutely — no data is copied at all.\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE schema2.city AS CLONE OF schema1.city;\n```\n\nCloning across **schemas within the same warehouse** is supported, which is exactly this scenario.\n\n- **D `CREATE TABLE … AS SELECT` (CTAS)** – the runner-up at 33% of the vote. It produces an independent physical copy: every row is read and rewritten. Correct result, maximum data movement.\n- **A `INSERT INTO … SELECT`** – also a full copy, and it requires `schema2.city` to exist first.\n- **B `SELECT * INTO`** – full copy again, and `SELECT … INTO` is not supported in Fabric Warehouse.\n\nThe clone is independent after creation: changes to either table do not affect the other, because Delta files are immutable and each table gets its own log going forward."
    },
