  {
    "id": "T1Q83",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou are creating an Azure Data Factory pipeline.\nYou have a stored procedure that returns the number of active customers and their average sales for the current month.\nYou need to add an activity that will execute the stored procedure in a warehouse. The returned values must be available to the downstream\nactivities of the pipeline.\nWhich type of activity should you add?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Switch"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Copy data"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Append variable"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Lookup"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q83",
    "sourceId": "T1Q83",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "Two requirements: execute a stored procedure, **and** make its result available downstream. Only **Lookup** does both. The doc: *\"Lookup activity reads and returns the content of a configuration file or table. **It also returns the result of executing a query or stored procedure.** The output can be a singleton value or an array of attributes, which can be consumed in a subsequent copy, transformation, or control flow activity.\"*\n\nDownstream activities then read it as `@activity('Lookup1').output.firstRow.<column>`.\n\n- **The Stored procedure activity** (not offered here) would execute the procedure but **discards the result set** — which is why Lookup is the answer when the values must flow onward.\n- **A Switch** – control flow branching on a value; it does not execute procedures.\n- **B Copy data** – can *run* a stored procedure as a source, but it writes the rows to a sink rather than exposing them to later activities.\n- **C Append variable** – adds a value to an existing array variable. It executes nothing.\n\n*Note:* a commenter who sat the exam reports a live variant whose options are Switch / Copy / Script / Stored procedure — a different option set that would need a different answer. With the options as printed here, Lookup is correct.",
    "answer": "D",
    "officialAnswer": "D",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "D",
      "answer": "D",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou are creating an Azure Data Factory pipeline.\nYou have a stored procedure that returns the number of active customers and their average sales for the current month.\nYou need to add an activity that will execute the stored procedure in a warehouse. The returned values must be available to the downstream\nactivities of the pipeline.\nWhich type of activity should you add?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Switch",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Copy data",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Append variable",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Lookup",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Two requirements: execute a stored procedure, **and** make its result available downstream. Only **Lookup** does both. The doc: *\"Lookup activity reads and returns the content of a configuration file or table. **It also returns the result of executing a query or stored procedure.** The output can be a singleton value or an array of attributes, which can be consumed in a subsequent copy, transformation, or control flow activity.\"*\n\nDownstream activities then read it as `@activity('Lookup1').output.firstRow.<column>`.\n\n- **The Stored procedure activity** (not offered here) would execute the procedure but **discards the result set** — which is why Lookup is the answer when the values must flow onward.\n- **A Switch** – control flow branching on a value; it does not execute procedures.\n- **B Copy data** – can *run* a stored procedure as a source, but it writes the rows to a sink rather than exposing them to later activities.\n- **C Append variable** – adds a value to an existing array variable. It executes nothing.\n\n*Note:* a commenter who sat the exam reports a live variant whose options are Switch / Copy / Script / Stored procedure — a different option set that would need a different answer. With the options as printed here, Lookup is correct."
    },
