  {
    "id": "T1Q84",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains two workspaces named Workspace1 and Workspace2. Workspace1 contains a lakehouse named\nLakehouse1. Workspace2 contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse2. Lakehouse1 contains a table named dbo.Sales. Lakehouse2 contains a table\nnamed dbo.Customers.\nYou need to ensure that you can write queries that reference both dbo.Sales and dbo.Customers in the same SQL query without making additional\ncopies of the tables.\nWhat should you use?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "a shortcut"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "a dataflow"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "a view"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "a managed table"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q84",
    "sourceId": "T1Q84",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "The tables live in **two different workspaces**, and the requirement is a single SQL query over both *\"without making additional copies of the tables\"*. That is the definition of a **shortcut**: *\"Shortcuts are objects in OneLake that point to other storage locations … Shortcuts allow you to reuse data **without copying it**.\"*\n\nCreate a shortcut in Lakehouse1 pointing at Lakehouse2's `dbo.Customers`; it then appears in Lakehouse1's Tables area and its SQL analytics endpoint, so `dbo.Sales` and the shortcut can be joined in one query.\n\n- **C a view** – a view cannot reach across workspaces. Cross-database queries work **within** a workspace; to span workspaces you must create the shortcut *first* (and then a view over it, if you want one).\n- **B a dataflow** – would copy the data into the target. Explicitly excluded.\n- **D a managed table** – also a copy.",
    "answer": "A",
    "officialAnswer": "A",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "A",
      "answer": "A",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains two workspaces named Workspace1 and Workspace2. Workspace1 contains a lakehouse named\nLakehouse1. Workspace2 contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse2. Lakehouse1 contains a table named dbo.Sales. Lakehouse2 contains a table\nnamed dbo.Customers.\nYou need to ensure that you can write queries that reference both dbo.Sales and dbo.Customers in the same SQL query without making additional\ncopies of the tables.\nWhat should you use?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "a shortcut",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "a dataflow",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "a view",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "a managed table",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The tables live in **two different workspaces**, and the requirement is a single SQL query over both *\"without making additional copies of the tables\"*. That is the definition of a **shortcut**: *\"Shortcuts are objects in OneLake that point to other storage locations … Shortcuts allow you to reuse data **without copying it**.\"*\n\nCreate a shortcut in Lakehouse1 pointing at Lakehouse2's `dbo.Customers`; it then appears in Lakehouse1's Tables area and its SQL analytics endpoint, so `dbo.Sales` and the shortcut can be joined in one query.\n\n- **C a view** – a view cannot reach across workspaces. Cross-database queries work **within** a workspace; to span workspaces you must create the shortcut *first* (and then a view over it, if you want one).\n- **B a dataflow** – would copy the data into the target. Explicitly excluded.\n- **D a managed table** – also a copy."
    },
