  {
    "id": "T1Q85",
    "type": "multi",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse.\nYou are designing a star schema model that will contain a customer dimension. The customer dimension table will be a Type 2 slowly changing\ndimension (SCD).\nYou need to recommend which columns to add to the table. The columns must NOT already exist in the source.\nWhich three types of columns should you recommend? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "a foreign key"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "a natural key"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "an effective end date and time"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "a surrogate key"
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "an effective start date and time"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q85",
    "sourceId": "T1Q85",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "A **Type 2** dimension keeps history by inserting a **new row** each time a tracked attribute changes. That forces three columns that do not exist in the source system:\n\n- **D — a surrogate key.** Once the same customer has several rows, the natural key is no longer unique, so it can no longer be the primary key. A model-generated surrogate key restores uniqueness and is what the fact table points at, so each fact row is tied to the version of the customer that was current at the time.\n- **E — an effective start date and time** and **C — an effective end date and time.** These bound the validity window of each version, so you can answer \"what did this customer look like on date X\".\n\nRejected:\n- **B — a natural key.** It comes *from* the source (the business key, e.g. `CustomerID`). It must be carried into the dimension, but it is not something you *add* — and the question says the columns *\"must NOT already exist in the source\"*.\n- **A — a foreign key.** Foreign keys live in the **fact** table, pointing at the dimension. A dimension in a star schema does not carry one (that would be snowflaking).",
    "answer": "CDE",
    "officialAnswer": "CDE",
    "original": {
      "type": "multi",
      "officialAnswer": "CDE",
      "answer": "CDE",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse.\nYou are designing a star schema model that will contain a customer dimension. The customer dimension table will be a Type 2 slowly changing\ndimension (SCD).\nYou need to recommend which columns to add to the table. The columns must NOT already exist in the source.\nWhich three types of columns should you recommend? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "a foreign key",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "a natural key",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "an effective end date and time",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "a surrogate key",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "E",
          "text": "an effective start date and time",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "A **Type 2** dimension keeps history by inserting a **new row** each time a tracked attribute changes. That forces three columns that do not exist in the source system:\n\n- **D — a surrogate key.** Once the same customer has several rows, the natural key is no longer unique, so it can no longer be the primary key. A model-generated surrogate key restores uniqueness and is what the fact table points at, so each fact row is tied to the version of the customer that was current at the time.\n- **E — an effective start date and time** and **C — an effective end date and time.** These bound the validity window of each version, so you can answer \"what did this customer look like on date X\".\n\nRejected:\n- **B — a natural key.** It comes *from* the source (the business key, e.g. `CustomerID`). It must be carried into the dimension, but it is not something you *add* — and the question says the columns *\"must NOT already exist in the source\"*.\n- **A — a foreign key.** Foreign keys live in the **fact** table, pointing at the dimension. A dimension in a star schema does not carry one (that would be snowflaking)."
    },
