      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a Microsoft Power BI report named Report1.\nReport1 is slow to render. You suspect that an inefficient DAX query is being executed.\nYou need to identify the slowest DAX query, and then review how long the query spends in the formula engine as compared to the storage engine.\nWhich five actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and\narrange them in the correct order.",
      "explanation": "The workflow crosses two tools: Performance analyzer **finds** the slow query, DAX Studio **explains** it.\n\n1. **From Performance analyzer, capture a recording.** *Optimize ribbon → Performance analyzer → Start recording*, then refresh the visuals. Each visual gets a row broken into DAX query / Visual display / Other.\n2. **Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order.** Puts the slowest work at the top.\n3. **Copy the first query to DAX Studio.** Performance analyzer offers *Copy query* on the DAX query line; paste it into DAX Studio.\n4. **Enable Query Timings and Server Timings. Run the query.** These two toggles must be on **before** you run, because the timings are collected from the trace during execution.\n5. **View the Server Timings tab.** This is the only place that reports the **FE** (formula engine) versus **SE** (storage engine) split the question asks for, along with the SE query count and scanned rows.\n\n**Why not \"View the Query Timings tab\"?** Query Timings shows the total and the phases of the run, but the FE/SE breakdown lives in **Server Timings**.\n\n*Note:* this variant offers two sort options — the plain one and *\"…by DAX query time\"*. The answer-key image marks the plain one; a commenter who sat the live exam reports it offered only the *by DAX query time* wording. Both orderings are accepted here."
    },
