  {
    "id": "T1Q94",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Microsoft Power BI semantic model that contains measures. The measures use multiple CALCULATE functions and a FILTER function.\nYou are evaluating the performance of the measures.\nIn which use case will replacing the FILTER function with the KEEPFILTERS function reduce execution time?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "when the FILTER function uses a nested calculate function"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "when the FILTER function references a measure"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "when the FILTER function references columns from multiple tables"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "when the FILTER function references a column from a single table that uses Import mode"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q94",
    "sourceId": "T1Q94",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "Microsoft's DAX best-practice article *Avoid using FILTER as a filter argument* explains the optimisation. A filter argument to `CALCULATE` can be written either as a **table expression** (`FILTER(...)`) or as a **Boolean expression** — and the Boolean form is faster, because the engine can push it down as a simple predicate instead of materialising and iterating a table. `KEEPFILTERS` is how you keep the intersect-with-existing-context semantics while using the Boolean form.\n\nBut a Boolean filter expression carries documented **restrictions**. It:\n- cannot reference columns from **multiple tables**,\n- cannot reference a **measure**,\n- cannot use a nested `CALCULATE`.\n\nOptions **A**, **B** and **C** each name one of those restrictions, so in each of those cases you *cannot* make the swap — `FILTER` is required. **D** describes the one case where the swap is both legal and beneficial: a single-table column reference. The article also notes it *\"is especially relevant for model calculations that apply filters to **Import** tables\"*, which is the rest of D.",
    "answer": "D",
    "officialAnswer": "D",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "D",
      "answer": "D",
      "stem": "You have a Microsoft Power BI semantic model that contains measures. The measures use multiple CALCULATE functions and a FILTER function.\nYou are evaluating the performance of the measures.\nIn which use case will replacing the FILTER function with the KEEPFILTERS function reduce execution time?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "when the FILTER function uses a nested calculate function",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "when the FILTER function references a measure",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "when the FILTER function references columns from multiple tables",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "when the FILTER function references a column from a single table that uses Import mode",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Microsoft's DAX best-practice article *Avoid using FILTER as a filter argument* explains the optimisation. A filter argument to `CALCULATE` can be written either as a **table expression** (`FILTER(...)`) or as a **Boolean expression** — and the Boolean form is faster, because the engine can push it down as a simple predicate instead of materialising and iterating a table. `KEEPFILTERS` is how you keep the intersect-with-existing-context semantics while using the Boolean form.\n\nBut a Boolean filter expression carries documented **restrictions**. It:\n- cannot reference columns from **multiple tables**,\n- cannot reference a **measure**,\n- cannot use a nested `CALCULATE`.\n\nOptions **A**, **B** and **C** each name one of those restrictions, so in each of those cases you *cannot* make the swap — `FILTER` is required. **D** describes the one case where the swap is both legal and beneficial: a single-table column reference. The article also notes it *\"is especially relevant for model calculations that apply filters to **Import** tables\"*, which is the rest of D."
    },
