  {
    "id": "T1Q95",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains five tables that all use Import mode. Model1 contains a dynamic row-level security\n(RLS) role named HR. The HR role filters employee data so that HR managers only see the data of the department to which they are assigned.\nYou publish Model1 to a Fabric tenant and configure RLS role membership. You share the model and related reports to users.\nAn HR manager reports that the data they see in a report is incomplete.\nWhat should you do to validate the data seen by the HR Manager?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Select Test as role to view the data as the HR role."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Filter the data in the report to match the intended logic of the filter for the HR department."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Select Test as role to view the report as the HR manager."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Ask the HR manager to open the report in Microsoft Power BI Desktop."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q95",
    "sourceId": "T1Q95",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "The word that decides this is **dynamic**. A dynamic RLS role filters on the *current user's identity* — typically `USERPRINCIPALNAME()` matched against an employee table. So the filter produces a **different result for every user**, and testing \"the HR role\" in the abstract tells you nothing about what this particular manager sees.\n\nIn the Fabric/Power BI service, **Security → Test as role** lets you select the role **and** enter a specific user to impersonate. Viewing the report **as the HR manager** reproduces their exact filter context, which is the only way to confirm whether the data is genuinely incomplete or the RLS logic is misfiring for them. That is **C**.\n\n- **A — Test as role to view the data as the HR role.** This is the right *tool* with the wrong *scope*. With **static** RLS it would be enough; with dynamic RLS there is no single \"HR role view\" to inspect, because the filter depends on who is asking. 41% of the community picks this, reasoning that the role is named \"HR\", not \"HR manager\" — but the role name is not the issue, the identity is.\n- **B — filter the report manually.** Reproduces what you *think* the logic should be, not what RLS actually does. It cannot validate anything.\n- **D — have the manager open it in Desktop.** Desktop's *View as* does not apply the service's role membership, and the manager may not even have the model.",
    "answer": "C",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "You have a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains five tables that all use Import mode. Model1 contains a dynamic row-level security\n(RLS) role named HR. The HR role filters employee data so that HR managers only see the data of the department to which they are assigned.\nYou publish Model1 to a Fabric tenant and configure RLS role membership. You share the model and related reports to users.\nAn HR manager reports that the data they see in a report is incomplete.\nWhat should you do to validate the data seen by the HR Manager?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Select Test as role to view the data as the HR role.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Filter the data in the report to match the intended logic of the filter for the HR department.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Select Test as role to view the report as the HR manager.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Ask the HR manager to open the report in Microsoft Power BI Desktop.",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The word that decides this is **dynamic**. A dynamic RLS role filters on the *current user's identity* — typically `USERPRINCIPALNAME()` matched against an employee table. So the filter produces a **different result for every user**, and testing \"the HR role\" in the abstract tells you nothing about what this particular manager sees.\n\nIn the Fabric/Power BI service, **Security → Test as role** lets you select the role **and** enter a specific user to impersonate. Viewing the report **as the HR manager** reproduces their exact filter context, which is the only way to confirm whether the data is genuinely incomplete or the RLS logic is misfiring for them. That is **C**.\n\n- **A — Test as role to view the data as the HR role.** This is the right *tool* with the wrong *scope*. With **static** RLS it would be enough; with dynamic RLS there is no single \"HR role view\" to inspect, because the filter depends on who is asking. 41% of the community picks this, reasoning that the role is named \"HR\", not \"HR manager\" — but the role name is not the issue, the identity is.\n- **B — filter the report manually.** Reproduces what you *think* the logic should be, not what RLS actually does. It cannot validate anything.\n- **D — have the manager open it in Desktop.** Desktop's *View as* does not apply the service's role membership, and the manager may not even have the model."
    },
