          "answer": "COALESCE"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named Sales.Products. Sales.Products contains the following columns.\nYou need to write a T-SQL query that will return the following columns.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**GREATEST — COALESCE.** Two requirements that sound similar but are not:\n\n| Output column | Requirement | Function |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `HighestSellingPrice` | *\"the **highest value** from ListPrice, WholesalePrice and AgentPrice\"* | **`GREATEST`** |\n| `TradePrice` | *\"AgentPrice **if present**, otherwise WholesalePrice if present, otherwise ListPrice\"* | **`COALESCE`** |\n\n**`GREATEST`** *\"returns the maximum value from a list of one or more expressions\"* — a **row-wise** comparison across columns.\n- **`MAX` is the trap.** It is an **aggregate**: it finds the maximum down a column across rows, and it needs a `GROUP BY`. It cannot compare three columns on one row.\n- `IIF` handles one condition and two outcomes; comparing three values would need nesting.\n\n**`COALESCE`** *\"evaluates the arguments in order and returns the current value of the first expression that initially doesn't evaluate to NULL\"* — the \"if present, otherwise…\" chain, exactly. It works here because `WholesalePrice` and `AgentPrice` are nullable while `ListPrice` is `NOT NULL`, so the chain always terminates.\n- `CHOOSE(index, …)` selects by ordinal position, not by nullability.\n\n*(Note the printed argument order for `TradePrice` is `(ListPrice, WholesalePrice, AgentPrice)`, which is the reverse of the stated precedence — an error in the question. The arguments are pre-filled and not part of the blank; `COALESCE` is still the only function that expresses the requirement.)*"
    },
    "sourceVisualReferences": {
