    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q99",
    "sourceId": "T1Q99",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**DATETRUNC — weekday.**\n\n```sql\nSELECT OrderID, CustomerID,\n  DATETRUNC(month, OrderDate)   AS PeriodDate,\n  DATENAME(weekday, OrderDate)  AS DayName\nFROM Sales.Orders;\n```\n\n**Blank 1 — `DATETRUNC`.** The spec is *\"a date representing the **first day of the month** for OrderDate\"*. `DATETRUNC` *\"returns an input date truncated to a specified datepart\"* — `DATETRUNC(month, '2024-03-17')` gives `2024-03-01`, a **date**.\n- **`DATEPART`** returns an **integer** (`3`), not a date. This is the trap, and the distinction is what the question tests.\n- `DATEFROMPARTS(y, m, d)` builds a date from three separate integers; it does not take a date as input.\n- `DATE_BUCKET` snaps a date to fixed-width buckets from an origin — more machinery than needed, and not a month-start truncation.\n\n**Blank 2 — `weekday`.** The spec is *\"the **name of the day**, such as Wednesday\"*. `DATENAME` returns a character string for the requested part, and the part that yields the weekday name is **`weekday`** (`dw`).\n- `day` returns the day **of the month** — `DATENAME(day, …)` gives `\"17\"`.\n- `dayofyear` gives the ordinal day in the year — `\"77\"`.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "______ (month, OrderDate) AS PeriodDate,",
          "options": [
            "DATE_BUCKET",
            "DATEFROMPARTS",
            "DATEPART",
            "DATETRUNC"
          ],
          "answer": "DATETRUNC"
        },
        {
          "label": "DATENAME(  ______ , OrderDate) AS DayName",
          "options": [
            "day",
            "dayofyear",
            "weekday"
          ],
          "answer": "weekday"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named Sales.Orders. Sales.Orders contains the following columns.\nYou need to write a T-SQL query that will return the following columns.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**DATETRUNC — weekday.**\n\n```sql\nSELECT OrderID, CustomerID,\n  DATETRUNC(month, OrderDate)   AS PeriodDate,\n  DATENAME(weekday, OrderDate)  AS DayName\nFROM Sales.Orders;\n```\n\n**Blank 1 — `DATETRUNC`.** The spec is *\"a date representing the **first day of the month** for OrderDate\"*. `DATETRUNC` *\"returns an input date truncated to a specified datepart\"* — `DATETRUNC(month, '2024-03-17')` gives `2024-03-01`, a **date**.\n- **`DATEPART`** returns an **integer** (`3`), not a date. This is the trap, and the distinction is what the question tests.\n- `DATEFROMPARTS(y, m, d)` builds a date from three separate integers; it does not take a date as input.\n- `DATE_BUCKET` snaps a date to fixed-width buckets from an origin — more machinery than needed, and not a month-start truncation.\n\n**Blank 2 — `weekday`.** The spec is *\"the **name of the day**, such as Wednesday\"*. `DATENAME` returns a character string for the requested part, and the part that yields the weekday name is **`weekday`** (`dw`).\n- `day` returns the day **of the month** — `DATENAME(day, …)` gives `\"17\"`.\n- `dayofyear` gives the ordinal day in the year — `\"77\"`."
    },
