    "id": "T1Q100",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains JSON files in OneLake. The files have one billion items.\nYou plan to perform time series analysis of the items.\nYou need to transform the data, visualize the data to find insights, perform anomaly detection, and share the insights with other business users.\nThe solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Use parallel processing.\n• Minimize the duplication of data.\n• Minimize how long it takes to load the data.\nWhat should you use to transform and visualize the data?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "the PySpark library in a Fabric notebook"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "the pandas library in a Fabric notebook"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "a Microsoft Power BI report that uses core visuals"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q100",
    "sourceId": "T1Q100",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "Three requirements, and **PySpark in a Fabric notebook** is the only option that satisfies all three for **one billion items**:\n\n- **Use parallel processing.** Spark distributes work across the executors of the pool. This alone eliminates **B**: pandas is **single-node, single-threaded** and holds the whole frame in one machine's memory — a billion rows will not fit, let alone run in parallel.\n- **Minimize duplication of data.** Spark reads the JSON in OneLake in place. A Power BI Import model would copy it; a DirectQuery model over raw JSON files is not viable.\n- **Transform *and* visualize, plus anomaly detection.** A notebook does all of it: PySpark for the transformation, `display()` and the chart view (or matplotlib/seaborn) for visuals, and Spark ML / SynapseML for anomaly detection.\n\n- **C — a Power BI report with core visuals** can visualise but cannot *transform* a billion JSON items, and core visuals have no anomaly detection over this shape of data.\n\n*(The \"share the insights\" part is then handled by the notebook itself or by a report built on the curated output — it is not what distinguishes the options.)*",
    "answer": "A",
    "officialAnswer": "A",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "A",
      "answer": "A",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains JSON files in OneLake. The files have one billion items.\nYou plan to perform time series analysis of the items.\nYou need to transform the data, visualize the data to find insights, perform anomaly detection, and share the insights with other business users.\nThe solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Use parallel processing.\n• Minimize the duplication of data.\n• Minimize how long it takes to load the data.\nWhat should you use to transform and visualize the data?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "the PySpark library in a Fabric notebook",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "the pandas library in a Fabric notebook",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "a Microsoft Power BI report that uses core visuals",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Three requirements, and **PySpark in a Fabric notebook** is the only option that satisfies all three for **one billion items**:\n\n- **Use parallel processing.** Spark distributes work across the executors of the pool. This alone eliminates **B**: pandas is **single-node, single-threaded** and holds the whole frame in one machine's memory — a billion rows will not fit, let alone run in parallel.\n- **Minimize duplication of data.** Spark reads the JSON in OneLake in place. A Power BI Import model would copy it; a DirectQuery model over raw JSON files is not viable.\n- **Transform *and* visualize, plus anomaly detection.** A notebook does all of it: PySpark for the transformation, `display()` and the chart view (or matplotlib/seaborn) for visuals, and Spark ML / SynapseML for anomaly detection.\n\n- **C — a Power BI report with core visuals** can visualise but cannot *transform* a billion JSON items, and core visuals have no anomaly detection over this shape of data.\n\n*(The \"share the insights\" part is then handled by the notebook itself or by a report built on the curated output — it is not what distinguishes the options.)*"
    },
