    "id": "T1Q107",
    "type": "yesno",
    "stem": "Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that\nmight meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1.\nYou discover that the following query performs slowly against Model1.\nYou need to reduce the execution time of the query.\nSolution: You replace line 4 by using the following code:\nISEMPTY ( RELATEDTABLE ( 'Order Item' ) )\nDoes this meet the goal?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "Original slow DAX query (line 4)",
        "language": "dax",
        "content": "EVALUATE\nFILTER (\n    VALUES ( Customer[Customer Name] ),\n    CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( 'Order Item' ) ) > 0\n)\nORDER BY Customer[Customer Name]"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q107",
    "sourceId": "T1Q107",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**No — the logic is inverted.** The original query is\n\n```dax\nEVALUATE\n  FILTER (\n    VALUES ( Customer[Customer Name] ),\n    CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( 'Order Item' ) ) > 0   -- line 4\n  )\nORDER BY Customer[Customer Name]\n```\n\nLine 4 keeps customers that **have** at least one order. The proposed replacement is\n\n```dax\nISEMPTY ( RELATEDTABLE ( 'Order Item' ) )\n```\n\nwhich is `TRUE` when the customer has **no** orders. It returns the exact complement of the intended set — customers *without* orders. Wrong result, so it cannot \"meet the goal\" no matter how fast it is.\n\nThe missing piece is the negation: **`NOT ISEMPTY ( ... )`** — which is what T1Q108 proposes, and why that one is **Yes**.\n\n*(`ISEMPTY` is genuinely the faster idiom: it stops at the first row instead of counting them all. The optimisation is right; the sign is wrong.)*",
    "answer": "B",
    "officialAnswer": "B",
    "original": {
      "type": "yesno",
      "officialAnswer": "B",
      "answer": "B",
      "stem": "Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that\nmight meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1.\nYou discover that the following query performs slowly against Model1.\nYou need to reduce the execution time of the query.\nSolution: You replace line 4 by using the following code:\nISEMPTY ( RELATEDTABLE ( 'Order Item' ) )\nDoes this meet the goal?",
