    "reviewNote": "Specified applicable numeric/string statistics rather than impossible text arithmetic.",
    "remaining": "Complete current primary-doc section review, source screenshot/case checks and native fixture where material; then independently challenge every final option.",
    "explanation": "**Yes.** `df.describe()` *\"computes basic statistics for numeric and string columns, including **count, mean, stddev, min, and max**. If no columns are given, this function computes statistics for all numerical or string columns.\"*\n\nThe requirement asks for **min, max, mean and standard deviation** across **string and numeric** columns — every one of those is in the list, and the no-argument form covers all columns. Wrapping it in `.show()` renders the resulting DataFrame, so the values are actually displayed.\n\n*Note:* 39% vote No, on the grounds that `summary()` is the broader function. That is true — `summary()` adds the quartiles and lets you pick statistics — but \"broader\" does not make `describe()` insufficient. Everything the question asks for is in `describe()`'s output, so the solution meets the goal. Both `df.summary()` (T1Q56) and `df.describe().show()` are Yes in this series.",
    "answer": "A",
    "officialAnswer": "A",
    "original": {
      "type": "yesno",
      "officialAnswer": "A",
      "answer": "A",
      "stem": "Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that\nmight meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a new semantic model in OneLake.\nYou use a Fabric notebook to read the data into a Spark DataFrame.\nYou need to evaluate the data to calculate the min, max, mean, and standard deviation values for all the string and numeric columns.\nSolution: You use the following PySpark expression:\ndf.describe().show()\nDoes this meet the goal?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Yes",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "No",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**Yes.** `df.describe()` *\"computes basic statistics for numeric and string columns, including **count, mean, stddev, min, and max**. If no columns are given, this function computes statistics for all numerical or string columns.\"*\n\nThe requirement asks for **min, max, mean and standard deviation** across **string and numeric** columns — every one of those is in the list, and the no-argument form covers all columns. Wrapping it in `.show()` renders the resulting DataFrame, so the values are actually displayed.\n\n*Note:* 39% vote No, on the grounds that `summary()` is the broader function. That is true — `summary()` adds the quartiles and lets you pick statistics — but \"broader\" does not make `describe()` insufficient. Everything the question asks for is in `describe()`'s output, so the solution meets the goal. Both `df.summary()` (T1Q56) and `df.describe().show()` are Yes in this series."
    },
