    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q115",
    "sourceId": "T1Q115",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**Remove the column — Split the column.** Both columns are enormous because VertiPaq compression collapses as **cardinality** rises, and each needs a different fix.\n\n**`TransactionKey` → Remove.** 2.5 billion distinct values, essentially one per row, costing **160 GB**. It is an *incrementing surrogate key* on a **fact** table — and a fact-table surrogate key has no analytical use: nobody slices, filters or aggregates by it, and no relationship needs it (relationships point *outward* to dimensions). Microsoft's data-reduction guidance says to remove unnecessary columns, and *\"consider removing… a primary key column of a fact table\"*. Dropping it is the single biggest win and speeds up refresh because there is that much less to encode.\n\n**`SaleDateTime` → Split.** The third requirement — *\"ensure that the **datetime value** for each sales transaction is available in the model\"* — forbids removing or truncating it. Splitting into a **date** column and a **time** column preserves the full value while collapsing cardinality: 6,850,273 distinct datetimes become a few thousand dates × 86,400 possible times, both of which compress well. This is Microsoft's documented technique: *\"split the column into separate date and time columns\"*.\n\nRejected:\n- **Truncate the data** on `SaleDateTime` would drop the time component, breaking the \"to the nearest second\" requirement.\n- **Change the data type** on either column is marginal — the cost is the *number of distinct values*, which a type change does not reduce.\n- **Remove** `SaleDateTime` violates the third requirement outright.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "TransactionKey:  (cardinality 2,500,350,000 — 160 GB — incrementing surrogate key for fact table SalesTransaction)",
          "options": [
            "Change the data type of the column.",
            "Remove the column.",
            "Split the column.",
            "Truncate the data."
          ],
          "answer": "Remove the column."
        },
        {
          "label": "SaleDateTime:  (cardinality 6,850,273 — 120 GB — datetime to the nearest second of when a sale occurred)",
          "options": [
            "Change the data type of the column.",
            "Remove the column.",
            "Split the column.",
            "Truncate the data."
          ],
          "answer": "Split the column."
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named model1. The two largest columns in model1 are shown in the following table.\nYou need to optimize model1. The solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Reduce the model size.\n• Increase refresh performance when using Import mode.\n• Ensure that the datetime value for each sales transaction is available in the model.\nWhat should you do on each column? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**Remove the column — Split the column.** Both columns are enormous because VertiPaq compression collapses as **cardinality** rises, and each needs a different fix.\n\n**`TransactionKey` → Remove.** 2.5 billion distinct values, essentially one per row, costing **160 GB**. It is an *incrementing surrogate key* on a **fact** table — and a fact-table surrogate key has no analytical use: nobody slices, filters or aggregates by it, and no relationship needs it (relationships point *outward* to dimensions). Microsoft's data-reduction guidance says to remove unnecessary columns, and *\"consider removing… a primary key column of a fact table\"*. Dropping it is the single biggest win and speeds up refresh because there is that much less to encode.\n\n**`SaleDateTime` → Split.** The third requirement — *\"ensure that the **datetime value** for each sales transaction is available in the model\"* — forbids removing or truncating it. Splitting into a **date** column and a **time** column preserves the full value while collapsing cardinality: 6,850,273 distinct datetimes become a few thousand dates × 86,400 possible times, both of which compress well. This is Microsoft's documented technique: *\"split the column into separate date and time columns\"*.\n\nRejected:\n- **Truncate the data** on `SaleDateTime` would drop the time component, breaking the \"to the nearest second\" requirement.\n- **Change the data type** on either column is marginal — the cost is the *number of distinct values*, which a type change does not reduce.\n- **Remove** `SaleDateTime` violates the third requirement outright."
    },
