    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q116",
    "sourceId": "T1Q116",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "The finished query:\n\n```sql\nSELECT TOP(1000) CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)\nFROM DimCustomer\nGROUP BY CustomerAltKey\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1;\n```\n\n`CustomerAltKey` is the email column (see the sample data). Group by it, then keep only the groups that occur more than once — those are the duplicates.\n\nThree distractors, each testing a specific rule:\n- **`LIMIT 1000`** – `LIMIT` is MySQL/PostgreSQL/Spark syntax. T-SQL uses **`TOP (n)`**, which is why the row cap is baked into the `SELECT` line rather than appended at the end.\n- **`SELECT CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)`** – correct projection but no row cap, so it fails *\"a maximum of 1,000 records\"*.\n- **`WHERE COUNT(*) > 1`** – invalid. `WHERE` filters rows **before** aggregation and cannot contain an aggregate function; filtering groups is what **`HAVING`** is for.\n\nThe clause order is fixed by T-SQL syntax: `SELECT … FROM … GROUP BY … HAVING …`.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
