    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**No — No — Yes.** The query is\n\n```sql\nSELECT e.[WWI Employee ID], e.Employee, e.[Preferred Name],\n       gdr.[WWI Employee ID] AS [Direct Report ID],\n       gdr.Employee            AS [Direct Report]\nFROM Dimension.Employee AS e\nOUTER APPLY Dimension.GetDirectReports(e.[Employee Key]) AS gdr;\n```\n\n**1. It is a scalar function → No.** *(The PDF answer-key image marks **Yes**; it is wrong.)* Two things in the query prove it is a **table-valued function**: it is given a **table alias** (`AS gdr`), and **individual columns are selected from that alias** (`gdr.[WWI Employee ID]`, `gdr.Employee`). A scalar function returns *one value* — it has no columns to alias and cannot appear as the right operand of `APPLY`. The community is clear on this too (+22, +10, +6 all answer No).\n\n**2. It will run only once → No.** `APPLY` is a **correlated** operator: the doc describes it as invoking the right-hand table expression *\"for each row of the left table expression\"*. Its argument here is `e.[Employee Key]`, which differs per row — so the function runs once per employee.\n\n**3. At least one output row per Employee row → Yes.** This is the difference between the two forms of APPLY:\n\n| Form | Left rows with no function output |\n| --- | --- |\n| `CROSS APPLY` | **dropped** (inner-join semantics) |\n| **`OUTER APPLY`** | **kept, with NULLs** (left-outer-join semantics) |\n\nBecause it is `OUTER APPLY`, an employee with no direct reports still produces one row, with NULL in the Direct Report columns. So every Employee row yields at least one output row.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-yesno",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "statements": [
        {
          "text": "Dimension.GetDirectReports is a scalar T-SQL function.",
          "answer": "No"
        },
        {
          "text": "The Dimension.GetDirectReports function will run only once when the query runs.",
          "answer": "No"
        },
        {
          "text": "The output rows will include at least one row for each row in the Dimension.Employee table.",
          "answer": "Yes"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named WH1.\nYou run the following T-SQL query against WH1.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**No — No — Yes.** The query is\n\n```sql\nSELECT e.[WWI Employee ID], e.Employee, e.[Preferred Name],\n       gdr.[WWI Employee ID] AS [Direct Report ID],\n       gdr.Employee            AS [Direct Report]\nFROM Dimension.Employee AS e\nOUTER APPLY Dimension.GetDirectReports(e.[Employee Key]) AS gdr;\n```\n\n**1. It is a scalar function → No.** *(The PDF answer-key image marks **Yes**; it is wrong.)* Two things in the query prove it is a **table-valued function**: it is given a **table alias** (`AS gdr`), and **individual columns are selected from that alias** (`gdr.[WWI Employee ID]`, `gdr.Employee`). A scalar function returns *one value* — it has no columns to alias and cannot appear as the right operand of `APPLY`. The community is clear on this too (+22, +10, +6 all answer No).\n\n**2. It will run only once → No.** `APPLY` is a **correlated** operator: the doc describes it as invoking the right-hand table expression *\"for each row of the left table expression\"*. Its argument here is `e.[Employee Key]`, which differs per row — so the function runs once per employee.\n\n**3. At least one output row per Employee row → Yes.** This is the difference between the two forms of APPLY:\n\n| Form | Left rows with no function output |\n| --- | --- |\n| `CROSS APPLY` | **dropped** (inner-join semantics) |\n| **`OUTER APPLY`** | **kept, with NULLs** (left-outer-join semantics) |\n\nBecause it is `OUTER APPLY`, an employee with no direct reports still produces one row, with NULL in the Direct Report columns. So every Employee row yields at least one output row."
    },
