  {
    "id": "T1Q120",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Microsoft Power BI Premium Per User (PPU) workspace that contains a semantic model.\nYou have an Azure App Service app named App1 that modifies row-level security (RLS) for the model by using the XMLA endpoint.\nApp1 requires users to sign in by using their Microsoft Entra credentials to access the XMLA endpoint.\nYou need to configure App1 to use a service account to access the model.\nWhat should you do first?\nApp1 is a multi-user middle-tier application acting on behalf of individual users. The proposed shared identity replaces their individual XMLA access tokens. Which change first removes the PPU licensing restriction?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Add a managed identity to the workspace."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Modify the XMLA Endpoint setting."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Upgrade the workspace to Premium capacity."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Add a managed identity to App1."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q120",
    "sourceId": "T1Q120",
    "gradingEnabled": true,
    "scientificStatus": "APPROVED_CORRECTED",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Terms of use distinguishes single-user administration from multi-user applications.",
    "remaining": "",
    "explanation": "C removes the stated multi-user PPU restriction. A does not change licensing. B enables writes but does not authorize a shared identity for this PPU architecture. D changes identity configuration without removing the restriction. After capacity migration, configure XMLA read/write and model permissions separately. This does not claim all single-user PPU automation is forbidden.",
    "answer": "C",
    "officialAnswer": "D",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "D",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "You have a Microsoft Power BI Premium Per User (PPU) workspace that contains a semantic model.\nYou have an Azure App Service app named App1 that modifies row-level security (RLS) for the model by using the XMLA endpoint.\nApp1 requires users to sign in by using their Microsoft Entra credentials to access the XMLA endpoint.\nYou need to configure App1 to use a service account to access the model.\nWhat should you do first?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Add a managed identity to the workspace.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Modify the XMLA Endpoint setting.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Upgrade the workspace to Premium capacity.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Add a managed identity to App1.",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**Upgrade the workspace to Premium capacity — the PDF answer (D) is wrong.**\n\nThe workspace is **Premium Per User (PPU)**, and PPU flatly forbids the pattern App1 needs. From the XMLA endpoint doc's *Terms of use*, for a **multi-user application** (which App1 is — it serves users and modifies their RLS):\n\n> **Premium Per User (PPU) workspaces** — The application must require each user to sign in to Power BI. For each user, the application uses an access token to access the semantic models. **The application can't use a service account or other app identity to perform tasks on behalf of individual users.** Each user must have their own Power BI account…\n>\n> **For Premium workspaces**, the application can use either a service account or app identity on behalf of end users without requiring each user to sign in to Power BI.\n\nSo on PPU no amount of identity configuration will work: the blocker is the **licensing mode of the workspace**. Moving it to a Premium (or Fabric F) capacity is what makes a service account legal — and the question asks what to do **first**.\n\n- **D — add a managed identity to App1.** You will need an app identity eventually, and this is the natural next step *after* the upgrade. Done first, against a PPU workspace, it simply will not be allowed to connect on users' behalf.\n- **A — add a managed identity to the workspace.** Managed identities belong to Azure resources (such as App Services). A Power BI workspace does not take one.\n- **B — modify the XMLA Endpoint setting.** Read-write is needed to modify RLS, but that is a *later* step and does not change who may authenticate.\n\n*Note:* the vote is an exact 50/50 split, and the commenter arguing C quotes the same limitation. Worth knowing separately: the doc also states *\"Service principals do not work with RLS and OLS, and cannot be added as model role members\"* — they can **define** roles through the endpoint, which is what App1 does, but not be members of them."
    },
    "sourceVisualReferences": {
      "exhibits": [],
      "answerImages": [],
      "optionImages": []
    },
    "visualResolution": "no_exhibit_needed",
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Microsoft documentation",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/enterprise/powerbi/service-premium-connect-tools"
      }
    ],
    "reviewKind": "documentation-approved derived practice"
  },
