    "id": "T1Q123",
    "type": "hotspot-yesno",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1. Warehouse1 contains a fact table named FactSales that has one billion\nrows.\nYou run the following T-SQL statement.\nCREATE TABLE test.FactSales AS CLONE OF dbo.FactSales;\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "statements": [
      {
        "text": "A replica of dbo.FactSales is created in the test schema by copying the metadata only.",
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "text": "Additional schema changes to dbo.FactSales will also apply to test.FactSales.",
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "text": "Additional data changes to dbo.FactSales will also apply to test.FactSales.",
        "answer": "No"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q123",
    "sourceId": "T1Q123",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**Yes — No — No.** Two documented properties of a Fabric table clone settle all three.\n\n**1. Metadata only → Yes.** The `CREATE TABLE AS CLONE OF` reference is explicit: *\"Creates a new table as a **zero-copy clone** of another table in Warehouse in Microsoft Fabric. **Only the metadata of the table is copied. The underlying data of the table, stored as parquet files, is not copied.**\"* That is why a clone of a one-billion-row fact table is near-instantaneous.\n\n**2 & 3. Later changes propagate → No, and No.** A clone is a point-in-time snapshot, not a link: *\"Upon creation, a table clone is an **independent and separate** entity… Any changes made to the source table after the clone is created do not reflect in the clone, and vice versa.\"* This works because Parquet files are immutable — the clone simply keeps referencing the set of files that existed at clone time, while new writes to `dbo.FactSales` create new files that only its own log knows about. Schema changes (2) and data changes (3) are both invisible to the clone.\n\n*Note:* one commenter argues all three are No, on the grounds that a clone \"includes the data\". It does make the data *visible*, but it does not **copy** it — which is precisely the wording of statement 1. The two other comments (+24, +6) and the answer key all give Yes/No/No.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-yesno",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "statements": [
        {
          "text": "A replica of dbo.FactSales is created in the test schema by copying the metadata only.",
          "answer": "Yes"
        },
        {
          "text": "Additional schema changes to dbo.FactSales will also apply to test.FactSales.",
          "answer": "No"
        },
        {
          "text": "Additional data changes to dbo.FactSales will also apply to test.FactSales.",
          "answer": "No"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1. Warehouse1 contains a fact table named FactSales that has one billion\nrows.\nYou run the following T-SQL statement.\nCREATE TABLE test.FactSales AS CLONE OF dbo.FactSales;\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**Yes — No — No.** Two documented properties of a Fabric table clone settle all three.\n\n**1. Metadata only → Yes.** The `CREATE TABLE AS CLONE OF` reference is explicit: *\"Creates a new table as a **zero-copy clone** of another table in Warehouse in Microsoft Fabric. **Only the metadata of the table is copied. The underlying data of the table, stored as parquet files, is not copied.**\"* That is why a clone of a one-billion-row fact table is near-instantaneous.\n\n**2 & 3. Later changes propagate → No, and No.** A clone is a point-in-time snapshot, not a link: *\"Upon creation, a table clone is an **independent and separate** entity… Any changes made to the source table after the clone is created do not reflect in the clone, and vice versa.\"* This works because Parquet files are immutable — the clone simply keeps referencing the set of files that existed at clone time, while new writes to `dbo.FactSales` create new files that only its own log knows about. Schema changes (2) and data changes (3) are both invisible to the clone.\n\n*Note:* one commenter argues all three are No, on the grounds that a clone \"includes the data\". It does make the data *visible*, but it does not **copy** it — which is precisely the wording of statement 1. The two other comments (+24, +6) and the answer key all give Yes/No/No."
    },
