  {
    "id": "T1Q125",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a data warehouse.\nYou need to load rows into a large Type 2 slowly changing dimension (SCD). The solution must minimize resource usage.\nWhich T-SQL statement should you use?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "UPDATE AND INSERT"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "MERGE"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "TRUNCATE TABLE and INSERT"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "CREATE TABLE AS SELECT"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q125",
    "sourceId": "T1Q125",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "BLOCKED_SOURCE",
    "r10Status": "AMBIGUOUS",
    "reviewNote": "Removed unsupported universal MERGE resource-win and incomplete SCD2 algorithm.",
    "remaining": "Author complete SCD2 fixture, duplicate-source handling, transaction/idempotence tests and compare actual Warehouse resource behavior.",
    "explanation": "**MERGE.** A Type 2 SCD load is an upsert with two shapes of change in one pass: **update** the rows whose tracked attributes changed (close off the old version) and **insert** the new versions plus genuinely new members. `MERGE` expresses that as a **single statement over a single scan** of the source and target — which is what \"minimize resource usage\" rewards on a large dimension.\n\n```sql\nMERGE INTO DimCustomer AS tgt\nUSING StagedCustomer AS src ON tgt.CustomerCode = src.CustomerCode AND tgt.IsCurrent = 1\nWHEN MATCHED AND (tgt.CustomerName <> src.CustomerName OR …)\n  THEN UPDATE SET tgt.EffectiveEnd = SYSDATETIME(), tgt.IsCurrent = 0\nWHEN NOT MATCHED BY TARGET\n  THEN INSERT (…) VALUES (…);\n```\n\n- **A UPDATE and INSERT** – produces the same result, but as **two statements**, each scanning and joining the tables independently. More work, more logging, and a window in which the two are inconsistent.\n- **C TRUNCATE and INSERT** – destroys the existing rows, which **is the history**. It turns a Type 2 dimension into a full reload and breaks every fact row pointing at an older surrogate key.\n- **D CTAS** – creates a *new* table. No history, and no way to incrementally version an existing one.\n\n**⚠ Doc drift worth knowing.** Several highly-voted comments argue for **A**, because when they were written `MERGE` was **not supported** in Fabric Warehouse. That has changed: the T-SQL surface-area doc now states *\"`MERGE` syntax is supported and is a **generally available** feature.\"* So B is correct both as the exam intends it and on current Fabric — but if you sat the exam in 2024 the community's objection was fair.",
    "answer": "B",
    "officialAnswer": "B",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "B",
      "answer": "B",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a data warehouse.\nYou need to load rows into a large Type 2 slowly changing dimension (SCD). The solution must minimize resource usage.\nWhich T-SQL statement should you use?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "UPDATE AND INSERT",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "MERGE",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "TRUNCATE TABLE and INSERT",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "CREATE TABLE AS SELECT",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**MERGE.** A Type 2 SCD load is an upsert with two shapes of change in one pass: **update** the rows whose tracked attributes changed (close off the old version) and **insert** the new versions plus genuinely new members. `MERGE` expresses that as a **single statement over a single scan** of the source and target — which is what \"minimize resource usage\" rewards on a large dimension.\n\n```sql\nMERGE INTO DimCustomer AS tgt\nUSING StagedCustomer AS src ON tgt.CustomerCode = src.CustomerCode AND tgt.IsCurrent = 1\nWHEN MATCHED AND (tgt.CustomerName <> src.CustomerName OR …)\n  THEN UPDATE SET tgt.EffectiveEnd = SYSDATETIME(), tgt.IsCurrent = 0\nWHEN NOT MATCHED BY TARGET\n  THEN INSERT (…) VALUES (…);\n```\n\n- **A UPDATE and INSERT** – produces the same result, but as **two statements**, each scanning and joining the tables independently. More work, more logging, and a window in which the two are inconsistent.\n- **C TRUNCATE and INSERT** – destroys the existing rows, which **is the history**. It turns a Type 2 dimension into a full reload and breaks every fact row pointing at an older surrogate key.\n- **D CTAS** – creates a *new* table. No history, and no way to incrementally version an existing one.\n\n**⚠ Doc drift worth knowing.** Several highly-voted comments argue for **A**, because when they were written `MERGE` was **not supported** in Fabric Warehouse. That has changed: the T-SQL surface-area doc now states *\"`MERGE` syntax is supported and is a **generally available** feature.\"* So B is correct both as the exam intends it and on current Fabric — but if you sat the exam in 2024 the community's objection was fair."
    },
    "sourceVisualReferences": {
      "exhibits": [],
      "answerImages": [],
      "optionImages": []
    },
    "visualResolution": "no_exhibit_needed",
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/tsql-surface-area?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "MERGE (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/merge-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "reviewKind": "original source practice — scientific grading held"
  },
