      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a PySpark notebook named Notebook1.\nYou define sas_token as a variable in the first cell of Notebook1 and store a shared access signature (SAS) token in the variable.\nIn the second cell, you run the following code.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**No — No — Yes.** The code:\n\n```python\ncustomers_path = f'wasbs://contacts@contoso.blob.core.windows.net/customers?{sas_token}'\ncustomers = spark.read.parquet(customers_path)\ncustomers.write \\\n  .format(\"delta\") \\\n  .mode(\"overwrite\") \\\n  .saveAsTable(\"Customers\")\n```\n\n**1. `customers` is a pandas DataFrame → No.** `spark.read.parquet(...)` returns a **Spark** DataFrame (`pyspark.sql.DataFrame`) — distributed across the cluster. A pandas DataFrame would require `pandas.read_parquet()` or an explicit `.toPandas()`, neither of which appears. The giveaway in the code itself is `customers.write.format(...).saveAsTable(...)`, a Spark-only API.\n\n**2. An error if the Delta table does not exist → No.** `mode(\"overwrite\")` **creates the table if it is absent** and replaces it if present. The mode that errors on a missing/existing table is `error` / `errorifexists`, which is the *default* — but it is overridden here.\n\n**3. Source is the `customers` folder in a container named `contacts` → Yes.** Read the WASBS URL by its parts: `wasbs://<container>@<account>.blob.core.windows.net/<path>`. So the container is **`contacts`**, the storage account is `contoso`, and the path within the container is **`customers`**. The `?{sas_token}` suffix is the SAS credential, not part of the path."
    },
