  {
    "id": "T1Q132",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named DW1 and a lakehouse named LH1. DW1 contains a table named Sales.Product. LH1\ncontains a table named Sales.Orders.\nYou plan to schedule an automated process that will create a new point-in-time (PIT) table named Sales.ProductOrder in DW1. Sales.ProductOrder\nwill be built by using the results of a query that will join Sales.Product and Sales.Orders.\nYou need to ensure that the types of columns in Sales.ProductOrder match the column types in the source tables. The solution must minimize the\nnumber of operations required to create the new table.\nWhich operation should you use?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "INSERT INTO"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS)"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "CREATE TABLE AS CLONE OF"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW AS SELECT"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q132",
    "sourceId": "T1Q132",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**CTAS.** Three constraints, and only `CREATE TABLE AS SELECT` satisfies all of them:\n\n- **The source is a join across two items.** `Sales.Product` is in the warehouse, `Sales.Orders` is in the lakehouse, and the new table is built *\"by using the results of a query that will join\"* them. CTAS takes an arbitrary `SELECT`, including a cross-item join.\n- **Column types must match the source.** CTAS **derives the schema from the query's result set**, so the types come across automatically. There is no `CREATE TABLE` DDL to write and keep in sync.\n- **Minimize the number of operations.** One statement creates and populates the table.\n\nRejected:\n- **A INSERT INTO** – needs the table to exist first, so you would write the `CREATE TABLE` DDL yourself (hand-maintaining every data type) and then insert. Two operations, and the type-matching requirement becomes manual.\n- **C CREATE TABLE AS CLONE OF** – clones a **single existing table**. It cannot take a query, let alone a join, and clones across items are not supported.\n- **D CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW AS SELECT** – would not produce a *table*, and the requirement is a scheduled point-in-time snapshot. (Materialized views are also listed as unsupported in the Fabric Warehouse T-SQL surface area.)",
    "answer": "B",
    "officialAnswer": "B",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "B",
      "answer": "B",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named DW1 and a lakehouse named LH1. DW1 contains a table named Sales.Product. LH1\ncontains a table named Sales.Orders.\nYou plan to schedule an automated process that will create a new point-in-time (PIT) table named Sales.ProductOrder in DW1. Sales.ProductOrder\nwill be built by using the results of a query that will join Sales.Product and Sales.Orders.\nYou need to ensure that the types of columns in Sales.ProductOrder match the column types in the source tables. The solution must minimize the\nnumber of operations required to create the new table.\nWhich operation should you use?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "INSERT INTO",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS)",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "CREATE TABLE AS CLONE OF",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW AS SELECT",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**CTAS.** Three constraints, and only `CREATE TABLE AS SELECT` satisfies all of them:\n\n- **The source is a join across two items.** `Sales.Product` is in the warehouse, `Sales.Orders` is in the lakehouse, and the new table is built *\"by using the results of a query that will join\"* them. CTAS takes an arbitrary `SELECT`, including a cross-item join.\n- **Column types must match the source.** CTAS **derives the schema from the query's result set**, so the types come across automatically. There is no `CREATE TABLE` DDL to write and keep in sync.\n- **Minimize the number of operations.** One statement creates and populates the table.\n\nRejected:\n- **A INSERT INTO** – needs the table to exist first, so you would write the `CREATE TABLE` DDL yourself (hand-maintaining every data type) and then insert. Two operations, and the type-matching requirement becomes manual.\n- **C CREATE TABLE AS CLONE OF** – clones a **single existing table**. It cannot take a query, let alone a join, and clones across items are not supported.\n- **D CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW AS SELECT** – would not produce a *table*, and the requirement is a scheduled point-in-time snapshot. (Materialized views are also listed as unsupported in the Fabric Warehouse T-SQL surface area.)"
    },
    "sourceVisualReferences": {
