        },
        {
          "label": "Row and column security is ______.",
          "options": [
            "configured to use object-level security (OLS)",
            "configured to use row-level security (RLS)",
            "undefined"
          ],
          "answer": "undefined"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains the semantic model shown in the following exhibit.\n\nUse the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**DirectQuery — undefined.**\n\n**Blank 1 — DirectQuery.** Direct Lake reads **Delta tables** straight from OneLake. A **view** is not a Delta table — it is a SQL object that must be evaluated by the SQL engine — so a Direct Lake model can never serve it from memory. The documented behaviour is that such queries *fall back to DirectQuery mode*, and DirectQuery is in any case the **only** mode Direct Lake can fall back to. *Automatic* is a setting name, not a query mode, and \"falling back to Direct Lake\" is self-contradictory.\n\n**Blank 2 — undefined.** Read it off the exhibit: the Model tree shows **`Roles (0)`**. RLS and OLS are both defined *inside roles* — RLS as a DAX table filter on a role, OLS as a per-role table/column permission. With zero roles there is nothing configured either way, so row and column security is **undefined**.\n\n**⚠ Read this before trusting blank 1.** The exhibit also shows **Direct Lake Behavior = `Direct Lake Only`**, and that setting *disables fallback*: *\"DirectLakeOnly: All queries use Direct Lake storage mode only. Fall back to DirectQuery mode is disabled. If data can't be loaded into memory, **an error is returned**.\"* Taken literally, then, querying a view on this model would **error rather than fall back** — and no option says that. Two commenters (+12, +6) quote exactly this and lean toward *Direct Lake*; one (+4) answers *DirectQuery*, matching the answer key. The statement presupposes that a fallback happens and only asks which mode, and the answer to that is always DirectQuery — but be aware the exhibit and the statement are in tension."
    },
