      "explanation": "Least privilege usually pushes you down the role list — but here the **first** task sets a floor. The domains doc states it plainly: *\"Only **Fabric admins** can create domains.\"* Nothing below that can do it, so the answer cannot be lower than **Fabric admin**.\n\nChecked against all four tasks:\n\n| Task | Minimum role |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Create a new domain** | **Fabric admin** |\n| Create subdomains | Fabric admin *or* domain admin |\n| Assign workspaces to a (sub)domain | Fabric admin, domain admin, or domain contributor |\n\n- **A domain admin** – can create **subdomains** of a domain they already own and manage its workspaces, but cannot create the top-level domain in the first place. This is the near-miss, and 20% of the community picks it.\n- **B domain contributor** – the weakest domain role: it can only assign workspaces it already administers to a domain.\n- **D workspace Admin** – scoped to a single workspace; no domain rights at all.\n\n*(Note that a Fabric admin would normally then **delegate** by assigning a domain admin — but the question asks which single role lets User1 do all four things.)*"
