    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q136",
    "sourceId": "T1Q136",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**No — No — No.** The whole question turns on one fact: **domains do not grant workspace roles.**\n\nWhat the setup actually does. Assigning **Domain1 as the *default domain* of Group1** means that when a member of Group1 creates a workspace, that workspace is **automatically associated with Domain1**. The doc describes it as a convenience for organising content: a default domain *\"will automatically be assigned to workspaces created by members of that group\"*. It is a **content-organisation** feature — it affects OneLake data hub filtering and domain-level governance, not who can open or edit anything.\n\nWorkspace access comes from exactly one place: an explicit role assignment (Admin / Member / Contributor / Viewer) on that workspace. So:\n\n1. **User2 → Contributor on Workspace3? No.** User2 is not even in Group1, and nobody assigned them anything on Workspace3. (User2 is the admin of Workspace2 and of Domain2 — neither reaches Workspace3.)\n2. **User3 → Viewer on Workspace3? No.** User3 *is* in Group1, so Workspace3 lands in Domain1 — but sharing a domain with a workspace confers no role in it. Only User1, who created Workspace3, is its admin.\n3. **User3 → Contributor on Workspace1? No.** Workspace1's admin is User1. No role was granted to User3 or to Group1.\n\n*Note:* one comment (+6) answers Yes to statement 2, reasoning that User3 \"likely inherits\" Viewer through the domain. There is no such inheritance; two other comments and the answer key all give No/No/No.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-yesno",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "statements": [
        {
          "text": "User2 is assigned the Contributor role for Workspace3.",
          "answer": "No"
        },
        {
          "text": "User3 is assigned the Viewer role for Workspace3.",
          "answer": "No"
        },
        {
          "text": "User3 is assigned the Contributor role for Workspace1.",
          "answer": "No"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains three users named User1, User2, and User3. The tenant contains a security group named Group1. User1\nand User3 are members of Group1.\nThe tenant contains the workspaces shown in the following table.\nThe tenant contains the domains shown in the following table.\nUser1 creates a new workspace named Workspace3.\nYou assign Domain1 as the default domain of Group1.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**No — No — No.** The whole question turns on one fact: **domains do not grant workspace roles.**\n\nWhat the setup actually does. Assigning **Domain1 as the *default domain* of Group1** means that when a member of Group1 creates a workspace, that workspace is **automatically associated with Domain1**. The doc describes it as a convenience for organising content: a default domain *\"will automatically be assigned to workspaces created by members of that group\"*. It is a **content-organisation** feature — it affects OneLake data hub filtering and domain-level governance, not who can open or edit anything.\n\nWorkspace access comes from exactly one place: an explicit role assignment (Admin / Member / Contributor / Viewer) on that workspace. So:\n\n1. **User2 → Contributor on Workspace3? No.** User2 is not even in Group1, and nobody assigned them anything on Workspace3. (User2 is the admin of Workspace2 and of Domain2 — neither reaches Workspace3.)\n2. **User3 → Viewer on Workspace3? No.** User3 *is* in Group1, so Workspace3 lands in Domain1 — but sharing a domain with a workspace confers no role in it. Only User1, who created Workspace3, is its admin.\n3. **User3 → Contributor on Workspace1? No.** Workspace1's admin is User1. No role was granted to User3 or to Group1.\n\n*Note:* one comment (+6) answers Yes to statement 2, reasoning that User3 \"likely inherits\" Viewer through the domain. There is no such inheritance; two other comments and the answer key all give No/No/No."
    },
