            "Contains customer names"
          ],
          [
            "VersionDate",
            "Date/Time",
            "Contains record timestamps"
          ]
        ]
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q139",
    "sourceId": "T1Q139",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "r10Status": "AMBIGUOUS",
    "reviewNote": "Specified unique latest timestamp and nonduplicating expansion fields.",
    "remaining": "Verify Power Query grouping/expansion semantics and actual M fixture with ties/NULLs; source screenshot not rechecked.",
    "explanation": "The classic Power Query \"keep the latest row per key\" pattern, in four steps:\n\n1. **Group by CustomerID, use the All Rows operation, and calculate the max version date per customer ID.** Two aggregations in one *Group By*: a `Max` over the version date, **and** an **All Rows** aggregation that stashes the original rows of each group in a nested table column. The All Rows part is what makes this work — a plain Group By would collapse every other column (customer name and the rest) out of existence.\n2. **Expand the All Rows detail column.** Unpacks the nested table, restoring the original columns — now with the group's max version date sitting alongside every row.\n3. **Filter the query where the version date value equals the max version date value.** Each row's own version date is compared to its customer's maximum; only the latest survives.\n4. **Remove the max version date column.** Satisfies *\"no new columns are loaded to the semantic model\"* — the helper column is dropped once it has done its job.\n\nRejected actions:\n- **Group by CustomerID and calculate the max version date per customer ID** (without All Rows) — returns two columns only. Everything else is lost, and there is nothing left to expand.\n- **Remove duplicates based on CustomerID** — keeps an arbitrary row per customer (whichever comes first in the current sort order), not the latest one. It is the tempting shortcut and it is wrong.\n\n*Note:* the PDF's answer image marks the four slots with connector lines that are unreadable in the scan. The sequence above is the unanimous reading of the community (+10, +6, +2, no dissent) and is the only ordering that satisfies both requirements.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "dragdrop",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "drag": {
        "items": [
          "Filter the query where the version date value equals the max version date value.",
          "Remove the max version date column.",
          "Expand the All Rows detail column.",
          "Group by CustomerID and calculate the max version date per customer ID.",
          "Remove duplicates based on CustomerID.",
          "Group by CustomerID, use the All Rows operation, and calculate the max version date per customer ID."
        ],
        "reuse": false,
        "targets": [
          {
            "label": "1",
            "answer": "Group by CustomerID, use the All Rows operation, and calculate the max version date per customer ID."
          },
          {
            "label": "2",
            "answer": "Expand the All Rows detail column."
          },
          {
            "label": "3",
            "answer": "Filter the query where the version date value equals the max version date value."
          },
          {
            "label": "4",
            "answer": "Remove the max version date column."
          }
        ]
      },
      "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace that contains a Dataflow Gen2 query. The query returns the following data.\nYou need to filter the results to ensure that only the latest version of each customer’s record is retained. The solution must ensure that no new\ncolumns are loaded to the semantic model.\nWhich four actions should you perform in sequence in Power Query Editor? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the\nanswer area and arrange them in the correct order.",
      "explanation": "The classic Power Query \"keep the latest row per key\" pattern, in four steps:\n\n1. **Group by CustomerID, use the All Rows operation, and calculate the max version date per customer ID.** Two aggregations in one *Group By*: a `Max` over the version date, **and** an **All Rows** aggregation that stashes the original rows of each group in a nested table column. The All Rows part is what makes this work — a plain Group By would collapse every other column (customer name and the rest) out of existence.\n2. **Expand the All Rows detail column.** Unpacks the nested table, restoring the original columns — now with the group's max version date sitting alongside every row.\n3. **Filter the query where the version date value equals the max version date value.** Each row's own version date is compared to its customer's maximum; only the latest survives.\n4. **Remove the max version date column.** Satisfies *\"no new columns are loaded to the semantic model\"* — the helper column is dropped once it has done its job.\n\nRejected actions:\n- **Group by CustomerID and calculate the max version date per customer ID** (without All Rows) — returns two columns only. Everything else is lost, and there is nothing left to expand.\n- **Remove duplicates based on CustomerID** — keeps an arbitrary row per customer (whichever comes first in the current sort order), not the latest one. It is the tempting shortcut and it is wrong.\n\n*Note:* the PDF's answer image marks the four slots with connector lines that are unreadable in the scan. The sequence above is the unanimous reading of the community (+10, +6, +2, no dissent) and is the only ordering that satisfies both requirements."
    },
