    "id": "T1Q140",
    "type": "hotspot-yesno",
    "stem": "You have a Microsoft Power Bl project that contains a file named definition.pbir. definition.pbir contains the following JSON.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "statements": [
      {
        "text": "definition.pbir is in the PBIR-Legacy format.",
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "text": "The semantic model referenced by definition.pbir is located in the Power BI service.",
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "text": "When the related report is opened, Power BI Desktop will open the semantic model in full edit mode.",
        "answer": "Yes"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "definition.pbir — original JSON",
        "language": "json",
        "content": "{\n  \"version\": \"1.0\",\n  \"datasetReference\": {\n    \"byPath\": {\n      \"path\": \"../Sales.Dataset\"\n    },\n    \"byConnection\": null\n  }\n}"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q140",
    "sourceId": "T1Q140",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**Yes — No — Yes.** The file is\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": \"1.0\",\n  \"datasetReference\": {\n    \"byPath\": { \"path\": \"../Sales.Dataset\" },\n    \"byConnection\": null\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**1. PBIR-Legacy → Yes.** *(The PDF answer-key image marks **No**; it is wrong.)* The `version` property is precisely what declares the report definition format, and the doc gives the mapping as a table:\n\n| Version | Supported formats |\n| --- | --- |\n| **1.0** | **Report definition must be stored as PBIR-Legacy in the `report.json` file.** |\n| 4.0 or higher | Report definition can be stored as PBIR-Legacy (`report.json`) **or** PBIR (`\\definition` folder). |\n\nAt `\"version\": \"1.0\"` the report definition *must* be PBIR-Legacy — there is no other option at that version. (Microsoft's own example of the current format uses `\"version\": \"4.0\"`.) The highest-voted comment asserts that *\"1.0 isn't legacy format … the version would be blank if it was legacy\"*, which the documented table contradicts; a later comment answers Yes.\n\n**2. The model is in the Power BI service → No.** The reference is **`byPath`**, a *relative path to a local folder* (`../Sales.Dataset`), and `byConnection` is explicitly `null`. `byConnection` is the one that *\"specifies the connection to a semantic model in a Fabric workspace by using a connection string\"* — it is not in use here.\n\n**3. Desktop opens the model in full edit mode → Yes.** Straight from the doc, on `byPath`: *\"When used, Power BI Desktop **also opens the semantic model in full edit mode**.\"* The contrast is stated in the next line: *\"When a `byConnection` reference is used, Power BI Desktop doesn't open the semantic model in edit mode.\"*",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-yesno",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "statements": [
        {
          "text": "definition.pbir is in the PBIR-Legacy format.",
          "answer": "Yes"
        },
        {
          "text": "The semantic model referenced by definition.pbir is located in the Power BI service.",
          "answer": "No"
        },
        {
          "text": "When the related report is opened, Power BI Desktop will open the semantic model in full edit mode.",
          "answer": "Yes"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Microsoft Power Bl project that contains a file named definition.pbir. definition.pbir contains the following JSON.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**Yes — No — Yes.** The file is\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": \"1.0\",\n  \"datasetReference\": {\n    \"byPath\": { \"path\": \"../Sales.Dataset\" },\n    \"byConnection\": null\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**1. PBIR-Legacy → Yes.** *(The PDF answer-key image marks **No**; it is wrong.)* The `version` property is precisely what declares the report definition format, and the doc gives the mapping as a table:\n\n| Version | Supported formats |\n| --- | --- |\n| **1.0** | **Report definition must be stored as PBIR-Legacy in the `report.json` file.** |\n| 4.0 or higher | Report definition can be stored as PBIR-Legacy (`report.json`) **or** PBIR (`\\definition` folder). |\n\nAt `\"version\": \"1.0\"` the report definition *must* be PBIR-Legacy — there is no other option at that version. (Microsoft's own example of the current format uses `\"version\": \"4.0\"`.) The highest-voted comment asserts that *\"1.0 isn't legacy format … the version would be blank if it was legacy\"*, which the documented table contradicts; a later comment answers Yes.\n\n**2. The model is in the Power BI service → No.** The reference is **`byPath`**, a *relative path to a local folder* (`../Sales.Dataset`), and `byConnection` is explicitly `null`. `byConnection` is the one that *\"specifies the connection to a semantic model in a Fabric workspace by using a connection string\"* — it is not in use here.\n\n**3. Desktop opens the model in full edit mode → Yes.** Straight from the doc, on `byPath`: *\"When used, Power BI Desktop **also opens the semantic model in full edit mode**.\"* The contrast is stated in the next line: *\"When a `byConnection` reference is used, Power BI Desktop doesn't open the semantic model in edit mode.\"*"
    },
