  {
    "id": "T1Q149",
    "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric lakehouse named Lakehouse1 that contains the following data.\nYou need build a T-SQL statement that will return the total sales amount by OrderDate only for the days that are holidays in Australia. The total\nsales amount must sum the quantity multiplied by the price on each row in the dbo.sales table.\nHow should you complete the statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "blanks": [
      {
        "label": "SELECT s.OrderDate,  ______  AS TotalSalesAmt",
        "options": [
          "s.Quantity * S.UnitPrice",
          "Sum(s.Quantity * s.UnitPrice)",
          "Sum(s.quantity) * s.UnitPrice",
          "Sum(s.Quantity) * Sum(s.UnitPrice)"
        ],
        "answer": "Sum(s.Quantity * s.UnitPrice)"
      },
      {
        "label": "______  JOIN [Lakehouse1].[dbo].[publicholidays] AS ph ON s.OrderDate = ph.date",
        "options": [
          "Cross",
          "Full outer",
          "Inner",
          "Right outer"
        ],
        "answer": "Inner"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "table",
        "title": "Lakehouse source schemas",
        "headers": [
          "Table",
          "Column",
          "Data type"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "dbo.publicholidays",
            "countryOrRegion",
            "Varchar(8000)"
          ],
          [
            "dbo.publicholidays",
            "holidayName",
            "Varchar(8000)"
          ],
          [
            "dbo.publicholidays",
            "Date",
            "Date"
          ],
          [
            "dbo.sales",
            "OrderDate",
            "Date"
          ],
          [
            "dbo.sales",
            "Quantity",
            "Float"
          ],
          [
            "dbo.sales",
            "UnitPrice",
            "Float"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "Source SQL completion template",
        "language": "sql",
        "content": "SELECT s.OrderDate,\n       [Dropdown 1] AS TotalSalesAmt\nFROM [Lakehouse1].[dbo].[sales] AS s\n[Dropdown 2] JOIN [Lakehouse1].[dbo].[publicholidays] AS ph\n    ON s.OrderDate = ph.date\nWHERE ph.countryOrRegion = 'Australia'\nGROUP BY s.OrderDate;"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q149",
    "sourceId": "T1Q149",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "r10Status": "AMBIGUOUS",
    "reviewNote": "Prevented multiplicative holiday matches with explicit uniqueness constraint.",
    "remaining": "Complete current primary-doc section review, source screenshot/case checks and native fixture where material; then independently challenge every final option.",
    "explanation": "**`Sum(s.Quantity * s.UnitPrice)` — `Inner`.**\n\n```sql\nSELECT s.OrderDate, Sum(s.Quantity * s.UnitPrice) AS TotalSalesAmt\nFROM [Lakehouse1].[dbo].[sales] AS s\nINNER JOIN [Lakehouse1].[dbo].[publicholidays] AS ph ON s.OrderDate = ph.date\nWHERE ph.countryOrRegion = 'Australia'\nGROUP BY s.OrderDate\n```\n\n**Blank 1 — `Sum(s.Quantity * s.UnitPrice)`.** The requirement is to *\"sum the quantity multiplied by the price **on each row**\"* — so multiply first, per row, then aggregate.\n- `s.Quantity * S.UnitPrice` has no aggregate at all, which breaks the `GROUP BY s.OrderDate` (a non-aggregated, non-grouped column).\n- `Sum(s.Quantity) * Sum(s.UnitPrice)` sums each column separately and multiplies the totals — mathematically a different (and wrong) number.\n- `Sum(s.quantity) * s.UnitPrice` is wrong for the same reason, **and** it would fail in any case: `s.UnitPrice` is neither aggregated nor in the `GROUP BY`.\n\n**Blank 2 — `Inner`.** The requirement is *\"only for the days that are holidays in Australia\"*. An **inner join** keeps only the sales rows whose `OrderDate` matches a row in `publicholidays` — non-holiday days are dropped, which is exactly what is wanted.\n- **Full outer** would keep all sales *and* all holidays, including non-holiday sales days (with NULLs) — and the `WHERE ph.countryOrRegion = 'Australia'` would then quietly convert it back into an inner join anyway.\n- **Right outer** would keep every holiday even with no sales.\n- **Cross** produces the Cartesian product; the `ON` clause is not even valid with `CROSS JOIN`.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "SELECT s.OrderDate,  ______  AS TotalSalesAmt",
          "options": [
            "s.Quantity * S.UnitPrice",
            "Sum(s.Quantity * s.UnitPrice)",
            "Sum(s.quantity) * s.UnitPrice",
            "Sum(s.Quantity) * Sum(s.UnitPrice)"
          ],
          "answer": "Sum(s.Quantity * s.UnitPrice)"
        },
        {
          "label": "______  JOIN [Lakehouse1].[dbo].[publicholidays] AS ph ON s.OrderDate = ph.date",
          "options": [
            "Cross",
            "Full outer",
            "Inner",
            "Right outer"
          ],
          "answer": "Inner"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric lakehouse named Lakehouse1 that contains the following data.\nYou need build a T-SQL statement that will return the total sales amount by OrderDate only for the days that are holidays in Australia. The total\nsales amount must sum the quantity multiplied by the price on each row in the dbo.sales table.\nHow should you complete the statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**`Sum(s.Quantity * s.UnitPrice)` — `Inner`.**\n\n```sql\nSELECT s.OrderDate, Sum(s.Quantity * s.UnitPrice) AS TotalSalesAmt\nFROM [Lakehouse1].[dbo].[sales] AS s\nINNER JOIN [Lakehouse1].[dbo].[publicholidays] AS ph ON s.OrderDate = ph.date\nWHERE ph.countryOrRegion = 'Australia'\nGROUP BY s.OrderDate\n```\n\n**Blank 1 — `Sum(s.Quantity * s.UnitPrice)`.** The requirement is to *\"sum the quantity multiplied by the price **on each row**\"* — so multiply first, per row, then aggregate.\n- `s.Quantity * S.UnitPrice` has no aggregate at all, which breaks the `GROUP BY s.OrderDate` (a non-aggregated, non-grouped column).\n- `Sum(s.Quantity) * Sum(s.UnitPrice)` sums each column separately and multiplies the totals — mathematically a different (and wrong) number.\n- `Sum(s.quantity) * s.UnitPrice` is wrong for the same reason, **and** it would fail in any case: `s.UnitPrice` is neither aggregated nor in the `GROUP BY`.\n\n**Blank 2 — `Inner`.** The requirement is *\"only for the days that are holidays in Australia\"*. An **inner join** keeps only the sales rows whose `OrderDate` matches a row in `publicholidays` — non-holiday days are dropped, which is exactly what is wanted.\n- **Full outer** would keep all sales *and* all holidays, including non-holiday sales days (with NULLs) — and the `WHERE ph.countryOrRegion = 'Australia'` would then quietly convert it back into an inner join anyway.\n- **Right outer** would keep every holiday even with no sales.\n- **Cross** produces the Cartesian product; the `ON` clause is not even valid with `CROSS JOIN`."
    },
