    "id": "T1Q150",
    "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 contains a warehouse named DW1. DW1 contains two\ntables named Employees and Sales. All users have read access to Dw1.\nYou need to implement access controls to meet the following requirements:\n• For the Sales table, ensure that the users can see only the sales data from their respective region.\n• For the Employees table, restrict access to all Personally Identifiable Information (PII).\n• Maintain access to unrestricted data for all the users.\nWhat should you use for each table? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "blanks": [
      {
        "label": "Employees:  (restrict access to all PII, keep the rest visible)",
        "options": [
          "Column-level security",
          "Item permissions",
          "Row-level security (RLS)",
          "Workspace permissions"
        ],
        "answer": "Column-level security"
      },
      {
        "label": "Sales:  (users see only their own region’s sales)",
        "options": [
          "Column-level security",
          "Item permissions",
          "Row-level security (RLS)",
          "Workspace permissions"
        ],
        "answer": "Row-level security (RLS)"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q150",
    "sourceId": "T1Q150",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**Column-level security — Row-level security.** Two requirements that differ along different axes of the table:\n\n| Requirement | Restricts | Feature |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Employees: *\"restrict access to all **Personally Identifiable Information**\"* | specific **columns** | **Column-level security (CLS)** |\n| Sales: *\"see only the sales data from their respective **region**\"* | specific **rows** | **Row-level security (RLS)** |\n\n- **CLS** — *\"allows you to restrict column access to protect sensitive data\"*. PII lives in named columns (SSN, date of birth, home address), so you deny those columns and the rest of the Employees table stays readable. This also satisfies *\"maintain access to unrestricted data for all the users\"*.\n- **RLS** — *\"control access to rows in a database table … you can ensure that workers access only those data rows that are pertinent to their department\"*. Region is a row attribute, so a filter predicate on it is the right tool.\n\nRejected:\n- **Item permissions** and **Workspace permissions** are all-or-nothing on the whole item or workspace. Revoking either would remove access to the unrestricted data too, breaking the third requirement — and the stem already says *\"all users have read access to DW1\"*.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "Employees:  (restrict access to all PII, keep the rest visible)",
          "options": [
            "Column-level security",
            "Item permissions",
            "Row-level security (RLS)",
            "Workspace permissions"
          ],
          "answer": "Column-level security"
        },
        {
          "label": "Sales:  (users see only their own region’s sales)",
          "options": [
            "Column-level security",
            "Item permissions",
            "Row-level security (RLS)",
            "Workspace permissions"
          ],
          "answer": "Row-level security (RLS)"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 contains a warehouse named DW1. DW1 contains two\ntables named Employees and Sales. All users have read access to Dw1.\nYou need to implement access controls to meet the following requirements:\n• For the Sales table, ensure that the users can see only the sales data from their respective region.\n• For the Employees table, restrict access to all Personally Identifiable Information (PII).\n• Maintain access to unrestricted data for all the users.\nWhat should you use for each table? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**Column-level security — Row-level security.** Two requirements that differ along different axes of the table:\n\n| Requirement | Restricts | Feature |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Employees: *\"restrict access to all **Personally Identifiable Information**\"* | specific **columns** | **Column-level security (CLS)** |\n| Sales: *\"see only the sales data from their respective **region**\"* | specific **rows** | **Row-level security (RLS)** |\n\n- **CLS** — *\"allows you to restrict column access to protect sensitive data\"*. PII lives in named columns (SSN, date of birth, home address), so you deny those columns and the rest of the Employees table stays readable. This also satisfies *\"maintain access to unrestricted data for all the users\"*.\n- **RLS** — *\"control access to rows in a database table … you can ensure that workers access only those data rows that are pertinent to their department\"*. Region is a row attribute, so a filter predicate on it is the right tool.\n\nRejected:\n- **Item permissions** and **Workspace permissions** are all-or-nothing on the whole item or workspace. Revoking either would remove access to the unrestricted data too, breaking the third requirement — and the stem already says *\"all users have read access to DW1\"*."
    },
