    "r10Status": "AMBIGUOUS",
    "reviewNote": "Held label-versus-DLP conflation until scope/rules are explicit.",
    "remaining": "Complete current primary-doc section review, source screenshot/case checks and native fixture where material; then independently challenge every final option.",
    "explanation": "**Apply sensitivity labels.** DLP policies for Fabric do not act on items directly — they act on **what an item is labelled or what sensitive information it contains**. From the Purview doc: *\"You define a DLP policy in the data loss prevention section of the Microsoft Purview portal. In the policy, you specify the conditions, such as **which sensitivity labels** and/or sensitive info types you want to detect.\"*\n\nSo to bring the semantic model and its two reports under DLP1, you label them; the policy then matches on the label and applies its actions (policy tips, alerts, blocking).\n\n- **A a workspace identity** – a managed identity that lets the *workspace* authenticate to Azure resources. Nothing to do with DLP.\n- **B / D endorsements** (Certified, Master data) – **discovery and trust** signals that help users find quality content. They carry no security or compliance enforcement and are not DLP conditions.",
    "answer": "C",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 contains a single semantic model that has two Microsoft\nPower BI reports.\nYou have a Microsoft 365 subscription that contains a data loss prevention (DLP) policy named DLP1.\nYou need to apply DLP1 to the items in Workspace1.\nWhat should you do?",
      "options": [
