    "id": "T1Q153",
    "type": "multi",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1 and a user named User1. User1 is assigned the Contributor role for\nWorkspace1.\nYou plan to configure Workspace1 to use an Azure DevOps repository for version control.\nYou need to ensure that User1 can commit items to the repository.\nWhich two settings should you enable for User1? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Users can sync workspace items with GitHub repositories"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Users can create and use Data workflows"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Users can create Fabric items"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Users can synchronize workspace items with their Git repositories"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q153",
    "sourceId": "T1Q153",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "Two tenant settings gate Git commits from a workspace:\n\n- **D — *Users can synchronize workspace items with their Git repositories*.** The master switch for Fabric Git integration. Without it there is no Connect/Commit/Update at all.\n- **C — *Users can create Fabric items*.** Committing writes item definitions, and syncing back creates and updates items in the workspace. Without the right to create Fabric items the user cannot complete the operation.\n\nRejected:\n- **A — *Users can sync workspace items with GitHub repositories*.** The deliberate trap. The scenario says **Azure DevOps**, which is covered by the generic *Git repositories* setting (D). GitHub has its own **separate** tenant switch, and turning it on does nothing for an Azure Repos connection. \"Azure DevOps uses Git\" ≠ \"Azure DevOps is GitHub\".\n- **B — *Users can create and use Data workflows*.** Apache Airflow jobs. Unrelated.\n\nNote that User1 already has the **Contributor** workspace role, which is the minimum role that can commit — so the remaining gaps are the two tenant settings.",
    "answer": "CD",
    "officialAnswer": "CD",
    "original": {
      "type": "multi",
      "officialAnswer": "CD",
      "answer": "CD",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1 and a user named User1. User1 is assigned the Contributor role for\nWorkspace1.\nYou plan to configure Workspace1 to use an Azure DevOps repository for version control.\nYou need to ensure that User1 can commit items to the repository.\nWhich two settings should you enable for User1? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Users can sync workspace items with GitHub repositories",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Users can create and use Data workflows",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Users can create Fabric items",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Users can synchronize workspace items with their Git repositories",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Two tenant settings gate Git commits from a workspace:\n\n- **D — *Users can synchronize workspace items with their Git repositories*.** The master switch for Fabric Git integration. Without it there is no Connect/Commit/Update at all.\n- **C — *Users can create Fabric items*.** Committing writes item definitions, and syncing back creates and updates items in the workspace. Without the right to create Fabric items the user cannot complete the operation.\n\nRejected:\n- **A — *Users can sync workspace items with GitHub repositories*.** The deliberate trap. The scenario says **Azure DevOps**, which is covered by the generic *Git repositories* setting (D). GitHub has its own **separate** tenant switch, and turning it on does nothing for an Azure Repos connection. \"Azure DevOps uses Git\" ≠ \"Azure DevOps is GitHub\".\n- **B — *Users can create and use Data workflows*.** Apache Airflow jobs. Unrelated.\n\nNote that User1 already has the **Contributor** workspace role, which is the minimum role that can commit — so the remaining gaps are the two tenant settings."
    },
