    "reviewNote": "Repaired projection-versus-input ambiguity.",
    "remaining": "Read YEAR/GROUP BY docs and run actual T-SQL fixture, including NULL status/date.",
    "explanation": "**Yes — Yes — No.** The query:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  ProductCategory,\n  Region,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN RefundStatus <> 'Refunded' THEN SalesAmount ELSE 0 END) AS TotalRevenue\nFROM Sales\nWHERE YEAR(TransactionDate) = YEAR(GETDATE())\nGROUP BY ProductCategory, Region;\n```\n\nThe key insight is **where** the refund logic lives: it is inside the `CASE`, **not** in the `WHERE` clause.\n\n**1. Returns Region values for refunded items → Yes.** Nothing filters refunded rows out of the result set. A refunded sale still belongs to its `ProductCategory`/`Region` group, so that group is still returned — the refund merely contributes **0** to its `TotalRevenue`. (Had the author written `WHERE RefundStatus <> 'Refunded'`, a region whose every sale was refunded would vanish. They did not.)\n\n**2. Only current-year transactions → Yes.** `WHERE YEAR(TransactionDate) = YEAR(GETDATE())` restricts the input rows to the current calendar year, so nothing outside it is considered.\n\n**3. TotalRevenue aggregates refunded SalesAmount values → No.** This is what the `CASE` prevents: for a refunded row the expression evaluates to the literal `0`, so the row's actual `SalesAmount` never enters the sum. Refunded rows are *counted in the grouping* but their amounts are *excluded from the total* — which is exactly the distinction statements 1 and 3 are testing from opposite sides.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-yesno",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "statements": [
        {
          "text": "The statement returns Region values when a Sales item has a RefundStatus of Refunded.",
          "answer": "Yes"
        },
        {
          "text": "The statement only returns TransactionDate values that occurred during the current year.",
          "answer": "Yes"
        },
        {
          "text": "The TotalRevenue calculation aggregates SalesAmount values that have a RefundStatus of Refunded.",
          "answer": "No"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have the following T-SQL statement.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**Yes — Yes — No.** The query:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  ProductCategory,\n  Region,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN RefundStatus <> 'Refunded' THEN SalesAmount ELSE 0 END) AS TotalRevenue\nFROM Sales\nWHERE YEAR(TransactionDate) = YEAR(GETDATE())\nGROUP BY ProductCategory, Region;\n```\n\nThe key insight is **where** the refund logic lives: it is inside the `CASE`, **not** in the `WHERE` clause.\n\n**1. Returns Region values for refunded items → Yes.** Nothing filters refunded rows out of the result set. A refunded sale still belongs to its `ProductCategory`/`Region` group, so that group is still returned — the refund merely contributes **0** to its `TotalRevenue`. (Had the author written `WHERE RefundStatus <> 'Refunded'`, a region whose every sale was refunded would vanish. They did not.)\n\n**2. Only current-year transactions → Yes.** `WHERE YEAR(TransactionDate) = YEAR(GETDATE())` restricts the input rows to the current calendar year, so nothing outside it is considered.\n\n**3. TotalRevenue aggregates refunded SalesAmount values → No.** This is what the `CASE` prevents: for a refunded row the expression evaluates to the literal `0`, so the row's actual `SalesAmount` never enters the sum. Refunded rows are *counted in the grouping* but their amounts are *excluded from the total* — which is exactly the distinction statements 1 and 3 are testing from opposite sides."
    },
