      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "Denormalized customer dimension view — original template",
        "language": "sql",
        "content": "CREATE VIEW DimCustomer AS\nSELECT C.CustomerID, C.CustomerName, C.CompanyName,\n       SR.SalesRegion, A.City, A.StateProvince,\n       A.CountryRegion, A.PostalCode\nFROM SalesLT.Customer C\n[Dropdown 1] SalesLT.SalesRegion SR\n    ON C.SalesRegionID = SR.SalesRegionID\nINNER JOIN SalesLT.CustomerAddress CA\n    ON CA.CustomerID = C.CustomerID\nINNER JOIN SalesLT.Address A\n    ON [Dropdown 2]\nWHERE CA.AddressType = 'Main Office';"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q162",
    "sourceId": "T1Q162",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "r10Status": "AMBIGUOUS",
    "reviewNote": "Added actual referential/unique Main Office constraints and removed unsupported speed guarantee.",
    "remaining": "Complete current primary-doc section review, source screenshot/case checks and native fixture where material; then independently challenge every final option.",
    "explanation": "**inner join — `A.AddressID = CA.AddressID`.**\n\n**Blank 2 — `A.AddressID = CA.AddressID`.** `CustomerAddress` is the bridge table between `Customer` and `Address`; it has already been joined to `Customer` on the line above (`CA.customerid = C.customerid`), so the remaining join is `Address` to that bridge, on **AddressID**.\n- `C.AddressID = CA.AddressID` — `Customer` has no `AddressID`; that is the whole reason the bridge table exists.\n- `C.CustomerID = A.CustomerID` — `Address` has no `CustomerID`, again because the relationship is many-to-many through the bridge.\n\n**Blank 1 — `inner join`, and here the PDF answer key is wrong** (it marks *left outer join*).\n\nThe stem hands you the two facts that decide it:\n- *\"Each customer is associated to a **single** SalesRegion value.\"*\n- *\"**All foreign key values are non-null**.\"*\n\nTogether these guarantee that **every** `Customer` row has exactly one matching `SalesRegion` row. When a match is certain, an inner join and a left outer join return **identical rows** — but they are not equally cheap. A left outer join obliges the optimizer to preserve every left row, which constrains join order and join algorithms and blocks some simplifications; an inner join leaves the optimizer free. With a **5-million-row** Customer table and an explicit requirement to *\"minimize query processing time and resources\"*, the inner join is the answer.\n\nIt is also the consistent choice: the other two joins in the same view are already `inner join`, for the same reason.\n\n*Community:* the three comments that address this (+16, +6, +4) all say inner join, each citing the non-null foreign keys. None defends the key.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "______  SalesLT.SalesRegion SR on C.SalesRegionID = SR.SalesRegionID",
          "options": [
            "full outer join",
            "inner join",
            "left outer join"
          ],
          "answer": "inner join"
        },
        {
          "label": "inner join [SalesLT].[Address] A on  ______",
          "options": [
            "A.AddressID = CA.AddressID",
            "C.AddressID = CA.AddressID",
            "C.CustomerID = A.CustomerID"
          ],
          "answer": "A.AddressID = CA.AddressID"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric warehouse that contains the following data.\nThe data has the following characteristics:\n• Each customer is assigned a unique CustomerID value.\n• Each customer is associated to a single SalesRegion value.\n• Each customer is associated to a single CustomerAddress value.\n• The Customer table contains 5 million rows.\n• All foreign key values are non-null.\nYou need to create a view to denormalize the data into a customer dimension that contains one row per distinct CustomerID value. The solution\nmust minimize query processing time and resources.\nHow should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\n\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**inner join — `A.AddressID = CA.AddressID`.**\n\n**Blank 2 — `A.AddressID = CA.AddressID`.** `CustomerAddress` is the bridge table between `Customer` and `Address`; it has already been joined to `Customer` on the line above (`CA.customerid = C.customerid`), so the remaining join is `Address` to that bridge, on **AddressID**.\n- `C.AddressID = CA.AddressID` — `Customer` has no `AddressID`; that is the whole reason the bridge table exists.\n- `C.CustomerID = A.CustomerID` — `Address` has no `CustomerID`, again because the relationship is many-to-many through the bridge.\n\n**Blank 1 — `inner join`, and here the PDF answer key is wrong** (it marks *left outer join*).\n\nThe stem hands you the two facts that decide it:\n- *\"Each customer is associated to a **single** SalesRegion value.\"*\n- *\"**All foreign key values are non-null**.\"*\n\nTogether these guarantee that **every** `Customer` row has exactly one matching `SalesRegion` row. When a match is certain, an inner join and a left outer join return **identical rows** — but they are not equally cheap. A left outer join obliges the optimizer to preserve every left row, which constrains join order and join algorithms and blocks some simplifications; an inner join leaves the optimizer free. With a **5-million-row** Customer table and an explicit requirement to *\"minimize query processing time and resources\"*, the inner join is the answer.\n\nIt is also the consistent choice: the other two joins in the same view are already `inner join`, for the same reason.\n\n*Community:* the three comments that address this (+16, +6, +4) all say inner join, each citing the non-null foreign keys. None defends the key."
    },
