    "stem": "You plan to use Fabric to store data.\nYou need to create a data store that supports the following:\n• Writing data by using T-SQL\n• Multi-table transactions\n• Dynamic data masking\nWhich type of data store should you create?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "KQL database"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "lakehouse"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "warehouse"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "semantic model"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q166",
    "sourceId": "T1Q166",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "Only the **warehouse** has all three capabilities:\n\n| Requirement | Warehouse | Lakehouse | KQL database |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Write data by using T-SQL** | ✔ | ✘ — its SQL analytics endpoint is **read-only**; you write with Spark | ✘ — KQL, not T-SQL |\n| **Multi-table transactions** | ✔ — full ACID `BEGIN/COMMIT/ROLLBACK` across tables | ✘ — Delta transactions are per-table | ✘ |\n| **Dynamic data masking** | ✔ | ✘ | ✘ |\n\nMulti-table transaction support is the clearest discriminator: it is a documented warehouse feature, and Delta Lake (and therefore the lakehouse) commits one table at a time. Dynamic data masking is documented as *\"Applies to: SQL analytics endpoint and **Warehouse** in Microsoft Fabric\"*.\n\n- **D a semantic model** is not a data store at all — it is an analytics layer over one.",
    "answer": "C",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "You plan to use Fabric to store data.\nYou need to create a data store that supports the following:\n• Writing data by using T-SQL\n• Multi-table transactions\n• Dynamic data masking\nWhich type of data store should you create?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "KQL database",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "lakehouse",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "warehouse",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "semantic model",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Only the **warehouse** has all three capabilities:\n\n| Requirement | Warehouse | Lakehouse | KQL database |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Write data by using T-SQL** | ✔ | ✘ — its SQL analytics endpoint is **read-only**; you write with Spark | ✘ — KQL, not T-SQL |\n| **Multi-table transactions** | ✔ — full ACID `BEGIN/COMMIT/ROLLBACK` across tables | ✘ — Delta transactions are per-table | ✘ |\n| **Dynamic data masking** | ✔ | ✘ | ✘ |\n\nMulti-table transaction support is the clearest discriminator: it is a documented warehouse feature, and Delta Lake (and therefore the lakehouse) commits one table at a time. Dynamic data masking is documented as *\"Applies to: SQL analytics endpoint and **Warehouse** in Microsoft Fabric\"*.\n\n- **D a semantic model** is not a data store at all — it is an analytics layer over one."
    },
