  {
    "id": "T1Q169",
    "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
    "stem": "You have two Microsoft Power BI queries named Employee and Retired Roles.\nYou need to merge the Employee query with the Retired Roles query. The solution must ensure that duplicate rows in each query are removed.\nWhich column and Join Kind should you use in Power Query Editor? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
    "blanks": [
      {
        "label": "Column (in the Employee query):",
        "options": [
          "ID",
          "Division",
          "Title Detail",
          "Role",
          "Department",
          "Department Number",
          "Division Number"
        ],
        "answer": "Division"
      },
      {
        "label": "Join Kind:",
        "options": [
          "Left Outer (all from first, matching from second)",
          "Right Outer (all from second, matching from first)",
          "Full Outer (all rows from both)",
          "Inner (only matching rows)",
          "Left Anti (rows only in first)",
          "Right Anti (rows only in second)"
        ],
        "answer": "Inner (only matching rows)"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "table",
        "title": "Power Query Merge dialog — left query",
        "headers": [
          "Employee columns",
          "Preview values / role"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "ID",
            "Numerical employee ID"
          ],
          [
            "Division",
            "Services"
          ],
          [
            "Title Detail",
            "Marketing, Business Development, Human Resources, HSE & Risk Management"
          ],
          [
            "Role",
            "Vice President"
          ],
          [
            "Department",
            "Marketing, Sales, Human Resources, Safety"
          ],
          [
            "Department Number",
            "22, 17, 13, 16"
          ],
          [
            "Division Number",
            "14"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "table",
        "title": "Power Query Merge dialog — right query",
        "headers": [
          "Retired Roles columns",
          "Preview values"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Division",
            "Services"
          ],
          [
            "Role",
            "Technical Analyst, Technical Support Analyst, Senior Engineer, Chief Technical Officer, Applied Technology Specialist"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "title": "Visible column selection",
        "content": "Employee query: Division and Division Number appear highlighted, with selection-order badges; Retired Roles: Division and Role have badges. Join Kind menu contains Left Outer, Right Outer, Full Outer, Inner, Left Anti and Right Anti."
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q169",
    "sourceId": "T1Q169",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "r10Status": "AMBIGUOUS",
    "reviewNote": "Separated deduplication from matching and corrected selected composite keys.",
    "remaining": "Add explicit unique Retired Roles key-pair step (or semi-join design), inspect source selection badges and execute Power Query duplicate-multiplication fixture.",
    "explanation": "**Division — Inner (only matching rows).**\n\n**Column — `Division`.** The **Retired Roles** query has only two columns, and the dialog already shows them both selected as match keys: `Role` is marked **1** and `Division` is marked **2**. The Employee side therefore needs the same two columns in the same order, and the one still to be picked is **Division**. The other Employee columns have no counterpart in Retired Roles — note especially **Division Number**, a numeric surrogate that would never match the text values (`Services`) in Retired Roles.\n\n**Join Kind — `Inner`.** Of the six kinds, only **Inner** keeps *nothing but* the rows that match on both sides:\n\n| Join kind | Keeps |\n| --- | --- |\n| Left Outer | all Employee rows, matched or not |\n| Right Outer | all Retired Roles rows |\n| Full Outer | everything from both |\n| **Inner** | **only rows present in both** |\n| Left Anti | only Employee rows with **no** match |\n| Right Anti | only Retired Roles rows with no match |\n\nEvery outer kind would drag through unmatched rows padded with nulls; the anti joins would return the complement of what is wanted.\n\n*Note on the wording:* the stem says the merge must *\"ensure that duplicate rows in each query are removed\"*, and one commenter (+6) reasonably objects that **no** join kind deduplicates. That is true — the phrasing is loose. What it is reaching for is \"drop the rows that do not correspond on both sides\", and Inner is the only option that does that.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "Column (in the Employee query):",
          "options": [
            "ID",
            "Division",
            "Title Detail",
            "Role",
            "Department",
            "Department Number",
            "Division Number"
          ],
          "answer": "Division"
        },
        {
          "label": "Join Kind:",
          "options": [
            "Left Outer (all from first, matching from second)",
            "Right Outer (all from second, matching from first)",
            "Full Outer (all rows from both)",
            "Inner (only matching rows)",
            "Left Anti (rows only in first)",
            "Right Anti (rows only in second)"
          ],
          "answer": "Inner (only matching rows)"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have two Microsoft Power BI queries named Employee and Retired Roles.\nYou need to merge the Employee query with the Retired Roles query. The solution must ensure that duplicate rows in each query are removed.\nWhich column and Join Kind should you use in Power Query Editor? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**Division — Inner (only matching rows).**\n\n**Column — `Division`.** The **Retired Roles** query has only two columns, and the dialog already shows them both selected as match keys: `Role` is marked **1** and `Division` is marked **2**. The Employee side therefore needs the same two columns in the same order, and the one still to be picked is **Division**. The other Employee columns have no counterpart in Retired Roles — note especially **Division Number**, a numeric surrogate that would never match the text values (`Services`) in Retired Roles.\n\n**Join Kind — `Inner`.** Of the six kinds, only **Inner** keeps *nothing but* the rows that match on both sides:\n\n| Join kind | Keeps |\n| --- | --- |\n| Left Outer | all Employee rows, matched or not |\n| Right Outer | all Retired Roles rows |\n| Full Outer | everything from both |\n| **Inner** | **only rows present in both** |\n| Left Anti | only Employee rows with **no** match |\n| Right Anti | only Retired Roles rows with no match |\n\nEvery outer kind would drag through unmatched rows padded with nulls; the anti joins would return the complement of what is wanted.\n\n*Note on the wording:* the stem says the merge must *\"ensure that duplicate rows in each query are removed\"*, and one commenter (+6) reasonably objects that **no** join kind deduplicates. That is true — the phrasing is loose. What it is reaching for is \"drop the rows that do not correspond on both sides\", and Inner is the only option that does that."
    },
