  {
    "id": "T1Q170",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1 and a user named User1. Workspace1 contains a warehouse named DW1.\nYou share DW1 with User1 and assign User1 the default permissions for DW1.\nWhat can User1 do?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Build reports by using the default dataset."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Read data from the tables in DW1."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Connect to DW1 via the Azure SQL Analytics endpoint."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Read the underlying Parquet files from OneLake."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q170",
    "sourceId": "T1Q170",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "Sharing a warehouse without ticking any of the extra boxes grants only the base **Read** permission, and the doc says precisely what that buys: *\"If no additional permissions are selected — the shared recipient by default receives **Read** permission, which **only allows the recipient to connect to the SQL analytics endpoint**.\"*\n\nConnecting is all it allows. Querying data requires more:\n\n| Capability | Permission needed | Granted by default? |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Connect to the SQL endpoint** | Read | **yes** |\n| Read data from the tables | **ReadData** | no |\n| Build reports on the default semantic model | **Build** | no |\n| Read the underlying Parquet in OneLake | **ReadAll** (OneLake/Spark) | no |\n\nSo **C**. Options **B**, **A** and **D** each map to one of the *additional* permissions that were not selected — which is exactly the distinction the question is testing.\n\n*Note:* one third of the community picks **B**, reading \"Read permission\" as \"can read the data\". In Fabric's warehouse sharing model those are deliberately different grants.",
    "answer": "C",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1 and a user named User1. Workspace1 contains a warehouse named DW1.\nYou share DW1 with User1 and assign User1 the default permissions for DW1.\nWhat can User1 do?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Build reports by using the default dataset.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Read data from the tables in DW1.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Connect to DW1 via the Azure SQL Analytics endpoint.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Read the underlying Parquet files from OneLake.",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Sharing a warehouse without ticking any of the extra boxes grants only the base **Read** permission, and the doc says precisely what that buys: *\"If no additional permissions are selected — the shared recipient by default receives **Read** permission, which **only allows the recipient to connect to the SQL analytics endpoint**.\"*\n\nConnecting is all it allows. Querying data requires more:\n\n| Capability | Permission needed | Granted by default? |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Connect to the SQL endpoint** | Read | **yes** |\n| Read data from the tables | **ReadData** | no |\n| Build reports on the default semantic model | **Build** | no |\n| Read the underlying Parquet in OneLake | **ReadAll** (OneLake/Spark) | no |\n\nSo **C**. Options **B**, **A** and **D** each map to one of the *additional* permissions that were not selected — which is exactly the distinction the question is testing.\n\n*Note:* one third of the community picks **B**, reading \"Read permission\" as \"can read the data\". In Fabric's warehouse sharing model those are deliberately different grants."
    },
