    "explanation": "**OneLake endpoint — OneLake file explorer.**\n\nRead the exhibit carefully. Of the three *Additional permissions*, only **Read all Apache Spark** is ticked:\n\n| Additional permission | Ticked? | What it grants |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Read all SQL endpoint data | ☐ | read through the SQL analytics endpoint (T-SQL) |\n| **Read all Apache Spark** | **☑** | *\"read all data using Apache Spark and **OneLake APIs**\"* |\n| Build reports on the default semantic model | ☐ | build over the default model |\n\n**Blank 1 — the OneLake endpoint.** That is the access path *Read all Apache Spark* opens. The **Tabular Data Stream (TDS)** endpoint is the wire protocol of the **SQL** analytics endpoint — which needs the unticked *Read all SQL endpoint data* — and the **default semantic model** needs the unticked Build permission.\n\n**Blank 2 — OneLake file explorer.** It is the only listed client that reaches the lakehouse over OneLake APIs, so it is the only one DBUser's permission actually covers. \n- **SSMS** connects over TDS to the SQL analytics endpoint — blocked, as above.\n- **a KQL queryset** queries a **KQL database / eventhouse**. It cannot target a lakehouse at all, whatever the permissions.\n\n*Note:* the top comment (+5) answers *KQL queryset* for blank 2. A KQL queryset has no connection to a lakehouse, so that cannot be right; the answer key gives OneLake file explorer. (The wording \"query\" is generous — File Explorer browses and reads files rather than running queries — but it is the only reachable option.)",
