    "id": "T1Q172",
    "type": "dragdrop",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1.\nYou have three groups named Group1, Group2, and Group3.\nYou need to assign a workspace role to each group. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege and meet the following requirements:\n• Group1 must be able to write data to Workspace1, but be unable to add members to Workspace1.\n• Group2 must be able to configure and maintain the settings of Workspace1.\n• Group3 must be able to write data and add members to Workspace1, but be unable to delete Workspace1.\nWhich workspace role should you assign to each group? To answer, drag the appropriate roles to the correct groups. Each role may be used once,\nmore than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "drag": {
      "items": [
        "Admin",
        "Contributor",
        "Member",
        "Viewer"
      ],
      "reuse": true,
      "targets": [
        {
          "label": "Group1",
          "answer": "Contributor"
        },
        {
          "label": "Group2",
          "answer": "Admin"
        },
        {
          "label": "Group3",
          "answer": "Member"
        }
      ]
    },
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q172",
    "sourceId": "T1Q172",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**Group1 → Contributor, Group2 → Admin, Group3 → Member.** Fabric has exactly four workspace roles, strictly nested: **Admin ⊃ Member ⊃ Contributor ⊃ Viewer**. Work the requirements from the bottom up and each group lands on the *smallest* role that still satisfies it.\n\n| Capability | Viewer | Contributor | Member | Admin |\n|---|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|\n| Read items / view reports | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ |\n| **Create and write items** (write data) | ✘ | **✔** | ✔ | ✔ |\n| **Add members** (grant Member/Contributor/Viewer) | ✘ | ✘ | **✔** | ✔ |\n| Add **Admins** | ✘ | ✘ | ✘ | ✔ |\n| **Update/​delete the workspace**, manage its settings | ✘ | ✘ | ✘ | **✔** |\n\n**Group1 — writes data, cannot add members → Contributor.** Contributor is the first role that can write, and it is the last one that still cannot grant access. Member would satisfy the write half but violates the \"unable to add members\" half.\n\n**Group2 — configures and maintains the workspace settings → Admin.** Updating and deleting the workspace, and managing its settings and access, are Admin-only. Nothing smaller works, so least privilege does not constrain this one.\n\n**Group3 — writes data and adds members, but cannot delete the workspace → Member.** This is the diagnostic row of the table: Member is defined precisely as \"can add other people with lower permissions\" *without* the workspace-lifecycle rights. Admin would also let them delete Workspace1, which the requirement forbids.\n\n**Viewer** is unused — every group has to write or administer something, and Viewer can do neither.\n\n*Watch the trap in Group1 vs Group3:* both \"write data\", and the only thing separating them is whether they may add members. That single clause is what forces Contributor for one and Member for the other.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "dragdrop",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "drag": {
        "items": [
          "Admin",
          "Contributor",
          "Member",
          "Viewer"
        ],
        "reuse": true,
        "targets": [
          {
            "label": "Group1",
            "answer": "Contributor"
          },
          {
            "label": "Group2",
            "answer": "Admin"
          },
          {
            "label": "Group3",
            "answer": "Member"
          }
        ]
      },
      "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1.\nYou have three groups named Group1, Group2, and Group3.\nYou need to assign a workspace role to each group. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege and meet the following requirements:\n• Group1 must be able to write data to Workspace1, but be unable to add members to Workspace1.\n• Group2 must be able to configure and maintain the settings of Workspace1.\n• Group3 must be able to write data and add members to Workspace1, but be unable to delete Workspace1.\nWhich workspace role should you assign to each group? To answer, drag the appropriate roles to the correct groups. Each role may be used once,\nmore than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**Group1 → Contributor, Group2 → Admin, Group3 → Member.** Fabric has exactly four workspace roles, strictly nested: **Admin ⊃ Member ⊃ Contributor ⊃ Viewer**. Work the requirements from the bottom up and each group lands on the *smallest* role that still satisfies it.\n\n| Capability | Viewer | Contributor | Member | Admin |\n|---|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|\n| Read items / view reports | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ |\n| **Create and write items** (write data) | ✘ | **✔** | ✔ | ✔ |\n| **Add members** (grant Member/Contributor/Viewer) | ✘ | ✘ | **✔** | ✔ |\n| Add **Admins** | ✘ | ✘ | ✘ | ✔ |\n| **Update/​delete the workspace**, manage its settings | ✘ | ✘ | ✘ | **✔** |\n\n**Group1 — writes data, cannot add members → Contributor.** Contributor is the first role that can write, and it is the last one that still cannot grant access. Member would satisfy the write half but violates the \"unable to add members\" half.\n\n**Group2 — configures and maintains the workspace settings → Admin.** Updating and deleting the workspace, and managing its settings and access, are Admin-only. Nothing smaller works, so least privilege does not constrain this one.\n\n**Group3 — writes data and adds members, but cannot delete the workspace → Member.** This is the diagnostic row of the table: Member is defined precisely as \"can add other people with lower permissions\" *without* the workspace-lifecycle rights. Admin would also let them delete Workspace1, which the requirement forbids.\n\n**Viewer** is unused — every group has to write or administer something, and Viewer can do neither.\n\n*Watch the trap in Group1 vs Group3:* both \"write data\", and the only thing separating them is whether they may add members. That single clause is what forces Contributor for one and Member for the other."
