  {
    "id": "T1Q173",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant named Tenant1 that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1.\nYou need to add data to Lakehouse1 from a CSV file in an Azure Storage account outside of Fabric. The solution must minimize development\neffort.\nWhat should you use to add the data?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "copy job"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "shortcut"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "pipeline"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Dataflow Gen2"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q173",
    "sourceId": "T1Q173",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**A — copy job.** The verb in the stem is *\"add data **to** Lakehouse1\"*, and only a copy actually lands bytes in the lakehouse.\n\n- **B shortcut** ✘ — a shortcut is a **reference**, not a copy: *\"Shortcuts are objects in OneLake that point to other storage locations… the data is not copied.\"* Lakehouse1 would *show* the CSV, but the data still lives in (and is still served from) the external storage account. This is the 48% answer, and it is a genuinely reasonable reading if you take \"add data\" to mean \"make the data available\" — but the exam consistently treats shortcut as *virtualize* and copy as *add*.\n- **C pipeline** ✘ — a pipeline *can* do it via a Copy activity, but that is more development effort than a copy job (you build and orchestrate the pipeline yourself). The stem's tiebreaker is *\"minimize development effort\"*.\n- **D Dataflow Gen2** ✘ — same objection, plus it is a transformation tool. You would author Power Query steps to move a file that needs no transformation.\n\n**Copy job** is the purpose-built, wizard-driven item for exactly this: pick a source, pick a destination, choose full or incremental, done — *\"a simplified experience… without needing to create a pipeline\"*. Among the three options that genuinely copy, it is the one with the least development effort.\n\n*Rule of thumb for this family of questions:* **\"add / ingest / load / copy\" ⇒ Copy job or Copy activity. \"access / reference / without duplicating / keep in place\" ⇒ shortcut.**",
    "answer": "A",
    "officialAnswer": "A",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "A",
      "answer": "A",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant named Tenant1 that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1.\nYou need to add data to Lakehouse1 from a CSV file in an Azure Storage account outside of Fabric. The solution must minimize development\neffort.\nWhat should you use to add the data?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "copy job",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "shortcut",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "pipeline",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Dataflow Gen2",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**A — copy job.** The verb in the stem is *\"add data **to** Lakehouse1\"*, and only a copy actually lands bytes in the lakehouse.\n\n- **B shortcut** ✘ — a shortcut is a **reference**, not a copy: *\"Shortcuts are objects in OneLake that point to other storage locations… the data is not copied.\"* Lakehouse1 would *show* the CSV, but the data still lives in (and is still served from) the external storage account. This is the 48% answer, and it is a genuinely reasonable reading if you take \"add data\" to mean \"make the data available\" — but the exam consistently treats shortcut as *virtualize* and copy as *add*.\n- **C pipeline** ✘ — a pipeline *can* do it via a Copy activity, but that is more development effort than a copy job (you build and orchestrate the pipeline yourself). The stem's tiebreaker is *\"minimize development effort\"*.\n- **D Dataflow Gen2** ✘ — same objection, plus it is a transformation tool. You would author Power Query steps to move a file that needs no transformation.\n\n**Copy job** is the purpose-built, wizard-driven item for exactly this: pick a source, pick a destination, choose full or incremental, done — *\"a simplified experience… without needing to create a pipeline\"*. Among the three options that genuinely copy, it is the one with the least development effort.\n\n*Rule of thumb for this family of questions:* **\"add / ingest / load / copy\" ⇒ Copy job or Copy activity. \"access / reference / without duplicating / keep in place\" ⇒ shortcut.**"
    },
