  {
    "id": "T1Q174",
    "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named Table1. Table1 contains the following data.\nYou need to create a T-SQL statement that meets the following requirements:\n• Outputs the item name of each item and returns a null value if the item name is longer than 20 characters\n• Outputs the PurchaseDate value in the format of МММ dd, yy\nHow should you complete the statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "blanks": [
      {
        "label": "SELECT item_id as ItemId, [answer choice] as ItemName",
        "options": [
          ",convert(varchar(20), item_name)",
          ",convert(varchar(max), item_name)",
          ",try_cast(item_name as varchar(20))"
        ],
        "answer": ",try_cast(item_name as varchar(20))"
      },
      {
        "label": "[answer choice] as PurchaseDate",
        "options": [
          ",convert(varchar, purchase_date, 7)",
          ",convert(varchar, purchase_date, 109)",
          ",convert(varchar, purchase_date, 112)"
        ],
        "answer": ",convert(varchar, purchase_date, 7)"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "table",
        "title": "Table1 source columns",
        "headers": [
          "Column",
          "Data type"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "item_id",
            "Integer"
          ],
          [
            "item_name",
            "varchar(50)"
          ],
          [
            "item_description",
            "varchar(50)"
          ],
          [
            "purchase_date",
            "Datetime"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "Original T-SQL completion template",
        "language": "sql",
        "content": "SELECT item_id AS ItemId,\n       [Dropdown 1] AS ItemName,\n       item_description AS ItemDescription,\n       [Dropdown 2] AS PurchaseDate\nFROM Table1\nWHERE item_type = @itemtype_parameter;"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q174",
    "sourceId": "T1Q174",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "BLOCKED_RUNTIME",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Screenshot confirms undeclared filter and no existing length-validating choice.",
    "remaining": "Execute length 19/20/21, NULL, trailing-space and English date fixtures in Fabric Warehouse.",
    "explanation": "**`,try_cast(item_name as varchar(20))`** and **`,convert(varchar, purchase_date, 7)`**.\n\n### Blank 1 — \"returns a **null** value if the item name is longer than 20 characters\"\n\nThe word *null* decides it. The `TRY_` family is the only thing in T-SQL that converts **or returns NULL instead of erroring**:\n\n| Expression | Name longer than 20 chars |\n|---|---|\n| `CONVERT(varchar(20), item_name)` | **Silently truncates** to 20 characters — no error, no NULL, and you have quietly corrupted the data |\n| `CONVERT(varchar(max), item_name)` | Returns the full name — no truncation *and* no NULL |\n| **`TRY_CAST(item_name AS varchar(20))`** | **NULL** ✔ |\n\nNote the subtlety that makes this question worth its point: `CAST`/`CONVERT` to a *shorter* character type does **not** raise an error the way an overflowing numeric conversion does — it truncates. `TRY_CAST`, however, treats the truncation as a failed conversion and yields `NULL`, which is precisely the requested behaviour.\n\n### Blank 2 — output format `MMM dd, yy`\n\nThis is the `CONVERT` style-number table. Only style **7** produces `Apr 20, 22`:\n\n| Style | Output | Matches `MMM dd, yy`? |\n|:-:|---|:-:|\n| **7** | `Mon dd, yy` → `Apr 20, 22` | **✔** |\n| 109 | `mon dd yyyy hh:mi:ss:mmmAM` → `Apr 20 2022 10:00:00:000AM` | ✘ four-digit year, plus a time |\n| 112 | `yyyymmdd` → `20220420` | ✘ ISO, no month name |\n\nUseful shortcuts for the styles that keep reappearing: **1** = `mm/dd/yy`, **101** = `mm/dd/yyyy`, **103** = `dd/mm/yyyy` (British/French), **112** = `yyyymmdd` (ISO, sort-safe), **120** = `yyyy-mm-dd hh:mi:ss` (ODBC canonical). The general pattern: **styles under 100 use a two-digit year; add 100 for the four-digit-year variant.** Style 7 and style 107 are the same layout, differing only in `yy` vs `yyyy` — which is exactly why the requirement spells out `yy`.\n\n*(The top-voted comment's complaint is fair — nobody memorises these. But `MMM dd, yy` narrows it to a two-digit-year style with a month abbreviation, and 109/112 are visibly neither.)*",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "SELECT item_id as ItemId, [answer choice] as ItemName",
          "options": [
            ",convert(varchar(20), item_name)",
            ",convert(varchar(max), item_name)",
            ",try_cast(item_name as varchar(20))"
          ],
          "answer": ",try_cast(item_name as varchar(20))"
        },
        {
          "label": "[answer choice] as PurchaseDate",
          "options": [
            ",convert(varchar, purchase_date, 7)",
            ",convert(varchar, purchase_date, 109)",
            ",convert(varchar, purchase_date, 112)"
          ],
          "answer": ",convert(varchar, purchase_date, 7)"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named Table1. Table1 contains the following data.\nYou need to create a T-SQL statement that meets the following requirements:\n• Outputs the item name of each item and returns a null value if the item name is longer than 20 characters\n• Outputs the PurchaseDate value in the format of МММ dd, yy\nHow should you complete the statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**`,try_cast(item_name as varchar(20))`** and **`,convert(varchar, purchase_date, 7)`**.\n\n### Blank 1 — \"returns a **null** value if the item name is longer than 20 characters\"\n\nThe word *null* decides it. The `TRY_` family is the only thing in T-SQL that converts **or returns NULL instead of erroring**:\n\n| Expression | Name longer than 20 chars |\n|---|---|\n| `CONVERT(varchar(20), item_name)` | **Silently truncates** to 20 characters — no error, no NULL, and you have quietly corrupted the data |\n| `CONVERT(varchar(max), item_name)` | Returns the full name — no truncation *and* no NULL |\n| **`TRY_CAST(item_name AS varchar(20))`** | **NULL** ✔ |\n\nNote the subtlety that makes this question worth its point: `CAST`/`CONVERT` to a *shorter* character type does **not** raise an error the way an overflowing numeric conversion does — it truncates. `TRY_CAST`, however, treats the truncation as a failed conversion and yields `NULL`, which is precisely the requested behaviour.\n\n### Blank 2 — output format `MMM dd, yy`\n\nThis is the `CONVERT` style-number table. Only style **7** produces `Apr 20, 22`:\n\n| Style | Output | Matches `MMM dd, yy`? |\n|:-:|---|:-:|\n| **7** | `Mon dd, yy` → `Apr 20, 22` | **✔** |\n| 109 | `mon dd yyyy hh:mi:ss:mmmAM` → `Apr 20 2022 10:00:00:000AM` | ✘ four-digit year, plus a time |\n| 112 | `yyyymmdd` → `20220420` | ✘ ISO, no month name |\n\nUseful shortcuts for the styles that keep reappearing: **1** = `mm/dd/yy`, **101** = `mm/dd/yyyy`, **103** = `dd/mm/yyyy` (British/French), **112** = `yyyymmdd` (ISO, sort-safe), **120** = `yyyy-mm-dd hh:mi:ss` (ODBC canonical). The general pattern: **styles under 100 use a two-digit year; add 100 for the four-digit-year variant.** Style 7 and style 107 are the same layout, differing only in `yy` vs `yyyy` — which is exactly why the requirement spells out `yy`.\n\n*(The top-voted comment's complaint is fair — nobody memorises these. But `MMM dd, yy` narrows it to a two-digit-year style with a month abbreviation, and 109/112 are visibly neither.)*"
    },
