    "id": "T1Q175",
    "type": "dragdrop",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric warehouse named Warehouse1 that contains a table named dbo.Product.\ndbo.Product contains the following columns.\nYou need to use a T-SQL query to add a column named PriceRange to dbo.Product. The column must categorize each product based on UnitPrice.\nThe solution must meet the following requirements:\n• If UnitPrice is 0, PriceRange is \"Not for resale\".\n• If UnitPrice is less than 50, PriceRange is \"Under $50\".\n• If UnitPrice is between 50 and 250, PriceRange is \"Under $250“.\n• In all other instances, PriceRange is \"$250+\".\nHow should you complete the query? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once,\nor not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "drag": {
      "items": [
        "BEGIN",
        "CASE",
        "ELSE",
        "END",
        "IF",
        "WHILE"
      ],
      "reuse": true,
      "targets": [
        {
          "label": "PriceRange = [1]",
          "answer": "CASE"
        },
        {
          "label": "[2] '$250+'",
          "answer": "ELSE"
        },
        {
          "label": "[3]  (closes the expression, before FROM)",
          "answer": "END"
        }
      ]
    },
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "table",
        "title": "dbo.Product fields",
        "headers": [
          "Name",
          "Description"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Item",
            "Name of the product"
          ],
          [
            "UnitPrice",
            "Price per unit in U.S. dollars for each product"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "Original PriceRange completion template",
        "language": "sql",
        "content": "SELECT Item,\n       UnitPrice,\n       PriceRange = [Target 1]\n           WHEN UnitPrice = 0 THEN 'Not for resale'\n           WHEN UnitPrice < 50 THEN 'Under $50'\n           WHEN UnitPrice >= 50 AND UnitPrice < 250 THEN 'Under $250'\n           [Target 2] '$250+'\n       [Target 3]\nFROM [Warehouse1].[dbo].[Product];"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q175",
    "sourceId": "T1Q175",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**CASE … ELSE … END.** The finished query is:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    Item,\n    UnitPrice,\n    PriceRange = CASE\n        WHEN UnitPrice = 0 THEN 'Not for resale'\n        WHEN UnitPrice < 50 THEN 'Under $50'\n        WHEN UnitPrice >= 50 AND UnitPrice < 250 THEN 'Under $250'\n        ELSE '$250+'\n    END\nFROM [Warehouse1].[dbo].[Product]\n```\n\nThree things are being tested:\n\n**1. `CASE`, not `IF`.** This is the one that catches people. `IF` in T-SQL is a **statement** — it controls which statements run; it cannot produce a value inside a `SELECT` list. `CASE` is an **expression** — it evaluates to a single scalar per row, which is exactly what a computed column needs. (`IIF()` would also work as an expression, but it is binary and is not on the list.)\n\n**2. `ELSE` for \"in all other instances\".** The requirement's wording is a direct translation of the `ELSE` branch. Leave `ELSE` out and unmatched rows get `NULL` rather than `'$250+'`.\n\n**3. `END` closes the expression.** Every `CASE` must be terminated by `END` before the query continues to `FROM`. Note the asymmetry worth remembering: `CASE` pairs with `END`, but **not** with `BEGIN` — `BEGIN…END` delimits a *statement block*, a completely different construct. `BEGIN`, `IF`, and `WHILE` are all procedural statement keywords and none of them belongs inside a `SELECT` list.\n\n*Also note the `WHEN` ordering, even though it is pre-filled:* the branches are evaluated **top to bottom, first match wins**. `WHEN UnitPrice = 0` has to come before `WHEN UnitPrice < 50`, or a price of 0 would be caught by the second branch and labelled `'Under $50'`.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "dragdrop",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "drag": {
        "items": [
          "BEGIN",
          "CASE",
          "ELSE",
          "END",
          "IF",
          "WHILE"
        ],
        "reuse": true,
        "targets": [
          {
            "label": "PriceRange = [1]",
            "answer": "CASE"
          },
          {
            "label": "[2] '$250+'",
            "answer": "ELSE"
          },
          {
            "label": "[3]  (closes the expression, before FROM)",
            "answer": "END"
          }
        ]
      },
      "stem": "You have a Fabric warehouse named Warehouse1 that contains a table named dbo.Product.\ndbo.Product contains the following columns.\nYou need to use a T-SQL query to add a column named PriceRange to dbo.Product. The column must categorize each product based on UnitPrice.\nThe solution must meet the following requirements:\n• If UnitPrice is 0, PriceRange is \"Not for resale\".\n• If UnitPrice is less than 50, PriceRange is \"Under $50\".\n• If UnitPrice is between 50 and 250, PriceRange is \"Under $250“.\n• In all other instances, PriceRange is \"$250+\".\nHow should you complete the query? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once,\nor not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**CASE … ELSE … END.** The finished query is:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    Item,\n    UnitPrice,\n    PriceRange = CASE\n        WHEN UnitPrice = 0 THEN 'Not for resale'\n        WHEN UnitPrice < 50 THEN 'Under $50'\n        WHEN UnitPrice >= 50 AND UnitPrice < 250 THEN 'Under $250'\n        ELSE '$250+'\n    END\nFROM [Warehouse1].[dbo].[Product]\n```\n\nThree things are being tested:\n\n**1. `CASE`, not `IF`.** This is the one that catches people. `IF` in T-SQL is a **statement** — it controls which statements run; it cannot produce a value inside a `SELECT` list. `CASE` is an **expression** — it evaluates to a single scalar per row, which is exactly what a computed column needs. (`IIF()` would also work as an expression, but it is binary and is not on the list.)\n\n**2. `ELSE` for \"in all other instances\".** The requirement's wording is a direct translation of the `ELSE` branch. Leave `ELSE` out and unmatched rows get `NULL` rather than `'$250+'`.\n\n**3. `END` closes the expression.** Every `CASE` must be terminated by `END` before the query continues to `FROM`. Note the asymmetry worth remembering: `CASE` pairs with `END`, but **not** with `BEGIN` — `BEGIN…END` delimits a *statement block*, a completely different construct. `BEGIN`, `IF`, and `WHILE` are all procedural statement keywords and none of them belongs inside a `SELECT` list.\n\n*Also note the `WHEN` ordering, even though it is pre-filled:* the branches are evaluated **top to bottom, first match wins**. `WHEN UnitPrice = 0` has to come before `WHEN UnitPrice < 50`, or a price of 0 would be caught by the second branch and labelled `'Under $50'`."
    },
