    "id": "T1Q176",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou are creating a Fabric Data Factory pipeline.\nYou have a stored procedure that returns the number of active customers and their average sales for the current month.\nYou need to add an activity that will execute the stored procedure in a warehouse. The returned values must be available to the downstream\nactivities of the pipeline.\nWhich type of activity should you add?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Append variable"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Script"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Stored procedure"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Get metadata"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q176",
    "sourceId": "T1Q176",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**B — Script.** The trap is that option C is named after the thing you want to do, and is still wrong.\n\nThe requirement that decides it is *\"the returned values must be available to the downstream activities\"*. Microsoft states the limitation plainly: **\"When the stored procedure has Output parameters, instead of using stored procedure activity, use lookup activity and Script activity. Stored procedure activity does not support calling SPs with Output parameter yet.\"**\n\n- **C Stored procedure** ✘ — it *executes* the procedure perfectly well, but it is fire-and-forget. It surfaces no result set and no output parameters to the pipeline, so nothing downstream can read the customer count or the average sales.\n- **B Script** ✔ — runs SQL (including `EXEC dbo.MyProc`) against the warehouse **and** exposes the results in its activity output as `resultSets`, referenceable downstream as `@activity('Script1').output.resultSets[0].rows[0].ColumnName`.\n- **A Append variable** ✘ — appends a value to an array variable. Executes nothing.\n- **D Get metadata** ✘ — returns metadata about files/folders/tables (name, size, structure). Executes nothing.\n\n**Lookup** is the other activity that would satisfy both halves, and it is the usual answer when it appears (see T1Q83, T1Q114, T1Q122, T1Q128, T1Q160). Here it is deliberately **not** offered, which forces Script — so read the option list before pattern-matching to Lookup.\n\n| Activity | Executes the proc? | Returns values downstream? |\n|---|:-:|:-:|\n| Stored procedure | ✔ | **✘** |\n| **Script** | ✔ | **✔** |\n| Lookup *(not offered here)* | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Append variable / Get metadata | ✘ | — |",
    "answer": "B",
    "officialAnswer": "B",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "B",
      "answer": "B",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou are creating a Fabric Data Factory pipeline.\nYou have a stored procedure that returns the number of active customers and their average sales for the current month.\nYou need to add an activity that will execute the stored procedure in a warehouse. The returned values must be available to the downstream\nactivities of the pipeline.\nWhich type of activity should you add?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Append variable",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Script",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Stored procedure",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Get metadata",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**B — Script.** The trap is that option C is named after the thing you want to do, and is still wrong.\n\nThe requirement that decides it is *\"the returned values must be available to the downstream activities\"*. Microsoft states the limitation plainly: **\"When the stored procedure has Output parameters, instead of using stored procedure activity, use lookup activity and Script activity. Stored procedure activity does not support calling SPs with Output parameter yet.\"**\n\n- **C Stored procedure** ✘ — it *executes* the procedure perfectly well, but it is fire-and-forget. It surfaces no result set and no output parameters to the pipeline, so nothing downstream can read the customer count or the average sales.\n- **B Script** ✔ — runs SQL (including `EXEC dbo.MyProc`) against the warehouse **and** exposes the results in its activity output as `resultSets`, referenceable downstream as `@activity('Script1').output.resultSets[0].rows[0].ColumnName`.\n- **A Append variable** ✘ — appends a value to an array variable. Executes nothing.\n- **D Get metadata** ✘ — returns metadata about files/folders/tables (name, size, structure). Executes nothing.\n\n**Lookup** is the other activity that would satisfy both halves, and it is the usual answer when it appears (see T1Q83, T1Q114, T1Q122, T1Q128, T1Q160). Here it is deliberately **not** offered, which forces Script — so read the option list before pattern-matching to Lookup.\n\n| Activity | Executes the proc? | Returns values downstream? |\n|---|:-:|:-:|\n| Stored procedure | ✔ | **✘** |\n| **Script** | ✔ | **✔** |\n| Lookup *(not offered here)* | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Append variable / Get metadata | ✘ | — |"
    },
