    "id": "T1Q177",
    "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1. Warehouse1 contains the following data.\nYou need to create a T-SQL statement that will denormalize the tables and include the ContractType and StartDate attributes in the results. The\nsolution must meet the following requirements:\n• Include attributes from matching rows in the Contract table.\n• Ensure that all the rows from the Employee table are preserved.\n• Return the total number of employees per contract type for all the contract types that have more than two employees.\nHow should you complete the statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "blanks": [
      {
        "label": "FROM Employee AS e [answer choice] Contract AS c on c.EmployeeID = e.EmployeeID)",
        "options": [
          "CROSS JOIN",
          "INNER JOIN",
          "LEFT OUTER JOIN",
          "RIGHT OUTER JOIN"
        ],
        "answer": "LEFT OUTER JOIN"
      },
      {
        "label": "GROUP BY ContractType [answer choice] COUNT(DISTINCT EmployeeID) > 2",
        "options": [
          "CONTAINS",
          "HAVING",
          "LIMIT",
          "WHERE"
        ],
        "answer": "HAVING"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "table",
        "title": "Source Employee / Contract schema",
        "headers": [
          "Table",
          "Column",
          "Data type"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Employee",
            "EmployeeID",
            "Int"
          ],
          [
            "Employee",
            "EmployeeName",
            "Varchar(128)"
          ],
          [
            "Employee",
            "EmployeePosition",
            "Varchar(64)"
          ],
          [
            "Contract",
            "EmployeeID",
            "Int"
          ],
          [
            "Contract",
            "ContractType",
            "Varchar(64)"
          ],
          [
            "Contract",
            "StartDate",
            "Datetime2"
          ],
          [
            "Contract",
            "EndDate",
            "Datetime2"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "Original SQL template",
        "language": "sql",
        "content": "WITH result AS (\n    SELECT e.EmployeeID, e.EmployeeName, e.EmployeePosition,\n           c.ContractType\n    FROM Employee AS e\n    [Dropdown 1] Contract AS c ON c.EmployeeID = e.EmployeeID\n)\nSELECT COUNT(DISTINCT EmployeeID) AS TotalEmployees, ContractType\nFROM result\nGROUP BY ContractType\n[Dropdown 2] COUNT(DISTINCT EmployeeID) > 2;"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q177",
    "sourceId": "T1Q177",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "BLOCKED_RUNTIME",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Separated contradictory row-preservation and aggregate-output requirements; restored StartDate in intermediate result.",
    "remaining": "Inspect screenshot and execute unmatched/multiple-contract/group-threshold fixtures.",
    "explanation": "**LEFT OUTER JOIN** and **HAVING**.\n\n### Blank 1 — the join\n\nTwo requirements pull in opposite directions and the join type has to satisfy both:\n\n- *\"Include attributes from matching rows in the Contract table\"* — so contract columns come along where a match exists.\n- *\"Ensure that **all** the rows from the Employee table are preserved\"* — so an employee with no contract must still appear.\n\nThe second requirement is the decisive one, and `Employee` is on the **left** of the join (`FROM Employee AS e … Contract AS c`):\n\n| Join | Employees with no contract | Verdict |\n|---|---|:-:|\n| INNER JOIN | **dropped** | ✘ violates \"all rows preserved\" |\n| RIGHT OUTER JOIN | **dropped** (preserves *Contract* instead) | ✘ right table is the wrong one |\n| CROSS JOIN | Cartesian product — every employee × every contract; the `ON` clause is not even legal | ✘ |\n| **LEFT OUTER JOIN** | kept, with `NULL` contract columns | **✔** |\n\n*Read the table order, not the option names.* `RIGHT OUTER JOIN` would be correct had the query been written `FROM Contract AS c … Employee AS e` — swapping the operands is exactly how this distractor is built.\n\n### Blank 2 — the filter\n\n*\"…for all the contract types that have **more than two employees**\"* filters on an **aggregate** (`COUNT(DISTINCT EmployeeID) > 2`), and the placement in the query is immediately after `GROUP BY`:\n\n- **HAVING** ✔ — filters **groups, after aggregation**. The only clause that may reference `COUNT(...)`.\n- **WHERE** ✘ — filters **rows, before aggregation**, and cannot contain an aggregate function; `WHERE COUNT(...) > 2` is a syntax error.\n- **LIMIT** ✘ — MySQL/PostgreSQL syntax. T-SQL uses `TOP` or `OFFSET…FETCH`, and it caps row counts rather than filtering on a condition.\n- **CONTAINS** ✘ — full-text search predicate for matching words inside character columns.\n\n**WHERE before GROUP BY, HAVING after** is the one-line rule; the appearance of an aggregate inside the condition settles it every time.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "FROM Employee AS e [answer choice] Contract AS c on c.EmployeeID = e.EmployeeID)",
          "options": [
            "CROSS JOIN",
            "INNER JOIN",
            "LEFT OUTER JOIN",
            "RIGHT OUTER JOIN"
          ],
          "answer": "LEFT OUTER JOIN"
        },
        {
          "label": "GROUP BY ContractType [answer choice] COUNT(DISTINCT EmployeeID) > 2",
          "options": [
            "CONTAINS",
            "HAVING",
            "LIMIT",
            "WHERE"
          ],
          "answer": "HAVING"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1. Warehouse1 contains the following data.\nYou need to create a T-SQL statement that will denormalize the tables and include the ContractType and StartDate attributes in the results. The\nsolution must meet the following requirements:\n• Include attributes from matching rows in the Contract table.\n• Ensure that all the rows from the Employee table are preserved.\n• Return the total number of employees per contract type for all the contract types that have more than two employees.\nHow should you complete the statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**LEFT OUTER JOIN** and **HAVING**.\n\n### Blank 1 — the join\n\nTwo requirements pull in opposite directions and the join type has to satisfy both:\n\n- *\"Include attributes from matching rows in the Contract table\"* — so contract columns come along where a match exists.\n- *\"Ensure that **all** the rows from the Employee table are preserved\"* — so an employee with no contract must still appear.\n\nThe second requirement is the decisive one, and `Employee` is on the **left** of the join (`FROM Employee AS e … Contract AS c`):\n\n| Join | Employees with no contract | Verdict |\n|---|---|:-:|\n| INNER JOIN | **dropped** | ✘ violates \"all rows preserved\" |\n| RIGHT OUTER JOIN | **dropped** (preserves *Contract* instead) | ✘ right table is the wrong one |\n| CROSS JOIN | Cartesian product — every employee × every contract; the `ON` clause is not even legal | ✘ |\n| **LEFT OUTER JOIN** | kept, with `NULL` contract columns | **✔** |\n\n*Read the table order, not the option names.* `RIGHT OUTER JOIN` would be correct had the query been written `FROM Contract AS c … Employee AS e` — swapping the operands is exactly how this distractor is built.\n\n### Blank 2 — the filter\n\n*\"…for all the contract types that have **more than two employees**\"* filters on an **aggregate** (`COUNT(DISTINCT EmployeeID) > 2`), and the placement in the query is immediately after `GROUP BY`:\n\n- **HAVING** ✔ — filters **groups, after aggregation**. The only clause that may reference `COUNT(...)`.\n- **WHERE** ✘ — filters **rows, before aggregation**, and cannot contain an aggregate function; `WHERE COUNT(...) > 2` is a syntax error.\n- **LIMIT** ✘ — MySQL/PostgreSQL syntax. T-SQL uses `TOP` or `OFFSET…FETCH`, and it caps row counts rather than filtering on a condition.\n- **CONTAINS** ✘ — full-text search predicate for matching words inside character columns.\n\n**WHERE before GROUP BY, HAVING after** is the one-line rule; the appearance of an aggregate inside the condition settles it every time."
    },
