  {
    "id": "T1Q179",
    "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace that contains a warehouse named DW1. DW1 contains the following tables and columns.\nYou need to summarize order quantities by year and product. The solution must include the yearly sum of order quantities for all the products in\neach row.\nHow should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "blanks": [
      {
        "label": "SELECT [answer choice] (SO.ModifiedDate) AS OrderDate",
        "options": [
          "CAST",
          "CONVERT",
          "YEAR"
        ],
        "answer": "YEAR"
      },
      {
        "label": "GROUP BY [answer choice]",
        "options": [
          "CUBE(YEAR(SO.ModifiedDate), P.Name)",
          "GROUPING SETS ((YEAR(SO.ModifiedDate), P.Name), (YEAR(SO.ModifiedDate)))",
          "ROLLUP(YEAR(SO.ModifiedDate), P.Name)",
          "YEAR(SO.ModifiedDate), P.Name"
        ],
        "answer": "ROLLUP(YEAR(SO.ModifiedDate), P.Name)"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "table",
        "title": "DW1 source tables and columns",
        "headers": [
          "Table",
          "Column"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "SalesOrderDetail",
            "ProductID"
          ],
          [
            "SalesOrderDetail",
            "ModifiedDate"
          ],
          [
            "SalesOrderDetail",
            "OrderQty"
          ],
          [
            "Product",
            "ProductID"
          ],
          [
            "Product",
            "Name"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "Original annual order-quantity query template",
        "language": "sql",
        "content": "SELECT [Dropdown 1](SO.ModifiedDate) AS OrderDate,\n       P.Name AS ProductName,\n       SUM(SO.OrderQty) AS OrderQty\nFROM [dbo].[SalesOrderDetail] SO\nINNER JOIN [dbo].[Product] P ON P.ProductID = SO.ProductID\nGROUP BY [Dropdown 2]\nORDER BY OrderDate;"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q179",
    "sourceId": "T1Q179",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "BLOCKED_RUNTIME",
    "r10Status": "AMBIGUOUS",
    "reviewNote": "Added explicit grand-total and excluded product-only subtotal requirements.",
    "remaining": "Read current GROUP BY/YEAR docs and execute grouping-level fixture.",
    "explanation": "**YEAR** and **ROLLUP(YEAR(SO.ModifiedDate), P.Name)**.\n\n### Blank 1 — YEAR\n\nBoth other options fail on **arity**: `CAST` requires the `AS type` syntax (`CAST(x AS int)`) and `CONVERT` requires a target type as its first argument (`CONVERT(int, x)`). Neither can be written as a single-argument call `xxx(SO.ModifiedDate)`. `YEAR()` takes exactly one date argument and returns the year as an integer — the only option that even compiles in that slot, and the only one that produces a \"summarize **by year**\" grouping key.\n\n### Blank 2 — ROLLUP (the contested one)\n\nThe requirement is *\"summarize order quantities by year and product… **must include the yearly sum of order quantities for all the products in each row**\"*. So the result needs **two levels**: a detail row per (year, product), **plus** a subtotal row per year covering all products.\n\n| GROUP BY | Grouping sets produced | Meets it? |\n|---|---|:-:|\n| `YEAR(...), P.Name` | (Year, Product) only | ✘ **no yearly subtotal at all** |\n| **`ROLLUP(YEAR(...), P.Name)`** | (Year, Product), **(Year)**, () | **✔** |\n| `GROUPING SETS ((Year, Product), (Year))` | (Year, Product), (Year) | ✔ also produces it |\n| `CUBE(YEAR(...), P.Name)` | all four, including **(Product)** across years | ✘ adds a per-product-across-all-years level nobody asked for |\n\n**Why ROLLUP over GROUPING SETS.** This is a real split in the discussion and both are defensible. `ROLLUP(a, b)` is by definition the shorthand for `GROUPING SETS ((a,b), (a), ())` — so the two offered options differ by exactly one row: ROLLUP adds a **grand total**. The requirement neither asks for nor forbids that grand total, and ROLLUP is the canonical, idiomatic way to express *\"a hierarchy of subtotals from most detailed to least\"*, which is precisely year → product. GROUPING SETS is the escape hatch for **non-hierarchical**, hand-picked combinations; spelling out a plain hierarchy with it is something you would only do to suppress the grand total. The answer key marks ROLLUP.\n\n**Why not the plain `YEAR(...), P.Name` option** (which one commenter prefers on the grounds that ROLLUP \"causes a few extra rows\"): a plain GROUP BY produces *only* the (year, product) detail, so the yearly sum across all products is nowhere in the output. That reading deletes the second sentence of the requirement, which is the only sentence that distinguishes the four options.\n\n*Tip for spotting these:* the words **\"subtotal\", \"total for all\", \"rolled up\", \"at each level\"** point at ROLLUP; **\"only these specific combinations\"** points at GROUPING SETS; **\"every possible combination / all dimensions crossed\"** points at CUBE.\n\n*(Compare T1Q6-style items where the requirement enumerates exactly which groupings are wanted — there GROUPING SETS wins.)*",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "SELECT [answer choice] (SO.ModifiedDate) AS OrderDate",
          "options": [
            "CAST",
            "CONVERT",
            "YEAR"
          ],
          "answer": "YEAR"
        },
        {
          "label": "GROUP BY [answer choice]",
          "options": [
            "CUBE(YEAR(SO.ModifiedDate), P.Name)",
            "GROUPING SETS ((YEAR(SO.ModifiedDate), P.Name), (YEAR(SO.ModifiedDate)))",
            "ROLLUP(YEAR(SO.ModifiedDate), P.Name)",
            "YEAR(SO.ModifiedDate), P.Name"
          ],
          "answer": "ROLLUP(YEAR(SO.ModifiedDate), P.Name)"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace that contains a warehouse named DW1. DW1 contains the following tables and columns.\nYou need to summarize order quantities by year and product. The solution must include the yearly sum of order quantities for all the products in\neach row.\nHow should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**YEAR** and **ROLLUP(YEAR(SO.ModifiedDate), P.Name)**.\n\n### Blank 1 — YEAR\n\nBoth other options fail on **arity**: `CAST` requires the `AS type` syntax (`CAST(x AS int)`) and `CONVERT` requires a target type as its first argument (`CONVERT(int, x)`). Neither can be written as a single-argument call `xxx(SO.ModifiedDate)`. `YEAR()` takes exactly one date argument and returns the year as an integer — the only option that even compiles in that slot, and the only one that produces a \"summarize **by year**\" grouping key.\n\n### Blank 2 — ROLLUP (the contested one)\n\nThe requirement is *\"summarize order quantities by year and product… **must include the yearly sum of order quantities for all the products in each row**\"*. So the result needs **two levels**: a detail row per (year, product), **plus** a subtotal row per year covering all products.\n\n| GROUP BY | Grouping sets produced | Meets it? |\n|---|---|:-:|\n| `YEAR(...), P.Name` | (Year, Product) only | ✘ **no yearly subtotal at all** |\n| **`ROLLUP(YEAR(...), P.Name)`** | (Year, Product), **(Year)**, () | **✔** |\n| `GROUPING SETS ((Year, Product), (Year))` | (Year, Product), (Year) | ✔ also produces it |\n| `CUBE(YEAR(...), P.Name)` | all four, including **(Product)** across years | ✘ adds a per-product-across-all-years level nobody asked for |\n\n**Why ROLLUP over GROUPING SETS.** This is a real split in the discussion and both are defensible. `ROLLUP(a, b)` is by definition the shorthand for `GROUPING SETS ((a,b), (a), ())` — so the two offered options differ by exactly one row: ROLLUP adds a **grand total**. The requirement neither asks for nor forbids that grand total, and ROLLUP is the canonical, idiomatic way to express *\"a hierarchy of subtotals from most detailed to least\"*, which is precisely year → product. GROUPING SETS is the escape hatch for **non-hierarchical**, hand-picked combinations; spelling out a plain hierarchy with it is something you would only do to suppress the grand total. The answer key marks ROLLUP.\n\n**Why not the plain `YEAR(...), P.Name` option** (which one commenter prefers on the grounds that ROLLUP \"causes a few extra rows\"): a plain GROUP BY produces *only* the (year, product) detail, so the yearly sum across all products is nowhere in the output. That reading deletes the second sentence of the requirement, which is the only sentence that distinguishes the four options.\n\n*Tip for spotting these:* the words **\"subtotal\", \"total for all\", \"rolled up\", \"at each level\"** point at ROLLUP; **\"only these specific combinations\"** points at GROUPING SETS; **\"every possible combination / all dimensions crossed\"** points at CUBE.\n\n*(Compare T1Q6-style items where the requirement enumerates exactly which groupings are wanted — there GROUPING SETS wins.)*"
