      "explanation": "**B — Select Large semantic model storage format for Workspace1.** Microsoft documents this under the heading *\"Optimize semantic models for write operations by enabling large models\"*: with the large-model format enabled, **write operations through the XMLA endpoint can be significantly faster**, because the model is stored in premium files rather than as a single in-memory blob that must be rewritten wholesale.\n\nEverything else changes the wrong variable:\n\n- **A Delete unused semantic models** ✘ — frees capacity memory. It has no effect on the throughput of a write to *this* model.\n- **C Direct Lake storage format** ✘ — Direct Lake is about **read** performance (query straight off Delta tables in OneLake, no import refresh). It is also the wrong direction here: the stem says Model1 *is updated by using an XMLA endpoint*, which is an import-model workflow.\n- **D Delete unused columns from Model1** ✘ — genuinely good modelling hygiene and it does shrink the model, but it is a one-off data reduction, not a change to how writes are processed. The exam wants the setting that targets XMLA write speed directly.\n\nSetting it: **Workspace settings → Semantic models → Large semantic model storage format**, or tenant-wide via the admin portal. Note it is a **workspace-level** (or per-model) setting, which is why the correct option names Workspace1 rather than Model1.\n\n*(Same underlying fact as T1Q141 — recognise \"XMLA write performance ⇒ large semantic model storage format\".)*"
