    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**A + D — enable Git integration for Workspace1, and save Model1 as a PBIP file.** The four requirements split cleanly into two pairs, one per answer.\n\n**A covers the versioning pair.**\n- *\"Can revert to previous versions\"* → Git history; `git revert` / restore a prior commit, then sync the workspace.\n- *\"Identifies differences between saved versions\"* → **diffs**. This is the requirement that rules out everything except source control: Fabric Git integration shows changed items and, because the definitions are plain text, line-level diffs in Git.\n\n**D covers the file-format pair.**\n- *\"Can edit item definition files by using Visual Studio Code\"* → **PBIP** (Power BI Project) saves the report and semantic model as a **folder of plain-text files** — `definition/` with TMDL or `model.bim`, `.pbir`, etc. — which VS Code can edit and Git can diff. A `.pbix` is an opaque binary; a diff of it is meaningless.\n- *\"Uses Power BI Desktop to publish to Workspace1\"* → PBIP is saved and published from Desktop as normal.\n\nThe two work together: PBIP produces the text files, Git integration versions them. Neither alone satisfies all four bullets — which is why this is a two-answer question.\n\n**Why not B or C:**\n- **B Save as a PBIT file** ✘ — the 14% answer. A `.pbit` **template** is still a single binary file; it merely strips the data. It gives you no text definitions for VS Code and no meaningful diffs. PBIT is for redistributing a model shell to other authors, not for source control.\n- **C Enable users to edit data models in the Power BI service** ✘ — browser-based model editing. It is the *opposite* of the stated workflow (which is explicitly Desktop + VS Code) and contributes nothing to versioning or diffing.\n\n*Recognition rule:* **\"edit definition files in VS Code / text-based / diffable\" ⇒ PBIP. \"version history, revert, compare versions\" ⇒ Git integration.**",
    "answer": "AD",
    "officialAnswer": "AD",
    "original": {
      "type": "multi",
      "officialAnswer": "AD",
      "answer": "AD",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1.\nYou need to create a semantic model named Model1 and publish Model1 to Workspace1. The solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Can revert to previous versions of Model1 as required.\n• Identifies differences between saved versions of Model1.\n• Uses Microsoft Power Bl=I Desktop to publish to Workspace1.\n• Can edit item definition files by using Microsoft Visual Studio Code.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Enable Git integration for Workspace1.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Save Model1 in Power BI Desktop as a PBIT file.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Enable users to edit data models in the Power BI service.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Save Model1 in Power BI Desktop as a PBIP file.",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**A + D — enable Git integration for Workspace1, and save Model1 as a PBIP file.** The four requirements split cleanly into two pairs, one per answer.\n\n**A covers the versioning pair.**\n- *\"Can revert to previous versions\"* → Git history; `git revert` / restore a prior commit, then sync the workspace.\n- *\"Identifies differences between saved versions\"* → **diffs**. This is the requirement that rules out everything except source control: Fabric Git integration shows changed items and, because the definitions are plain text, line-level diffs in Git.\n\n**D covers the file-format pair.**\n- *\"Can edit item definition files by using Visual Studio Code\"* → **PBIP** (Power BI Project) saves the report and semantic model as a **folder of plain-text files** — `definition/` with TMDL or `model.bim`, `.pbir`, etc. — which VS Code can edit and Git can diff. A `.pbix` is an opaque binary; a diff of it is meaningless.\n- *\"Uses Power BI Desktop to publish to Workspace1\"* → PBIP is saved and published from Desktop as normal.\n\nThe two work together: PBIP produces the text files, Git integration versions them. Neither alone satisfies all four bullets — which is why this is a two-answer question.\n\n**Why not B or C:**\n- **B Save as a PBIT file** ✘ — the 14% answer. A `.pbit` **template** is still a single binary file; it merely strips the data. It gives you no text definitions for VS Code and no meaningful diffs. PBIT is for redistributing a model shell to other authors, not for source control.\n- **C Enable users to edit data models in the Power BI service** ✘ — browser-based model editing. It is the *opposite* of the stated workflow (which is explicitly Desktop + VS Code) and contributes nothing to versioning or diffing.\n\n*Recognition rule:* **\"edit definition files in VS Code / text-based / diffable\" ⇒ PBIP. \"version history, revert, compare versions\" ⇒ Git integration.**"
    },
