    "id": "T1Q182",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that is assigned to a newly created Fabric capacity named Capacity1.\nYou create a semantic model named Model1 and deploy Model1 to Workspace1.\nYou need to publish changes to Model1 directly from Tabular Editor.\nWhat should you do?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "For Workspace1, enable Git integration."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "For Model1, enable external sharing."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "For Workspace1, create a managed private endpoint."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "For Capacity1, set XMLA Endpoint to Read Write."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q182",
    "sourceId": "T1Q182",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**D — For Capacity1, set XMLA Endpoint to Read Write.** External tools talk to a semantic model over the **XMLA endpoint**, and Microsoft spells out the requirement for this exact tool: *\"**Tabular Editor** — … Requires XMLA **read-only** for query operations. Requires **read-write** for metadata operations.\"* Publishing changes *is* a metadata write, so read-only is not enough.\n\nThe detail that makes the question worth asking: on a **newly created capacity the XMLA endpoint defaults to `Read Only`**, and the setting lives at the **capacity** level (Admin portal → Capacity settings → Power BI workloads → Semantic models → XMLA Endpoint), **not** on the workspace or the model. The stem plants both clues — \"newly created Fabric capacity\" and \"directly from Tabular Editor\".\n\n- **A Enable Git integration** ✘ — a deployment/source-control path (commit the change, sync the workspace). It is a perfectly good way to get changes into a workspace, but it is not \"**directly** from Tabular Editor\", and Tabular Editor still cannot connect without the endpoint. This is the 11% answer.\n- **B Enable external sharing for Model1** ✘ — governs sharing content with users **outside your organisation** (B2B). Nothing to do with tool connectivity.\n- **C Create a managed private endpoint** ✘ — lets Fabric reach **into** a network-isolated *data source*. It is inbound-to-the-source networking, the opposite direction from a client tool connecting to the model.\n\n*Same fact family as T1Q180:* XMLA is the single door used by Tabular Editor, SSMS, DAX Studio, ALM Toolkit, SQL Server Profiler and Analysis Services deployment. **Read anything ⇒ Read Only suffices. Write anything ⇒ Read Write.**",
    "answer": "D",
    "officialAnswer": "D",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "D",
      "answer": "D",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that is assigned to a newly created Fabric capacity named Capacity1.\nYou create a semantic model named Model1 and deploy Model1 to Workspace1.\nYou need to publish changes to Model1 directly from Tabular Editor.\nWhat should you do?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "For Workspace1, enable Git integration.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "For Model1, enable external sharing.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "For Workspace1, create a managed private endpoint.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "For Capacity1, set XMLA Endpoint to Read Write.",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**D — For Capacity1, set XMLA Endpoint to Read Write.** External tools talk to a semantic model over the **XMLA endpoint**, and Microsoft spells out the requirement for this exact tool: *\"**Tabular Editor** — … Requires XMLA **read-only** for query operations. Requires **read-write** for metadata operations.\"* Publishing changes *is* a metadata write, so read-only is not enough.\n\nThe detail that makes the question worth asking: on a **newly created capacity the XMLA endpoint defaults to `Read Only`**, and the setting lives at the **capacity** level (Admin portal → Capacity settings → Power BI workloads → Semantic models → XMLA Endpoint), **not** on the workspace or the model. The stem plants both clues — \"newly created Fabric capacity\" and \"directly from Tabular Editor\".\n\n- **A Enable Git integration** ✘ — a deployment/source-control path (commit the change, sync the workspace). It is a perfectly good way to get changes into a workspace, but it is not \"**directly** from Tabular Editor\", and Tabular Editor still cannot connect without the endpoint. This is the 11% answer.\n- **B Enable external sharing for Model1** ✘ — governs sharing content with users **outside your organisation** (B2B). Nothing to do with tool connectivity.\n- **C Create a managed private endpoint** ✘ — lets Fabric reach **into** a network-isolated *data source*. It is inbound-to-the-source networking, the opposite direction from a client tool connecting to the model.\n\n*Same fact family as T1Q180:* XMLA is the single door used by Tabular Editor, SSMS, DAX Studio, ALM Toolkit, SQL Server Profiler and Analysis Services deployment. **Read anything ⇒ Read Only suffices. Write anything ⇒ Read Write.**"
    },
