    "id": "T1Q183",
    "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
    "stem": "You are creating a report and a semantic model in Microsoft Power BI Desktop.\nThe Value measure has the expression shown in the following exhibit.\n\nUse the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "blanks": [
      {
        "label": "A [answer choice] was added to the Value measure.",
        "options": [
          "calculation group",
          "data category",
          "dynamic format string",
          "synonym"
        ],
        "answer": "dynamic format string"
      },
      {
        "label": "The Value measure can return values formatted as [answer choice].",
        "options": [
          "percentages only",
          "percentages or decimals",
          "whole numbers only",
          "percentages or whole numbers"
        ],
        "answer": "percentages or whole numbers"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "table",
        "title": "Power BI measure formatting panel",
        "headers": [
          "Control",
          "Displayed value"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Measure name",
            "Value"
          ],
          [
            "Home table",
            "Fact"
          ],
          [
            "Format",
            "Dynamic"
          ],
          [
            "Data category",
            "Uncategorized"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "Original dynamic format string expression",
        "language": "dax",
        "content": "SWITCH(\n    SELECTEDVALUE(Metric[Metric]),\n    \"# of Customers\", \"#\",\n    \"Gross Margin %\", \"0.00%\",\n    \"#,###\"\n)"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q183",
    "sourceId": "T1Q183",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**A dynamic format string was added**, and the measure can return **percentages or whole numbers**.\n\n### Blank 1 — read the ribbon, not the DAX\n\nThe exhibit shows the measure ribbon with **Format** set to **`Dynamic`**, and the formula bar switched to the **Format** dropdown (left of the expression) rather than the measure expression. That combination is the definition of a **dynamic format string**: a second DAX expression attached to the measure that returns the *format string* to apply, evaluated in the current filter context.\n\nThe expression being edited is the format string itself:\n\n```dax\nSWITCH(SELECTEDVALUE(Metric[Metric]),\n    \"# of Customers\", \"#\",\n    \"Gross Margin %\", \"0.00%\",\n    \"#,###\")\n```\n\n- **calculation group** ✘ — the plausible distractor, and the choice the highly-voted comment argues for. Dynamic format strings *can* be defined on a calculation item, but the exhibit shows the **Format → Dynamic** property of a single measure named `Value` on home table `Fact`, with no calculation group in sight. The question also asks what was added **to the Value measure** — a calculation group is a separate model object, not something added to a measure. Since Power BI Desktop shipped dynamic format strings **for measures** (May 2023 preview, GA later), this is exactly what that UI is.\n- **data category** ✘ — tags a column as Address / Web URL / Image URL etc. for maps and visuals. Note the exhibit shows Data category = **Uncategorized**, so this one is visibly ruled out on screen.\n- **synonym** ✘ — an alternate name for Q&A natural-language queries. No formatting role at all.\n\n### Blank 2 — enumerate what the SWITCH can return\n\nTake each branch of the format string literally:\n\n| Selected metric | Format string returned | Renders as |\n|---|---|---|\n| `# of Customers` | `\"#\"` | **whole number**, no thousands separator → `85` |\n| `Gross Margin %` | `\"0.00%\"` | **percentage** → `12.34%` |\n| anything else (Gross Margin, Total COGS, Total Revenue) | `\"#,###\"` | **whole number** with thousands separator → `1,234` |\n\nSo across all branches the measure yields **percentages or whole numbers** — and never a decimal, because no branch's format string contains a decimal place except the `%` one. That kills **\"percentages or decimals\"**: `0.00%` displays two decimals *of a percentage*, which is still a percentage, not a decimal number. **\"Percentages only\"** and **\"whole numbers only\"** each ignore two of the three branches.\n\n*The technique for this whole question type:* read the `SWITCH` branch by branch and classify each returned **format string**, not each underlying value. `#` and `#,###` are integer masks; `0.00%` is a percentage mask; `0.00` would have been a decimal mask and is absent.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "A [answer choice] was added to the Value measure.",
          "options": [
            "calculation group",
            "data category",
            "dynamic format string",
            "synonym"
          ],
          "answer": "dynamic format string"
        },
        {
          "label": "The Value measure can return values formatted as [answer choice].",
          "options": [
            "percentages only",
            "percentages or decimals",
            "whole numbers only",
            "percentages or whole numbers"
          ],
          "answer": "percentages or whole numbers"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You are creating a report and a semantic model in Microsoft Power BI Desktop.\nThe Value measure has the expression shown in the following exhibit.\n\nUse the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**A dynamic format string was added**, and the measure can return **percentages or whole numbers**.\n\n### Blank 1 — read the ribbon, not the DAX\n\nThe exhibit shows the measure ribbon with **Format** set to **`Dynamic`**, and the formula bar switched to the **Format** dropdown (left of the expression) rather than the measure expression. That combination is the definition of a **dynamic format string**: a second DAX expression attached to the measure that returns the *format string* to apply, evaluated in the current filter context.\n\nThe expression being edited is the format string itself:\n\n```dax\nSWITCH(SELECTEDVALUE(Metric[Metric]),\n    \"# of Customers\", \"#\",\n    \"Gross Margin %\", \"0.00%\",\n    \"#,###\")\n```\n\n- **calculation group** ✘ — the plausible distractor, and the choice the highly-voted comment argues for. Dynamic format strings *can* be defined on a calculation item, but the exhibit shows the **Format → Dynamic** property of a single measure named `Value` on home table `Fact`, with no calculation group in sight. The question also asks what was added **to the Value measure** — a calculation group is a separate model object, not something added to a measure. Since Power BI Desktop shipped dynamic format strings **for measures** (May 2023 preview, GA later), this is exactly what that UI is.\n- **data category** ✘ — tags a column as Address / Web URL / Image URL etc. for maps and visuals. Note the exhibit shows Data category = **Uncategorized**, so this one is visibly ruled out on screen.\n- **synonym** ✘ — an alternate name for Q&A natural-language queries. No formatting role at all.\n\n### Blank 2 — enumerate what the SWITCH can return\n\nTake each branch of the format string literally:\n\n| Selected metric | Format string returned | Renders as |\n|---|---|---|\n| `# of Customers` | `\"#\"` | **whole number**, no thousands separator → `85` |\n| `Gross Margin %` | `\"0.00%\"` | **percentage** → `12.34%` |\n| anything else (Gross Margin, Total COGS, Total Revenue) | `\"#,###\"` | **whole number** with thousands separator → `1,234` |\n\nSo across all branches the measure yields **percentages or whole numbers** — and never a decimal, because no branch's format string contains a decimal place except the `%` one. That kills **\"percentages or decimals\"**: `0.00%` displays two decimals *of a percentage*, which is still a percentage, not a decimal number. **\"Percentages only\"** and **\"whole numbers only\"** each ignore two of the three branches.\n\n*The technique for this whole question type:* read the `SWITCH` branch by branch and classify each returned **format string**, not each underlying value. `#` and `#,###` are integer masks; `0.00%` is a percentage mask; `0.00` would have been a decimal mask and is absent."
    },
