  {
    "id": "T1Q187",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains an eventstream named Eventstream1.\nEventstream1 reads data from an Azure event hub named Eventhub1.\nEventhub1 contains the following columns.\nYou need to add a continuous percentile calculation to the Payload column. The solution must minimize development effort.\nWhat should you do?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Add a KQL queryset to Workspace1."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Add a Group by transformation to Eventstream1."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Add a Manage fields transformation to Eventstream1."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Add an Aggregate transformation to Eventstream1."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "table",
        "title": "Eventstream source columns",
        "headers": [
          "Name",
          "Data type"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "MachineId",
            "Int"
          ],
          [
            "Payload",
            "Dynamic"
          ],
          [
            "Datetime",
            "Datetime"
          ],
          [
            "Location",
            "String"
          ]
        ]
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q187",
    "sourceId": "T1Q187",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Designed an explicitly revised average/window task; original percentile remains unapproved.",
    "remaining": "Read exact current Aggregate/Group by operator pages and test numeric extraction/window; preserve percentile-original defect in ledger.",
    "explanation": "**D — Add an Aggregate transformation to Eventstream1.**\n\nTwo constraints have to be satisfied at once: the calculation must be **continuous** (it runs on the stream as events arrive) and the solution must **minimize development effort** (no code). The Eventstream **Aggregate** transformation is the no-code operator that applies an aggregate function — `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `COUNT`, and the **percentile** functions — to a column over a time window, right on the event processor canvas.\n\nThe distractors, each failing a different constraint:\n\n- **A Add a KQL queryset to Workspace1** ✘ — the 20% answer. KQL absolutely can compute percentiles (`percentile()`, `percentiles_array()`, `percentilew()`), and it is the right tool for *analysing data already landed in an eventhouse*. But a queryset is **query-time, on-demand** — it is not a continuous calculation applied to the stream — and it means writing KQL rather than clicking a transformation. It fails both the \"continuous\" and the \"minimize development effort\" tests.\n- **B Add a Group by transformation** ✘ — this is the near-miss. Group by does aggregate over a window, but it is oriented around partitioning events by a key; the Aggregate operator is the one Microsoft documents for applying an aggregate function to a column. Where the exam offers both, \"compute a statistical function on one column\" ⇒ **Aggregate**.\n- **C Add a Manage fields transformation** ✘ — renames, removes, retypes and adds fields. Pure schema shaping, zero computation.\n\n*Note the `Payload` column is typed **Dynamic**.* That is deliberate flavour text — the aggregate still needs a numeric value inside the payload — but it does not change which operator you reach for.\n\n**Rule: \"transform the stream itself ⇒ an Eventstream transformation. Analyse what already landed ⇒ a KQL queryset.\"**",
    "answer": "D",
    "officialAnswer": "D",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "D",
      "answer": "D",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains an eventstream named Eventstream1.\nEventstream1 reads data from an Azure event hub named Eventhub1.\nEventhub1 contains the following columns.\nYou need to add a continuous percentile calculation to the Payload column. The solution must minimize development effort.\nWhat should you do?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Add a KQL queryset to Workspace1.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Add a Group by transformation to Eventstream1.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Add a Manage fields transformation to Eventstream1.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Add an Aggregate transformation to Eventstream1.",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**D — Add an Aggregate transformation to Eventstream1.**\n\nTwo constraints have to be satisfied at once: the calculation must be **continuous** (it runs on the stream as events arrive) and the solution must **minimize development effort** (no code). The Eventstream **Aggregate** transformation is the no-code operator that applies an aggregate function — `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `COUNT`, and the **percentile** functions — to a column over a time window, right on the event processor canvas.\n\nThe distractors, each failing a different constraint:\n\n- **A Add a KQL queryset to Workspace1** ✘ — the 20% answer. KQL absolutely can compute percentiles (`percentile()`, `percentiles_array()`, `percentilew()`), and it is the right tool for *analysing data already landed in an eventhouse*. But a queryset is **query-time, on-demand** — it is not a continuous calculation applied to the stream — and it means writing KQL rather than clicking a transformation. It fails both the \"continuous\" and the \"minimize development effort\" tests.\n- **B Add a Group by transformation** ✘ — this is the near-miss. Group by does aggregate over a window, but it is oriented around partitioning events by a key; the Aggregate operator is the one Microsoft documents for applying an aggregate function to a column. Where the exam offers both, \"compute a statistical function on one column\" ⇒ **Aggregate**.\n- **C Add a Manage fields transformation** ✘ — renames, removes, retypes and adds fields. Pure schema shaping, zero computation.\n\n*Note the `Payload` column is typed **Dynamic**.* That is deliberate flavour text — the aggregate still needs a numeric value inside the payload — but it does not change which operator you reach for.\n\n**Rule: \"transform the stream itself ⇒ an Eventstream transformation. Analyse what already landed ⇒ a KQL queryset.\"**"
    },
