  {
    "id": "T1Q189",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Microsoft Power BI semantic model.\nYou need to identify any surrogate key columns in the model that have the Summarize By property set to a value other than to None. The solution\nmust minimize effort.\nWhat should you use?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Performance Analyzer in Power BI Desktop"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Model explorer in Microsoft Power BI Desktop"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Model view in Microsoft Power BI Desktop"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "DAX Formatter in DAX Studio"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q189",
    "sourceId": "T1Q189",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**C — Model view in Power BI Desktop.** `Summarize By` is a **column property**, and the Properties pane of **Model view** is where column properties are inspected and edited. Select a column on the diagram (or several at once with Ctrl-click) and the pane on the right shows **Advanced → Summarize by**, so you can both spot the offenders and set them to `None` without leaving the view. That multi-select is what satisfies *\"minimize effort\"*.\n\n**Why not B — Model explorer** (the 33% answer, and the closest call): Model explorer is a **navigation tree** of semantic model objects (tables, measures, roles, calculation groups, relationships) that lives *inside* Model view. It helps you *find* objects in a large model; it does not itself display the `Summarize by` setting. If you use it, you are still reading the answer out of Model view's Properties pane — so Model view is both the necessary and the sufficient answer.\n\n- **A Performance Analyzer** ✘ — records visual render and query durations to diagnose slow reports. It reports no metadata.\n- **D DAX Formatter in DAX Studio** ✘ — pretty-prints DAX expressions. It does not read column properties, and `Summarize By` is not DAX.\n\n### Why this matters (the modelling point behind the question)\n\nA **surrogate key** is a meaningless integer whose only job is to join tables. Leaving `Summarize By` at its default (`Sum`, which Power BI applies automatically to numeric columns) means the column carries the **Σ** icon and a user can drag `CustomerKey` into a visual and get a nonsensical total of ID numbers. Setting `Summarize by = None` on every key column is standard model hygiene — alongside hiding the key columns entirely.\n\n*Real-world footnote:* the genuinely lowest-effort way to audit this across a whole model is **Tabular Editor's Best Practice Analyzer**, which ships with a rule for exactly this. It is not among the options, and a commenter raises it for the same reason.",
    "answer": "C",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "You have a Microsoft Power BI semantic model.\nYou need to identify any surrogate key columns in the model that have the Summarize By property set to a value other than to None. The solution\nmust minimize effort.\nWhat should you use?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Performance Analyzer in Power BI Desktop",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Model explorer in Microsoft Power BI Desktop",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Model view in Microsoft Power BI Desktop",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "DAX Formatter in DAX Studio",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**C — Model view in Power BI Desktop.** `Summarize By` is a **column property**, and the Properties pane of **Model view** is where column properties are inspected and edited. Select a column on the diagram (or several at once with Ctrl-click) and the pane on the right shows **Advanced → Summarize by**, so you can both spot the offenders and set them to `None` without leaving the view. That multi-select is what satisfies *\"minimize effort\"*.\n\n**Why not B — Model explorer** (the 33% answer, and the closest call): Model explorer is a **navigation tree** of semantic model objects (tables, measures, roles, calculation groups, relationships) that lives *inside* Model view. It helps you *find* objects in a large model; it does not itself display the `Summarize by` setting. If you use it, you are still reading the answer out of Model view's Properties pane — so Model view is both the necessary and the sufficient answer.\n\n- **A Performance Analyzer** ✘ — records visual render and query durations to diagnose slow reports. It reports no metadata.\n- **D DAX Formatter in DAX Studio** ✘ — pretty-prints DAX expressions. It does not read column properties, and `Summarize By` is not DAX.\n\n### Why this matters (the modelling point behind the question)\n\nA **surrogate key** is a meaningless integer whose only job is to join tables. Leaving `Summarize By` at its default (`Sum`, which Power BI applies automatically to numeric columns) means the column carries the **Σ** icon and a user can drag `CustomerKey` into a visual and get a nonsensical total of ID numbers. Setting `Summarize by = None` on every key column is standard model hygiene — alongside hiding the key columns entirely.\n\n*Real-world footnote:* the genuinely lowest-effort way to audit this across a whole model is **Tabular Editor's Best Practice Analyzer**, which ships with a rule for exactly this. It is not among the options, and a commenter raises it for the same reason."
    },
