  {
    "id": "T1Q190",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse.\nYou use a dataflow to load a new dataset from OneLake to the warehouse.\nYou need to add a Power Query step to identify the maximum values for the numeric columns.\nWhich function should you include in the step?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Table.Range"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Table.MaxN"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Table.Profile"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q190",
    "sourceId": "T1Q190",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**C — `Table.Profile`.** It *\"returns a profile for the columns in a table\"* — one output row per column, with the statistics that data profiling needs:\n\n| Profile column | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `Min`, **`Max`** | smallest / **largest** value |\n| `Average`, `StandardDeviation` | for numeric columns |\n| `Count`, `NullCount`, `DistinctCount` | row and cardinality counts |\n\nSo a single `Table.Profile(Source)` step surfaces the maximum for **every** numeric column at once — exactly the request.\n\n- **A `Table.Range`** ✘ — `Table.Range(table, offset, count)` returns a **slice of rows** starting at an offset, the Power Query equivalent of a skip/take. \"Range\" here means a range of *rows*, not a min–max range — that is the whole trick of this distractor.\n- **B `Table.MaxN`** ✘ — tempting because of the name, but it returns the **N rows** with the largest values according to a comparison criterion (`Table.MaxN(table, \"Sales\", 5)` → the top five rows). It gives you rows, for one specified column, not the maximum value of each numeric column. (`List.Max` would give a single column's maximum — also not offered.)\n\nIn the Power Query UI the same feature is the **Column profile / Column statistics** pane under the **View** ribbon; `Table.Profile` is what it calls under the covers.",
    "answer": "C",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse.\nYou use a dataflow to load a new dataset from OneLake to the warehouse.\nYou need to add a Power Query step to identify the maximum values for the numeric columns.\nWhich function should you include in the step?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Table.Range",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Table.MaxN",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Table.Profile",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**C — `Table.Profile`.** It *\"returns a profile for the columns in a table\"* — one output row per column, with the statistics that data profiling needs:\n\n| Profile column | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `Min`, **`Max`** | smallest / **largest** value |\n| `Average`, `StandardDeviation` | for numeric columns |\n| `Count`, `NullCount`, `DistinctCount` | row and cardinality counts |\n\nSo a single `Table.Profile(Source)` step surfaces the maximum for **every** numeric column at once — exactly the request.\n\n- **A `Table.Range`** ✘ — `Table.Range(table, offset, count)` returns a **slice of rows** starting at an offset, the Power Query equivalent of a skip/take. \"Range\" here means a range of *rows*, not a min–max range — that is the whole trick of this distractor.\n- **B `Table.MaxN`** ✘ — tempting because of the name, but it returns the **N rows** with the largest values according to a comparison criterion (`Table.MaxN(table, \"Sales\", 5)` → the top five rows). It gives you rows, for one specified column, not the maximum value of each numeric column. (`List.Max` would give a single column's maximum — also not offered.)\n\nIn the Power Query UI the same feature is the **Column profile / Column statistics** pane under the **View** ribbon; `Table.Profile` is what it calls under the covers."
    },
