  {
    "id": "T1Q191",
    "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric warehouse named Warehouse1 that contains a table named TaxiTrips.\nTaxiTrips contains the following columns.\nYou need to create a query that shows the top three taxi companies based on the total miles traveled.\nHow should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "blanks": [
      {
        "label": "SELECT [answer choice] company AS TaxiCompany, …",
        "options": [
          "FETCH NEXT 3 ROWS ONLY",
          "Limit (3)",
          "Top (3)"
        ],
        "answer": "Top (3)"
      },
      {
        "label": "FROM [Warehouse1].[dbo].[TaxiTrips] [answer choice]",
        "options": [
          "GROUP BY company",
          "GROUP BY TaxiCompany",
          "ORDER BY tripDistance"
        ],
        "answer": "GROUP BY company"
      },
      {
        "label": "[answer choice] DESC;",
        "options": [
          "GROUP BY company",
          "ORDER BY sum(tripDistance)",
          "ORDER BY tripDistance"
        ],
        "answer": "ORDER BY sum(tripDistance)"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "table",
        "title": "Warehouse1.dbo.TaxiTrips schema",
        "headers": [
          "Column",
          "Data type",
          "Description"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Company",
            "Varchar(100)",
            "Taxi company name"
          ],
          [
            "passengerCount",
            "Integer",
            "Passengers per trip"
          ],
          [
            "tripDistance",
            "Decimal(10,4)",
            "Miles traveled per trip"
          ],
          [
            "pickupDate",
            "Datetime2",
            "Pickup date and time"
          ],
          [
            "FareAmount",
            "Decimal(10,4)",
            "Cost of each trip"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "Original SQL top-three taxi-company query",
        "language": "sql",
        "content": "SELECT [Dropdown 1] company AS TaxiCompany,\n       SUM(passengerCount) AS TotalPassengers,\n       SUM(tripDistance) AS TotalDistance\nFROM [Warehouse1].[dbo].[TaxiTrips]\n[Dropdown 2]\n[Dropdown 3] DESC;"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q191",
    "sourceId": "T1Q191",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**`TOP (3)`**, **`GROUP BY company`**, **`ORDER BY sum(tripDistance)`**. The finished statement:\n\n```sql\nSELECT TOP (3)\n    company AS TaxiCompany,\n    SUM(passengerCount) AS TotalPassengers,\n    SUM(tripDistance) AS TotalDistance\nFROM [Warehouse1].[dbo].[TaxiTrips]\nGROUP BY company\nORDER BY SUM(tripDistance) DESC;\n```\n\n### Blank 1 — `TOP (3)`\n\nPosition decides it: the blank sits **immediately after `SELECT`**, and `TOP (n)` is the only one of the three that belongs there.\n\n- `FETCH NEXT 3 ROWS ONLY` ✘ — valid T-SQL, but it goes at the **end** of the statement and requires a preceding `OFFSET … ROWS` clause (`ORDER BY … OFFSET 0 ROWS FETCH NEXT 3 ROWS ONLY`). It cannot appear after `SELECT`.\n- `LIMIT (3)` ✘ — MySQL/PostgreSQL/SQLite syntax. **T-SQL has no `LIMIT`.** (Same distractor as T1Q177 blank 2.)\n\n### Blank 2 — `GROUP BY company`\n\nTwo aggregates in the `SELECT` list (`SUM(passengerCount)`, `SUM(tripDistance)`) alongside a non-aggregated column force a `GROUP BY` on that column.\n\n- `GROUP BY TaxiCompany` ✘ — **the alias trap.** `TaxiCompany` is a column alias defined in the `SELECT` list, and `GROUP BY` is evaluated **before** `SELECT`, so the alias does not exist yet. You must group by the underlying column, `company`. (`ORDER BY` is the one clause that *can* see `SELECT` aliases, because it runs last.)\n- `ORDER BY tripDistance` ✘ — wrong clause entirely for this slot, and the query needs a `GROUP BY`.\n\n### Blank 3 — `ORDER BY SUM(tripDistance)` DESC\n\n*\"Top three taxi companies based on **the total miles travelled**\"* — the ranking is on the **total**, so the sort key must be the aggregate.\n\n- `ORDER BY tripDistance` ✘ — the tempting near-miss. After a `GROUP BY company`, the bare column `tripDistance` is not available (it is neither grouped nor aggregated), so this is an error — and even conceptually it would rank on a single trip rather than the company total.\n- `GROUP BY company` ✘ — already used, and a statement has one `GROUP BY`.\n\n**`TOP` is only meaningful with `ORDER BY`.** Together they are the T-SQL idiom for \"top N by X\": `SELECT TOP (N) … GROUP BY … ORDER BY AGG(X) DESC`. Without the `ORDER BY`, `TOP (3)` returns an arbitrary three rows.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "SELECT [answer choice] company AS TaxiCompany, …",
          "options": [
            "FETCH NEXT 3 ROWS ONLY",
            "Limit (3)",
            "Top (3)"
          ],
          "answer": "Top (3)"
        },
        {
          "label": "FROM [Warehouse1].[dbo].[TaxiTrips] [answer choice]",
          "options": [
            "GROUP BY company",
            "GROUP BY TaxiCompany",
            "ORDER BY tripDistance"
          ],
          "answer": "GROUP BY company"
        },
        {
          "label": "[answer choice] DESC;",
          "options": [
            "GROUP BY company",
            "ORDER BY sum(tripDistance)",
            "ORDER BY tripDistance"
          ],
          "answer": "ORDER BY sum(tripDistance)"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric warehouse named Warehouse1 that contains a table named TaxiTrips.\nTaxiTrips contains the following columns.\nYou need to create a query that shows the top three taxi companies based on the total miles traveled.\nHow should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**`TOP (3)`**, **`GROUP BY company`**, **`ORDER BY sum(tripDistance)`**. The finished statement:\n\n```sql\nSELECT TOP (3)\n    company AS TaxiCompany,\n    SUM(passengerCount) AS TotalPassengers,\n    SUM(tripDistance) AS TotalDistance\nFROM [Warehouse1].[dbo].[TaxiTrips]\nGROUP BY company\nORDER BY SUM(tripDistance) DESC;\n```\n\n### Blank 1 — `TOP (3)`\n\nPosition decides it: the blank sits **immediately after `SELECT`**, and `TOP (n)` is the only one of the three that belongs there.\n\n- `FETCH NEXT 3 ROWS ONLY` ✘ — valid T-SQL, but it goes at the **end** of the statement and requires a preceding `OFFSET … ROWS` clause (`ORDER BY … OFFSET 0 ROWS FETCH NEXT 3 ROWS ONLY`). It cannot appear after `SELECT`.\n- `LIMIT (3)` ✘ — MySQL/PostgreSQL/SQLite syntax. **T-SQL has no `LIMIT`.** (Same distractor as T1Q177 blank 2.)\n\n### Blank 2 — `GROUP BY company`\n\nTwo aggregates in the `SELECT` list (`SUM(passengerCount)`, `SUM(tripDistance)`) alongside a non-aggregated column force a `GROUP BY` on that column.\n\n- `GROUP BY TaxiCompany` ✘ — **the alias trap.** `TaxiCompany` is a column alias defined in the `SELECT` list, and `GROUP BY` is evaluated **before** `SELECT`, so the alias does not exist yet. You must group by the underlying column, `company`. (`ORDER BY` is the one clause that *can* see `SELECT` aliases, because it runs last.)\n- `ORDER BY tripDistance` ✘ — wrong clause entirely for this slot, and the query needs a `GROUP BY`.\n\n### Blank 3 — `ORDER BY SUM(tripDistance)` DESC\n\n*\"Top three taxi companies based on **the total miles travelled**\"* — the ranking is on the **total**, so the sort key must be the aggregate.\n\n- `ORDER BY tripDistance` ✘ — the tempting near-miss. After a `GROUP BY company`, the bare column `tripDistance` is not available (it is neither grouped nor aggregated), so this is an error — and even conceptually it would rank on a single trip rather than the company total.\n- `GROUP BY company` ✘ — already used, and a statement has one `GROUP BY`.\n\n**`TOP` is only meaningful with `ORDER BY`.** Together they are the T-SQL idiom for \"top N by X\": `SELECT TOP (N) … GROUP BY … ORDER BY AGG(X) DESC`. Without the `ORDER BY`, `TOP (3)` returns an arbitrary three rows."
    },
