  {
    "id": "T1Q193",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1 and a user named User1. Workspace1 contains a warehouse named DW1.\nYou share DW1 with User1 and select no additional permissions. User1 has no workspace role, no prior object grants and no other inherited access.\nWhat can User1 do?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Read data from the tables in DW1."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Build reports on an existing semantic model without a separate model permission grant."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Read the underlying Parquet files from OneLake."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Connect to DW1 via the TDS (Tabular Data Stream) endpoint."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q193",
    "sourceId": "T1Q193",
    "gradingEnabled": true,
    "scientificStatus": "APPROVED_CORRECTED",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Read without additional sharing permissions is CONNECT, not ReadData.",
    "remaining": "",
    "explanation": "D. The base share grants Read/CONNECT to the Warehouse TDS endpoint. It does not grant SELECT on tables (A), Build on a semantic model (B), or ReadAll on OneLake files (C). Additional SQL grants can later authorize specific objects.",
    "answer": "D",
    "officialAnswer": "A",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "A",
      "answer": "D",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1 and a user named User1. Workspace1 contains a warehouse named DW1.\nYou share DW1 with User1 and assign User1 the default permissions for DW1.\nWhat can User1 do?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Read data from the tables in DW1.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Build reports by using the default dataset.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Read the underlying Parquet files from OneLake.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Connect to DW1 via the TDS (Tabular Data Stream) endpoint.",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**D — Connect to DW1 via the TDS (Tabular Data Stream) endpoint.**\n\n> ⚠️ **Flagged: this overrides the PDF answer key,** which marks **A** (*read data from the tables*). Microsoft's documentation states the opposite in as many words. The discussion is split (A 60% / D 30%), but the 30% are quoting the docs.\n\nThe decisive sentence, from *Share your warehouse and manage permissions*:\n\n> **\"If no additional permissions are selected** — The shared recipient by default receives **\"Read\"** permission, which **only allows the recipient to *connect* to the SQL analytics endpoint**, the equivalent of `CONNECT` permissions in SQL Server. **The shared recipient won't be able to query any table or view** or execute any function or stored procedure unless they're provided access to objects within the Warehouse using T-SQL `GRANT`.\"\n\nSo the default grant is *connect and nothing more* — and the Fabric warehouse's SQL connection string **is** the TDS endpoint (TDS is the wire protocol every SQL client, from SSMS to Power BI, speaks to it). Option D is a restatement of the default permission; option A describes what the docs explicitly say you **cannot** do.\n\n### The sharing dialog, in full\n\n| What you tick | Permission | What the recipient gains |\n|---|---|---|\n| *(nothing — always granted)* | **Read** | **connect to the SQL/TDS endpoint** — `CONNECT` only |\n| \"Read all data using SQL\" | **ReadData** | `SELECT` on all tables and views — ≈ `db_datareader` |\n| \"Read all data using Apache Spark\" | **ReadAll** | read the underlying Parquet/OneLake files via ABFS |\n| \"Build reports on the default semantic model\" | **Build** | build reports on the default semantic model |\n\nMap the options straight onto that table:\n\n- **A Read data from the tables** = **ReadData** — an *optional* checkbox, not the default. ✘\n- **B Build reports using the default dataset** = **Build** — also optional. ✘\n- **C Read the underlying Parquet files from OneLake** = **ReadAll** — also optional. ✘\n- **D Connect via the TDS endpoint** = **Read** — the default. ✔\n\nNote the doc's own tip that **ReadData** and **ReadAll** *\"are separate permissions that do not overlap\"* — Spark access does not imply SQL access, or vice versa.\n\n**Why the question is written this way:** the \"share with no extra permissions, then `GRANT` object by object\" pattern is Microsoft's documented recipe for granular access. The default Read permission exists precisely so that an admin can open the door to the endpoint and then hand out table-level rights individually.\n\n*(If your exam marks A, the examiner is treating \"default permissions\" loosely as \"the Viewer-style read access\". Know the distinction anyway — Read vs ReadData is a recurring DP-600 security theme.)*"
    },
    "sourceVisualReferences": {
      "exhibits": [],
      "answerImages": [],
      "optionImages": []
    },
    "visualResolution": "no_exhibit_needed",
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Microsoft documentation",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/share-warehouse-manage-permissions"
      },
      {
        "title": "Microsoft documentation",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/connectivity"
      }
    ],
    "reviewKind": "documentation-approved derived practice"
  },
  {
    "id": "T1Q194",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains two workspaces named Workspace1 and Workspace2.\nWorkspace1 is used as the development environment. Workspace2 is used as the production environment. Each environment uses a different\nstorage account.\nWorkspace1 contains a Dataflow Gen2 named Dataflow1. The data source of Dataflow1 is a CSV file in blob storage.\nYou plan to implement a deployment pipeline to deploy items from Workspace1 to Workspace2.\nYou need to ensure that the data source references the correct location in the production environment.\nWhat should you do?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Create a data source rule only."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Create a parameter rule only."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Create a data source rule and a parameter rule."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "After implementing the deployment pipeline, manually change the data source"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q194",
    "sourceId": "T1Q194",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "BLOCKED_SOURCE",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Blocked unsupported Gen1-to-Gen2 deployment-rule inference.",
    "remaining": "Verify current Gen2 CI/CD and public parameter/connection workflows; author and test new complete alternatives.",
    "explanation": "**C — create a data source rule *and* a parameter rule.** Deployment pipelines offer exactly three rule types, and the two relevant ones solve two different halves of a dev→prod data source switch:\n\n| Rule type | What it overrides in the target stage |\n|---|---|\n| **Data source rule** | the **connection** — swap the dev server/account for the prod one. *\"You can only change to a data source from the same type.\"* |\n| **Parameter rule** | the **value of a Power Query parameter** — e.g. the container, folder path, or file name inside that account |\n| Default lakehouse rule | which lakehouse a **notebook** defaults to (not relevant here) |\n\nThe scenario gives you both halves: *\"each environment uses a different **storage account**\"* (the connection) and the dataflow points at a *\"**CSV file** in blob storage\"* (a path within it). The data source rule redirects the account; the parameter rule redirects the path. Together they make the deployed Dataflow1 read the production file without anyone touching it.\n\n- **A data source rule only** ✘ (the 33% answer) — swaps the account but leaves the path pinned to whatever dev used. Defensible only if you assume the container and file name are identical in both environments, which the stem does not say.\n- **B parameter rule only** ✘ *by the exam's logic* — see the caveat below, where this is stronger than it looks.\n- **D manually change the data source after deployment** ✘ — defeats the purpose of a pipeline. Deployment rules exist so this is automatic and repeatable; a manual step has to be redone after **every** deployment and is the classic wrong answer in ALM questions.\n\n> ⚠️ **Doc drift worth knowing.** Today's documentation makes this item shakier than the key admits, and if you meet it in the wild the reasoning has moved on:\n> - The rules matrix lists data source and parameter rules for **Dataflow *Gen1***, semantic models, paginated reports and mirrored databases. **Dataflow Gen2 is not in the table at all.**\n> - Data source rules are supported only for a fixed list of source types — AAS, Azure Synapse, SSAS, Azure SQL, SQL Server, OData, Oracle, SAP HANA, SharePoint, Teradata. **Azure Blob Storage is not on it**, and Microsoft says: *\"For other data sources, we recommend using parameters to configure your data source.\"*\n> - A listed limitation: *\"Adding data source rules… on data sources which are **parametrized**\"* is **not supported** — so a data source rule and a parameter cannot both target the same source.\n>\n> Taken literally, that guidance points at **parameters** (option B) for a blob-storage-backed Dataflow Gen2. The exam item predates Dataflow Gen2 CI/CD support and tests the conceptual pairing instead. **Answer C on the exam; use parameters in production.**",
    "answer": "C",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains two workspaces named Workspace1 and Workspace2.\nWorkspace1 is used as the development environment. Workspace2 is used as the production environment. Each environment uses a different\nstorage account.\nWorkspace1 contains a Dataflow Gen2 named Dataflow1. The data source of Dataflow1 is a CSV file in blob storage.\nYou plan to implement a deployment pipeline to deploy items from Workspace1 to Workspace2.\nYou need to ensure that the data source references the correct location in the production environment.\nWhat should you do?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Create a data source rule only.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Create a parameter rule only.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Create a data source rule and a parameter rule.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "After implementing the deployment pipeline, manually change the data source",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**C — create a data source rule *and* a parameter rule.** Deployment pipelines offer exactly three rule types, and the two relevant ones solve two different halves of a dev→prod data source switch:\n\n| Rule type | What it overrides in the target stage |\n|---|---|\n| **Data source rule** | the **connection** — swap the dev server/account for the prod one. *\"You can only change to a data source from the same type.\"* |\n| **Parameter rule** | the **value of a Power Query parameter** — e.g. the container, folder path, or file name inside that account |\n| Default lakehouse rule | which lakehouse a **notebook** defaults to (not relevant here) |\n\nThe scenario gives you both halves: *\"each environment uses a different **storage account**\"* (the connection) and the dataflow points at a *\"**CSV file** in blob storage\"* (a path within it). The data source rule redirects the account; the parameter rule redirects the path. Together they make the deployed Dataflow1 read the production file without anyone touching it.\n\n- **A data source rule only** ✘ (the 33% answer) — swaps the account but leaves the path pinned to whatever dev used. Defensible only if you assume the container and file name are identical in both environments, which the stem does not say.\n- **B parameter rule only** ✘ *by the exam's logic* — see the caveat below, where this is stronger than it looks.\n- **D manually change the data source after deployment** ✘ — defeats the purpose of a pipeline. Deployment rules exist so this is automatic and repeatable; a manual step has to be redone after **every** deployment and is the classic wrong answer in ALM questions.\n\n> ⚠️ **Doc drift worth knowing.** Today's documentation makes this item shakier than the key admits, and if you meet it in the wild the reasoning has moved on:\n> - The rules matrix lists data source and parameter rules for **Dataflow *Gen1***, semantic models, paginated reports and mirrored databases. **Dataflow Gen2 is not in the table at all.**\n> - Data source rules are supported only for a fixed list of source types — AAS, Azure Synapse, SSAS, Azure SQL, SQL Server, OData, Oracle, SAP HANA, SharePoint, Teradata. **Azure Blob Storage is not on it**, and Microsoft says: *\"For other data sources, we recommend using parameters to configure your data source.\"*\n> - A listed limitation: *\"Adding data source rules… on data sources which are **parametrized**\"* is **not supported** — so a data source rule and a parameter cannot both target the same source.\n>\n> Taken literally, that guidance points at **parameters** (option B) for a blob-storage-backed Dataflow Gen2. The exam item predates Dataflow Gen2 CI/CD support and tests the conceptual pairing instead. **Answer C on the exam; use parameters in production.**"
    },
