    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**B — a bridge table with relationships to DimCustomer and DimAccount.**\n\nRead the grain first:\n\n- `FactTransaction` has `AccountKey`, `Date`, `Amount` — it is at **account** grain and has **no `CustomerKey`**.\n- *\"A customer can have one or more accounts. Each account can be associated to multiple customers.\"* — that is a **many-to-many between two dimensions**, `DimCustomer` ↔ `DimAccount` (think joint accounts).\n\nThe relationship that is missing is therefore **between the two dimensions**, not between the fact and a dimension. The standard resolution is a **bridge table** (also called a factless fact or an account-customer mapping table) holding one row per valid `CustomerKey` / `AccountKey` pair, with a one-to-many relationship from each dimension into it:\n\n```\nDimCustomer (1) ──< BridgeCustomerAccount >── (1) DimAccount (1) ──< FactTransaction\n```\n\nFilter now propagates `DimCustomer → Bridge → DimAccount → FactTransaction`, so `SUM(FactTransaction[Amount])` sliced by customer gives the total transaction amount per customer. (Cross-filtering on the `DimAccount`↔`Bridge` relationship must be set to **Both** for the filter to travel up and back down.)\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **A many-to-many relationship between FactTransaction and DimCustomer** ✘ — `FactTransaction` has no customer column to relate on, so there is nothing to build the relationship from. Power BI's native many-to-many (many-to-many cardinality) is also discouraged between a fact and a dimension: it produces ambiguous filter paths and unexpected blank-row behaviour.\n- **C a bridge table with relationships to FactTransaction and DimCustomer** ✘ — the plausible-looking one. It bridges the **wrong pair**. The many-to-many is customer↔**account**; the fact already joins cleanly to `DimAccount` on `AccountKey` and needs no bridge of its own.\n- **D add CustomerKey to FactTransaction and relate it to DimCustomer** ✘ — this *appears* to be the clean star-schema fix, and it is the one to think hardest about. It fails because a transaction on a joint account belongs to **several** customers: you would have to either pick one arbitrarily (losing the others) or duplicate the transaction row per customer (**double-counting the amount**). Neither is acceptable. Denormalising a key only works when the relationship is genuinely many-to-**one**.\n\n**The pattern to recognise: two dimensions with a many-to-many between them ⇒ bridge table between *those two dimensions*.**",
    "answer": "B",
    "officialAnswer": "B",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "B",
      "answer": "B",
      "stem": "You have a semantic model named Model1 that contains data that relates to customers and their bank account balances.\nModel1 has the following tables and columns.\nA customer can have one or more accounts. Each account can be associated to multiple customers.\nYou need to ensure that users can query Model1 to identify the total transaction amounts by customer.\nWhat should you add to Model1?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "a many-to-many relationship between FactTransaction and Dim Customer",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "a bridge table with relationships to DimCustomer and DimAccount",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "a bridge table with relationships to FactTransaction and DimCustomer",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "the CustomerKey column in FactTransaction and a relationship to DimCustomer",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**B — a bridge table with relationships to DimCustomer and DimAccount.**\n\nRead the grain first:\n\n- `FactTransaction` has `AccountKey`, `Date`, `Amount` — it is at **account** grain and has **no `CustomerKey`**.\n- *\"A customer can have one or more accounts. Each account can be associated to multiple customers.\"* — that is a **many-to-many between two dimensions**, `DimCustomer` ↔ `DimAccount` (think joint accounts).\n\nThe relationship that is missing is therefore **between the two dimensions**, not between the fact and a dimension. The standard resolution is a **bridge table** (also called a factless fact or an account-customer mapping table) holding one row per valid `CustomerKey` / `AccountKey` pair, with a one-to-many relationship from each dimension into it:\n\n```\nDimCustomer (1) ──< BridgeCustomerAccount >── (1) DimAccount (1) ──< FactTransaction\n```\n\nFilter now propagates `DimCustomer → Bridge → DimAccount → FactTransaction`, so `SUM(FactTransaction[Amount])` sliced by customer gives the total transaction amount per customer. (Cross-filtering on the `DimAccount`↔`Bridge` relationship must be set to **Both** for the filter to travel up and back down.)\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **A many-to-many relationship between FactTransaction and DimCustomer** ✘ — `FactTransaction` has no customer column to relate on, so there is nothing to build the relationship from. Power BI's native many-to-many (many-to-many cardinality) is also discouraged between a fact and a dimension: it produces ambiguous filter paths and unexpected blank-row behaviour.\n- **C a bridge table with relationships to FactTransaction and DimCustomer** ✘ — the plausible-looking one. It bridges the **wrong pair**. The many-to-many is customer↔**account**; the fact already joins cleanly to `DimAccount` on `AccountKey` and needs no bridge of its own.\n- **D add CustomerKey to FactTransaction and relate it to DimCustomer** ✘ — this *appears* to be the clean star-schema fix, and it is the one to think hardest about. It fails because a transaction on a joint account belongs to **several** customers: you would have to either pick one arbitrarily (losing the others) or duplicate the transaction row per customer (**double-counting the amount**). Neither is acceptable. Denormalising a key only works when the relationship is genuinely many-to-**one**.\n\n**The pattern to recognise: two dimensions with a many-to-many between them ⇒ bridge table between *those two dimensions*.**"
    },
