  {
    "id": "T1Q196",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains 30 CSV files in OneLake. The files are updated daily.\nYou create a Microsoft Power BI semantic model named Model1 that uses the CSV files as a data source. You configure incremental refresh for\nModel1 and publish the model to an F64 capacity in the Fabric tenant.\nWhen you initiate a refresh of Model1, the refresh fails after running out of resources.\nWhat is a possible cause of the failure?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "XMLA Endpoint is set to Read Only."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Query folding is occurring."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "The data type of the column used to partition the data has changed."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Only refresh complete days is selected."
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "Query folding is NOT occurring."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q196",
    "sourceId": "T1Q196",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**E — Query folding is NOT occurring.**\n\nIncremental refresh is built entirely on **query folding**. You define `RangeStart` / `RangeEnd` datetime parameters and filter the table on them; Power Query is supposed to translate that filter into the source's own query language so that **only the rows in the partition window ever leave the source**. When folding works, refreshing one day's partition transfers one day of data.\n\nWhen folding **fails**, the mashup engine has no choice but to pull the **entire dataset** into memory and apply the `RangeStart`/`RangeEnd` filter locally — for *every partition*. Thirty daily-updated files, refreshed partition by partition, each reading everything: the model exhausts the capacity's memory and the refresh dies with a resource error. That is precisely the symptom described.\n\n**The detail that makes this the intended answer:** the source is **CSV files**. Flat files have no query engine to fold into, so folding is impossible by construction. Microsoft is explicit that incremental refresh should target sources that fold — relational databases, SQL endpoints — and warns against file-based sources for exactly this reason. The F64 capacity is a red herring: no SKU saves you from loading 30 full files per partition.\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **B Query folding IS occurring** ✘ — the exact opposite. Folding is the thing that *prevents* this failure.\n- **C The data type of the partition column has changed** ✘ — the 40% answer, and it is a real failure mode, but the wrong one. Changing the type of the `RangeStart`/`RangeEnd` column breaks the **policy**: you get a validation or partition error (\"the filter must be on a date/datetime column\"), not *\"ran out of resources\"*. Match the symptom to the cause — this question hands you a memory-exhaustion symptom.\n- **A XMLA Endpoint is Read Only** ✘ — blocks third-party tools writing metadata. A scheduled refresh does not go through XMLA write, and it would fail with a permission error, not resource exhaustion.\n- **D Only refresh complete days is selected** ✘ — a legitimate policy option that *shrinks* the work (it skips today's partial day). If anything it reduces resource use.\n\n**Always verify folding before shipping an incremental refresh policy:** right-click the last applied step → **View Native Query**. If it is greyed out, the query is not folding and incremental refresh will quietly degrade into a full load.",
    "answer": "E",
    "officialAnswer": "E",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "E",
      "answer": "E",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains 30 CSV files in OneLake. The files are updated daily.\nYou create a Microsoft Power BI semantic model named Model1 that uses the CSV files as a data source. You configure incremental refresh for\nModel1 and publish the model to an F64 capacity in the Fabric tenant.\nWhen you initiate a refresh of Model1, the refresh fails after running out of resources.\nWhat is a possible cause of the failure?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "XMLA Endpoint is set to Read Only.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Query folding is occurring.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "The data type of the column used to partition the data has changed.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Only refresh complete days is selected.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "E",
          "text": "Query folding is NOT occurring.",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**E — Query folding is NOT occurring.**\n\nIncremental refresh is built entirely on **query folding**. You define `RangeStart` / `RangeEnd` datetime parameters and filter the table on them; Power Query is supposed to translate that filter into the source's own query language so that **only the rows in the partition window ever leave the source**. When folding works, refreshing one day's partition transfers one day of data.\n\nWhen folding **fails**, the mashup engine has no choice but to pull the **entire dataset** into memory and apply the `RangeStart`/`RangeEnd` filter locally — for *every partition*. Thirty daily-updated files, refreshed partition by partition, each reading everything: the model exhausts the capacity's memory and the refresh dies with a resource error. That is precisely the symptom described.\n\n**The detail that makes this the intended answer:** the source is **CSV files**. Flat files have no query engine to fold into, so folding is impossible by construction. Microsoft is explicit that incremental refresh should target sources that fold — relational databases, SQL endpoints — and warns against file-based sources for exactly this reason. The F64 capacity is a red herring: no SKU saves you from loading 30 full files per partition.\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **B Query folding IS occurring** ✘ — the exact opposite. Folding is the thing that *prevents* this failure.\n- **C The data type of the partition column has changed** ✘ — the 40% answer, and it is a real failure mode, but the wrong one. Changing the type of the `RangeStart`/`RangeEnd` column breaks the **policy**: you get a validation or partition error (\"the filter must be on a date/datetime column\"), not *\"ran out of resources\"*. Match the symptom to the cause — this question hands you a memory-exhaustion symptom.\n- **A XMLA Endpoint is Read Only** ✘ — blocks third-party tools writing metadata. A scheduled refresh does not go through XMLA write, and it would fail with a permission error, not resource exhaustion.\n- **D Only refresh complete days is selected** ✘ — a legitimate policy option that *shrinks* the work (it skips today's partial day). If anything it reduces resource use.\n\n**Always verify folding before shipping an incremental refresh policy:** right-click the last applied step → **View Native Query**. If it is greyed out, the query is not folding and incremental refresh will quietly degrade into a full load."
    },
