  {
    "id": "T1Q198",
    "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric eventhouse that contains a KQL database. The database contains a table named TaxiData that stores the following data.\nYou need to create a column named FirstPickupDateTime that will contain the first value of each hour from tpep_pickup_datetime partitioned by\npayment_type.\nHow should you complete the query? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "blanks": [
      {
        "label": "| extend FirstPickupDateTime = [answer choice] (tpep_pickup_datetime, 1h, 0m, payment_type … prev(payment_type))",
        "options": [
          "row_cumsum",
          "row_rank_dense",
          "row_rank_min",
          "row_window_session"
        ],
        "answer": "row_window_session"
      },
      {
        "label": "… payment_type [answer choice] prev(payment_type))",
        "options": [
          "!=",
          "<=",
          "==",
          ">="
        ],
        "answer": "!="
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "table",
        "title": "TaxiData sample rows",
        "headers": [
          "VendorID",
          "tpep_pickup_datetime",
          "payment_type",
          "total_amount"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "2",
            "2022-06-06T11:08:32Z",
            "2",
            "7.12"
          ],
          [
            "2",
            "2022-06-06T11:12:05Z",
            "1",
            "10.56"
          ],
          [
            "1",
            "2022-06-06T11:15:00Z",
            "2",
            "17.12"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "KQL sessionized-pickup completion template",
        "language": "kql",
        "content": "TaxiData\n| sort by tpep_pickup_datetime asc, payment_type asc\n| extend FirstPickupDateTime = [Dropdown 1](\n    tpep_pickup_datetime, 1h, 0m,\n    payment_type [Dropdown 2] prev(payment_type))"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q198",
    "sourceId": "T1Q198",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Replaced incorrect sessionization with a specified aggregate variant.",
    "remaining": "Read exact summarize/min/bin docs, inspect source sample and execute KQL fixture including interleaved payment types/hour boundaries.",
    "explanation": "**`row_window_session`** and **`!=`**. The finished query:\n\n```kusto\nTaxiData\n| sort by tpep_pickup_datetime asc, payment_type asc\n| extend FirstPickupDateTime = row_window_session(tpep_pickup_datetime, 1h, 0m, payment_type != prev(payment_type))\n```\n\n### Blank 1 — row_window_session\n\nThe requirement is *\"the **first value of each hour** from `tpep_pickup_datetime`, partitioned by `payment_type`\"* — i.e. group rows into one-hour sessions and stamp every row with the timestamp the session **started**. That is the literal definition of the function: *\"Calculates **session start values** of a column in a serialized row set… If a new session is created, the function returns the current value of Expr. Otherwise, it returns the previous value.\"*\n\nThe argument shape in the exhibit confirms it before you even read the names — `(datetime_column, 1h, 0m, boolean)` matches `row_window_session(Expr, MaxDistanceFromFirst, MaxDistanceBetweenNeighbors [, Restart])` and nothing else:\n\n| Argument | Value here | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Expr` | `tpep_pickup_datetime` | the datetime being sessionized |\n| `MaxDistanceFromFirst` | `1h` | **a session can't span more than an hour** — this is the \"each hour\" |\n| `MaxDistanceBetweenNeighbors` | `0m` | no gap-based split; neighbours never break the session on their own |\n| `Restart` | `payment_type != prev(payment_type)` | force a new session when the partition changes |\n\n- **`row_rank_min`** ✘ — the highly-voted comment's answer, and wrong. It returns a **rank** (a number: 1, 2, 3…), not a datetime, so it cannot produce a column called `FirstPickupDateTime`. It also takes one argument, not four.\n- **`row_rank_dense`** ✘ — dense ranking. Same objections.\n- **`row_cumsum`** ✘ — a running total over a numeric column.\n\n### Blank 2 — `!=`, not `==`\n\nThis is the half most people get backwards. Read the parameter definition literally: ***Restart*** — *\"If specified, every value that evaluates to **`true`** immediately **restarts** the session.\"*\n\nYou want a fresh session whenever the `payment_type` **changes**, so the expression must be **true on change** → `payment_type != prev(payment_type)`. Writing `==` inverts it: the session would restart on every row where the payment type *stayed the same* and continue across the boundary where it changed — exactly wrong.\n\nMicrosoft's own documented example is this query with the names swapped:\n\n```kusto\n| sort by ID asc, Timestamp asc\n| extend SessionStarted = row_window_session(Timestamp, 1h, 5m, ID != prev(ID))\n```\n\n*Note the `| sort by … asc` that precedes it (already filled in).* Like `prev()`, `row_window_session()` is a **serialized row-set** function: it reads rows in their current order, so the data must be sorted by partition key **and** timestamp first or the sessions are meaningless.\n\n**Mnemonic for `Restart`: it is \"start a new one when TRUE\", so the condition describes the *boundary*, not the *continuation*.**",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "| extend FirstPickupDateTime = [answer choice] (tpep_pickup_datetime, 1h, 0m, payment_type … prev(payment_type))",
          "options": [
            "row_cumsum",
            "row_rank_dense",
            "row_rank_min",
            "row_window_session"
          ],
          "answer": "row_window_session"
        },
        {
          "label": "… payment_type [answer choice] prev(payment_type))",
          "options": [
            "!=",
            "<=",
            "==",
            ">="
          ],
          "answer": "!="
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric eventhouse that contains a KQL database. The database contains a table named TaxiData that stores the following data.\nYou need to create a column named FirstPickupDateTime that will contain the first value of each hour from tpep_pickup_datetime partitioned by\npayment_type.\nHow should you complete the query? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**`row_window_session`** and **`!=`**. The finished query:\n\n```kusto\nTaxiData\n| sort by tpep_pickup_datetime asc, payment_type asc\n| extend FirstPickupDateTime = row_window_session(tpep_pickup_datetime, 1h, 0m, payment_type != prev(payment_type))\n```\n\n### Blank 1 — row_window_session\n\nThe requirement is *\"the **first value of each hour** from `tpep_pickup_datetime`, partitioned by `payment_type`\"* — i.e. group rows into one-hour sessions and stamp every row with the timestamp the session **started**. That is the literal definition of the function: *\"Calculates **session start values** of a column in a serialized row set… If a new session is created, the function returns the current value of Expr. Otherwise, it returns the previous value.\"*\n\nThe argument shape in the exhibit confirms it before you even read the names — `(datetime_column, 1h, 0m, boolean)` matches `row_window_session(Expr, MaxDistanceFromFirst, MaxDistanceBetweenNeighbors [, Restart])` and nothing else:\n\n| Argument | Value here | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Expr` | `tpep_pickup_datetime` | the datetime being sessionized |\n| `MaxDistanceFromFirst` | `1h` | **a session can't span more than an hour** — this is the \"each hour\" |\n| `MaxDistanceBetweenNeighbors` | `0m` | no gap-based split; neighbours never break the session on their own |\n| `Restart` | `payment_type != prev(payment_type)` | force a new session when the partition changes |\n\n- **`row_rank_min`** ✘ — the highly-voted comment's answer, and wrong. It returns a **rank** (a number: 1, 2, 3…), not a datetime, so it cannot produce a column called `FirstPickupDateTime`. It also takes one argument, not four.\n- **`row_rank_dense`** ✘ — dense ranking. Same objections.\n- **`row_cumsum`** ✘ — a running total over a numeric column.\n\n### Blank 2 — `!=`, not `==`\n\nThis is the half most people get backwards. Read the parameter definition literally: ***Restart*** — *\"If specified, every value that evaluates to **`true`** immediately **restarts** the session.\"*\n\nYou want a fresh session whenever the `payment_type` **changes**, so the expression must be **true on change** → `payment_type != prev(payment_type)`. Writing `==` inverts it: the session would restart on every row where the payment type *stayed the same* and continue across the boundary where it changed — exactly wrong.\n\nMicrosoft's own documented example is this query with the names swapped:\n\n```kusto\n| sort by ID asc, Timestamp asc\n| extend SessionStarted = row_window_session(Timestamp, 1h, 5m, ID != prev(ID))\n```\n\n*Note the `| sort by … asc` that precedes it (already filled in).* Like `prev()`, `row_window_session()` is a **serialized row-set** function: it reads rows in their current order, so the data must be sorted by partition key **and** timestamp first or the sessions are meaningless.\n\n**Mnemonic for `Restart`: it is \"start a new one when TRUE\", so the condition describes the *boundary*, not the *continuation*.**"
    },
    "sourceVisualReferences": {
      "exhibits": [
        "p442_i1.png",
        "p442_i2.png"
      ],
      "answerImages": [
        "p442_i3.png"
      ],
      "optionImages": []
    },
    "visualResolution": "verified_transcription",
    "refs": [
