        ]
      },
      "stem": "You have Fabric tenant that contains four workspaces named Development, Test, QA, and Production. All the workspaces are in Premium Per User\n(PPU) license mode.\nYou plan to use a release pipeline to support the development lifecycle from Development to Production.\nWhich three actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and\narrange them in the correct order.",
      "explanation": "**Create a deployment pipeline → Create the QA stage → Assign workspaces.**\n\n> ⚠️ **Flagged: this overrides the PDF answer key,** which gives *Create a deployment pipeline → Assign workspaces → Create a deployment rule*. Microsoft's setup documentation makes that sequence impossible to execute. The (thin) discussion also rejects the key, though the two commenters disagree with each other.\n\n### Why the QA stage must come second\n\nThe scenario has **four** workspaces — Development, Test, **QA**, Production — and a new pipeline ships with **three** stages. The doc is blunt about when you can fix that:\n\n> *\"When you create a pipeline, you define how many stages it should have and what they should be called. **The number of stages are permanent and can't be changed after the pipeline is created.**\"*\n> *\"By default, the pipeline has three stages named Development, Test, and Production. You can accept these default stages or change the number of stages and their names. You can have anywhere between **2-10 stages**.\"*\n\nSo the QA stage has to be defined while the pipeline is being set up — and in any case **before** Step 3, because you cannot assign a workspace to a stage that does not exist. The order is forced.\n\n### Why *Create a deployment rule* is not one of the three\n\nIn Microsoft's own numbered walkthrough, creating deployment rules is **Step 7 — and it is marked *(optional)***. Rules exist to rewrite data sources and parameters per stage; this scenario mentions no data sources, no parameters, and no per-stage configuration at all. Nothing in the stem requires one. (Rules also cannot be created in the development stage, and cannot be created before the items they target have been deployed — so placing one third, immediately after assigning workspaces, does not even work.)\n\n### Why *Move each workspace to Pro license mode* is wrong\n\nIt is the reverse of the actual requirement. Deployment pipelines need capacity-backed workspaces, and **PPU qualifies** — the docs' own warning is that you get a prompt only *\"if the workspace isn't assigned to your organization's capacity, **or to your PPU capacity**\"*. The four workspaces are already PPU, so nothing needs moving; downgrading them to Pro would remove the capacity backing that deployment pipelines depend on. (The one real PPU caveat: if any stage is PPU, **all** of them must be, which is already true here.)\n\n**The canonical setup sequence: create the pipeline → define its stages → assign a workspace to each stage → deploy → (optionally) add deployment rules.**"
    },
