  {
    "id": "T1Q201",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Microsoft Power BI semantic model that contains a single table named Orders. Orders contains the following columns:\n• Product Name\n• Order Date\n• Quantity\nYou have a Power BI report that contains a page named Inventory. Inventory contains a slicer for the Product Name column and a clustered\ncolumn chart that shows Quantity by Order Date.\nYou need to ensure that users can select whether to display Quantity by Product Name or Quantity by Order Date in the chart. The solution must\nminimize development effort.\nWhat should you create?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "a measure"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "a dynamic format sting"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "a field parameter"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "a calculation group"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q201",
    "sourceId": "T1Q201",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**C — a field parameter.** Microsoft describes them in exactly the terms of the requirement: *\"Field parameters let report readers **dynamically change the measures or dimensions** they're analyzing in a report.\"*\n\nYou create one in **Modeling → New parameter → Fields**, add `Product Name` and `Order Date` to it, and drop the resulting parameter table onto the page as a slicer. The chart's axis then switches between the two columns as the user clicks — **no DAX, no bookmarks, no duplicate visuals**, which is what wins on *\"minimize development effort\"*.\n\n- **A a measure** ✘ — a measure can switch which *value* is displayed (via `SWITCH`/`SELECTEDVALUE`), but the requirement is to switch the chart's **axis** — a column, not an aggregation. A measure cannot be placed on an axis.\n- **B a dynamic format string** ✘ — changes how a measure's result is *formatted* (see T1Q183). Formatting, not field selection.\n- **D a calculation group** ✘ — the real contender, and the reason to know the distinction. Calculation groups apply reusable **calculation logic** to measures (YTD, prior year, % of total) via calculation items. They operate on measures, not on which dimension sits on the axis. They are also markedly more work: you author them in Tabular Editor with DAX per item.\n\n**The discriminator: \"let users pick which *field / column / dimension* to see\" ⇒ field parameter. \"Let users pick which *calculation* to apply to a measure\" ⇒ calculation group.**\n\n*(The existing slicer on `Product Name` is scenery — it filters the data. The new field parameter slicer chooses the axis.)*",
    "answer": "C",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "You have a Microsoft Power BI semantic model that contains a single table named Orders. Orders contains the following columns:\n• Product Name\n• Order Date\n• Quantity\nYou have a Power BI report that contains a page named Inventory. Inventory contains a slicer for the Product Name column and a clustered\ncolumn chart that shows Quantity by Order Date.\nYou need to ensure that users can select whether to display Quantity by Product Name or Quantity by Order Date in the chart. The solution must\nminimize development effort.\nWhat should you create?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "a measure",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "a dynamic format sting",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "a field parameter",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "a calculation group",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**C — a field parameter.** Microsoft describes them in exactly the terms of the requirement: *\"Field parameters let report readers **dynamically change the measures or dimensions** they're analyzing in a report.\"*\n\nYou create one in **Modeling → New parameter → Fields**, add `Product Name` and `Order Date` to it, and drop the resulting parameter table onto the page as a slicer. The chart's axis then switches between the two columns as the user clicks — **no DAX, no bookmarks, no duplicate visuals**, which is what wins on *\"minimize development effort\"*.\n\n- **A a measure** ✘ — a measure can switch which *value* is displayed (via `SWITCH`/`SELECTEDVALUE`), but the requirement is to switch the chart's **axis** — a column, not an aggregation. A measure cannot be placed on an axis.\n- **B a dynamic format string** ✘ — changes how a measure's result is *formatted* (see T1Q183). Formatting, not field selection.\n- **D a calculation group** ✘ — the real contender, and the reason to know the distinction. Calculation groups apply reusable **calculation logic** to measures (YTD, prior year, % of total) via calculation items. They operate on measures, not on which dimension sits on the axis. They are also markedly more work: you author them in Tabular Editor with DAX per item.\n\n**The discriminator: \"let users pick which *field / column / dimension* to see\" ⇒ field parameter. \"Let users pick which *calculation* to apply to a measure\" ⇒ calculation group.**\n\n*(The existing slicer on `Product Name` is scenery — it filters the data. The new field parameter slicer chooses the axis.)*"
    },
