    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**Deploy dev → test, deploy test → production, then refresh Model1 in production.**\n\n### Deploy first, refresh last\n\nThe fact that drives the whole answer: **deployment pipelines copy metadata, not data.** When a semantic model is deployed, the item definition (tables, relationships, measures, Power Query) moves forward — the imported data does **not**, and neither does the refresh schedule. A model landing in a new stage arrives empty or stale until something refreshes it.\n\nSo refreshing in development or test would be wasted work: the refreshed *data* cannot travel with the next deployment. Only the **final** refresh, in the stage where the data is actually consumed, matters. That is why two of the five tiles — *\"Refresh Model1 in the development stage\"* and *\"Refresh Model1 in the test stage\"* — are distractors, and why the refresh is step 3 rather than step 1.\n\n### You cannot skip the test stage\n\n*\"As quickly as possible\"* might tempt you to deploy straight from development to production, but a deployment pipeline moves content **one stage at a time**, between adjacent stages. With Workspace1 → development, Workspace2 → test and Workspace3 → production, reaching production requires both hops, in order.\n\n### \"Must NOT affect the availability of Report1\"\n\nReport1 lives in Workspace1 (development) and is the reason nothing is done to the development stage. Deploying **out of** a stage is a read operation on the source — it does not lock, alter or interrupt anything there, so Report1 keeps serving throughout. A refresh of Model1 in development, by contrast, would put the model Report1 depends on under load for no benefit.\n\n**Remember the pair of facts this question tests: (1) pipelines deploy definitions, never data or refresh schedules; (2) deployment is stage-by-stage. Everything else follows.**",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "dragdrop",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "drag": {
        "items": [
          "Refresh Model1 in the test stage.",
          "Run Pipeline1 from the development stage to the test stage.",
          "Refresh Model1 in the development stage.",
          "Run Pipeline1 from the test stage to the production stage.",
          "Refresh Model1 in the production stage."
        ],
        "reuse": false,
        "targets": [
          {
            "label": "Step 1",
            "answer": "Run Pipeline1 from the development stage to the test stage."
          },
          {
            "label": "Step 2",
            "answer": "Run Pipeline1 from the test stage to the production stage."
          },
          {
            "label": "Step 3",
            "answer": "Refresh Model1 in the production stage."
          }
        ]
      },
      "stem": "You have three Fabric workspaces named Workspace1, Workspace2, and Workspace3. Workspace1 contains a report named Report1 and a\nsemantic model named Model1 in Import mode.\nYou perform the following actions:\n• Create a deployment pipeline named Pipeline1 that has three stages named development, test, and production.\n• Assign Workspace1 to the development stage of Pipeline1.\n• Assign Workspace2 to the test stage of Pipeline1.\n• Assign Workspace3 to the production stage of Pipeline1.\n• Modify Model1.\nYou need to ensure that changes to Model1 are available in the production stage as quickly as possible. The solution must NOT affect the\navailability of Report1.\nWhich three actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and\narrange them in the correct order.",
      "explanation": "**Deploy dev → test, deploy test → production, then refresh Model1 in production.**\n\n### Deploy first, refresh last\n\nThe fact that drives the whole answer: **deployment pipelines copy metadata, not data.** When a semantic model is deployed, the item definition (tables, relationships, measures, Power Query) moves forward — the imported data does **not**, and neither does the refresh schedule. A model landing in a new stage arrives empty or stale until something refreshes it.\n\nSo refreshing in development or test would be wasted work: the refreshed *data* cannot travel with the next deployment. Only the **final** refresh, in the stage where the data is actually consumed, matters. That is why two of the five tiles — *\"Refresh Model1 in the development stage\"* and *\"Refresh Model1 in the test stage\"* — are distractors, and why the refresh is step 3 rather than step 1.\n\n### You cannot skip the test stage\n\n*\"As quickly as possible\"* might tempt you to deploy straight from development to production, but a deployment pipeline moves content **one stage at a time**, between adjacent stages. With Workspace1 → development, Workspace2 → test and Workspace3 → production, reaching production requires both hops, in order.\n\n### \"Must NOT affect the availability of Report1\"\n\nReport1 lives in Workspace1 (development) and is the reason nothing is done to the development stage. Deploying **out of** a stage is a read operation on the source — it does not lock, alter or interrupt anything there, so Report1 keeps serving throughout. A refresh of Model1 in development, by contrast, would put the model Report1 depends on under load for no benefit.\n\n**Remember the pair of facts this question tests: (1) pipelines deploy definitions, never data or refresh schedules; (2) deployment is stage-by-stage. Everything else follows.**"
    },
