            "Fact"
          ],
          "answer": "Bridging"
        },
        {
          "label": "Cardinality:",
          "options": [
            "Many-to-many",
            "One-to-many",
            "One-to-one"
          ],
          "answer": "One-to-many"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1.\nModel1 contains the following tables.\nProduct and Store can be part of multiple campaigns, and multiple active campaigns can exist simultaneously.\nYou plan to create a report named Report1 that will display sales made after a campaign for a given store.\nYou need to add a table to Model1 and configure cardinality for the table's relationships.\nWhat should you configure? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**Type of table → Bridging. Cardinality → One-to-many.**\n\n### Why a bridging table\n\nLook at what `Campaign` actually is: `CampaignDate, CampaignID, ProductID, StoreID, Name, Description`. It holds **two foreign keys** and *\"Product and Store can be part of **multiple campaigns**, and **multiple active campaigns** can exist simultaneously\"* — so the same `ProductID` appears on many campaign rows and the same `StoreID` appears on many campaign rows. There is a **many-to-many between `Product` and `Store` (via campaigns)**, and neither dimension can relate directly to the other.\n\nThe resolution is the same pattern as T1Q195: insert a **bridge table** holding the valid `ProductID` / `StoreID` combinations, with a relationship from each dimension into it.\n\n- **Dimension** ✘ — a dimension describes *one* entity and hangs off the fact by a single key. This table's job is to connect two entities, not describe one.\n- **Fact** ✘ — the closest wrong answer, because a bridge is structurally similar to a \"factless fact\" table. But a fact table stores **measures** you aggregate (`Sales` has the numbers). The new table stores only key combinations, so its role in the model is bridging.\n\n### Why one-to-many\n\nThis is the part people over-think. A bridge table works **precisely because it converts one many-to-many into two one-to-many relationships**:\n\n```\nProduct (1) ──< Bridge >── (1) Store\n    one row per product        one row per store\n    many rows in the bridge    many rows in the bridge\n```\n\nEach relationship runs from the **one** side (the dimension, where the key is unique) to the **many** side (the bridge, where it repeats). Set the bridge-side cross-filter direction to **Both** so filters propagate through.\n\n- **Many-to-many** ✘ — the intuitive-but-wrong pick. Yes the *business* relationship is many-to-many; the whole point of adding the bridge is that you no longer have to model it that way. Power BI's native many-to-many cardinality creates limited relationships, adds a blank row for unmatched keys, and produces ambiguous filter paths — which is exactly what the bridge avoids.\n- **One-to-one** ✘ — would require each product to map to exactly one bridge row, contradicting *\"can be part of multiple campaigns\"*.\n\n**The pattern: many-to-many between two dimensions ⇒ bridge table ⇒ two one-to-many relationships, bidirectional filtering.**"
    },
