    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q204",
    "sourceId": "T1Q204",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**B — a personal access token (PAT) and the URL of Repo1.** Connecting a workspace to GitHub asks for exactly three things: a **display name**, a **personal access token**, and the **repository URL**. Only option B names a pair from that list.\n\nBreak the options into their two halves and every distractor falls apart:\n\n**The credential.** GitHub authenticates Fabric with a **fine-grained PAT** carrying `Contents` read/write permission on the repo. The alternatives belong to Azure Storage, not to Git:\n\n- **SAS token** — a time-limited, scoped URL signature for Azure Blob/ADLS.\n- **Access key** — one of the two shared keys on an Azure Storage account.\n\nNeither means anything to GitHub. (For **Azure DevOps** the connection is different again — it uses your Entra ID sign-in, no token at all.)\n\n**The locator.** You supply the **repository URL** (`https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>`). The branch is not typed in as free text — after the connection is authorized, Fabric reads the repo and you **pick the branch and folder from dropdowns**. That is what eliminates A and C even if you were unsure about the credential.\n\n| | Credential | Locator | |\n|---|---|---|:-:|\n| A | SAS token — Azure Storage | branch name — chosen from a list | ✘✘ |\n| **B** | **PAT** | **repository URL** | **✔** |\n| C | access key — Azure Storage | branch name | ✘✘ |\n| D | access key — Azure Storage | repository URL | ✘ |\n\n*Workspace → Workspace settings → Git integration → Git provider: GitHub → Display name / Personal access token / Repository URL → Connect → then choose branch and folder.*",
    "answer": "B",
    "officialAnswer": "B",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "B",
      "answer": "B",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1.\nYou have a GitHub repository named Repo1.\nYou need to connect Workspace1 to the main branch of Repo1.\nWhich information should you provide?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "a shared access signature (SAS) token and the branch name of Repo1",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "a personal access token (PAT) and the URL of Repo1",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "an access key and the branch name of Repo1",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "an access key and the URL of Repo1",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**B — a personal access token (PAT) and the URL of Repo1.** Connecting a workspace to GitHub asks for exactly three things: a **display name**, a **personal access token**, and the **repository URL**. Only option B names a pair from that list.\n\nBreak the options into their two halves and every distractor falls apart:\n\n**The credential.** GitHub authenticates Fabric with a **fine-grained PAT** carrying `Contents` read/write permission on the repo. The alternatives belong to Azure Storage, not to Git:\n\n- **SAS token** — a time-limited, scoped URL signature for Azure Blob/ADLS.\n- **Access key** — one of the two shared keys on an Azure Storage account.\n\nNeither means anything to GitHub. (For **Azure DevOps** the connection is different again — it uses your Entra ID sign-in, no token at all.)\n\n**The locator.** You supply the **repository URL** (`https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>`). The branch is not typed in as free text — after the connection is authorized, Fabric reads the repo and you **pick the branch and folder from dropdowns**. That is what eliminates A and C even if you were unsure about the credential.\n\n| | Credential | Locator | |\n|---|---|---|:-:|\n| A | SAS token — Azure Storage | branch name — chosen from a list | ✘✘ |\n| **B** | **PAT** | **repository URL** | **✔** |\n| C | access key — Azure Storage | branch name | ✘✘ |\n| D | access key — Azure Storage | repository URL | ✘ |\n\n*Workspace → Workspace settings → Git integration → Git provider: GitHub → Display name / Personal access token / Repository URL → Connect → then choose branch and folder.*"
    },
