  {
    "id": "T1Q205",
    "type": "dragdrop",
    "stem": "You have Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains the following tables.\nYou are designing a Microsoft Power BI report named Report1 that will deliver near-real-time (NRT) shipping data. Report1 will contain data about\nmany shipped packages.\nYou need to ensure that each table is in the appropriate storage mode to support Report1. The solution must support near-real-time data delivery\nwhile maintaining high query performance.\nWhich storage mode should you select for each table? To answer, drag the appropriate storage modes to the correct tables. Each mode may be\nused once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "drag": {
      "items": [
        "DirectQuery",
        "Dual",
        "Hybrid",
        "Import"
      ],
      "reuse": true,
      "targets": [
        {
          "label": "FactPackageEvents",
          "answer": "Hybrid"
        },
        {
          "label": "DimPackage",
          "answer": "DirectQuery"
        },
        {
          "label": "DimCarrier",
          "answer": "Dual"
        }
      ]
    },
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "table",
        "title": "Model1 source tables",
        "headers": [
          "Name",
          "Columns"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "FactPackageEvents",
            "PackageID, CarrierID"
          ],
          [
            "DimPackage",
            "PackageID"
          ],
          [
            "DimCarrier",
            "CarrierID"
          ]
        ]
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q205",
    "sourceId": "T1Q205",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Repaired asymmetrical dimension modes with explicit source-group/cache assumptions.",
    "remaining": "Complete current primary-doc section review, source screenshot/case checks and native fixture where material; then independently challenge every final option.",
    "explanation": "**FactPackageEvents → Hybrid. DimPackage → DirectQuery. DimCarrier → Dual.** Two requirements are in tension — *near-real-time* (argues for DirectQuery) and *high query performance* (argues for Import) — and the answer resolves them **per table**.\n\n### FactPackageEvents → Hybrid\n\nA **hybrid table** is an incremental-refresh table with one extra partition: historical data sits in **imported** partitions (fast, compressed, in-memory) while the newest slice is a **DirectQuery** partition queried live. You get history at Import speed and the latest events with no refresh latency. That is the definition of near-real-time on a large fact table, and it is uncontested here.\n\n### DimPackage → DirectQuery\n\nThe decisive clue is *\"Report1 will contain data about **many shipped packages**\"*. `DimPackage` has one row **per package**, so it grows continuously and in lockstep with the fact — a newly shipped package creates a new *dimension* row, not just a new fact row.\n\nIf `DimPackage` were Import or Dual, its imported copy would only contain packages as of the last refresh. The hybrid fact's DirectQuery partition would return live events for packages that **do not exist in the imported dimension**, and the report would show blanks or drop rows. Near-real-time is only real if the dimension is live too. It is also the largest table of the three, so importing it is the most expensive and least useful thing you could do.\n\n### DimCarrier → Dual\n\n`DimCarrier` is the opposite: small, and carriers are onboarded rarely. **Dual** means the table is *both* — it keeps an imported copy and can also be queried in DirectQuery mode, and the engine picks per query:\n\n- joined only to **imported** partitions → served from memory, fast;\n- joined to the **DirectQuery** partition (or to DirectQuery `DimPackage`) → folded into the same source query, avoiding a slow **limited relationship** across storage modes.\n\nDual on the small dimensions is Microsoft's standard companion to a DirectQuery or hybrid fact, and it is why leaving `DimCarrier` as plain Import would hurt: every live query would have to be stitched together in the mashup engine instead of folded.\n\n| Mode | Data location | Latency | Speed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Import | in-memory copy | as of last refresh | fastest |\n| DirectQuery | source only | **live** | slowest |\n| **Dual** | both, chosen per query | live when needed | fast when it can be |\n| **Hybrid** | imported history + live newest partition | **live at the edge** | fast for history |\n\n> 💬 **Contested.** The one discussion comment (+3) marks `DimPackage` as **Dual**, which is the common rule of thumb (\"dimensions alongside a DirectQuery fact should be Dual\"). It is a reasonable default and would be right for a *static* dimension — but Dual imports the table, so it cannot surface packages created since the last refresh. The exam key's DirectQuery is the answer that actually honours \"near-real-time\", and the phrase *\"many shipped packages\"* is there to tell you this dimension is large and fast-moving. Worth knowing both readings.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "dragdrop",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "drag": {
        "items": [
          "DirectQuery",
          "Dual",
          "Hybrid",
          "Import"
        ],
        "reuse": true,
        "targets": [
          {
            "label": "FactPackageEvents",
            "answer": "Hybrid"
          },
          {
            "label": "DimPackage",
            "answer": "DirectQuery"
          },
          {
            "label": "DimCarrier",
            "answer": "Dual"
          }
        ]
      },
      "stem": "You have Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains the following tables.\nYou are designing a Microsoft Power BI report named Report1 that will deliver near-real-time (NRT) shipping data. Report1 will contain data about\nmany shipped packages.\nYou need to ensure that each table is in the appropriate storage mode to support Report1. The solution must support near-real-time data delivery\nwhile maintaining high query performance.\nWhich storage mode should you select for each table? To answer, drag the appropriate storage modes to the correct tables. Each mode may be\nused once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**FactPackageEvents → Hybrid. DimPackage → DirectQuery. DimCarrier → Dual.** Two requirements are in tension — *near-real-time* (argues for DirectQuery) and *high query performance* (argues for Import) — and the answer resolves them **per table**.\n\n### FactPackageEvents → Hybrid\n\nA **hybrid table** is an incremental-refresh table with one extra partition: historical data sits in **imported** partitions (fast, compressed, in-memory) while the newest slice is a **DirectQuery** partition queried live. You get history at Import speed and the latest events with no refresh latency. That is the definition of near-real-time on a large fact table, and it is uncontested here.\n\n### DimPackage → DirectQuery\n\nThe decisive clue is *\"Report1 will contain data about **many shipped packages**\"*. `DimPackage` has one row **per package**, so it grows continuously and in lockstep with the fact — a newly shipped package creates a new *dimension* row, not just a new fact row.\n\nIf `DimPackage` were Import or Dual, its imported copy would only contain packages as of the last refresh. The hybrid fact's DirectQuery partition would return live events for packages that **do not exist in the imported dimension**, and the report would show blanks or drop rows. Near-real-time is only real if the dimension is live too. It is also the largest table of the three, so importing it is the most expensive and least useful thing you could do.\n\n### DimCarrier → Dual\n\n`DimCarrier` is the opposite: small, and carriers are onboarded rarely. **Dual** means the table is *both* — it keeps an imported copy and can also be queried in DirectQuery mode, and the engine picks per query:\n\n- joined only to **imported** partitions → served from memory, fast;\n- joined to the **DirectQuery** partition (or to DirectQuery `DimPackage`) → folded into the same source query, avoiding a slow **limited relationship** across storage modes.\n\nDual on the small dimensions is Microsoft's standard companion to a DirectQuery or hybrid fact, and it is why leaving `DimCarrier` as plain Import would hurt: every live query would have to be stitched together in the mashup engine instead of folded.\n\n| Mode | Data location | Latency | Speed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Import | in-memory copy | as of last refresh | fastest |\n| DirectQuery | source only | **live** | slowest |\n| **Dual** | both, chosen per query | live when needed | fast when it can be |\n| **Hybrid** | imported history + live newest partition | **live at the edge** | fast for history |\n\n> 💬 **Contested.** The one discussion comment (+3) marks `DimPackage` as **Dual**, which is the common rule of thumb (\"dimensions alongside a DirectQuery fact should be Dual\"). It is a reasonable default and would be right for a *static* dimension — but Dual imports the table, so it cannot surface packages created since the last refresh. The exam key's DirectQuery is the answer that actually honours \"near-real-time\", and the phrase *\"many shipped packages\"* is there to tell you this dimension is large and fast-moving. Worth knowing both readings."
    },
