        "text": "Microsoft SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS)"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q206",
    "sourceId": "T1Q206",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**A + E — Tabular Editor and SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS).** Table partitions are **model metadata**, so creating and managing them requires a tool that can *write* metadata to the semantic model over the **XMLA endpoint** (set to Read Write). Exactly two options qualify, and the question says each is a complete solution on its own.\n\n- **A Tabular Editor** ✔ — exposes the `Partitions` collection of every table in its object tree. Add, rename, delete, edit each partition's M or native query, and set processing options. Writes via TOM over XMLA.\n- **E SSMS** ✔ — connects to the workspace's XMLA endpoint as though it were an Analysis Services server. Right-click a table → **Partitions** for the GUI, or run **TMSL** (`createOrReplace`, `refresh` with `\"type\": \"full\"` on a named partition) in an XMLA query window.\n\nWhy the others cannot:\n\n- **B DAX Studio** ✘ — a *query and tracing* tool. It runs DAX, captures server timings and reads VertiPaq Analyzer statistics (it will happily **show** you existing partitions and their row counts), but it has no metadata-write path. Read-only by design.\n- **C Power BI Desktop** ✘ — the important one to understand. Desktop has **no partition UI at all**. The only partitions it can produce are the ones an **incremental refresh policy** generates automatically once the model is published — you define the policy, the service creates the partitions. You cannot author a custom partition scheme there.\n- **D the Power BI service** ✘ — same story from the other end: the service *applies* an incremental refresh policy and shows refresh history, but its UI exposes no partition management.\n\n**The dividing line: XMLA read-write metadata tools (Tabular Editor, SSMS, ALM Toolkit, TOM/TMSL scripts) can manage partitions; authoring and consumption surfaces (Desktop, the service, DAX Studio) cannot.** See T1Q182 for the XMLA Read Write prerequisite.",
    "answer": "AE",
    "officialAnswer": "AE",
    "original": {
      "type": "multi",
      "officialAnswer": "AE",
      "answer": "AE",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains a fact table with millions of rows of shipment data.\nYou need to enable partitioning to improve the query performance and manageability of Model1.\nWhich two tools can you use to achieve the goal? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
      "options": [
