  {
    "id": "T1Q207",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric semantic model named Model1 that contains a table named Sales.\nYou need to enable incremental refresh for the Sales table.\nWhat should you do first?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Move Model1 to Premium Per User (PPU) workspace."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Create parameters in Power Query."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "From Settings, set Large semantic model storage format to On."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Automate the data preparation by using Dataflow Gen2."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q207",
    "sourceId": "T1Q207",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**B — create parameters in Power Query.** The word that matters is ***first***. Before the incremental refresh policy dialog will even open, the table must be filtered by two reserved parameters:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| **`RangeStart`** | `Date/Time` | lower bound of the partition window |\n| **`RangeEnd`** | `Date/Time` | upper bound of the partition window |\n\nThe names are **case-sensitive and reserved**, and the type must be `Date/Time` (not Date, not Text). You then filter the `Sales` date column with them, conventionally `>= RangeStart` and `< RangeEnd` so no row is counted in two partitions. Only once that filter exists can you right-click the table → **Incremental refresh** and define the archive/refresh ranges — the service substitutes its own values for the parameters to generate each partition.\n\nWhy the others are not first (or not required at all):\n\n- **A Move Model1 to a PPU workspace** ✘ — incremental refresh is available on **Pro** as well; PPU/Premium/Fabric only add capabilities like real-time hybrid partitions and XMLA partition management. Not a prerequisite.\n- **C Set Large semantic model storage format to On** ✘ — raises the model size ceiling and speeds XMLA writes (see T1Q180). Useful alongside a big incremental model, but unrelated to enabling the policy.\n- **D Automate the data preparation by using Dataflow Gen2** ✘ — a different ingestion approach entirely, and adding a dataflow in the middle can actually *break* incremental refresh by preventing query folding (see T1Q196).\n\n**The sequence to remember: `RangeStart`/`RangeEnd` parameters → filter the table on them → define the incremental refresh policy → publish → first refresh builds the partitions.** And confirm the query still folds, or the policy is worse than useless.",
    "answer": "B",
    "officialAnswer": "B",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "B",
      "answer": "B",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric semantic model named Model1 that contains a table named Sales.\nYou need to enable incremental refresh for the Sales table.\nWhat should you do first?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Move Model1 to Premium Per User (PPU) workspace.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Create parameters in Power Query.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "From Settings, set Large semantic model storage format to On.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Automate the data preparation by using Dataflow Gen2.",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**B — create parameters in Power Query.** The word that matters is ***first***. Before the incremental refresh policy dialog will even open, the table must be filtered by two reserved parameters:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| **`RangeStart`** | `Date/Time` | lower bound of the partition window |\n| **`RangeEnd`** | `Date/Time` | upper bound of the partition window |\n\nThe names are **case-sensitive and reserved**, and the type must be `Date/Time` (not Date, not Text). You then filter the `Sales` date column with them, conventionally `>= RangeStart` and `< RangeEnd` so no row is counted in two partitions. Only once that filter exists can you right-click the table → **Incremental refresh** and define the archive/refresh ranges — the service substitutes its own values for the parameters to generate each partition.\n\nWhy the others are not first (or not required at all):\n\n- **A Move Model1 to a PPU workspace** ✘ — incremental refresh is available on **Pro** as well; PPU/Premium/Fabric only add capabilities like real-time hybrid partitions and XMLA partition management. Not a prerequisite.\n- **C Set Large semantic model storage format to On** ✘ — raises the model size ceiling and speeds XMLA writes (see T1Q180). Useful alongside a big incremental model, but unrelated to enabling the policy.\n- **D Automate the data preparation by using Dataflow Gen2** ✘ — a different ingestion approach entirely, and adding a dataflow in the middle can actually *break* incremental refresh by preventing query folding (see T1Q196).\n\n**The sequence to remember: `RangeStart`/`RangeEnd` parameters → filter the table on them → define the incremental refresh policy → publish → first refresh builds the partitions.** And confirm the query still folds, or the policy is worse than useless."
    },
