  {
    "id": "T1Q208",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have an Azure SQL database named DB1 and a Fabric workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 contains a lakehouse named LH1 and a\nDataflow Gen2 named Dataflow1. Dataflow1 includes a query named Query1 that loads data from DB1, applies transformations to the data, and\nthen filters the data.\nYou discover that Query1 loads all the data before applying the transformations.\nYou need to ensure that Query1 uses query folding.\nWhat should you do?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Remove unused columns from Query1."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Enable incremental refresh for Query1."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Replace DB1 with a Microsoft SQL Server database."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Move the filter steps before the transformation steps in Query1."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q208",
    "sourceId": "T1Q208",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**D — move the filter steps before the transformation steps in Query1.**\n\nQuery folding is **all-or-nothing from the point it breaks onward**. The mashup engine walks the applied steps in order and translates each one into native SQL for as long as it can. The moment it hits a step with no SQL equivalent, folding stops — and **every step after that runs locally in Power Query**, which means the engine must first pull the full table out of DB1.\n\nThat is exactly the reported symptom: *\"Query1 loads all the data before applying the transformations.\"* A non-foldable transformation is sitting in front of the filter, so the filter can never reach the database.\n\n```\n❌ Source → [non-foldable transform] → Filter      →  full table pulled, filtered in memory\n✅ Source → Filter → [non-foldable transform]          →  WHERE clause runs in DB1, only matching rows travel\n```\n\nFilters (`Table.SelectRows`) are among the most reliably foldable operations there are — they become a `WHERE` clause. Putting them immediately after the source is both the fix here and a general best practice: **fold the row- and column-reducing steps first, then do whatever local work you must.**\n\n- **A Remove unused columns** ✘ — good hygiene, and column removal *is* foldable (it becomes a narrower `SELECT` list), but it does nothing about a folding chain that is already broken earlier in the query. It reduces width, not the \"loads all the data\" problem.\n- **B Enable incremental refresh** ✘ — backwards. Incremental refresh **depends on** query folding; it does not create it. Enabling it on a non-folding query makes things worse, because each partition then pulls the whole table (see T1Q196).\n- **C Replace DB1 with a SQL Server database** ✘ — Azure SQL Database already folds perfectly. The source is not the problem; the step order is.\n\n**How to verify:** right-click each applied step → **View Native Query**. The last step where it is still enabled is where folding stops. Anything you can move above that line gets pushed to the source for free.",
    "answer": "D",
    "officialAnswer": "D",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "D",
      "answer": "D",
      "stem": "You have an Azure SQL database named DB1 and a Fabric workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 contains a lakehouse named LH1 and a\nDataflow Gen2 named Dataflow1. Dataflow1 includes a query named Query1 that loads data from DB1, applies transformations to the data, and\nthen filters the data.\nYou discover that Query1 loads all the data before applying the transformations.\nYou need to ensure that Query1 uses query folding.\nWhat should you do?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Remove unused columns from Query1.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Enable incremental refresh for Query1.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Replace DB1 with a Microsoft SQL Server database.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Move the filter steps before the transformation steps in Query1.",
          "mostVoted": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**D — move the filter steps before the transformation steps in Query1.**\n\nQuery folding is **all-or-nothing from the point it breaks onward**. The mashup engine walks the applied steps in order and translates each one into native SQL for as long as it can. The moment it hits a step with no SQL equivalent, folding stops — and **every step after that runs locally in Power Query**, which means the engine must first pull the full table out of DB1.\n\nThat is exactly the reported symptom: *\"Query1 loads all the data before applying the transformations.\"* A non-foldable transformation is sitting in front of the filter, so the filter can never reach the database.\n\n```\n❌ Source → [non-foldable transform] → Filter      →  full table pulled, filtered in memory\n✅ Source → Filter → [non-foldable transform]          →  WHERE clause runs in DB1, only matching rows travel\n```\n\nFilters (`Table.SelectRows`) are among the most reliably foldable operations there are — they become a `WHERE` clause. Putting them immediately after the source is both the fix here and a general best practice: **fold the row- and column-reducing steps first, then do whatever local work you must.**\n\n- **A Remove unused columns** ✘ — good hygiene, and column removal *is* foldable (it becomes a narrower `SELECT` list), but it does nothing about a folding chain that is already broken earlier in the query. It reduces width, not the \"loads all the data\" problem.\n- **B Enable incremental refresh** ✘ — backwards. Incremental refresh **depends on** query folding; it does not create it. Enabling it on a non-folding query makes things worse, because each partition then pulls the whole table (see T1Q196).\n- **C Replace DB1 with a SQL Server database** ✘ — Azure SQL Database already folds perfectly. The source is not the problem; the step order is.\n\n**How to verify:** right-click each applied step → **View Native Query**. The last step where it is still enabled is where folding stops. Anything you can move above that line gets pushed to the source for free."
    },
    "sourceVisualReferences": {
      "exhibits": [],
      "answerImages": [],
      "optionImages": []
    },
    "visualResolution": "no_exhibit_needed",
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Power Query query folding",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/query-folding-basics?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
