    "id": "T1Q210",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 and a warehouse named Warehouse1. Workspace1 contains a user named User1. User1 is\nassigned the Viewer role for Workspace1.\nYou need to ensure that User1 can share Warehouse1 with other users. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege.\nWhich role should you assign to User1?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Member for Workspace1"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "db_owner for Warehouse1"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Contributor for Workspace1"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Admin for Workspace1"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q210",
    "sourceId": "T1Q210",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**A — Member for Workspace1.** The capability being granted is **Reshare**, and the permissions table is unambiguous about who has it:\n\n| Permission | Given by default to |\n|---|---|\n| Read / ReadData | Admin, Member, Contributor, Viewer |\n| ReadAll / Write | Admin, Member, Contributor |\n| **Reshare** | **Admin, Member** |\n| Restore / Audit | Admin |\n\nMicrosoft states the same thing as a prerequisite on the sharing article itself: ***\"You must be an admin or member in your workspace to share an item in Microsoft Fabric.\"*** With only two qualifying roles, *\"principle of least privilege\"* picks the lower one — **Member**.\n\n- **C Contributor** ✘ — the near-miss that trips people up. Contributor can *create and write* items, which feels like plenty of power, but it is explicitly **not** granted Reshare. Write access and the right to hand out access are deliberately separate in Fabric.\n- **D Admin** ✘ — would work, but it also allows deleting the workspace, managing all access, and adding other admins. Strictly more privilege than the task needs, so it loses on the tiebreaker.\n- **B db_owner for Warehouse1** ✘ — a **SQL Server** database role, not a Fabric concept. Fabric warehouses use workspace roles plus item permissions plus T-SQL `GRANT`; there is no `db_owner` to assign. (Note the role assignment would also be at the wrong scope: the requirement is to share the *item*, which is a Fabric permission operation, not a database one.)\n\nUser1's current **Viewer** role is the starting point the question gives you — read-only, no resharing — and assigning Member replaces it.\n\n**Escalation ladder for these questions: Viewer (read) → Contributor (write) → Member (write + share) → Admin (everything, including delete). \"Can share with others\" is the bright line between Contributor and Member.**",
    "answer": "A",
    "officialAnswer": "A",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "A",
      "answer": "A",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 and a warehouse named Warehouse1. Workspace1 contains a user named User1. User1 is\nassigned the Viewer role for Workspace1.\nYou need to ensure that User1 can share Warehouse1 with other users. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege.\nWhich role should you assign to User1?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Member for Workspace1",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "db_owner for Warehouse1",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Contributor for Workspace1",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Admin for Workspace1",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**A — Member for Workspace1.** The capability being granted is **Reshare**, and the permissions table is unambiguous about who has it:\n\n| Permission | Given by default to |\n|---|---|\n| Read / ReadData | Admin, Member, Contributor, Viewer |\n| ReadAll / Write | Admin, Member, Contributor |\n| **Reshare** | **Admin, Member** |\n| Restore / Audit | Admin |\n\nMicrosoft states the same thing as a prerequisite on the sharing article itself: ***\"You must be an admin or member in your workspace to share an item in Microsoft Fabric.\"*** With only two qualifying roles, *\"principle of least privilege\"* picks the lower one — **Member**.\n\n- **C Contributor** ✘ — the near-miss that trips people up. Contributor can *create and write* items, which feels like plenty of power, but it is explicitly **not** granted Reshare. Write access and the right to hand out access are deliberately separate in Fabric.\n- **D Admin** ✘ — would work, but it also allows deleting the workspace, managing all access, and adding other admins. Strictly more privilege than the task needs, so it loses on the tiebreaker.\n- **B db_owner for Warehouse1** ✘ — a **SQL Server** database role, not a Fabric concept. Fabric warehouses use workspace roles plus item permissions plus T-SQL `GRANT`; there is no `db_owner` to assign. (Note the role assignment would also be at the wrong scope: the requirement is to share the *item*, which is a Fabric permission operation, not a database one.)\n\nUser1's current **Viewer** role is the starting point the question gives you — read-only, no resharing — and assigning Member replaces it.\n\n**Escalation ladder for these questions: Viewer (read) → Contributor (write) → Member (write + share) → Admin (everything, including delete). \"Can share with others\" is the bright line between Contributor and Member.**"
    },
