    "id": "T1Q211",
    "type": "hotspot-yesno",
    "stem": "You have a semantic model that contains the following tables:\n• Sales\n• Product\n• Location\nYou are creating a DAX measure named Measure1 that will calculate the percentage of all the sales of a selected product. Measure1 will use the\nfollowing DAX formula.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "statements": [
      {
        "text": "Measure1 will return an error if there are no sales for all products.",
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "text": "Measure1 will return a decimal value that represents the ratio of the current product's total sales to the total sales across all products.",
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "text": "The denominator of Measure1 will be calculated by using the modified filter context created by the REMOVEFILTERS('Product') DAX function.",
        "answer": "Yes"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "Original DAX ratio measure",
        "language": "dax",
        "content": "[Percentage of All Product Sales] =\nDIVIDE (\n    [Total Sales Amount],\n    CALCULATE (\n        [Total Sales Amount],\n        REMOVEFILTERS ( 'Product' )\n    )\n)"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q211",
    "sourceId": "T1Q211",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**No — Yes — Yes.** The measure:\n\n```dax\n[Percentage of All Product Sales] =\nDIVIDE(\n    [Total Sales Amount],\n    CALCULATE(\n        [Total Sales Amount],\n        REMOVEFILTERS( 'Product' )\n    )\n)\n```\n\nThis is the canonical \"percent of total\" pattern: the numerator is evaluated in the **current** filter context (the selected product), and the denominator re-evaluates the same measure with the `Product` filters stripped away, giving the all-products total.\n\n**1. Returns an error if there are no sales for all products → No.** This statement is testing whether you know `DIVIDE` from the `/` operator. If no product has any sales the denominator is blank/zero — and `DIVIDE(numerator, denominator [, alternateResult])` *\"performs division and returns an alternate result or BLANK() on division by 0\"*. No error is raised; you get `BLANK()` (or the alternate result, had one been supplied). Writing `[Total Sales Amount] / CALCULATE(...)` instead would risk an actual divide-by-zero. **That safety is the entire reason `DIVIDE` exists**, and the statement is false.\n\n**2. Returns a decimal ratio of the current product to all products → Yes.** Exactly what the expression computes. Note it returns a **ratio** (0.25), not a percentage (25%) — turning it into \"25.00%\" is a *formatting* concern, handled by the format string (see T1Q183 / T1Q218). The statement says \"decimal value… ratio\", which is correct.\n\n**3. The denominator uses the modified filter context from `REMOVEFILTERS('Product')` → Yes.** `CALCULATE` evaluates its first argument in a filter context modified by the filter arguments that follow it. `REMOVEFILTERS('Product')` clears every filter on the entire `Product` table, so the inner `[Total Sales Amount]` is computed over all products regardless of what the visual has selected. That is the definition of the denominator here.\n\n*Two refinements worth carrying into the exam:*\n- `REMOVEFILTERS` is the modern, explicit name for `ALL` used as a `CALCULATE` modifier — it can **only** be used as a filter-removal modifier, whereas `ALL` doubles as a table function. Prefer `REMOVEFILTERS` for readability.\n- It clears filters on `Product` **only**. Filters from `Location` or a date slicer still apply to the denominator, so this measure means \"share of all products *within the rest of the current context*\", which is usually what you want.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-yesno",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "statements": [
        {
          "text": "Measure1 will return an error if there are no sales for all products.",
          "answer": "No"
        },
        {
          "text": "Measure1 will return a decimal value that represents the ratio of the current product's total sales to the total sales across all products.",
          "answer": "Yes"
        },
        {
          "text": "The denominator of Measure1 will be calculated by using the modified filter context created by the REMOVEFILTERS('Product') DAX function.",
          "answer": "Yes"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a semantic model that contains the following tables:\n• Sales\n• Product\n• Location\nYou are creating a DAX measure named Measure1 that will calculate the percentage of all the sales of a selected product. Measure1 will use the\nfollowing DAX formula.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**No — Yes — Yes.** The measure:\n\n```dax\n[Percentage of All Product Sales] =\nDIVIDE(\n    [Total Sales Amount],\n    CALCULATE(\n        [Total Sales Amount],\n        REMOVEFILTERS( 'Product' )\n    )\n)\n```\n\nThis is the canonical \"percent of total\" pattern: the numerator is evaluated in the **current** filter context (the selected product), and the denominator re-evaluates the same measure with the `Product` filters stripped away, giving the all-products total.\n\n**1. Returns an error if there are no sales for all products → No.** This statement is testing whether you know `DIVIDE` from the `/` operator. If no product has any sales the denominator is blank/zero — and `DIVIDE(numerator, denominator [, alternateResult])` *\"performs division and returns an alternate result or BLANK() on division by 0\"*. No error is raised; you get `BLANK()` (or the alternate result, had one been supplied). Writing `[Total Sales Amount] / CALCULATE(...)` instead would risk an actual divide-by-zero. **That safety is the entire reason `DIVIDE` exists**, and the statement is false.\n\n**2. Returns a decimal ratio of the current product to all products → Yes.** Exactly what the expression computes. Note it returns a **ratio** (0.25), not a percentage (25%) — turning it into \"25.00%\" is a *formatting* concern, handled by the format string (see T1Q183 / T1Q218). The statement says \"decimal value… ratio\", which is correct.\n\n**3. The denominator uses the modified filter context from `REMOVEFILTERS('Product')` → Yes.** `CALCULATE` evaluates its first argument in a filter context modified by the filter arguments that follow it. `REMOVEFILTERS('Product')` clears every filter on the entire `Product` table, so the inner `[Total Sales Amount]` is computed over all products regardless of what the visual has selected. That is the definition of the denominator here.\n\n*Two refinements worth carrying into the exam:*\n- `REMOVEFILTERS` is the modern, explicit name for `ALL` used as a `CALCULATE` modifier — it can **only** be used as a filter-removal modifier, whereas `ALL` doubles as a table function. Prefer `REMOVEFILTERS` for readability.\n- It clears filters on `Product` **only**. Filters from `Location` or a date slicer still apply to the denominator, so this measure means \"share of all products *within the rest of the current context*\", which is usually what you want."
    },
