  {
    "id": "T1Q212",
    "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace that contains a large warehouse.\nYou plan to create a lakehouse named Lakehouse1 for a sales dataset. Lakehouse1 will contain the following tables:\n• Sales: Contains sales transactions\n• Stores: Contains a unique list of store names and locations\n• Loyalty: Contains a list of customers and their preferred stores\n• Customers: Contains a unique list of customer names and addresses\n• Products: Contains a unique list of available products and their descriptions\nYou need to configure a star schema for Lakehouse1.\nWhich table should you define as the fact table, and which type of relationship should you configure from the Sales table to the Customers table?\nTo answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "blanks": [
      {
        "label": "Fact table:",
        "options": [
          "Customers",
          "Products",
          "Sales",
          "Stores"
        ],
        "answer": "Sales"
      },
      {
        "label": "Relationship (Sales → Customers):",
        "options": [
          "Many-to-one",
          "One-to-one",
          "One-to-many"
        ],
        "answer": "Many-to-one"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q212",
    "sourceId": "T1Q212",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**Fact table → Sales. Relationship → Many-to-one.**\n\n### The fact table\n\nA fact table records **events or transactions** and holds the numeric measures you aggregate; dimensions hold the descriptive attributes you slice by. Read the five descriptions and only one of them describes events:\n\n| Table | Description | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Sales** | *contains sales **transactions*** | **fact** |\n| Stores | *a **unique list** of store names and locations* | dimension |\n| Customers | *a **unique list** of customer names and addresses* | dimension |\n| Products | *a **unique list** of available products and descriptions* | dimension |\n| Loyalty | *a list of customers and their preferred stores* | **bridge / factless fact** — two foreign keys, no measures |\n\nThe giveaway is the repeated phrase *\"a unique list of…\"* — one row per entity means a dimension. `Sales` is the only table whose rows are occurrences rather than entities.\n\n*(`Loyalty` is the interesting one: it relates customers to stores and carries no measures, so it is a bridge, not a second fact table. Compare T1Q195 and T1Q203.)*\n\n### The cardinality — read it in the stated direction\n\nThe question asks for the relationship **from `Sales` to `Customers`**, and direction is the whole trick:\n\n- **Many** sales rows per customer — a customer buys repeatedly.\n- **One** customer row per sale — each transaction belongs to exactly one customer.\n\nReading `Sales → Customers` gives **many-to-one**. The *same relationship* read the other way, `Customers → Sales`, is **one-to-many** — which is why that option is sitting there. Always anchor on which table the question names first.\n\n- **One-to-one** ✘ — would mean each customer made exactly one purchase and each purchase had its own customer record. Contradicts a \"unique list of customer names\".\n\nIn a star schema every fact-to-dimension relationship is many-to-one with the filter flowing **from** the dimension **to** the fact — dimensions filter facts, never the reverse.",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-dropdown",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "blanks": [
        {
          "label": "Fact table:",
          "options": [
            "Customers",
            "Products",
            "Sales",
            "Stores"
          ],
          "answer": "Sales"
        },
        {
          "label": "Relationship (Sales → Customers):",
          "options": [
            "Many-to-one",
            "One-to-one",
            "One-to-many"
          ],
          "answer": "Many-to-one"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace that contains a large warehouse.\nYou plan to create a lakehouse named Lakehouse1 for a sales dataset. Lakehouse1 will contain the following tables:\n• Sales: Contains sales transactions\n• Stores: Contains a unique list of store names and locations\n• Loyalty: Contains a list of customers and their preferred stores\n• Customers: Contains a unique list of customer names and addresses\n• Products: Contains a unique list of available products and their descriptions\nYou need to configure a star schema for Lakehouse1.\nWhich table should you define as the fact table, and which type of relationship should you configure from the Sales table to the Customers table?\nTo answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**Fact table → Sales. Relationship → Many-to-one.**\n\n### The fact table\n\nA fact table records **events or transactions** and holds the numeric measures you aggregate; dimensions hold the descriptive attributes you slice by. Read the five descriptions and only one of them describes events:\n\n| Table | Description | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Sales** | *contains sales **transactions*** | **fact** |\n| Stores | *a **unique list** of store names and locations* | dimension |\n| Customers | *a **unique list** of customer names and addresses* | dimension |\n| Products | *a **unique list** of available products and descriptions* | dimension |\n| Loyalty | *a list of customers and their preferred stores* | **bridge / factless fact** — two foreign keys, no measures |\n\nThe giveaway is the repeated phrase *\"a unique list of…\"* — one row per entity means a dimension. `Sales` is the only table whose rows are occurrences rather than entities.\n\n*(`Loyalty` is the interesting one: it relates customers to stores and carries no measures, so it is a bridge, not a second fact table. Compare T1Q195 and T1Q203.)*\n\n### The cardinality — read it in the stated direction\n\nThe question asks for the relationship **from `Sales` to `Customers`**, and direction is the whole trick:\n\n- **Many** sales rows per customer — a customer buys repeatedly.\n- **One** customer row per sale — each transaction belongs to exactly one customer.\n\nReading `Sales → Customers` gives **many-to-one**. The *same relationship* read the other way, `Customers → Sales`, is **one-to-many** — which is why that option is sitting there. Always anchor on which table the question names first.\n\n- **One-to-one** ✘ — would mean each customer made exactly one purchase and each purchase had its own customer record. Contradicts a \"unique list of customer names\".\n\nIn a star schema every fact-to-dimension relationship is many-to-one with the filter flowing **from** the dimension **to** the fact — dimensions filter facts, never the reverse."
    },
