  {
    "id": "T1Q213",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1 and a lakehouse named Lakehouse1.\nWarehouse1 contains a table named Table1. Lakehouse1 contains a delta table named Table2.\nYou need to persist data from Table1 to Table2. The solution must use a low-code interface.\nWhat should you do?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Use a notebook to read the data from Table2 and insert the data into Table1."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Use a notebook to read the data from Table1 and insert the data into Table2."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Use a Dataflow Gen2 to read the data from Table1 and insert the data into Table2."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Use stored procedure to read the data from Table2 and insert the data into Table1."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q213",
    "sourceId": "T1Q213",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**C — use a Dataflow Gen2 to read the data from Table1 and insert it into Table2.** Two filters narrow the four options down to one.\n\n**Filter 1 — direction.** *\"Persist data **from Table1 to Table2**\"*: read from **Warehouse1.Table1**, write to **Lakehouse1.Table2**. Options **A** and **D** both run the other way (read Table2, insert into Table1), so they are eliminated on direction alone — before you consider the tooling at all. Always check the direction in this style of question; it is the cheapest elimination available.\n\n**Filter 2 — *\"must use a low-code interface\"*.** That leaves B and C:\n\n- **B a notebook** ✘ — correct direction, wrong tooling. A notebook means hand-written PySpark or Spark SQL. That is **code**, which the requirement rules out. (Technically it would work fine, which is what makes it the intended distractor.)\n- **C a Dataflow Gen2** ✔ — authored in **Power Query**, a graphical, no-code/low-code experience, and it supports a **lakehouse as a data destination**. Point it at the warehouse's SQL analytics endpoint as the source, set Lakehouse1 as the destination, and it writes Table2 as a Delta table.\n\nNote that \"persist\" matters too: the data must actually be **written** into Lakehouse1, not merely referenced. A shortcut would surface Table1 inside Lakehouse1 without copying it — low-code, but it does not persist anything (compare T1Q173). A Dataflow Gen2 materialises the rows.\n\n**Rule: \"low-code / no-code\" ⇒ Dataflow Gen2, a pipeline Copy activity, or a copy job. \"notebook / Spark job definition\" ⇒ code, and is excluded by that phrase.**",
    "answer": "C",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1 and a lakehouse named Lakehouse1.\nWarehouse1 contains a table named Table1. Lakehouse1 contains a delta table named Table2.\nYou need to persist data from Table1 to Table2. The solution must use a low-code interface.\nWhat should you do?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Use a notebook to read the data from Table2 and insert the data into Table1.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Use a notebook to read the data from Table1 and insert the data into Table2.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Use a Dataflow Gen2 to read the data from Table1 and insert the data into Table2.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Use stored procedure to read the data from Table2 and insert the data into Table1.",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**C — use a Dataflow Gen2 to read the data from Table1 and insert it into Table2.** Two filters narrow the four options down to one.\n\n**Filter 1 — direction.** *\"Persist data **from Table1 to Table2**\"*: read from **Warehouse1.Table1**, write to **Lakehouse1.Table2**. Options **A** and **D** both run the other way (read Table2, insert into Table1), so they are eliminated on direction alone — before you consider the tooling at all. Always check the direction in this style of question; it is the cheapest elimination available.\n\n**Filter 2 — *\"must use a low-code interface\"*.** That leaves B and C:\n\n- **B a notebook** ✘ — correct direction, wrong tooling. A notebook means hand-written PySpark or Spark SQL. That is **code**, which the requirement rules out. (Technically it would work fine, which is what makes it the intended distractor.)\n- **C a Dataflow Gen2** ✔ — authored in **Power Query**, a graphical, no-code/low-code experience, and it supports a **lakehouse as a data destination**. Point it at the warehouse's SQL analytics endpoint as the source, set Lakehouse1 as the destination, and it writes Table2 as a Delta table.\n\nNote that \"persist\" matters too: the data must actually be **written** into Lakehouse1, not merely referenced. A shortcut would surface Table1 inside Lakehouse1 without copying it — low-code, but it does not persist anything (compare T1Q173). A Dataflow Gen2 materialises the rows.\n\n**Rule: \"low-code / no-code\" ⇒ Dataflow Gen2, a pipeline Copy activity, or a copy job. \"notebook / Spark job definition\" ⇒ code, and is excluded by that phrase.**"
    },
