    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q214",
    "sourceId": "T1Q214",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**C — sort the `Fiscal Month` column by another column** (namely `Month Number`).\n\nThe problem: `Fiscal Month` holds text — `Jan`, `Feb`, `Mar`… — and Power BI sorts text **alphabetically** by default, producing *Apr, Aug, Dec, Feb, Jan…* on every axis and in every slicer. The `Month Number` column (1–12) already encodes the right order; you just have to tell the model to use it.\n\n**Column tools → Sort by column → Month Number**, applied once to `Fiscal Month`. Every visual in every report using that column then renders chronologically, forever. That is what wins on *\"minimize administrative effort\"* — it is a **model-level** fix, done once.\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **A Sort the `Month Number` column by Column** ✘ — the 20% answer, and it has the two columns the wrong way round. `Month Number` is already numeric and already sorts correctly (1, 2, 3…); sorting it *by* `Fiscal Month` would actively break it into alphabetical order. The rule is: **sort the column you display, by the column that holds the order.**\n- **B Sort the visuals by using Sort ascending on Fiscal Month** ✘ — the maximum-effort answer. It is a **per-visual** setting that has to be reapplied to every chart, every slicer and every new report — and it sorts alphabetically anyway, which is the original problem.\n- **D Sort the `Date` table by Column** ✘ — Sort by Column is a **column** property; there is no table-level equivalent.\n\n*Two gotchas worth knowing:* the sorting column must have a **one-to-one (or many-to-one) correspondence** with the displayed column — each `Fiscal Month` value must map to exactly one `Month Number`, or Power BI rejects the setting. And remember to **hide** `Month Number` afterwards so report authors are not tempted to use it directly.",
    "answer": "C",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "C",
      "stem": "You have a Microsoft Power BI semantic model that contains a table named Date. Date contains the following data.\nYou need to ensure that the visuals that use the Date table appear in chronological order. The solutions must minimize administrative effort.\nWhat should you do?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Sort the Month Number column by Column.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Sort the visuals by using the Sort ascending option on Fiscal Month.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Sort the Fiscal Month column by Column.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Sort the Date table by Column.",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**C — sort the `Fiscal Month` column by another column** (namely `Month Number`).\n\nThe problem: `Fiscal Month` holds text — `Jan`, `Feb`, `Mar`… — and Power BI sorts text **alphabetically** by default, producing *Apr, Aug, Dec, Feb, Jan…* on every axis and in every slicer. The `Month Number` column (1–12) already encodes the right order; you just have to tell the model to use it.\n\n**Column tools → Sort by column → Month Number**, applied once to `Fiscal Month`. Every visual in every report using that column then renders chronologically, forever. That is what wins on *\"minimize administrative effort\"* — it is a **model-level** fix, done once.\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **A Sort the `Month Number` column by Column** ✘ — the 20% answer, and it has the two columns the wrong way round. `Month Number` is already numeric and already sorts correctly (1, 2, 3…); sorting it *by* `Fiscal Month` would actively break it into alphabetical order. The rule is: **sort the column you display, by the column that holds the order.**\n- **B Sort the visuals by using Sort ascending on Fiscal Month** ✘ — the maximum-effort answer. It is a **per-visual** setting that has to be reapplied to every chart, every slicer and every new report — and it sorts alphabetically anyway, which is the original problem.\n- **D Sort the `Date` table by Column** ✘ — Sort by Column is a **column** property; there is no table-level equivalent.\n\n*Two gotchas worth knowing:* the sorting column must have a **one-to-one (or many-to-one) correspondence** with the displayed column — each `Fiscal Month` value must map to exactly one `Month Number`, or Power BI rejects the setting. And remember to **hide** `Month Number` afterwards so report authors are not tempted to use it directly."
    },
