  {
    "id": "T1Q215",
    "type": "multi",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains a fact table named FactSales. FactSales has a\nrelationship to a dimension table named DimDate on a column named OrderDate.\nTo FactSales, you add a second relationship between FactSales and DimDate based on a column named ShippedDate.\nYou need to filter by using ShippedDate in a new report. The solution must NOT affect the function of existing reports.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Enable the ShippedDate relationship."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Use a RELATEDTABLE DAX function."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Use a CROSSFILTER DAX function."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Use a USERELATIONSHIP DAX function."
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "Disable the ShippedDate relationship between the tables."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q215",
    "sourceId": "T1Q215",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**D + E — disable the `ShippedDate` relationship, and use `USERELATIONSHIP` in the new measures.**\n\nA tabular model allows **only one active relationship between any two tables**. `FactSales` already has an active relationship to `DimDate` on `OrderDate`, so the new `ShippedDate` relationship must be **inactive** — the model will not even permit two active ones, and Power BI creates the second as inactive (dashed line) automatically.\n\nThe pair of answers maps onto the two requirements:\n\n- **E Disable the ShippedDate relationship** → *\"must NOT affect the function of existing reports.\"* Every existing measure resolves through the active `OrderDate` path. Leaving `ShippedDate` inactive guarantees nothing about that changes.\n- **D Use `USERELATIONSHIP`** → *\"filter by using ShippedDate in a new report.\"* Inside `CALCULATE`, it activates an inactive relationship **for that evaluation only**:\n\n```dax\nSales by Ship Date =\nCALCULATE(\n    [Total Sales],\n    USERELATIONSHIP( FactSales[ShippedDate], DimDate[Date] )\n)\n```\n\nThe new measure follows the ship-date path; every other measure in the model is untouched. This is the **role-playing dimension** pattern — one `DimDate` serving several date roles.\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **A Enable the ShippedDate relationship** ✘ — the direct contradiction of the requirement. If it could even be done, it would deactivate the `OrderDate` relationship and silently change the meaning of **every existing report**.\n- **B `RELATEDTABLE`** ✘ — returns the related rows of another table (the table-valued counterpart of `RELATED`). It follows whichever relationship is **active**; it cannot switch to an inactive one.\n- **C `CROSSFILTER`** ✘ — the tempting one, because it *is* a `CALCULATE` relationship modifier. But it changes a relationship's **cross-filter direction** (`OneWay`, `Both`, `None`) — it cannot activate an inactive relationship. `USERELATIONSHIP` changes *which* relationship is used; `CROSSFILTER` changes *how* filters travel along one.\n\n*Alternative worth knowing (not offered here):* duplicate `DimDate` into `DimOrderDate` and `DimShipDate` and make both relationships active. That avoids `USERELATIONSHIP` in every measure at the cost of a second date table, and is often preferred when report authors — not modellers — will be building the visuals.",
    "answer": "DE",
    "officialAnswer": "DE",
    "original": {
      "type": "multi",
      "officialAnswer": "DE",
      "answer": "DE",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains a fact table named FactSales. FactSales has a\nrelationship to a dimension table named DimDate on a column named OrderDate.\nTo FactSales, you add a second relationship between FactSales and DimDate based on a column named ShippedDate.\nYou need to filter by using ShippedDate in a new report. The solution must NOT affect the function of existing reports.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Enable the ShippedDate relationship.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Use a RELATEDTABLE DAX function.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Use a CROSSFILTER DAX function.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Use a USERELATIONSHIP DAX function.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "E",
          "text": "Disable the ShippedDate relationship between the tables.",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**D + E — disable the `ShippedDate` relationship, and use `USERELATIONSHIP` in the new measures.**\n\nA tabular model allows **only one active relationship between any two tables**. `FactSales` already has an active relationship to `DimDate` on `OrderDate`, so the new `ShippedDate` relationship must be **inactive** — the model will not even permit two active ones, and Power BI creates the second as inactive (dashed line) automatically.\n\nThe pair of answers maps onto the two requirements:\n\n- **E Disable the ShippedDate relationship** → *\"must NOT affect the function of existing reports.\"* Every existing measure resolves through the active `OrderDate` path. Leaving `ShippedDate` inactive guarantees nothing about that changes.\n- **D Use `USERELATIONSHIP`** → *\"filter by using ShippedDate in a new report.\"* Inside `CALCULATE`, it activates an inactive relationship **for that evaluation only**:\n\n```dax\nSales by Ship Date =\nCALCULATE(\n    [Total Sales],\n    USERELATIONSHIP( FactSales[ShippedDate], DimDate[Date] )\n)\n```\n\nThe new measure follows the ship-date path; every other measure in the model is untouched. This is the **role-playing dimension** pattern — one `DimDate` serving several date roles.\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **A Enable the ShippedDate relationship** ✘ — the direct contradiction of the requirement. If it could even be done, it would deactivate the `OrderDate` relationship and silently change the meaning of **every existing report**.\n- **B `RELATEDTABLE`** ✘ — returns the related rows of another table (the table-valued counterpart of `RELATED`). It follows whichever relationship is **active**; it cannot switch to an inactive one.\n- **C `CROSSFILTER`** ✘ — the tempting one, because it *is* a `CALCULATE` relationship modifier. But it changes a relationship's **cross-filter direction** (`OneWay`, `Both`, `None`) — it cannot activate an inactive relationship. `USERELATIONSHIP` changes *which* relationship is used; `CROSSFILTER` changes *how* filters travel along one.\n\n*Alternative worth knowing (not offered here):* duplicate `DimDate` into `DimOrderDate` and `DimShipDate` and make both relationships active. That avoids `USERELATIONSHIP` in every measure at the cost of a second date table, and is often preferred when report authors — not modellers — will be building the visuals."
    },
    "sourceVisualReferences": {
      "exhibits": [],
