  {
    "id": "T1Q216",
    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou need to create a semantic model that delivers fast report rendering, minimizes query latency, and maximizes flexibility for DAX calculations.\nWhich storage mode should you select?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Import"
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Direct Lake"
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Composite Mode"
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "DirectQuery"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q216",
    "sourceId": "T1Q216",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED_WITH_NONBLOCKING_EDITS",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**A — Import.** Three requirements are listed, and the **third** is the one that decides it.\n\n| Requirement | Import | Direct Lake | DirectQuery | Composite |\n|---|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|\n| Fast report rendering | ✔ | ✔ | ✘ | mixed |\n| Minimal query latency | ✔ | ✔ | ✘ | mixed |\n| **Maximum DAX flexibility** | **✔** | **✘** | ✘ | mixed |\n\n**Import wins on DAX flexibility.** The full model is loaded into the VertiPaq in-memory engine, so **everything** is available: calculated columns, calculated tables, every DAX function, any relationship configuration, no formula restrictions.\n\n**Direct Lake is the 33% answer and the one to think about.** It genuinely delivers the first two requirements — near-Import query speed by loading Delta/Parquet columns straight into memory on demand, with no refresh. But it is **restricted on DAX**: Direct Lake tables do not support **calculated columns or calculated tables**, and certain modelling constructs are unavailable. A model that hits an unsupported construct also **falls back to DirectQuery**, at which point requirements 1 and 2 collapse too. When a question explicitly asks for *maximum flexibility for DAX calculations*, that clause exists to exclude Direct Lake.\n\n- **D DirectQuery** ✘ — fails every requirement. Each visual issues a live query to the source, so rendering is slow, latency is high, and DAX is the most restricted of all (no calculated tables, many functions blocked, and measures must be foldable).\n- **C Composite** ✘ — mixes storage modes per table. Useful for real engineering trade-offs (see T1Q205), but it *adds* complexity and its DirectQuery portions inherit DirectQuery's limits. It maximises nothing.\n\n*The trade-off Direct Lake buys you, which this question deliberately does not ask for, is **data freshness** — no refresh cycle, data is current as of the last Delta commit. If the stem had said \"always reflects the latest data in OneLake\", the answer would flip to B.*",
    "answer": "A",
    "officialAnswer": "A",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "A",
      "answer": "A",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou need to create a semantic model that delivers fast report rendering, minimizes query latency, and maximizes flexibility for DAX calculations.\nWhich storage mode should you select?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Import",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Direct Lake",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Composite Mode",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "DirectQuery",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**A — Import.** Three requirements are listed, and the **third** is the one that decides it.\n\n| Requirement | Import | Direct Lake | DirectQuery | Composite |\n|---|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|\n| Fast report rendering | ✔ | ✔ | ✘ | mixed |\n| Minimal query latency | ✔ | ✔ | ✘ | mixed |\n| **Maximum DAX flexibility** | **✔** | **✘** | ✘ | mixed |\n\n**Import wins on DAX flexibility.** The full model is loaded into the VertiPaq in-memory engine, so **everything** is available: calculated columns, calculated tables, every DAX function, any relationship configuration, no formula restrictions.\n\n**Direct Lake is the 33% answer and the one to think about.** It genuinely delivers the first two requirements — near-Import query speed by loading Delta/Parquet columns straight into memory on demand, with no refresh. But it is **restricted on DAX**: Direct Lake tables do not support **calculated columns or calculated tables**, and certain modelling constructs are unavailable. A model that hits an unsupported construct also **falls back to DirectQuery**, at which point requirements 1 and 2 collapse too. When a question explicitly asks for *maximum flexibility for DAX calculations*, that clause exists to exclude Direct Lake.\n\n- **D DirectQuery** ✘ — fails every requirement. Each visual issues a live query to the source, so rendering is slow, latency is high, and DAX is the most restricted of all (no calculated tables, many functions blocked, and measures must be foldable).\n- **C Composite** ✘ — mixes storage modes per table. Useful for real engineering trade-offs (see T1Q205), but it *adds* complexity and its DirectQuery portions inherit DirectQuery's limits. It maximises nothing.\n\n*The trade-off Direct Lake buys you, which this question deliberately does not ask for, is **data freshness** — no refresh cycle, data is current as of the last Delta commit. If the stem had said \"always reflects the latest data in OneLake\", the answer would flip to B.*"
    },
