  {
    "id": "T1Q219",
    "type": "hotspot-yesno",
    "stem": "You have a data warehouse that contains the following tables:\n• DimCustomer\n• DimEmployee\n• DimGeography\n• FactInternetSales\nEach table has a primary key and the following relationships:\n• CustomerKey = FactInternetSales.CustomerKey\n• EmployeeKey = FactInternetSales.EmployeeKey\n• Geography = DimCustomer.GeographyKey\nYou have a T-SQL query named Query1 that contains the following statements.\n\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
    "statements": [
      {
        "text": "Query1 will return multiple rows for a single customer if the customer has placed more than one online sales order.",
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "text": "If a customer record in DimCustomer has a GeographyKey value that does NOT exist in DimGeography, the customer's sales data will still appear in the Query1 results.",
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "text": "Adding the clause GROUP BY g.EnglishCountryRegionName to Query1 will calculate the total sales amount by country.",
        "answer": "No"
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [
      {
        "kind": "code",
        "title": "Query1 — original SQL joins",
        "language": "sql",
        "content": "SELECT\n    c.CustomerKey,\n    c.FirstName,\n    c.LastName,\n    g.CountryRegionCode,\n    g.EnglishCountryRegionName AS Country,\n    e.EmployeeKey,\n    e.FirstName AS SalesPersonFirstName,\n    e.LastName AS SalesPersonLastName,\n    f.SalesOrderNumber,\n    f.OrderDate,\n    f.SalesAmount\nFROM FactInternetSales AS f\nINNER JOIN DimCustomer AS c\n    ON f.CustomerKey = c.CustomerKey\nINNER JOIN DimEmployee AS e\n    ON f.EmployeeKey = e.EmployeeKey\nINNER JOIN DimGeography AS g\n    ON c.GeographyKey = g.GeographyKey;"
      }
    ],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q219",
    "sourceId": "T1Q219",
    "gradingEnabled": false,
    "scientificStatus": "R10_APPROVAL_CANDIDATE_NOT_REVALIDATED",
    "r10Status": "APPROVED",
    "reviewNote": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "remaining": "R11 second-pass review not performed; retained R10 finding is a lead, not new verification.",
    "explanation": "**Yes — No — No.** The query is a plain fact-to-dimension join with **no aggregation and no grouping**:\n\n```sql\nSELECT c.CustomerKey, c.FirstName, c.LastName,\n       g.CountryRegionCode, g.EnglishCountryRegionName AS Country,\n       e.EmployeeKey, e.FirstName AS SalesPersonFirstName, e.LastName AS SalesPersonLastName,\n       f.SalesOrderNumber, f.OrderDate, f.SalesAmount\nFROM FactInternetSales AS f\nINNER JOIN DimCustomer AS c ON f.CustomerKey = c.CustomerKey\nINNER JOIN DimEmployee AS e ON f.EmployeeKey = e.EmployeeKey\nINNER JOIN DimGeography AS g ON c.GeographyKey = g.GeographyKey;\n```\n\n**1. Multiple rows for one customer with several orders → Yes.** The query is at **fact grain**: one row per `FactInternetSales` row. Joining a fact to a dimension on a many-to-one key does not collapse anything — the customer's attributes are simply repeated on each of their order rows. Three orders, three rows. (`SELECT` has no `DISTINCT` and no `GROUP BY` to change that.)\n\n**2. Customer with an orphan GeographyKey still appears → No.** This is the statement the question is really built on. The join to `DimGeography` is an **`INNER JOIN`**, which keeps only rows that match on **both** sides. A `GeographyKey` with no counterpart in `DimGeography` fails the predicate, so that customer's rows are **silently dropped from the result set entirely** — their sales disappear, even though `FactInternetSales` and `DimCustomer` matched perfectly. This is the classic referential-integrity trap: one bad dimension key quietly deletes revenue from the report. A `LEFT OUTER JOIN` to `DimGeography` would preserve the rows with `NULL` country (compare T1Q177).\n\n**3. Adding `GROUP BY g.EnglishCountryRegionName` totals sales by country → No.** Two independent reasons, either one fatal:\n- **It would not even run.** Every column in the `SELECT` list must be either in the `GROUP BY` or wrapped in an aggregate. Grouping by country alone leaves `CustomerKey`, `FirstName`, `SalesOrderNumber`, `OrderDate` and nine others unaccounted for → *\"Column … is invalid in the select list because it is not contained in either an aggregate function or the GROUP BY clause.\"*\n- **There is no aggregate to total.** `f.SalesAmount` is selected raw. `GROUP BY` does not sum anything by itself — you would have to change it to `SUM(f.SalesAmount)` **and** reduce the `SELECT` list to the grouped columns.\n\n**`GROUP BY` alone never aggregates — it only defines the groups. The aggregation has to be written explicitly in the `SELECT` list.**\n\n*(The exhibit contains two typos carried over from the exam — `DimCustomr` and `f.Employee`. Neither affects the three statements.)*",
    "answer": null,
    "officialAnswer": "",
    "original": {
      "type": "hotspot-yesno",
      "officialAnswer": "",
      "statements": [
        {
          "text": "Query1 will return multiple rows for a single customer if the customer has placed more than one online sales order.",
          "answer": "Yes"
        },
        {
          "text": "If a customer record in DimCustomer has a GeographyKey value that does NOT exist in DimGeography, the customer's sales data will still appear in the Query1 results.",
          "answer": "No"
        },
        {
          "text": "Adding the clause GROUP BY g.EnglishCountryRegionName to Query1 will calculate the total sales amount by country.",
          "answer": "No"
        }
      ],
      "stem": "You have a data warehouse that contains the following tables:\n• DimCustomer\n• DimEmployee\n• DimGeography\n• FactInternetSales\nEach table has a primary key and the following relationships:\n• CustomerKey = FactInternetSales.CustomerKey\n• EmployeeKey = FactInternetSales.EmployeeKey\n• Geography = DimCustomer.GeographyKey\nYou have a T-SQL query named Query1 that contains the following statements.\n\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**Yes — No — No.** The query is a plain fact-to-dimension join with **no aggregation and no grouping**:\n\n```sql\nSELECT c.CustomerKey, c.FirstName, c.LastName,\n       g.CountryRegionCode, g.EnglishCountryRegionName AS Country,\n       e.EmployeeKey, e.FirstName AS SalesPersonFirstName, e.LastName AS SalesPersonLastName,\n       f.SalesOrderNumber, f.OrderDate, f.SalesAmount\nFROM FactInternetSales AS f\nINNER JOIN DimCustomer AS c ON f.CustomerKey = c.CustomerKey\nINNER JOIN DimEmployee AS e ON f.EmployeeKey = e.EmployeeKey\nINNER JOIN DimGeography AS g ON c.GeographyKey = g.GeographyKey;\n```\n\n**1. Multiple rows for one customer with several orders → Yes.** The query is at **fact grain**: one row per `FactInternetSales` row. Joining a fact to a dimension on a many-to-one key does not collapse anything — the customer's attributes are simply repeated on each of their order rows. Three orders, three rows. (`SELECT` has no `DISTINCT` and no `GROUP BY` to change that.)\n\n**2. Customer with an orphan GeographyKey still appears → No.** This is the statement the question is really built on. The join to `DimGeography` is an **`INNER JOIN`**, which keeps only rows that match on **both** sides. A `GeographyKey` with no counterpart in `DimGeography` fails the predicate, so that customer's rows are **silently dropped from the result set entirely** — their sales disappear, even though `FactInternetSales` and `DimCustomer` matched perfectly. This is the classic referential-integrity trap: one bad dimension key quietly deletes revenue from the report. A `LEFT OUTER JOIN` to `DimGeography` would preserve the rows with `NULL` country (compare T1Q177).\n\n**3. Adding `GROUP BY g.EnglishCountryRegionName` totals sales by country → No.** Two independent reasons, either one fatal:\n- **It would not even run.** Every column in the `SELECT` list must be either in the `GROUP BY` or wrapped in an aggregate. Grouping by country alone leaves `CustomerKey`, `FirstName`, `SalesOrderNumber`, `OrderDate` and nine others unaccounted for → *\"Column … is invalid in the select list because it is not contained in either an aggregate function or the GROUP BY clause.\"*\n- **There is no aggregate to total.** `f.SalesAmount` is selected raw. `GROUP BY` does not sum anything by itself — you would have to change it to `SUM(f.SalesAmount)` **and** reduce the `SELECT` list to the grouped columns.\n\n**`GROUP BY` alone never aggregates — it only defines the groups. The aggregation has to be written explicitly in the `SELECT` list.**\n\n*(The exhibit contains two typos carried over from the exam — `DimCustomr` and `f.Employee`. Neither affects the three statements.)*"
    },
