      "stem": "You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named LH1 and a notebook.\nYou have a Parquet file named invoice1 that contains a column named InvoiceDateKey of the timestamp data type.\nYou need to create a PySpark script that will import invoice1 and create a table named fact_sale in LH1. The solution must meet the following\nrequirements:\n• A new column named Year must be added to fact_sale.\n• The values in Year must use the InvoiceDateKey column.\nHow should you complete the script? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.",
      "explanation": "**`withColumn`** and **`col`**. The finished script:\n\n```python\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import *\ntable_name = 'Table/fact_sale'\ndf = spark.read.format(\"parquet\").load('Files/parquet/full/invoice1')\ndf = df.withColumn('Year', year(col(\"InvoiceDateKey\")))\ndf.write.mode(\"overwrite\").format(\"delta\").partitionBy(\"Year\").save(table_name)\n```\n\n### Blank 1 — `withColumn`\n\nThe requirement is *\"a **new column** named `Year` must be **added**\"*, and `DataFrame.withColumn(colName, col)` is the PySpark method for exactly that: it returns a new DataFrame with a column added (or replaced, if the name already exists). Its signature — a **name** followed by a **Column expression** — matches the two arguments visible in the exhibit.\n\n| Method | What it does | |\n|---|---|:-:|\n| **`withColumn(name, expr)`** | **add or replace a column** | **✔** |\n| `withColumnRenamed(old, new)` | **rename** an existing column — takes two *strings*, adds nothing | ✘ |\n| `withMetadata(name, metadata)` | attach metadata to an existing column; no data change | ✘ |\n| `transform(func)` | apply a whole function to the DataFrame for chaining; takes a callable, not `(name, expr)` | ✘ |\n\n### Blank 2 — `col`\n\n`year()` needs a **Column object**, and `col(\"InvoiceDateKey\")` is how you reference an existing column by name. `year()` then extracts the year from the timestamp — which is the second requirement, *\"the values in `Year` must use the `InvoiceDateKey` column\"*.\n\n- **`lit`** ✘ — the instructive wrong answer. `lit(\"InvoiceDateKey\")` creates a **literal constant**: it would hand `year()` the *string* `\"InvoiceDateKey\"` rather than the column's values, so every row would get the same (null) result. `lit` is for constants, `col` is for columns.\n- **`extract`** ✘ — not a function for this. Spark SQL has an `extract(field FROM source)` **expression** (`extract(YEAR FROM ts)`), but it is not the way to reference a column inside `year()`, and wrapping it here is syntactically wrong.\n\n*(`df[\"InvoiceDateKey\"]` and `F.col(...)` are equivalent ways to write the same reference; the wildcard import at the top is what makes the bare `year` and `col` available.)*\n\nThe final line writes the result as a **Delta** table partitioned by the new `Year` column — the standard lakehouse landing pattern."
