    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "B",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains the following items:\n• A warehouse named Warehouse1\n• A semantic model named Model1\n• An interactive report named Report1\nYou need to allow a user named User1 to access a single table in Warehouse1. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege.\nWhat should you do first?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Assign the Viewer role to User1 for Workspace1.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Share Warehouse1 with User1.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Assign object-level permissions to User1 for Warehouse1.",
          "mostVoted": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Assign the db_datareader role to User1 for Warehouse1.",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**B — share Warehouse1 with User1** (with no additional permissions selected).\n\n> ⚠️ **Flagged: this overrides the PDF answer key,** which marks **C** (assign object-level permissions). The discussion leans C (75%), but the lone dissenter is quoting Microsoft's documented procedure, and the question asks what to do ***first***.\n\nMicrosoft spells out the two-step recipe for exactly this requirement:\n\n> *\"If you want to provide **granular access to specific objects** within the Warehouse, **share the Warehouse with no additional permissions**, then provide granular access to specific objects using T-SQL `GRANT` statement.\"*\n\nThe order is not arbitrary. Sharing grants the **Read** permission, which is *\"the equivalent of `CONNECT` permissions in SQL Server\"* — without it User1 cannot even open a connection to the warehouse, and a `GRANT SELECT ON dbo.Table1` would have no one to apply to. You must open the door before you can unlock one room inside it.\n\n| Step | Action | Effect |\n|:-:|---|---|\n| **1** | **Share Warehouse1, no boxes ticked** | **Read** → User1 can *connect*, sees no data |\n| 2 | `GRANT SELECT ON dbo.Table1 TO [User1]` | User1 can query **that one table** |\n\nThe result is the narrowest possible access: one table, in one warehouse, and nothing else in Workspace1 — which is what *\"principle of least privilege\"* demands.\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **C Assign object-level permissions** ✘ *as the **first** step* — this is step **2**, and it is the right *second* action. Attempting it first leaves User1 unable to connect. (If your exam marks C, the examiner is reading \"first\" loosely; know the dependency either way.)\n- **A Assign the Viewer role for Workspace1** ✘ — workspace-wide. Viewer carries **ReadData**, so User1 would be able to read **every table in Warehouse1**, plus see Model1 and Report1. Far broader than one table.\n- **D Assign the db_datareader role** ✘ — grants `SELECT` on **all** tables and views. It is the definition of the opposite of granular, and Fabric warehouses use item permissions plus T-SQL `GRANT` rather than SQL Server database roles.\n\n*(Same Read-vs-ReadData distinction as T1Q193, applied one step further.)*"
    },
