    "type": "mcq",
    "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a semantic model and a report named Report1. Workspace1 is connected to a Git\nrepository named Git1.\nYou have a deployment pipeline that contains three stages named Development, Test, and Production.\nYou need to publish Report1 to the Production stage.\nWhat should you do?\nWorkspace1 is assigned to Development and already contains the desired report and model versions. Publish all supported content forward through Test and then Production, with any required checks completed at each stage.",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Run a Git commit."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Deploy all content from Development to Test, then deploy all content from Test to Production."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Sync Workspace1 with Git1."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Run a backward deployment."
      }
    ],
    "semanticVisuals": [],
    "caseStudy": null,
    "hansaId": "new-json-t1q222",
    "sourceId": "T1Q222",
    "gradingEnabled": true,
    "scientificStatus": "APPROVED_CORRECTED",
    "r10Status": "CORRECTION_PROPOSED",
    "reviewNote": "Deploy all and selective deployment operate on adjacent stages.",
    "remaining": "",
    "explanation": "B performs two forward deployments between adjacent stages. A records workspace changes in Git but does not promote them. C updates only the Git-connected workspace. D moves content in the opposite direction. A single adjacent-stage deployment does not by itself reach Production from Development.",
    "answer": "B",
    "officialAnswer": "C",
    "original": {
      "type": "mcq",
      "officialAnswer": "C",
      "answer": "B",
      "stem": "You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a semantic model and a report named Report1. Workspace1 is connected to a Git\nrepository named Git1.\nYou have a deployment pipeline that contains three stages named Development, Test, and Production.\nYou need to publish Report1 to the Production stage.\nWhat should you do?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Run a Git commit.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "Run a full deployment.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Sync Workspace1 with Git1.",
          "mostVoted": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Run a backward deployment.",
          "mostVoted": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "**B — run a full deployment.**\n\n> ⚠️ **Flagged: this overrides the PDF answer key,** which marks **C** (sync Workspace1 with Git1). The discussion is **100% for B**, and the two mechanisms move content in different directions.\n\nThe question asks how to get Report1 **into the Production stage**, and Production is a **deployment pipeline** stage. Deployment pipelines are the only mechanism here that moves content *between workspaces*:\n\n- **Full deployment** = deploy **all** content from the current stage to the next one. Run it Development → Test, then Test → Production, and Report1 (with its semantic model) lands in Production.\n- **Selective deployment** would also work if you only wanted Report1 — but it is not offered, and \"full deployment\" is the option that performs a deployment at all.\n\n**Why C is wrong.** Git integration and deployment pipelines are **orthogonal**, and this is the distinction the question is really about:\n\n| | What it connects | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Git integration** | one **workspace** ↔ one **branch** | commit (workspace → Git) and update/sync (Git → workspace) |\n| **Deployment pipelines** | **workspace → workspace** | Development → Test → Production |\n\nSyncing Workspace1 with Git1 pulls the branch's state **into Workspace1**. Workspace1 is the *Development* workspace — the sync never touches Test or Production. You could sync all day and Report1 would still not exist in Production.\n\n- **A Run a Git commit** ✘ — the other half of the same misdirection: it pushes Workspace1's state **up to Git1**. Useful for version control, irrelevant to reaching Production.\n- **D Run a backward deployment** ✘ — deploys from a **later** stage to an **earlier** one (Production → Test, say), which is the opposite of what is needed. It is also only possible when the target stage is empty.\n\n**Rule: Git = version control for one workspace. Deployment pipelines = promotion between workspaces. \"Publish to Production\" is always a deployment.**"
    },
